export const MOR_UNITS = [{"id":"u7","title":"Understanding the MOR Process","chapters":["m71","m72","m73"]},{"id":"u8","title":"Desk Review Preparation","chapters":["m81","m82","m83"]},{"id":"u9","title":"Tenant Files & Certifications","chapters":["m91","m92","m93","m94"]},{"id":"u10","title":"Income, Assets & EIV Compliance","chapters":["m101","m102","m103"]},{"id":"u11","title":"Waiting List, TSP & Fair Housing","chapters":["m111","m112","m113"]},{"id":"u12","title":"VAWA, Leases & Financial Management","chapters":["m121","m122","m123"]},{"id":"u13","title":"Building Your MOR Consulting Business","chapters":["m131","m132","m133"]}];
export const MOR_CHAPTERS = [{"id":"m71","unit":"u7","number":1,"title":"What Is a MOR? Purpose, Authority & HUD Form 9834","subtitle":"","sections":[{"heading":"Purpose and Legal Authority of the MOR","body":"A Management and Occupancy Review (MOR) is HUD's on-the-record judgment about whether an assisted multifamily property is being ADMINISTERED in compliance with its HAP contract, its regulatory agreement, and the occupancy rules in Handbook 4350.3 REV-1. It is a review of paperwork, policies, processes and management practice. It is not a physical inspection: physical condition is scored separately under NSPIRE, on a different schedule, by different inspectors, with a different score that does not roll into the MOR rating.\n\nThe authority is contractual before it is regulatory, and that ordering matters when a client asks \"can they really make me do this?\"\n\n• The HAP contract itself obligates the owner to permit HUD or its designee to review project management and to examine tenant records. Refusing entry is a contract breach, not merely an act of non-cooperation.\n• The Section 8 program regulations impose the review requirement directly. For Section 8 New Construction/Substantial Rehabilitation the management review provision sits at 24 CFR 880.612; the Loan Management and Property Disposition programs carry the parallel provision at 24 CFR 886.130. The June 27, 2022 final rule, \"Streamlining Management and Occupancy Reviews for Section 8 Housing Assistance Programs\" (87 FR 38227, effective September 26, 2022), amended those sections to install the risk-based schedule taught in Section 4 of this chapter.\n• The regulatory agreement (for insured and 202/811 capital-advance properties) independently obligates the owner to maintain the books, records and reserve accounts a reviewer will ask for.\n• HUD Handbook 4350.1, Chapter 6 supplies the procedural rules that govern the review itself: how it is conducted, how the report is transmitted, and how it is appealed.\n\nWorked example 1 — the \"we're privately owned\" objection. An owner of a 120-unit Section 8 New Construction property tells you the CA has no right to look at tenant files because the property is privately owned and the tenants are private parties. The correct answer is not a fair-housing lecture; it is a contract reading. The owner signed a HAP contract in exchange for subsidy payments, and that contract conditions payment on HUD's access to the records that substantiate the payment. The privacy interest belongs to the TENANT, and the tenant has already released it: the HUD-9887/9887-A signed at move-in and at each recertification authorizes HUD and its designees to obtain and review the information. Nothing about private ownership changes either fact.\n\nWorked example 2 — the boundary between MOR and NSPIRE. A property scores 61 on its NSPIRE inspection and the owner assumes the MOR will be bad too. Wrong analysis. The two do not feed each other arithmetically. But they DO interact through one specific channel: Form 9834 asks about the maintenance MANAGEMENT system — work order tracking, turnaround time, preventive maintenance schedules, follow-up on inspection findings. A property can have failing physical conditions and still document a disciplined work order system (which will show up as unaddressed capital needs, not as a maintenance-management deficiency), and a property in beautiful condition can be rated deficient because there is no work order log at all. Teach clients to separate the two: NSPIRE scores the building, the MOR scores the office.\n\nWorked example 3 — the sub-agent problem. A management agent subcontracts its certification processing to a third-party compliance firm. The certifications come back late and wrong. The owner argues the finding belongs to the vendor. It does not. The MOR is issued to the OWNER; the owner is the party to the HAP contract, and the owner remains responsible for the acts of the agent and of anyone the agent hires. As a consultant this is the moment to reframe the engagement: the client's exposure is not reduced by outsourcing, so the client's oversight of the vendor becomes part of what you build.\n\nWorked example 4 — the ambiguous case worth arguing. A CA cites a deficiency for failure to maintain a document that is not required by 4350.3, the HAP contract, the regulatory agreement, or Form 9834 — it is the CA's own preferred practice. This happens. The response is not refusal; it is a written request that the finding identify its authority. Findings must rest on a requirement. A well-drafted response that says \"we have implemented the practice, and we request the citation supporting it as a requirement so that it can be incorporated into our written policy\" resolves most of these without a fight, and creates a record if it does not.\n\nThe consulting frame: your client is not studying for a test. They are meeting binding contractual obligations whose breach can produce repayment demands, suspension of HAP payments, forced replacement of the management agent, or contract termination. That is what justifies the fee."},{"heading":"HUD Form 9834 — The MOR Instrument","body":"Form HUD-9834, \"Management Review for Multifamily Housing Projects,\" is the instrument. Every MOR in the country is conducted on it, and every finding a reviewer writes must map to a line on it. If a client wants to know exactly what will be asked, the answer is not a rumor from another property manager; it is the 47-page form, which is public.\n\nStructure — memorize this, because it drives your entire engagement design:\n\n• PART I — DESK REVIEW. Completed BEFORE the reviewer arrives, using documents the owner submits plus HUD system data the reviewer pulls independently (TRACS, iREMS, APPS, financial statements in FASS, EIV where the reviewer has access). By the time the reviewer walks in, a substantial share of the rating is already provisionally formed. This is the single most under-appreciated fact about MOR preparation, and Unit 8 of this course exists because of it.\n• PART II — ON-SITE REVIEW, carrying three addenda: Addendum A, the Tenant File Checklist, used file-by-file for the sampled tenants; Addendum B, the financial addendum, which is NOT reviewed by all Contract Administrators — some CA contracts exclude it and leave financial review to HUD; and Addendum C, the Required Documents List, which is effectively the reviewer's packing list of what must be produced.\n• PART III — SUMMARY REPORT. The narrative, the ratings, the findings, and the corrective actions with due dates.\n\nThe review is organized into SEVEN lettered categories, A through G. Category D is reserved for HUD staff and Mortgagee reviewers; Contract Administrators do not rate it. Everything else is in play for a CA review. The practical consequence: the same property reviewed by a CA and by HUD staff will not be evaluated on an identical set of items, and a consultant who promises a client \"here is everything they will look at\" without asking who is conducting the review is over-promising.\n\nWorked example 1 — using the form as the engagement scope. You are engaged by a 200-unit Section 8 property whose CA does not review Addendum B. A consultant who builds a 40-hour engagement around reconstructing three years of reserve-for-replacement documentation has mis-scoped the work: that material will not be scored in this review. The right move is to confirm the CA's scope in writing at intake, then weight your hours toward Addendum A tenant files and the Part I desk review, where the rating will actually be decided. (Note the strategic caveat: financial deficiencies not scored by the CA can still be raised by HUD asset management separately. You tell the client the truth — out of scope for THIS review, still a real obligation.)\n\nWorked example 2 — the self-assessment build. The highest-yield deliverable in MOR consulting is a mirror of Form 9834 turned into an internal checklist, run by site staff quarterly. Take Addendum A, put it at the front of every tenant file as a cover sheet, and initial each item at each certification. A file that has been walked through Addendum A four times before the reviewer sees it fails on Addendum A almost never. This one habit moves more properties from Satisfactory to Above Average than any other intervention.\n\nWorked example 3 — the item that is not on the form. A reviewer notes in the narrative that the property's community room is used for a for-profit tax preparation service on weekends. There is no 9834 line for that. It is not scored — but it appears in the report as a narrative observation and it can be referred to HUD asset management as a possible regulatory agreement issue. Teach clients the distinction: SCORED items are on the form; OBSERVED items can be anywhere and can still cause trouble.\n\nWorked example 4 — the fact that surprises everyone. Form HUD-9834's OMB approval has, in the formal sense, been expired since 2018 — and HUD has nonetheless continued to direct reviewers to use the current version. A tenant advocate or an aggressive owner's attorney occasionally seizes on this and argues the form cannot be used. Practically, this argument goes nowhere: the underlying obligations are contractual and regulatory, and the form is the vehicle, not the source. But it is worth knowing for two reasons. First, a consultant who is caught unaware by it looks unprepared. Second, it is a clean illustration of a habit of mind this profession requires: the formal regulatory status of a document and its operational status are two different things, and a working consultant has to track both. You will meet this gap again with handbook chapters that were superseded in substance but never reissued, and with HOTMA compliance dates that moved three times.\n\nWhere to get it: the current form and its instructions are posted on HUD's forms site. Keep a copy dated, because when it is revised, the revision is the first thing that changes about your practice."},{"heading":"Who Conducts the MOR — HUD Staff vs. Contract Administrators","body":"Two different bodies conduct MORs, and the difference changes what your client should expect.\n\n• HUD STAFF (the Multifamily Regional Center or Satellite Office, working through the Account Executive assigned to the property) conduct reviews directly for properties not covered by a Performance-Based Contract Administrator, and for properties where HUD elects to review directly. HUD staff rate all seven categories including Category D.\n• PERFORMANCE-BASED CONTRACT ADMINISTRATORS (PBCAs) — usually a state housing finance agency or a designated entity, under an Annual Contributions Contract with HUD — conduct most Section 8 project-based MORs. They rate categories A through G except D. Their contract with HUD specifies scope, timing and reporting, which is why practice varies between states in ways that confuse owners with portfolios in more than one state.\n\nThe variation is real and you must handle it explicitly with clients. HUD's five-level rating scale (Chapter 2) is national. What is NOT national:\n\n• Whether Addendum B financial review is in the CA's scope.\n• Whether the CA overlays a numeric scoring band on the five levels, and where the cut points sit. One state agency anchors its bands at 95 / 85 / 75 / 65; another treats anything under 70 as below-average or unacceptable. Same HUD scale, different arithmetic on the cover page of the report.\n• How much notice is given, whether files are sampled in advance or drawn on arrival, and whether the desk review package is submitted through a portal or by mail.\n• Local addenda: some CAs attach state-specific checklists for state-funded layers on the same property.\n\nWorked example 1 — the two-state portfolio. A client owns eight properties, five in State A and three in State B. In State A the last three MORs came back \"92 — Above Average.\" In State B the reports say only \"Above Average,\" with no number. The client concludes State B is hiding something. Nothing is hidden: the numeric band is the CA's own convention, not HUD's. Your job at intake is to obtain the last two MOR reports for EACH property and read the actual scoring convention off the report, rather than generalizing from the state the client knows best.\n\nWorked example 2 — the naive-reading trap. A consultant reads on a message board that \"under 70 is a failing MOR\" and tells a client in a different state that their 74 is nearly failing. In that state, 75 is the Satisfactory anchor and 74 is a mild miss, while in the state the message board came from, 74 was comfortably mid-range. The rule to teach: the five HUD levels are the substance; any number attached to them is local and must be read off the specific CA's methodology, which you request in writing.\n\nWorked example 3 — who to call when the finding is wrong. A CA reviewer cites a deficiency that the owner believes misreads the handbook. The escalation path is NOT to call HUD headquarters. It runs: (1) raise it at the exit conference, on the record; (2) address it in the written response to the report; (3) if unresolved and the rating is Below Average or Unsatisfactory, use the formal appeal (Section 5). Note also that Below Average and Unsatisfactory ratings are forwarded to the HUD Account Executive regardless — so the AE will see the file whether or not the owner appeals.\n\nWorked example 4 — a change of contract administrator mid-cycle. A state's PBCA contract is re-competed and a new entity takes over. The property's next MOR is conducted by a CA that samples differently and applies its own numeric band. Nothing about the property changed, and the rating moves from \"Above Average, 91\" to \"Satisfactory.\" This is a real and recurring source of client panic. Your analysis: compare the FINDINGS lists, not the labels. If the new report cites three findings the old one never mentioned, ask whether those items were previously out of scope, previously reviewed and passed, or newly deficient. Only the third case is a management problem.\n\nWorked example 5 — the mortgagee review. For some HUD-insured properties, the mortgagee (lender) conducts a servicing review that overlaps MOR territory, and Category D exists partly for this world. Owners frequently conflate a mortgagee's physical/financial servicing review with a MOR. They are different reviews with different consequences; when a client says \"we just had our HUD review,\" always ask who conducted it and ask to see the transmittal letter."},{"heading":"MOR Frequency — What Triggers a Review","body":"Before September 26, 2022, the operating assumption was an annual MOR for essentially everyone. That is gone, and a consultant still saying \"your MOR is due every year\" is describing a world that no longer exists.\n\nThe 2022 final rule installed a RISK-BASED, PERFORMANCE-BASED schedule. The interval to the NEXT MOR is a function of TWO inputs: (1) the property's risk classification under Multifamily's risk-based management model — Troubled, Potentially Troubled, or Not Troubled — and (2) the score on the PREVIOUS MOR.\n\nThe matrix:\n\n• Below Average or Unsatisfactory, at ANY risk classification (Troubled, Potentially Troubled, or Not Troubled) → next MOR within 12 MONTHS.\n• Satisfactory, at Troubled or Potentially Troubled → within 24 MONTHS. Satisfactory at Not Troubled → within 24 MONTHS.\n• Above Average or Superior, at Troubled → within 24 MONTHS. Above Average or Superior at Potentially Troubled or Not Troubled → within 36 MONTHS.\n\nAnd one override that ignores all of the above: regardless of risk classification and regardless of score, a property must have a new MOR WITHIN 6 MONTHS of any HUD-approved change of ownership or change of management agent.\n\nRead the matrix carefully and notice what it says: a poor score accelerates the schedule no matter how healthy the property looks financially, and a Troubled classification caps the reward for a great score at 24 months rather than 36.\n\nWorked example 1 — the two-cycle improvement story (build this for every client). Maple Court, 84 units, Section 8 PBRA, risk classification Not Troubled.\n  Cycle 1: MOR conducted March 2024, rated BELOW AVERAGE (late annual recertifications, EIV master file gaps).\n    Next MOR due: within 12 months → by March 2025.\n  Cycle 2: MOR conducted February 2025 after a year of corrective work, rated SATISFACTORY.\n    Next MOR due: within 24 months → by February 2027.\n  Cycle 3: MOR conducted January 2027, rated ABOVE AVERAGE.\n    Next MOR due: within 36 months → by January 2030.\n  Count what happened: over the six years from March 2024 to January 2030, the property went from a review every 12 months to a review every 36 months. Three reviews instead of six. Each review costs the client staff time, consultant fees, and disruption. THAT is the number to put in your proposal — not \"you'll get a better letter,\" but \"you will be reviewed half as often.\"\n\nWorked example 2 — the same improvement, different risk classification. Identical property, identical MOR ratings, but classified TROUBLED because of financial distress. Cycle 3's Above Average buys 24 months, not 36. The client did everything right on the occupancy side and still faces more frequent review, because risk classification is driven substantially by things outside the occupancy department. The consulting lesson: if a client is Troubled, tell them plainly that MOR excellence alone will not get them to a 36-month cycle, and identify who inside the organization owns the financial side.\n\nWorked example 3 — the trigger everyone forgets. A well-run property, Not Troubled, Superior on its 2025 MOR, next review scheduled for 2028. In August 2026 the owner replaces the management agent (HUD-approved, previous agent's contract not renewed). A new MOR is now due within 6 months — by roughly February 2027 — notwithstanding the Superior rating and the 2028 date. This is the single most common scheduling surprise in the business, and it is the best-timed consulting opportunity there is: a new agent inheriting files they did not create, on a 6-month clock. Set a standing intake question: \"Has there been, or is there pending, any change in ownership or management agent?\"\n\nWorked example 4 — the ambiguous trigger. An ownership entity restructures: the same principals, a new single-purpose LLC as mortgagor, HUD-approved through the previews/APPS process. Is that a \"change of ownership\" for the 6-month rule? Treat it as yes until the CA or AE tells you otherwise, and ask in writing at the time of approval. The cost of preparing for a review that does not come is a fraction of the cost of being surprised by one that does.\n\nWhy HUD did this — teach the rationale, because it is your sales argument. In HUD's stated justification for the rule, over 94 percent of properties reviewed in FY2021 scored Satisfactory, Above Average or Superior. HUD concluded that reviewing high performers annually was a poor use of review resources and reallocated that capacity toward lower-scoring and higher-risk properties. The corollary is exactly the business case for MOR consulting: for a well-run property, quality is not just a nicer report, it is measurably less regulatory contact. And for a struggling property, the schedule tightens around it precisely when its capacity to respond is lowest — which is when it needs you most and can least afford you. Price accordingly and structure payment terms accordingly."},{"heading":"MOR Consequences — From Findings to Enforcement","body":"A MOR produces three tiers of output, and clients routinely conflate them.\n\n• OBSERVATIONS / narrative comments. Not scored, no required response, but they appear in the report and can be referred elsewhere.\n• FINDINGS. Deficiencies tied to a requirement. Each carries a required corrective action and a due date. The owner must respond in writing. Findings drive the rating.\n• RATING. The overall judgment, on the five-level scale, which drives the next review date and, when low, drives escalation.\n\nThe escalation ladder, in the order clients will actually encounter it:\n\n1. Written response to the report / Corrective Action Plan, with documentation of cure by the stated due date.\n2. Follow-up or targeted review to verify the cure.\n3. Referral to the HUD Account Executive. For Below Average and Unsatisfactory ratings, this happens as a matter of course — the findings go to the AE.\n4. Repayment. Where subsidy was overpaid because of certification errors, HUD can require the owner to repay HAP received in error, and the owner in turn must collect tenant-side overpayments under a repayment agreement or absorb them.\n5. Sanctions with contractual teeth: suspension or abatement of HAP payments, a demand that the management agent be replaced, flagging of principals in APPS/2530 for future participation reviews, referral to the Departmental Enforcement Center, and in the extreme, HAP contract termination or mortgage default proceedings.\n\nTHE APPEAL RIGHT — the deadline your client will miss if you do not calendar it. An owner or agent may appeal a rating of BELOW AVERAGE or UNSATISFACTORY. The appeal must be submitted IN WRITING WITHIN 30 CALENDAR DAYS of the MOR report/transmittal date, per HUD Handbook 4350.1 Chapter 6. Appeal decisions are typically due back within 45 calendar days. Note the two limits carefully: the clock runs from the transmittal date, not from the date the owner opened the envelope or the date a busy regional VP forwarded it; and Satisfactory and above are not appealable, because there is nothing adverse to appeal.\n\nWorked example 1 — the clean appeal. A property is rated Below Average on the strength of four findings, one of which — \"waiting list not maintained in accordance with the Tenant Selection Plan\" — rests on the reviewer having worked from a superseded TSP that the property replaced eight months before the review. The owner has a dated transmittal showing the current TSP was submitted to the CA. Appeal within 30 days, attach the transmittal and the current TSP, ask specifically that the finding be withdrawn and the rating recomputed. This is the appeal that wins: a factual error, documented, with a specific requested remedy.\n\nWorked example 2 — the appeal that should not be filed. A property is rated Below Average on eleven findings, all substantiated, and the owner wants to appeal because \"the reviewer was hostile.\" There is no factual error to correct, and an appeal consumes the 30 days that should have gone into curing. Counsel the client to spend the time on the corrective action plan instead. A consultant whose value proposition is \"we always appeal\" is selling the client an expensive way to lose a month.\n\nWorked example 3 — the partial appeal, which is the most common real-world case. Nine findings; two are wrong (one duplicates another, one cites a file that was not in the sample) and seven are correct. File the appeal on the two, and simultaneously submit the corrective action documentation on the seven. Do not wait for the appeal to resolve before curing the rest — the corrective action due dates run independently of the appeal.\n\nWorked example 4 — the naive reading that costs the client the right. The report is dated and transmitted March 4. The regional office receives it March 11, the property manager forwards it to the owner March 26, and the owner's attorney calls you April 2 asking to appeal. Day 30 from March 4 is April 3. You have one day. The rule to teach every client: the day the report arrives ANYWHERE in the organization, it goes on a calendar with a hard 30-day deadline and a 15-day internal draft deadline, and nobody waits for a decision about whether to appeal before starting the draft.\n\nWorked example 5 — where the money actually shows up. A review finds that 6 of 25 sampled files miscalculated income, and the CA extrapolates a concern across the portfolio of 180 units. The scored consequence is a finding. The FINANCIAL consequence arrives later, from a different direction: corrected 50059s pushed through TRACS generate retroactive HAP adjustments, some of which reduce future vouchers, plus tenant-side underpayments that must be pursued through repayment agreements the tenants may be unable to pay. Prepare owners for the sequencing — the rating is the headline, the recomputation is the bill, and it arrives after the consultant's engagement usually ends. Scope your engagement to include the recomputation, or scope it out explicitly in writing."}]},{"id":"m72","unit":"u7","number":2,"title":"MOR Ratings — Understanding the Five-Level Scale","subtitle":"","sections":[{"heading":"The Five MOR Ratings Explained","body":"HUD rates a Management and Occupancy Review on a five-level scale. This scale is the national standard and it is what appears in HUD guidance and in Contract Administrator materials across the country:\n\n• SUPERIOR\n• ABOVE AVERAGE\n• SATISFACTORY\n• BELOW AVERAGE\n• UNSATISFACTORY\n\nWhat each level means in practice — and this is the framing to give a client, because the labels are more useful as descriptions of a management SYSTEM than as grades:\n\nSUPERIOR. Compliance is not merely present, it is systematic. Files are uniform because a process produces uniform files, not because a diligent person happened to assemble them. Written policies exist, are current, and match what staff actually do. Findings are absent or trivially few and self-identified. In practice a Superior rating usually requires that the property have caught and cured its own problems before the reviewer arrived — which is precisely what a mock MOR produces.\n\nABOVE AVERAGE. Full compliance in substance, with minor deficiencies that do not indicate systemic failure. A handful of files missing a signature; one policy that references a superseded handbook chapter. Corrective action is straightforward. For a Not Troubled or Potentially Troubled property this is the threshold that buys a 36-month review interval, which makes the jump from Satisfactory to Above Average the single most economically valuable move in this business.\n\nSATISFACTORY. Compliance is adequate. Deficiencies exist, some of them recurring, but the property is meeting its obligations and no serious risk to the program is apparent. This is where the large majority of properties sit. It is not a bad rating — but it costs the owner a 24-month rather than 36-month interval.\n\nBELOW AVERAGE. Significant deficiencies, or patterns of deficiency that indicate a process failure rather than an isolated slip. Appeal rights attach here. The next MOR comes within 12 months at any risk classification, and the findings are forwarded to the HUD Account Executive.\n\nUNSATISFACTORY. Serious, pervasive, or willful noncompliance; failure to correct previously cited deficiencies; conditions that put program funds or tenants at risk. Same 12-month clock, same referral, plus the realistic prospect of enforcement — repayment demands, a required change of management agent, or contract action.\n\nTHE NUMERIC OVERLAY — the thing that confuses every owner with a multi-state portfolio. HUD's scale is qualitative and national. Many individual Contract Administrators overlay their own numeric bands on top of it, and those bands are NOT uniform. One state agency anchors its bands at 95 / 85 / 75 / 65 (roughly: 95+ Superior, 85+ Above Average, 75+ Satisfactory, 65+ Below Average, under that Unsatisfactory). Another treats anything below 70 as below-average or unacceptable. A reader in a third state will open their report and find yet another convention, or none at all.\n\nWorked example 1 — reading the label, not the number. A client's report from a CA that uses the 95/85/75/65 anchors reads \"88 — Above Average.\" A second property, same owner, different state, reads simply \"Above Average.\" These are the same HUD rating. The 88 tells you where the property sits inside the band in THAT state's methodology; it tells you nothing transferable. Do not benchmark across states on numbers.\n\nWorked example 2 — the number that moves without the property moving. A property scores 91 in 2024 and 86 in 2026 under the same CA, with the SAME four findings both times plus one new minor item. The owner reads a five-point collapse. What actually happened is that one additional minor deficiency was weighted into a band. The correct client conversation compares FINDINGS year over year, then explains the band. Numbers invite a false precision that the underlying instrument does not support.\n\nWorked example 3 — the boundary case that decides the review interval. A Not Troubled property has six minor findings, none systemic, all cured on the spot during the review. Is that Above Average or Satisfactory? This is a judgment call at the reviewer's discretion, and it is worth exactly one review cycle — 36 months versus 24. It is also the case where the exit conference matters most: findings that were CURED DURING THE REVIEW, documented in front of the reviewer, are the strongest argument for the higher rating. Teach site staff to fix and document during the visit, not after.\n\nWorked example 4 — the naive reading that gets a client hurt. An owner sees \"Satisfactory\" and reports to the board that the property \"passed.\" Two cycles of Satisfactory in a row with the same recurring findings is a property drifting toward Below Average, because unresolved repeat findings read very differently the second time. Satisfactory is a floor to build from, not a destination."},{"heading":"How Deficiencies Are Weighted and Accumulated","body":"A MOR rating is not a mechanical average of checkboxes. Reviewers weigh deficiencies along four dimensions, and understanding those dimensions is how you predict a rating before the report arrives.\n\n1. SEVERITY. Does the deficiency affect eligibility, subsidy amount, tenant rights, or program funds? A miscalculated income that overpaid HAP for eighteen months is not the same as a missing initials line.\n2. FREQUENCY / PATTERN. One file out of twenty-five is an error. Six files out of twenty-five is a process. The reviewer's question is always whether the deficiency is random or produced by the system.\n3. RECURRENCE. Was this cited on the prior MOR? A repeat finding is the single heaviest aggravating factor there is, because it converts \"we made a mistake\" into \"we were told and did not fix it.\"\n4. SELF-IDENTIFICATION AND CURE. A deficiency the property found, documented, and corrected before the review — with a written record of the correction — is materially lighter than the identical deficiency found by the reviewer.\n\nWorked example 1 — same error, four different weights. The error: a $480 dependent deduction taken for a 19-year-old who is not a full-time student.\n  (a) One file, caught by the property in a self-audit, corrected via a corrected 50059 with the tenant notified — likely an observation or a very light finding.\n  (b) One file, found by the reviewer, no pattern — a minor finding, cure and move on.\n  (c) Five of twenty-five files, all with the same misunderstanding of the dependent definition — a systemic finding, with a corrective action requiring retraining and a 100 percent file review, and real downward pressure on the rating.\n  (d) Five of twenty-five files, and the SAME finding appears in the prior MOR report — this is the Below Average scenario, because the property was told and the process did not change.\nSame regulatory error. Four different outcomes. The variable is not the error, it is the system around it.\n\nWorked example 2 — severity beating frequency. Twelve of twenty-five files are missing the tenant's signature on the HUD-9887 renewal at one recertification, an error the property caught and re-executed within the same month. Meanwhile, ONE file shows an ineligible student household that received assistance for two years. Twelve is a bigger number; the single student file is a bigger problem, because it goes to eligibility and produces a repayment. Do not let a client triage by count.\n\nWorked example 3 — the sampling arithmetic that clients misread. A reviewer samples 20 files at a 150-unit property and finds errors in 4. The owner says \"that's only 4 files out of 150 — under 3 percent.\" Wrong denominator. The reviewer's error rate is 4 of 20 — 20 percent — and the entire purpose of sampling is to infer the population from the sample. Expect the corrective action to require review of ALL files, not just the four.\n\nWorked example 4 — the ambiguous \"is it one finding or three?\" case. A property has no written EIV policy, its EIV master file is missing the last two quarters of reports, and one user's access was not terminated when they left. Is that one EIV finding or three? Reviewers vary. It matters because in a numeric-band state, three findings deduct three times. Your practical response is the same either way: cure all three elements and document each separately, so that whichever way the reviewer counts, the response is complete. Never write a corrective action response that addresses a bundled finding at the bundle level; address every underlying element.\n\nWorked example 5 — the deficiency that is not the property's fault and still counts. A CA cites late annual recertifications for six households. Investigation shows the property's software vendor pushed a bad update that suppressed the 120-day reminder notices for one quarter. The finding stands — the obligation belongs to the owner. But the CORRECTIVE ACTION is where the vendor failure belongs: document the root cause, document the vendor fix, and add a manual monthly verification of the reminder queue as a compensating control. A corrective action plan that identifies a true root cause and a compensating control reads very differently from one that says \"staff will be reminded.\"\n\nThe single most useful predictive question to ask before a review: \"Show me the last MOR report, and show me what changed in the PROCESS as a result.\" If nothing changed in the process, expect the same findings, weighted heavier."},{"heading":"The Corrective Action Plan (CAP) Process","body":"Every finding carries a required corrective action and a due date. The written response — commonly called the Corrective Action Plan — is the owner's formal answer, and it is graded, informally but really, by whoever reads it.\n\nAnatomy of a corrective action response that works. For EACH finding, separately:\n• Restate the finding as written, with its citation.\n• State whether the owner concurs. If not, say so plainly and factually here (and see the appeal discussion in Chapter 1 for ratings-level disputes).\n• State the ROOT CAUSE. Not \"human error\" — what in the process allowed it.\n• State the CORRECTION: what was done for the specific instances cited, with dates and document references.\n• State the CORRECTIVE ACTION: what changed in the process so it does not recur — the policy revision, the checklist, the second-signature requirement, the calendar automation.\n• State the SCOPE OF REVIEW: whether the owner reviewed only the cited files or all files, and what that review found.\n• Attach EVIDENCE. Corrected 50059s, revised policy pages with revision dates, training sign-in sheets, screenshots of the new system control.\n• Name a responsible person and a completion date.\n\nWorked example 1 — the weak response and its rewrite.\n  Weak: \"Finding 3 — Late annual recertifications. Response: Staff has been counseled on the importance of timely recertifications. This will not happen again.\"\n  Why it fails: no root cause, no evidence, no process change, no scope, no owner, no date. It invites a repeat finding, and a repeat finding is the heaviest aggravating factor in the rating.\n  Rewrite: \"Finding 3 — Six annual recertifications effective 4/1/25 through 9/1/25 were completed after the effective date, contrary to 4350.3 REV-1 paragraph 7-7. Root cause: the 120/90/60-day reminder notices were generated manually from a spreadsheet maintained by a single staff member who was on leave from February 3 to April 14, 2025, and no backup process existed. Correction: all six certifications were completed and transmitted; corrected 50059s attached as Exhibit 3-A, with TRACS confirmations. Scope: 100 percent of the 148 active files were reviewed for recertification timeliness; no additional late certifications were identified (review log, Exhibit 3-B). Corrective action: effective 6/1/25 the recertification calendar is generated automatically by the property software, the notice queue is verified monthly by the Community Manager against a printed report, and the Regional Manager reviews the queue report monthly (revised Recertification Procedure, Exhibit 3-C, and monthly verification log, Exhibit 3-D). Responsible: J. Alvarez, Community Manager. Completed 6/1/25.\"\n  The rewrite is not longer for the sake of length. Every added element answers a question the reviewer would otherwise have to ask.\n\nWorked example 2 — the due date you cannot meet. A finding requires all 148 files be reviewed and corrected within 30 days, and the property has two occupancy staff. Do not miss the date silently — that converts a curable finding into a failure to correct, which is Unsatisfactory territory. Write BEFORE the due date, request an extension with a specific alternative schedule and an interim deliverable (\"60 files completed by the original date, remaining 88 by day 60, weekly progress reports\"), and document the response. Extensions requested in advance are routinely granted; extensions requested after the fact are a second finding.\n\nWorked example 3 — the finding that cannot be cured. A finding cites that a household was admitted in 2022 without an Existing Tenant Search having been run. You cannot retroactively run a pre-admission search for an admission that already happened. The correct response separates the uncurable historical fact from the curable forward obligation: acknowledge the historical omission, run the search now and document the result, and put a control in place that makes the search a mandatory gate before any move-in (with evidence — the revised move-in checklist showing the search as a required, initialed step). Reviewers accept \"cannot be undone, here is the control\" far more readily than a response that pretends the past was fixed.\n\nWorked example 4 — the response that creates new exposure. A property responds to an income-calculation finding by attaching corrected 50059s that reveal, in passing, that the same household also had an unreported asset for three years. The owner has just disclosed a second problem in a document they were compelled to write. This is not a reason to hide it — it is a reason to KNOW before you write. Run the full recomputation internally first, quantify the total exposure, decide on the repayment approach, and then write one complete response. Discovering exposure in your own submission is the worst possible sequence.\n\nWorked example 5 — the CAP as a consulting deliverable. For a Below Average property with eleven findings, the corrective action response is a 40-to-80-page document with exhibits. This is billable, high-value work with a hard deadline and an anxious client — the best-defined engagement in MOR consulting. Price it as a fixed-fee project with a defined exhibit list, not hourly, and make the client's document-gathering obligations explicit in the engagement letter."},{"heading":"Performance Improvement Plans and Management Agent Replacement","body":"When findings and corrective actions are not enough, HUD's response escalates from paperwork toward the management structure itself.\n\nThe escalation, in rough order:\n\n1. FOLLOW-UP REVIEW. A targeted return visit, usually limited to the previously cited items. Failing a follow-up is materially worse than the original finding, because the record now shows a failure to correct.\n2. INCREASED MONITORING. More frequent reporting to the Account Executive; monthly submissions; a required conference call cadence.\n3. PERFORMANCE IMPROVEMENT PLAN. A comprehensive, HUD-approved plan covering staffing, training, systems, and timelines, typically imposed after an Unsatisfactory rating or a failed follow-up. Unlike a CAP, which answers findings, a PIP addresses CAPACITY: does this organization have the people and systems to run this property.\n4. REQUIRED CHANGE OF MANAGEMENT AGENT. HUD can require the owner to terminate the agent and engage a new one, subject to HUD approval of the replacement through the previews/2530 process.\n5. CONTRACT AND MORTGAGE ACTION. HAP abatement or termination, referral to the Departmental Enforcement Center, flagging of principals for future participation, and in insured properties, default remedies.\n\nWorked example 1 — the identity-of-interest agent. A 180-unit property is managed by an agent owned by the same principals as the ownership entity. Two consecutive Unsatisfactory ratings and a failed follow-up put agent replacement on the table. The owner's instinct is to argue that no other agent knows the property. The realistic path: propose a Performance Improvement Plan with a genuinely independent element — an outside compliance consultant with defined authority and direct reporting to ownership, a named experienced occupancy specialist hired onto the site team, and monthly third-party file audits with results submitted to the AE. HUD's concern is capacity and independence, not the agent's name. A PIP that adds real, verifiable capacity is often accepted where \"we'll try harder\" is not.\n\nWorked example 2 — the new agent inheriting a disaster. An agent takes over a property with an Unsatisfactory MOR, and a new MOR is due within 6 months of the approved agent change. The trap is that the new agent is now the author of the corrective actions for problems it did not create, on a clock it did not set. The engagement design: in the first 30 days, run a complete file inventory and a documented baseline assessment, dated and signed, that establishes the condition at takeover. That baseline is the single most valuable document in the engagement — it separates inherited deficiencies from new ones for the next two review cycles, and it is the reason the new agent's next report can read \"corrected\" instead of \"recurring.\"\n\nWorked example 3 — the PIP that fails on staffing math. A property with 200 assisted units has one occupancy specialist processing all annuals, interims, move-ins and EIV. The PIP proposes \"improved procedures.\" Run the arithmetic in front of the client: 200 annual recertifications plus interims plus move-ins plus the EIV report cycle, against roughly 2,080 working hours, minus training, leave and tenant contact. If each certification consumes 3 to 4 hours end to end, annuals alone are 600 to 800 hours. The credible PIP adds a position or a vendor; the incredible one adds adjectives. HUD reviewers do this arithmetic too.\n\nWorked example 4 — the ambiguous case: is a PIP a finding? No. A PIP is a forward-looking capacity plan, not a scored item, and it does not itself appear as a deficiency on the next 9834. But failure to MEET a PIP milestone will appear, and it will appear as a failure to correct — the heaviest possible framing. Advise clients to negotiate milestone dates they can actually meet at the moment the PIP is drafted, not to accept aggressive dates and hope.\n\nWorked example 5 — reading the room on an ownership change. When enforcement pressure rises, some owners respond by selling or by restructuring ownership. Note the interaction with Chapter 1's scheduling rule: any HUD-approved change of ownership or management agent triggers a new MOR within 6 months. A troubled property that changes hands does not get a fresh start on the calendar — it gets a review sooner. Say this out loud to a client considering a transfer as a compliance strategy."},{"heading":"Reading and Using Prior MOR Reports","body":"The prior MOR report is the most information-dense document a consultant can obtain about a property, and it is the first thing to request at intake. Ask for the last TWO, plus the owner's written responses and any follow-up correspondence. The responses matter as much as the reports: they tell you what the property PROMISED, and unkept promises are what a reviewer looks for.\n\nHow to read one, in order:\n\n1. The rating and the transmittal date. The transmittal date starts appeal and corrective-action clocks and anchors the next-review calculation.\n2. The findings list. Read it twice: once for content, once for CATEGORY distribution. Findings clustered in one category point to one broken process; findings scattered across all categories point to a capacity problem.\n3. The corrective actions and due dates. Then check whether each was actually completed, with evidence, by the date.\n4. The narrative. Reviewers put things in the narrative that are not findings but signal what they will look at next time.\n5. The owner's response. Compare its promises against current practice, item by item.\n\nWorked example 1 — the repeat-finding audit, the highest-yield 90 minutes in this business. Lay the last two reports side by side and build a three-column table: Finding / Cited in 2023? / Cited in 2025? Any row with two checkmarks is a red-alert item. In a typical engagement you will find two to four. Those items get first priority, a documented root cause, and a control — not because they are the most severe, but because a third citation of the same item is the most reliable path from Satisfactory to Below Average.\n\nWorked example 2 — the promise that was never kept. The 2023 response says \"effective 10/1/23, the Community Manager will conduct a monthly EIV report review documented on a log maintained in the EIV master file.\" At intake in 2025, ask for the log. If it exists for October, November and December 2023 and stops, you have found both a live compliance gap and a management-oversight story that the reviewer will find too. Fix the gap; then fix the reason the log stopped, which is almost always that nobody was reviewing the log.\n\nWorked example 3 — the narrative comment that becomes next cycle's finding. The 2024 narrative notes, without citing a finding: \"The tenant selection plan does not address the property's policy on live-in aides.\" That is a courtesy warning. If the TSP still does not address live-in aides in 2026, it will be a finding, and it will be a finding at a property that was told. Treat every narrative observation as a finding with a two-year fuse.\n\nWorked example 4 — the report that shows an improving trend and a hidden problem. Ratings go Below Average (2021), Satisfactory (2023), Satisfactory (2025). The client is proud of the trend. But the 2025 findings include two items that did not exist in 2021 — a new EIV security lapse and a waiting list problem — while the original 2021 findings are gone. The property did not get better at compliance; it fixed what it was told and generated new gaps elsewhere. That is the signature of a property doing REACTIVE compliance. The consulting recommendation is a standing self-assessment against the full 9834, not another round of fixes.\n\nWorked example 5 — the missing report. An owner cannot locate the prior MOR. Options, in order: the management agent's files; the Contract Administrator (owners may request a copy); the HUD Account Executive. Meanwhile, do not stall the engagement — proceed with a full baseline assessment. But note the fact itself: an organization that cannot produce its own last MOR report has a records problem that the review will find in other places too.\n\nWorked example 6 — reading a report from a different CA than the one who will conduct the next review. Portfolio properties change CAs; a report from the prior CA is still useful for FINDINGS content, but its numeric band and its scope (Addendum B in or out) may not carry forward. Separate what is about the property from what is about the reviewer, and confirm the incoming CA's scope in writing.\n\nThe deliverable to build from all of this: a one-page \"prior review posture\" summary for the client — rating history, repeat findings, unkept promises, narrative warnings, and next-review due date computed from the risk/score matrix. It takes two hours, it makes the client feel seen, and it sets the scope for everything that follows."}]},{"id":"m73","unit":"u7","number":3,"title":"The On-Site Review — What Happens During a MOR","subtitle":"","sections":[{"heading":"Scheduling, Notice, and Pre-Visit Preparation","body":"The on-site visit is Part II of Form HUD-9834. By the time it happens, Part I — the desk review — is already done, and the reviewer arrives with a working hypothesis. Your client's job on site is to confirm the good hypothesis or dislodge the bad one.\n\nHow the visit gets scheduled. The reviewer (HUD staff or the Contract Administrator) issues a notification letter that typically states the date and time, the reviewer's name, the documents to be produced on site, and the desk review submission deadline. Practice varies by administrator: advance notice of two to six weeks is typical, some CAs schedule by phone and confirm in writing, and some conduct unannounced or short-notice reviews when there is cause. Nothing entitles an owner to a particular amount of notice, so the only durable strategy is continuous readiness rather than a scramble.\n\nThe pre-visit checklist that actually changes outcomes:\n\n• Confirm the scope in writing. Who is the reviewer, is Addendum B (financial) in scope, how many files will be sampled, will the sample be provided in advance or drawn on arrival, and which units will be inspected.\n• Reconcile the rent roll to TRACS BEFORE the visit. Every mismatch between the property's rent roll, the 50059s in the files, and what TRACS shows is a finding waiting to be written.\n• Print and organize the required documents list (Addendum C) as a physical or electronic binder with a table of contents. A reviewer who can find things forms a different impression than one who waits.\n• Prepare the workspace: a private room with a table, power, and no foot traffic. This sounds trivial. It is not. A reviewer working at the leasing desk hears every tenant interaction and every staff conversation for two days.\n• Brief the staff (Section 4).\n• Do a physical walk of the units likely to be viewed and the common areas, and fix what can be fixed.\n\nWorked example 1 — the two-week notice with a known problem. A property gets notice on the 3rd for a review on the 17th, and the manager knows nine annual recertifications are late. Do NOT wait to be found. In two weeks, complete as many as possible, document each with dates, and prepare a one-page written summary for the reviewer: how many were late, why, how many are now complete, what control was installed. The finding may still be written. Its WEIGHT changes substantially, and self-identification is one of the four factors that moves a rating.\n\nWorked example 2 — the request to reschedule. The community manager is on approved medical leave the week of the review and the assistant has been in the role for six weeks. Requesting a reschedule is reasonable and often granted once, if made promptly, in writing, with a specific alternative window. What is not reasonable — and what reviewers see through instantly — is a reschedule request made three days out because \"we're not ready.\" Repeated or late reschedule requests become a narrative comment about management capacity.\n\nWorked example 3 — the short-notice review. A CA calls Monday for a Thursday visit. Triage in this order: (1) tenant files in the likely sample — verify the current 50059, the 9887/9887-A, and the lease and lease addenda are present in each; (2) the EIV master file, because EIV gaps are quick to spot and heavily weighted; (3) the required documents binder; (4) everything else. Do not spend Wednesday reorganizing the policy binder while the EIV master file is missing two quarters.\n\nWorked example 4 — the ambiguous case: what if the notice letter asks for something the property does not have? A CA's document list includes a \"resident council roster\" and the property has no resident council. Respond IN WRITING before the visit stating that the item does not exist and why, rather than leaving a blank in the binder. Silence reads as disorganization; a written statement reads as control. The same technique applies to any item that is genuinely not applicable — put an N/A page in the binder with a one-line explanation.\n\nWorked example 5 — the consultant's pre-visit engagement. The most valuable window is 60 to 90 days before a scheduled review, and the deliverable is a mock MOR (Chapter 20). But a 2-week engagement is still worth taking. Scope it honestly: \"In two weeks we can verify and cure file-level and EIV items, and prepare your response posture. We cannot rebuild two years of documentation.\" Say what you cannot do, in writing, at intake. It protects the client's expectations and your reputation when the report arrives."},{"heading":"Tenant File Sampling — The Statistical Logic","body":"Addendum A of Form 9834 is the Tenant File Checklist, and it is applied file by file to a SAMPLE. Understanding how the sample works is the difference between a client who is anxious and a client who is prepared.\n\nThe mechanics, as practiced:\n\n• Sample size scales with property size, typically in the range of roughly 10 percent of units with practical floors and ceilings — a small property may see 5 to 10 files; a large property might see 20 to 30. Contract Administrators set their own sampling protocols within HUD's framework, which is another reason to confirm scope in writing.\n• The sample is not purely random. Reviewers deliberately include categories that carry known risk: recent move-ins, recent interim certifications, households with zero or very low reported income, households with live-in aides, large households, households with EIV income discrepancies, and any file connected to a prior finding.\n• Some reviewers provide the list in advance; others draw it on arrival specifically to see what the files look like unprepared.\n• If the sample shows a systemic problem, the reviewer can expand it. An expanded sample is a signal — it means the reviewer believes the first errors were not isolated.\n\nThe consulting consequence is decisive: because the sample is risk-weighted rather than random, PREPARING ONLY A SAMPLE OF FILES IS A LOSING STRATEGY. But you can predict the risk categories and audit those first.\n\nWorked example 1 — predicting the sample. A 96-unit property. Build the predicted sample list: all move-ins in the last 12 months (7 files); all interims in the last 12 months (11 files); all households reporting zero income (3 files); all households with a live-in aide (2 files); all files cited in the prior MOR (4 files); any household appearing on the EIV Income Discrepancy Report (5 files). After removing overlaps, that is roughly 25 files — about the size of a real sample at that property, and a very good approximation of which files it will contain. Audit those 25 first, then work outward.\n\nWorked example 2 — the expanded sample. The reviewer starts with 20 files and finds income calculation errors in the first 5. She asks for 10 more. The client wants to know whether to object. No — and the response that helps is proactive: offer the property's own list of every file processed by the same staff member during the same period. It looks like cooperation because it is, and it moves the narrative from \"the reviewer had to dig\" to \"the property understands its own exposure.\" The finding is coming either way; what is still in play is the rating.\n\nWorked example 3 — the file that is missing entirely. The reviewer asks for the file on unit 214 and it cannot be found. This is not a paperwork error, it is a records failure, and it is weighted accordingly. If the documents exist electronically, produce them immediately and state on the record that the property maintains electronic files with paper backup. If they genuinely cannot be produced, reconstruct what can be reconstructed from TRACS and the tenant, disclose plainly, and install a file inventory control. Never let a reviewer leave believing a file is missing if it merely could not be located that afternoon — but never claim it exists if it does not.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: move-outs in the sample. Reviewers may sample recently terminated households, because move-out processing (final 50059 termination in TRACS, security deposit accounting, notice documentation) is a common failure point and the property has no ongoing relationship to catch errors. Clients almost never audit move-out files. Add the last 12 months of move-outs to every pre-review audit.\n\nWorked example 5 — the naive reading. \"The reviewer only found errors in 3 of 20 files, so we're 85 percent compliant.\" That framing is wrong twice. First, the reviewer's inference runs from sample to population, so 3 of 20 implies roughly 15 percent of the whole portfolio of files, which at 96 units is about 14 files. Second, the corrective action will almost certainly require a 100 percent review, which is where the real cost is. Prepare clients for the second bill.\n\nWorked example 6 — the ambiguous file. A household's file contains two 50059s with the same effective date and different figures — a correction was processed but the superseded document was never marked. The reviewer cannot tell which is operative. Even if the correct figures are in TRACS, this is a finding for file organization, and it can look like something worse. The control: every superseded document gets stamped or labeled as superseded with a date, and every correction carries a one-line file note explaining what changed and why. This costs seconds per file and prevents a class of findings entirely."},{"heading":"Unit Inspections During the MOR","body":"A MOR is not a physical inspection — NSPIRE is. But reviewers do walk units and common areas, and the purpose is different from what owners assume. The reviewer is testing MANAGEMENT SYSTEMS by looking at their physical output.\n\nWhat the walk is actually testing:\n\n• Whether the work order system reflects reality. The reviewer notices a broken item, then asks for the work order history for that unit.\n• Whether unit turnaround is being managed. Vacant unit condition and days-vacant against the property's own turnaround standard.\n• Whether preventive maintenance is scheduled and documented, not just performed when something breaks.\n• Whether NSPIRE findings from the last inspection were actually corrected, and whether the corrections were documented.\n• Whether accessible units are occupied appropriately and whether accessibility features are intact (a UFAS-compliant kitchen with a cabinet installed under the sink is a Section 504 problem, not a maintenance problem).\n• Whether the property is being marketed and maintained consistent with its AFHMP and its own house rules.\n\nWorked example 1 — the work order test. The reviewer sees a dripping faucet in a sampled unit and asks for the unit's work order history. Three outcomes:\n  (a) There is a work order dated four days ago, parts on order, tenant notified — the system works, and the drip is now EVIDENCE OF COMPLIANCE.\n  (b) There is no work order and the tenant says she called three weeks ago — the drip is now a work order system finding.\n  (c) There is a work order marked \"completed\" two months ago for the same faucet — worse than (b), because the records are unreliable. A completed work order that did not fix the problem raises a question about every other completed work order.\nTeach staff that the physical defect is rarely the finding. The record is the finding.\n\nWorked example 2 — the vacant unit. A unit has been vacant 71 days. The reviewer asks why. If the answer is \"we're waiting on a HUD-approved reserve release for the flooring, requested on form HUD-9250 on the 12th, here is the submission,\" the vacancy is documented management. If the answer is a shrug, the reviewer now has both a turnaround question and a question about whether the property is filling units from its waiting list — which reaches the occupancy category and potentially the HAP billing.\n\nWorked example 3 — the accessible unit occupied by a household that does not need the features. This is common and the correct handling is nuanced. Section 504 and the property's own policies generally require that accessible units be offered first to households needing the features, and that leases for accessible units let the property require a transfer when a qualifying household needs the unit. If the file shows the unit was offered to the waiting list's qualified households first, was declined, and was leased with the required transfer provision, the property is fine. If there is no documentation of the offer sequence, the finding is a fair housing/504 finding, not a maintenance one — and those are serious. Audit accessible unit occupancy documentation before every review.\n\nWorked example 4 — the ambiguous case: physical problems that are really tenant problems. A sampled unit is severely cluttered and a common area door is propped open by a resident. Neither is a management deficiency by itself. What the reviewer will look for is whether the property has a lease-enforcement record: housekeeping inspections, notices, follow-up, and — if it has reached that point — reasonable accommodation consideration where a disability may be involved. A property that documents its response is in good shape; one that has simply avoided the conflict for two years is not.\n\nWorked example 5 — the NSPIRE interaction that catches owners out. The last NSPIRE inspection cited a defective smoke alarm in three units, and the property certified the correction. During the MOR walk, the reviewer finds a missing smoke alarm in a fourth unit. The MOR finding is not the alarm — it is the reliability of the property's inspection-response and self-inspection systems, and it casts doubt on the earlier certification. If a client has certified corrections to HUD, make sure the underlying system actually produces those conditions property-wide, because the MOR is where inconsistency surfaces.\n\nWorked example 6 — what to fix in the 48 hours before a review, in priority order: life safety items (alarms, egress, extinguishers with current tags), accessibility features in accessible units, common area lighting, posted required notices (fair housing poster, EIV/VAWA-related notices where required, the property's grievance and accommodation contact information), and vacant unit condition. Not paint. Reviewers are not scoring aesthetics; they are scoring whether the systems work."},{"heading":"Staff Interviews — Preparing Your Team","body":"Reviewers talk to people: the site manager, occupancy or compliance staff, maintenance supervisors, and sometimes residents. These conversations are not depositions, but they carry real weight, because they test whether the WRITTEN policies describe the ACTUAL practice. The most damaging findings in MOR work come from a gap between the binder and the behavior.\n\nWhat reviewers typically probe:\n\n• Walk me through how you process an annual recertification, start to finish.\n• What do you do when a tenant reports an income change mid-year?\n• How do you use EIV, and who has access?\n• How do you take an application and place someone on the waiting list?\n• What happens when an applicant is rejected?\n• How do you handle a request for a reasonable accommodation?\n• How do you handle a tenant who discloses domestic violence?\n• What training have you had, and when?\n\nHow to prepare staff — the honest way, which is also the effective way:\n\n1. Staff must know where the written policy is and be able to find it. \"I'd have to check the Tenant Selection Plan, it's right here\" is an excellent answer. Improvising an answer that contradicts the plan is the worst possible answer.\n2. Never guess. \"I don't handle that — Maria does, and here is our written procedure\" is fine and normal.\n3. Never speculate about the past. \"I started in March; that certification predates me. Here's the file\" is a complete answer.\n4. Do not volunteer opinions about the owner, the agent, or HUD. Reviewers write narrative sections.\n5. Rehearse with real questions. A 45-minute practice interview with the actual staff, using the questions above, surfaces more risk than a day of file review.\n\nWorked example 1 — the answer that creates a finding out of nothing. Reviewer: \"How do you handle interim recertifications?\" Staff member: \"If someone's income goes up we usually wait until the annual, because it's a lot of paperwork and it's not fair to raise their rent mid-year.\" Nothing in the files may show this. The statement alone is a finding, because it describes a practice contrary to the property's policy and to 4350.3's interim requirements. The correct answer describes the written policy and the actual process, including the notice given to tenants about their reporting obligations.\n\nWorked example 2 — the sympathetic answer that is still wrong. Reviewer: \"What do you do when a tenant tells you they're a victim of domestic violence?\" Staff member: \"I tell them to file a police report so we have documentation.\" Well-intentioned, and wrong: under VAWA a survivor may self-certify on form HUD-5382, and the provider may not demand a police report or medical records as additional proof unless there is conflicting information. Fix this in training, and make sure the written procedure and the staff answer match.\n\nWorked example 3 — the maintenance interview. Reviewer to the maintenance supervisor: \"How do you get work orders?\" Answer: \"Tenants text me and I take care of it.\" Even if every repair is genuinely made, the answer describes a system with no record, which means no work order tracking, no turnaround data, no evidence that emergency repairs met the property's response standard. The fix is not to script the answer. It is to build the system, then let the honest answer be a good one.\n\nWorked example 4 — the disagreement in front of the reviewer. The site manager says annual notices go out 120 days ahead; the occupancy specialist says 90. Both are attempting to be helpful. The reviewer now has an inconsistency to test in the files. Preparation prevents this: before the review, the team agrees on and writes down the actual process, and the person who owns each process is the one who answers questions about it.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: what if a staff member does not know an answer that they SHOULD know? Do not coach them to bluff. Coach them to say \"I'm not certain — let me get the procedure\" and then produce it. A staff member who reliably finds the right answer in the written policy demonstrates that the policy is operative, which is exactly what the reviewer is trying to determine. A staff member who confidently states a wrong rule demonstrates the opposite.\n\nWorked example 6 — resident conversations. Reviewers sometimes speak with residents or a resident organization. Do not manage this, and never suggest to residents what to say — that is the kind of thing that turns a compliance review into an enforcement matter. What you CAN do legitimately: make sure residents have been given the notices they are entitled to, that the accommodation and grievance procedures are posted and accurate, and that any open complaint has a documented response. Residents who have been dealt with straightforwardly say so.\n\nThe consulting deliverable here is a staff interview prep session — 90 minutes, role-played, with a written one-page \"who owns what\" reference. It is inexpensive to deliver, clients value it highly because it reduces fear, and it prevents an entire class of avoidable findings."},{"heading":"The Exit Conference and Post-Review Process","body":"The exit conference is the last point at which the owner can influence the report before it is written. Most properties waste it.\n\nWhat it is: a meeting at the end of the on-site visit, in which the reviewer summarizes preliminary observations and potential findings. It is preliminary — the reviewer will complete the analysis and issue the report afterward — but the reviewer's understanding of the facts is being finalized right there.\n\nHow to use it:\n\n• Have the right people present: site manager, occupancy staff, regional/asset management, and the consultant if engaged. Someone with authority to commit to corrective action should be in the room.\n• TAKE NOTES. Assign one person to do nothing but record each potential finding, the reviewer's stated basis, and any document referenced. Send a written summary to the reviewer within 24 to 48 hours confirming what was discussed. This single habit prevents most \"the report says something different than what they told us\" disputes.\n• Correct factual errors immediately and produce the document. This is the highest-value activity of the entire review.\n• Do not argue interpretation at the exit conference. Note the disagreement respectfully, state that the owner will address it in writing, and move on. Arguing consumes the time you need for factual corrections.\n• Ask specifically what will happen next and when: expected report date, response deadline convention, and how the report will be transmitted.\n\nWorked example 1 — the factual correction that removes a finding. The reviewer states that the property has no current Affirmative Fair Housing Marketing Plan on file. The manager retrieves the AFHMP with HUD's approval stamp dated 14 months ago and the transmittal proving submission. The potential finding evaporates in ninety seconds. Had the property waited to address this in a written response, it would have consumed 30 days and appeared in the report as a finding that was later withdrawn — a materially worse record.\n\nWorked example 2 — the correction that must be documented, not argued. The reviewer says six annual recertifications were late. The manager believes two of them were timely because the tenant delayed returning paperwork. Do not argue the legal point in the room. Instead: \"For those two, we have the 120, 90, and 60-day notices and the tenant's late response documented — may we provide those now?\" Produce the documents. The distinction between \"the property was late\" and \"the tenant was late despite documented notice\" is exactly the kind of thing the file can prove and an argument cannot.\n\nWorked example 3 — the finding you should concede. The reviewer notes the EIV master file is missing two quarters of reports. It is. Concede immediately, state what will be done and by when, and — if possible — print the missing reports before the reviewer leaves and show them. A property that fixes a problem during the visit converts a finding into, at worst, a lightly weighted one, and demonstrates the exact capacity the reviewer is assessing.\n\nWorked example 4 — the ambiguous case: the reviewer mentions something at the exit conference that does NOT appear in the report. Do not raise it. It was preliminary, and the reviewer resolved it. Conversely, if the report contains a finding that was NEVER mentioned at the exit conference, your 24-hour written summary is the document that lets you say so credibly. This is why you write the summary.\n\nWorked example 5 — the naive reading that costs a rating. \"The exit conference went great, she said we're in good shape.\" Exit conference tone is not a rating. Reviewers are professionals being polite in someone's workplace, and the analysis is completed afterward, against the desk review, against prior findings, and against the whole 9834. Never let a client relax on the strength of a friendly exit conference; the work between the conference and the report — the written summary, the documents promised, the items cured — is what actually moves the outcome.\n\nThe post-review sequence to calendar, in order:\n1. Day 0-2: internal debrief and written summary to the reviewer.\n2. Day 2-14: cure everything curable, gather every document referenced.\n3. Report arrives (timing varies by administrator; 30 to 60 days is common). Calendar the transmittal date immediately.\n4. If the rating is Below Average or Unsatisfactory: 30 calendar days to file a written appeal, per Handbook 4350.1 Chapter 6, with appeal decisions typically due back within 45 calendar days. Start the draft on day 1, decide whether to file on day 15.\n5. Corrective action responses by each stated due date, regardless of any appeal.\n6. Compute the next review date from the risk/score matrix and put it on the client's calendar. Then start preparing for it — which is how a one-time MOR engagement becomes a retainer."}]},{"id":"m81","unit":"u8","number":1,"title":"The Desk Review — Documents Required Before the On-Site Visit","subtitle":"","sections":[{"heading":"What Is the Desk Review and Why It Matters","body":"Part I of Form HUD-9834 is the DESK REVIEW, and it is completed before the reviewer sets foot on the property. The reviewer works from two streams: documents the owner submits in response to the notification letter, and data the reviewer pulls independently from HUD systems.\n\nThe independent stream is the part owners forget. Before anyone asks the property for anything, the reviewer can already see:\n\n• TRACS — every 50059 and 50059-A transmitted, certification effective dates, gaps, late transmissions, unit-by-unit assistance history, and the voucher history.\n• iREMS — property, contract, ownership and agent data, prior MOR ratings and dates, physical inspection scores, and open items.\n• APPS/2530 — previous participation records for owners, agents and principals.\n• FASS submissions — the audited annual financial statements filed under the 90-day rule.\n• EIV — depending on the reviewer's role and access, income discrepancy and related report data.\n\nThe practical consequence, which should reframe how your client thinks about the entire review: THE DESK REVIEW IS SCORED, and a substantial share of the reviewer's provisional impression is formed before the site visit. A property that submits a disorganized, incomplete, late package has already answered the reviewer's central question — is this organization in control of its own records — before the first tenant file is opened.\n\nWorked example 1 — the invisible finding. A property submits an excellent desk review package. Meanwhile, the reviewer pulls TRACS and finds that 11 annual recertifications over the past 18 months were transmitted 45 to 90 days after their effective dates. Nothing in the owner's package mentioned this, because nothing in the package was ABOUT transmission timing. The finding is written from HUD's own data. The lesson to teach every client: audit yourself against the data HUD can see, not just against the documents you choose to send.\n\nWorked example 2 — the reconciliation that prevents a cascade. Before submitting, run a three-way reconciliation: the property's rent roll, the current 50059 in each tenant file, and the TRACS certification history. Mismatches fall into predictable buckets — a corrected certification never transmitted; a move-out never terminated in TRACS so the unit still shows assisted; a unit transfer processed as a new move-in; a rent change effective date differing by one month between the file and TRACS. Each of these produces a finding AND a HAP billing consequence. Finding them yourself, correcting them, and disclosing the correction in the package converts five findings into one well-documented remediation narrative.\n\nWorked example 3 — the boundary case: what if fixing it looks worse? A property discovers, two weeks before the desk review deadline, that it has been billing HAP for a unit whose tenant moved out four months ago. Correcting it means repaying roughly four months of assistance on that unit. Some owners want to leave it and hope. Do not. The reviewer will see the TRACS record and the rent roll, and an uncorrected overbilling discovered by the reviewer is a materially different matter — potentially a program integrity issue rather than an administrative error. Correct it, document the correction date, and state it plainly.\n\nWorked example 4 — the ambiguous case: what does \"submitted\" mean. A CA's letter requires the desk review package by a stated date. The property mails it on that date and it arrives four days later. Some administrators treat the postmark as compliant; others treat receipt as the operative date. Do not guess: submit electronically where permitted, request written confirmation of receipt, and keep the transmission record. A \"late desk review submission\" finding is the most avoidable finding in the entire process, and it colors everything that follows.\n\nWorked example 5 — the consultant's leverage point. Because Part I is completed in advance and drives the reviewer's hypothesis, the desk review package is where a consultant has the most influence per hour spent. A clean, indexed, complete, self-aware package — including a short cover memo that identifies known issues and their remediation — is worth more to a rating than two extra days of file cleanup. It is also visible: it is the first work product of yours the reviewer sees."},{"heading":"Occupancy and Tenant Data Documents","body":"The occupancy half of the desk review package establishes who lives at the property, on what terms, and whether the subsidy being billed matches reality.\n\nThe core items, and what each is really being used for:\n\n• RENT ROLL as of a stated date. The reviewer reconciles it to TRACS. It must show unit, tenant, unit size, contract rent, tenant rent, assistance payment, utility allowance, move-in date, and certification effective date. A rent roll without effective dates is nearly useless to a reviewer and reads as such.\n• OCCUPANCY / VACANCY REPORT with vacancy durations. Vacancies over 60 days invite questions about waiting list management and unit turnaround.\n• MOVE-IN AND MOVE-OUT LOG for the review period. This is the reviewer's index to the highest-risk files.\n• UNIT TRANSFER LOG. Transfers are a frequent error source, because they are processed as terminations plus new admissions when they should be transfers, or vice versa.\n• CERTIFICATION SCHEDULE showing each household's annual recertification anniversary and the current certification effective date.\n• LIST OF HOUSEHOLDS WITH LIVE-IN AIDES, ZERO INCOME, POLICE/SECURITY UNITS, EMPLOYEE UNITS, and any unit not receiving assistance.\n• SECURITY DEPOSIT LEDGER, reconciled to the deposit account.\n\nWorked example 1 — the rent roll that produces four findings by itself. A 120-unit rent roll shows unit 3B with a contract rent of $1,145, a tenant rent of $0, and no certification effective date. Four questions follow: Is the household paying $0 legitimately (zero income with $0 TTP after utility allowance, which is possible), or is this a data error? If TTP is $0 and the utility allowance exceeds it, is a utility REIMBURSEMENT being paid, and does it appear on the voucher? When was the last certification? And is TRACS carrying the same figures? One line, four threads. Audit the rent roll line by line before submission and be able to answer each of these for every anomalous line.\n\nWorked example 2 — the transfer processed wrong. The Ruiz household moves from a 1-bedroom to a 2-bedroom in the same property after the birth of a child. The site processes a move-out from unit 104 and a move-in to unit 220. Consequences: TRACS records a termination and a new admission; the household's ANNUAL RECERTIFICATION ANNIVERSARY is reset to the new \"move-in\" date, which is wrong; the initial certification for the \"new\" admission may have triggered a fresh eligibility determination that was not required; and the security deposit accounting is wrong. The correct processing is a unit transfer, which preserves the anniversary. This error is nearly invisible in the file and glaring in TRACS.\n\nWorked example 3 — the zero-income list. Three households report zero income. The reviewer will sample all three. Before submission, verify for each: a current zero-income certification, documented follow-up at the property's stated interval (many properties commit in writing to quarterly zero-income reviews and then do not perform them — do not commit to what you will not do), the EIV No Income Reported report, and a documented conversation about how basic expenses are met. Zero income is legitimate and common; undocumented zero income is a reliable finding.\n\nWorked example 4 — the employee unit. A maintenance technician lives in unit 1A rent-free. Is that unit assisted? Is it included in the HAP contract? Is it counted in the vacancy rate? Owners get this wrong in both directions — billing assistance for an employee unit (a serious problem) or excluding a unit that should be counted for other purposes. Document the unit's status, the authority for it, and how it is treated on the voucher.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: the tenant who has not lived there for months. A household's file is current, TRACS shows assistance, and the manager mentions that the tenant has been in a nursing facility since January and the family says she is coming back. This is genuinely ambiguous — absence from the unit, temporary absence policies, and household composition rules interact, and the property's written policy on extended absence governs a great deal of it. What is NOT ambiguous is that the property must have a written policy, must apply it, and must document the facts. A property that has simply left the assistance running for nine months without any documented determination will be cited, regardless of which answer the policy produces.\n\nWorked example 6 — reconciling the security deposit ledger. Move-out files are the standard failure point. For each move-out in the period, verify: itemized statement provided within the state-law deadline, deposit disposition documented, refund issued or balance billed, and — critically — the termination transmitted in TRACS effective the correct date. A deposit ledger balance that does not tie to the sum of individual tenant balances is an accounting finding that will be repeated in the financial review."},{"heading":"EIV System Documentation for the Desk Review","body":"EIV documentation is one of the most heavily weighted areas of a modern MOR, and one of the easiest to fail on the record. Use of HUD's Enterprise Income Verification system is MANDATED by regulation at 24 CFR 5.233; the operational requirements are in Handbook 4350.3 REV-1 Chapter 9 and the EIV housing notices.\n\nWhat a desk review package should contain on EIV:\n\n• The property's written EIV policies and procedures.\n• The EIV MASTER FILE contents for the review period — the reports that live at the property level rather than in individual tenant files. In practice this includes the reports used for portfolio-level monitoring: identity verification / failed verification reports, the New Hires summary, Multiple Subsidy, and Deceased Tenants reports, retained for three years.\n• Evidence of the required user access administration: the list of authorized users with roles, signed access authorization forms and Rules of Behavior, coordinator access records, and documentation of TERMINATED users' access removal.\n• Evidence of annual security awareness training for every user.\n• The Income Discrepancy Report for the period and documentation of how each discrepancy was resolved. Note the retention distinction: Income Discrepancy and No Income reports belong in the TENANT file and are retained for the term of tenancy plus three years, while the master-file reports listed above are retained three years.\n• Evidence that an EIV Existing Tenant Search was run for each applicant before move-in.\n\nWorked example 1 — the failure that is invisible until it is fatal. A property has excellent tenant files with EIV income reports in every one — and no EIV master file at all, because the manager believed EIV documentation lived only in tenant files. The finding is not about any tenant. It is about the property-level monitoring system, and it is written as a systemic EIV deficiency. Building the master file takes an afternoon; explaining its absence takes a rating.\n\nWorked example 2 — the terminated user. An occupancy specialist resigned in March. In September, the reviewer finds the user's EIV access was never terminated. This is a security finding with teeth, because EIV carries federal privacy obligations and improper access is not merely an administrative slip. The control: termination of EIV access joins the property's employee offboarding checklist alongside keys and email, with the coordinator's confirmation retained. Ask for that checklist at every intake — it is a 30-second question that finds a real problem surprisingly often.\n\nWorked example 3 — the discrepancy that was resolved but not documented. The Income Discrepancy Report flags a household. The specialist investigated, determined the discrepancy arose from a job the tenant left before the certification period (correctly excluded), and moved on. Nothing was written down. At review, the file shows a flagged discrepancy and no resolution. The finding is written even though the underlying calculation was RIGHT. The rule to teach: the resolution memo — what the discrepancy was, what was verified, what was concluded, dated and signed, filed with the report — is the deliverable. The correct answer without the memo scores the same as the wrong answer.\n\nWorked example 4 — the Existing Tenant Search that was skipped. A property admits a household in June without running the Existing Tenant Search. In August, HUD data shows the household was simultaneously receiving assistance at another property for two months. Now there are three problems: the missing pre-admission search, a double-subsidy that must be resolved and likely repaid, and a tenant relationship that has become adversarial. This is why the search is a pre-move-in GATE, not a post-move-in formality. Build it into the move-in checklist as an initialed step with the search result printed and filed.\n\nWorked example 5 — the boundary case: EIV data that is not available. A newly admitted household has no EIV data yet because the certification has not been transmitted long enough for the system to populate. The property cannot produce an EIV income report at move-in for that household. This is not a deficiency — it is the normal operation of the system — but it MUST be documented in the file with a note stating why the report is absent and when it will be obtained. Undocumented absence looks identical to noncompliance.\n\nWorked example 6 — the ambiguous case: what if EIV and third-party verification disagree, and the tenant disputes both? The hierarchy is a topic in its own right (Chapter 12), but for desk review purposes what matters is that the FILE shows the dispute, the additional verification obtained, the determination made, and the effective date of any resulting change. A file that shows a messy, documented, reasoned process is compliant. A file that shows only the final number is not — and reviewers are specifically trained to look for the reasoning behind numbers that do not match EIV."},{"heading":"Waiting List and Marketing Documents","body":"The waiting list and marketing documents test whether the property is filling units fairly, affirmatively, and consistently with its own written plan.\n\nWhat to include:\n\n• The current WAITING LIST in the format actually maintained, showing application date and time, household size, unit size sought, preference claims, and current status of each applicant.\n• The APPLICATION LOG for the period, including applications received, rejected, withdrawn, and housed.\n• The AFFIRMATIVE FAIR HOUSING MARKETING PLAN (form HUD-935.2A) with evidence of HUD approval, plus documentation of the marketing actually performed under it.\n• REJECTION LETTERS issued during the period, with the documented reason and evidence that the applicant was informed of the right to respond or request a meeting.\n• Notices of waiting list openings and closings, and documentation of any purge.\n• The TENANT SELECTION PLAN, which is covered in depth in Chapter 15 but is also a desk review item.\n• Evidence of preference application, if the property uses preferences.\n\nWorked example 1 — the list that does not prove its own order. A property maintains its waiting list in a spreadsheet sorted by name. Application dates are present; times are not. Two applicants applied the same day for the same unit size, and one was housed. The property cannot demonstrate why. This is not a technical foul — waiting list order is the mechanism by which fair housing compliance is proven, and an unprovable order is treated as an absent one. The control: date AND time stamp, a system that logs its own changes, and a preserved record of the list as it stood at each selection.\n\nWorked example 2 — the AFHMP that is current but unperformed. The plan on file is HUD-approved and identifies specific outreach to a least-likely-to-apply population, naming three community organizations and a foreign-language publication. The property has done none of it in two years. The AFHMP is a commitment, not a filing. The finding is written against the plan's own terms. The cure is either to perform the marketing — with dated evidence, ad copies, letters, contact logs — or to amend the plan to what the property will actually do and get the amendment approved. Never leave a plan on file describing outreach nobody performs.\n\nWorked example 3 — the purge that removed a protected applicant. The property purges the waiting list by sending update letters and removing non-responders after 30 days. One applicant had a disability-related reason for not responding and had previously requested that communications be sent in an accessible format, which the property did not do. The purge itself was reasonable; its execution created a fair housing exposure. The rule: purge procedures must be in the TSP, must be applied uniformly, and must accommodate known communication needs. Keep the purge notices, the responses, and the removal decisions.\n\nWorked example 4 — the rejection letter that says too little. A rejection letter states only \"your application has been denied based on our screening criteria.\" Two problems: the applicant cannot respond meaningfully, and the property cannot later demonstrate that the criterion applied was in its TSP and was applied consistently. A defensible letter states the specific reason, identifies the source of the information relied upon, informs the applicant of the right to dispute the accuracy of that information, and explains how to request a meeting to discuss the rejection. The consistency test is the one reviewers actually run: pull five rejections and check whether the same criterion was applied to each.\n\nWorked example 5 — the boundary case: an open list with no applicants. A property in a soft submarket has an open waiting list with three names and chronic vacancy. Is the AFHMP being followed? Now the marketing documentation matters MORE, not less, because the reviewer's question becomes whether the property is marketing affirmatively to populations least likely to apply. Vacancy plus thin marketing evidence plus a stale AFHMP is a common combination at struggling properties and produces both an occupancy finding and a fair housing exposure.\n\nWorked example 6 — the ambiguous case: preferences and their proof. A property has a HUD-approved residency preference and applies it. The reviewer asks how the preference interacts with the AFHMP, because residency preferences can have disparate effects depending on the demographics of the drawn area. Have the analysis ready: the authority for the preference, its approval, its written terms in the TSP, and the record of how it was applied. Preferences are legitimate. Undocumented preferences are how properties end up in fair housing complaints."},{"heading":"Policy Documents and Administrative Records","body":"The final block of the desk review package is the property's written governance: the documents that state what the property has committed to do. Reviewers use them in a specific way — they read the policy, then look for evidence of the practice. The gap between the two is where findings live.\n\nThe standard set:\n\n• TENANT SELECTION PLAN, current and dated.\n• HOUSE RULES, with evidence of proper adoption and of distribution to tenants.\n• The HUD MODEL LEASE in current form, with all required addenda: the HUD-91067 VAWA lease addendum, the pet rules addendum where applicable, and any lead-based paint disclosure required for the property's vintage.\n• The EMERGENCY TRANSFER PLAN required under VAWA, and the VAWA notices (HUD-5380) and self-certification form (HUD-5382) the property uses.\n• REASONABLE ACCOMMODATION policy and the log of requests, decisions and dates.\n• GRIEVANCE procedure.\n• The property's own written procedures for certifications, EIV, waiting list, and repayment agreements.\n• Personnel and training records evidencing fair housing, VAWA and EIV security training.\n• The MANAGEMENT AGREEMENT and evidence of HUD approval of the agent, plus the current management certification.\n• Insurance certificates, current, naming the required parties.\n• Copies of prior MOR reports and the owner's responses.\n\nWorked example 1 — the policy that cites a superseded rule. A TSP states that applicants with net family assets over $5,000 must provide third-party asset verification, and the property's HOTMA implementation date is approaching. Or a policy references \"HUD Handbook 4350.3 Change-3\" when Change-4 governs. Neither error changes what staff actually do, and both are findings — the policy is required to be current. Build a policy review calendar: every written policy gets a review date, an owner, and a revision log. A policy with a revision history dated within twelve months signals a managed organization; one dated 2016 signals the opposite.\n\nWorked example 2 — the house rules that conflict with the lease. House rules prohibit \"any guest staying more than 7 consecutive days,\" while the HUD model lease and the property's own TSP contemplate guest stays under a different standard, and the property has a live-in aide in residence. Three problems: an internal conflict, a rule that may not be enforceable as written, and a potential fair housing issue if the rule is applied to a live-in aide or to a person's care provider. House rules must be consistent with the lease, must be adopted properly, and must not conflict with reasonable accommodation obligations. Read them side by side with the lease at every engagement — this takes twenty minutes and finds something almost every time.\n\nWorked example 3 — the accommodation log that does not exist. A property has granted several accommodations informally over the years — a reserved parking space, a permitted assistance animal, a modified payment date. Nothing is logged. Two consequences: the property cannot demonstrate a consistent, non-discriminatory process, and it cannot demonstrate that it responded promptly. Reviewers ask for the log. Build one even retroactively: date of request, requester, nature of request, verification obtained (only where the disability or need is not obvious), decision, date of decision, and any interactive process notes.\n\nWorked example 4 — the VAWA documents that are present but misused. The property has HUD-5380 and HUD-5382 in the binder and distributes them at every annual recertification, believing more is better. The required distribution points are specific: at DENIAL of admission or assistance, at ADMISSION, and with any notice of EVICTION or TERMINATION of assistance. Distributing at recertification is not itself a violation, but it is not what the requirement asks for, and properties that over-distribute frequently turn out to be UNDER-distributing at one of the three required points — most often at denial. Test all three points, not the one that is easy.\n\nWorked example 5 — the management certification and the agent approval. A property changed management agents 14 months ago. The desk review package contains the new agent's management agreement but no evidence of HUD approval of the agent, and no current management certification. Beyond the finding, remember the scheduling consequence from Chapter 1: an approved agent change triggers a new MOR within 6 months. If the agent change was approved 14 months ago and no MOR has occurred, something is off in the record — and it is better for the consultant to discover that than for the reviewer to.\n\nWorked example 6 — the ambiguous case: which version is \"current\"? A property revised its TSP in March; the board approved it in May; it was submitted to the CA in June; the CA has not responded. Which version governs in September? Operate under the newest version the property has adopted and submitted, keep the prior version with its date, and include a cover note in the desk review package explaining the sequence and the pending submission. Reviewers rarely cite a documented, disclosed transition. They routinely cite an undisclosed one."}]},{"id":"m82","unit":"u8","number":2,"title":"Financial Documents — What HUD Requires and Why","subtitle":"","sections":[{"heading":"Audited Financial Statements","body":"Financial documentation enters the MOR through Addendum B of Form 9834 — the financial addendum that is NOT reviewed by all Contract Administrators. Confirm scope in writing at intake. But even where a CA does not score Addendum B, the underlying obligations run to HUD asset management independently, and a consultant who tells a client \"not in scope, therefore not a problem\" is giving incomplete advice.\n\nThe core obligation. Under HUD's uniform financial reporting standards at 24 CFR Part 5, Subpart H, covered multifamily owners must submit financial information to HUD ANNUALLY, NO LATER THAN 90 DAYS AFTER THE END OF THE FISCAL YEAR, electronically through the Financial Assessment Subsystem (FASSUB). For most assisted and insured properties this means AUDITED financial statements prepared in accordance with generally accepted auditing standards and HUD's audit requirements, accompanied by the auditor's opinion, supplemental schedules, the surplus cash computation, footnotes, the owner's certifications, and reports on internal control and on compliance.\n\nWhat the reviewer does with them:\n\n• Confirms timely submission. A late filing is visible in HUD's system regardless of what the owner's package contains.\n• Reads the AUDITOR'S OPINION. A qualified opinion, a going-concern paragraph, or a disclaimer is a serious signal.\n• Reads the FINDINGS in the compliance and internal control reports. Audit findings frequently overlap MOR territory — deficient tenant file documentation, improper distributions, reserve funding shortfalls.\n• Compares the statements to the property's operations: occupancy, vacancy loss, HAP revenue against TRACS, reserve deposits against the required schedule, and surplus cash against distributions taken.\n\nWorked example 1 — the timing arithmetic clients get wrong. A property's fiscal year ends December 31. The submission is due within 90 days — by roughly March 31. A property with a June 30 fiscal year end is due by roughly September 28. Owners with multiple properties on different fiscal years routinely apply one deadline to all of them. Build a fiscal-year calendar for every client portfolio at intake; it takes fifteen minutes and prevents an entirely avoidable finding. (HUD has at times granted filing extensions; treat any extension as specific to the year and property it was granted for, and keep the written evidence.)\n\nWorked example 2 — the audit finding that becomes a MOR finding. The auditor's compliance report cites that \"tenant files tested did not consistently contain evidence of third-party income verification.\" Six months later the MOR reviewer reads the same report during the desk review, and now has a documented, owner-acknowledged pattern before opening a single file. Two lessons: the audit is an early-warning system a consultant should read FIRST at intake, and audit findings must be remediated on their own schedule, not left to be rediscovered.\n\nWorked example 3 — the going-concern paragraph. An auditor includes a substantial-doubt-about-going-concern emphasis based on negative working capital and deferred maintenance. This will not usually appear as a MOR occupancy finding, but it feeds the property's RISK CLASSIFICATION, and the risk classification drives MOR frequency under the 2022 rule. A property whose occupancy compliance is excellent and whose finances are distressed can be Troubled — and a Troubled property with a Superior MOR still gets reviewed within 24 months rather than 36. Tell clients this explicitly so they understand why great files did not buy them the longest interval.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: small properties and audit thresholds. Not every HUD-connected property files the same way; requirements differ by program, by whether the property is insured, and by the size and nature of federal assistance received. A 12-unit Section 8 property under one program may face different submission requirements than a 200-unit insured property. Do not generalize from the last client. Confirm the specific requirement from the regulatory agreement, the HAP contract, and current HUD guidance for that program — and if you cannot confirm it, say so and get the owner's accountant on the call rather than guessing.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: the statement that does not tie to TRACS. HAP revenue per the audited statements is $1,412,880; the sum of TRACS vouchers for the same period is $1,398,240 — a $14,640 difference. Possible explanations include timing differences at year end, adjustment vouchers processed in a later period, special claims, or an actual billing error. The reviewer's concern is not the difference itself; it is whether the owner can EXPLAIN it. Prepare a one-page reconciliation with the audited statements. A prepared reconciliation ends the conversation; an unprepared one starts an inquiry."},{"heading":"Budget and Financial Planning Documents","body":"The operating budget is where the reviewer tests whether the property is being managed to a plan or run month to month.\n\nWhat is typically required or requested:\n\n• The current approved OPERATING BUDGET, with the supporting worksheet where a budget-based rent adjustment was pursued (form HUD-92547-A is the budget worksheet used in that process, including the reserve for replacement deposit line).\n• Budget-to-actual comparisons, with variance explanations.\n• The most recent rent adjustment documentation: whether the adjustment was taken by OPERATING COST ADJUSTMENT FACTOR (OCAF) — the factor HUD calculates and publishes annually, applied to eligible contracts renewed under MAHRA — or as a BUDGET-BASED rent adjustment prepared under HUD Handbook 4350.1 Chapter 7 procedures.\n• Utility analysis supporting the utility allowance, and evidence of the required periodic review.\n• Evidence of the owner's tenant notice and comment process for rent increases where required by the contract and regulations.\n\nWorked example 1 — OCAF versus budget-based, decided correctly. A property's operating costs rose 9 percent in a year driven by a 40 percent insurance premium increase. The published OCAF for the year is well below that. An OCAF adjustment leaves the property short; a budget-based adjustment can capture the actual increase but requires documentation, justification, and processing time — and it invites scrutiny of every line. The right analysis: quantify the gap in dollars, weigh it against the preparation cost and the risk of a reduced approval, and decide deliberately. The wrong analysis — the common one — is to take OCAF every year because it is easier, then complain about operating deficits that feed the risk classification.\n\nWorked example 2 — the variance nobody explained. Budget shows maintenance payroll of $148,000; actual is $211,000. There may be an excellent reason (a vacancy in a contracted service replaced by in-house labor, a storm response, a one-time turnover surge). If the property cannot state the reason, the reviewer's next questions run toward whether payroll is properly allocated, whether identity-of-interest entities are involved, and whether the property is charging the project for services it did not receive. Variance explanation is cheap. Its absence is expensive.\n\nWorked example 3 — the utility allowance nobody has touched in six years. Utility allowances must be reviewed and adjusted using an appropriate methodology; a stale allowance produces two harms. If the allowance is too LOW, tenants pay more of their utilities out of a rent calculation that assumed less, which is a tenant-side harm and a common source of complaints. If it is too HIGH, HAP is overpaid and utility reimbursements may be overstated, which is a program-side harm and a repayment risk. Reviewers ask for the analysis and its date. Six years is a finding in either direction.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: the reimbursement that nobody is paying. In a property where tenants pay their own utilities, a household's utility allowance exceeds its Total Tenant Payment. The difference is a UTILITY REIMBURSEMENT owed to the tenant, and it must actually be paid on the schedule the program requires and must appear correctly on the voucher. Properties with few such households sometimes never build the process. Audit for it specifically: any household whose TTP is below its utility allowance should be receiving a reimbursement, and the ledger should prove it.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: a budget that assumes an occupancy the waiting list cannot support. The budget projects 97 percent occupancy; the property has run 89 percent for two years and its waiting list has eleven names. This is where the financial review and the occupancy review meet. The reviewer will read the vacancy loss line and ask about waiting list management and marketing. The consulting answer is not to change the budget assumption; it is to fix the pipeline — the AFHMP, the outreach, the application processing time — and then budget honestly. A budget built on an occupancy the property cannot achieve guarantees an unexplainable variance every quarter.\n\nWorked example 6 — the naive reading. \"We're under budget, so the financial review will go well.\" Being under budget on maintenance and capital lines while deferred maintenance accumulates is not a strength; it is a signal that operating results are being propped up by underinvestment, which shows up in NSPIRE scores, in the reserve analysis, and eventually in the risk classification. Read variances in both directions."},{"heading":"Reserve for Replacement — Requirements and Documentation","body":"The Reserve for Replacement is a HUD-controlled account funded by required monthly deposits and drawn only with HUD approval, for capital items. It is one of the most reliably deficient areas in multifamily compliance because it involves three parties — owner, agent, and HUD — and a form that people fill out badly.\n\nThe mechanics:\n\n• The regulatory agreement (and, for many properties, the HAP contract or its renewal terms) establishes the required monthly deposit. The deposit amount can be adjusted by HUD, and adjustments are frequently missed when they occur.\n• Funds are held in an interest-bearing account in HUD's control, typically requiring HUD's signature or approval for release.\n• Releases are requested on FORM HUD-9250, \"Funds Authorizations,\" which is the form used for both Reserve for Replacement and Residual Receipts releases. Supporting documentation — invoices, bids, evidence of completion — accompanies the request.\n• Records must be retained: HUD's guidance on forms HUD-9250 authorizing releases contemplates retention for the current year and the prior three years.\n• The reserve balance and deposit activity appear in the audited financial statements and are reconciled by the reviewer.\n\nWorked example 1 — the deposit that stopped. A property's required deposit is $4,180 per month. Deposits were made monthly through August 2024, then stopped for four months during a cash crunch, then resumed. The shortfall is $16,720. Consequences: a compliance finding, a regulatory agreement default in substance, a likely restriction on distributions, and a note in the audited statements. The remediation is straightforward — fund the arrears and document the catch-up — but the finding is written for the period. The control every client needs: the reserve deposit is an automated transfer, scheduled, and reconciled monthly by someone other than the person who initiates it.\n\nWorked example 2 — the release request that gets rejected, and why. A property submits a HUD-9250 for $62,000 to replace roofing on two buildings, attaching a single contractor proposal and no scope detail. Common rejection reasons: insufficient documentation of the need, no evidence of competitive procurement where expected, items that are operating expenses rather than capital replacements, work already performed without prior approval, or an amount that would drop the reserve below a level HUD is willing to accept. The prepared request states the item, its age and condition, the scope, the procurement process, the amount, the reserve balance before and after, and the completion timeline.\n\nWorked example 3 — the expense charged to the wrong bucket. A property charges $9,400 of unit turnover painting and carpet cleaning to the Reserve for Replacement. Painting and cleaning on turnover are ordinarily OPERATING expenses; carpet REPLACEMENT on a defined useful-life cycle may be a capital item. Miscategorization does two things: it depletes a reserve intended for capital needs, and it flatters operating results. Auditors catch this, and MOR reviewers read audits. Teach the distinction with a written capitalization policy the property actually applies.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: emergency work performed before approval. A boiler fails in February. The property replaces it immediately for $38,000 and submits the HUD-9250 afterward. Emergency work is a real category and HUD does approve after-the-fact releases in genuine emergencies — but the property's position depends entirely on documentation: the failure, the health and safety consequence, the attempt to contact HUD or the AE, the procurement done under the circumstances, and the prompt submission afterward. A property that documents contemporaneously is usually fine. A property that submits six months later with one invoice is not.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: reserve adequacy versus reserve compliance. A property makes every required deposit on time and has $211,000 in reserve. Its capital needs assessment shows $1.4 million of needs over ten years. Is it compliant? Yes. Is it adequate? No. The MOR tests compliance with the deposit requirement; asset management and the risk classification test adequacy. This distinction is a genuine consulting opportunity: a compliant-but-underfunded reserve is the argument for a deposit increase or a budget-based rent adjustment, and framing it that way turns a compliance engagement into a strategic one.\n\nWorked example 6 — residual receipts, the account owners forget. Where the regulatory agreement requires it, surplus cash above allowable distributions is deposited to a residual receipts account, also under HUD control and also released on HUD-9250. Owners who take distributions without correctly computing surplus cash create a residual receipts deficiency AND an improper distribution — two findings with one act. Compute surplus cash from the audited statements, annually, before any distribution is taken."},{"heading":"HAP Billing and Voucher Documentation","body":"HAP billing is where occupancy compliance turns into money. Every certification error eventually becomes a billing error, and billing errors are the findings that produce repayment demands.\n\nThe mechanics: the owner or agent transmits tenant certification data (the 50059 family) to TRACS, and bills monthly through the voucher process. HUD or the Contract Administrator pays against the voucher. The reviewer compares the voucher history to the certification history to the rent roll to the tenant files.\n\nWhat reviewers look for:\n\n• Timeliness of certification transmission. Certifications transmitted after their effective dates create retroactive adjustments and are visible in TRACS.\n• Assistance billed for units or households not eligible for it — a move-out not terminated, a unit transfer mishandled, an ineligible student household, a household over an applicable limit.\n• Adjustments and special claims: vacancy claims, unpaid rent and damage claims where the program permits them, and whether they are supported by the required documentation.\n• Repayment activity: amounts owed by tenants, repayment agreements, and amounts owed back to HUD.\n• Consistency between the utility reimbursement amounts on the voucher and the tenant ledgers.\n\nWorked example 1 — the certification transmitted late, and its two consequences. An annual recertification effective July 1 is transmitted September 12. Consequence one: a compliance finding for untimely processing. Consequence two, the one owners miss: the July and August vouchers were billed at the OLD assistance amount. If the recertification reduced assistance (income went up), the property has been overpaid for two months and the correction reduces a future voucher. If it increased assistance, the property has been underpaid and must claim the difference. Either way, a retroactive adjustment appears in the record, and a pattern of them is a finding in itself.\n\nWorked example 2 — the move-out that kept paying. A tenant vacates March 18. The termination is transmitted in TRACS effective June 1 because the file sat in a to-do pile. Assistance was billed for late March, April, and May. That is an overpayment that must be repaid, and depending on the facts it can implicate special claim rules for the vacancy period. The control: the move-out checklist ends with the TRACS termination, and a monthly reconciliation compares the rent roll's occupied units to TRACS's assisted units. Any unit assisted in TRACS but vacant on the rent roll is investigated the same week.\n\nWorked example 3 — the arithmetic of an income error, all the way through. A household's annual income was understated by $3,600 because a second job was never counted. Their TTP was understated by roughly $90 per month (30 percent of $3,600 divided by 12 is $90). The error persisted 14 months. Tenant-side underpayment: $1,260. HAP overpayment: the same $1,260, because the assistance made up the difference. So the property must repay HUD $1,260 and separately pursue $1,260 from the tenant under a repayment agreement — and if the tenant cannot pay, the owner absorbs it. Show clients this arithmetic once and file-level accuracy stops being an abstraction.\n\nWorked example 4 — the repayment agreement done wrong. The property enters a repayment agreement requiring $310 per month from a household whose monthly adjusted income is $840 and whose tenant rent is $252. HUD's guidance frames repayment terms around affordability, with the widely used benchmark that the monthly repayment plus the tenant rent should not exceed roughly 40 percent of monthly adjusted income. Here, $252 plus $310 is $562 against $840 — about 67 percent. The agreement is unaffordable, the tenant defaults, the property initiates termination, and now there is a wrongful-termination exposure on top of an uncollected balance. Structure repayment agreements that can actually be performed, document the affordability analysis, and revisit them when income changes.\n\nWorked example 5 — the boundary case: the tenant who moves out owing money. A repayment balance of $2,140 remains when the household vacates. The obligation does not disappear, but collection becomes a general receivable, and the amount owed to HUD does not depend on whether the owner collects. Owners frequently assume the two are linked. They are not: the owner's obligation to repay improperly received assistance stands on its own. Budget for it.\n\nWorked example 6 — the ambiguous case: whose error was it? A certification error originated from an EIV report the property relied on in good faith, which itself reflected an employer's erroneous wage reporting. Does the owner still repay? Generally the subsidy paid in error is still subject to recovery, but the owner's GOOD FAITH and documented process matter enormously to how the finding is written and to whether it is treated as an administrative error or as something worse. Documentation of the verification process — what was obtained, when, from whom, and what was reasonably concluded — is the difference between a correctable error and a program integrity finding."},{"heading":"Insurance, Taxes, and Regulatory Agreement Compliance","body":"The last financial block covers the obligations that sit in the regulatory agreement and the mortgage documents rather than in the occupancy handbook. Owners treat these as back-office items until one of them produces a default.\n\nINSURANCE. The regulatory agreement and mortgage require coverage in specified types and amounts, with HUD or the mortgagee named as required. Reviewers verify current certificates and adequacy relative to the property's replacement cost.\n\n• Property/hazard coverage adequate to replacement cost, with attention to coinsurance provisions.\n• General liability at required limits.\n• Fidelity bond or crime coverage for those handling project funds — routinely missing, and specifically required in many regulatory agreements.\n• Flood insurance where the property is in a special flood hazard area. This one is non-negotiable and is a frequent finding.\n• Workers' compensation as required by state law.\n• Boiler and machinery where applicable.\n\nTAXES AND ASSESSMENTS. Real estate taxes current, or documented abatement/exemption status where the property holds one. Delinquent taxes are a default under most regulatory agreements and mortgages.\n\nREGULATORY AGREEMENT COMPLIANCE. Distributions only from surplus cash, only at permitted times, only in permitted amounts; project funds used only for project purposes; books and records maintained at the project or an approved location; no unapproved encumbrances, leases of project property, or changes in ownership or management.\n\nWorked example 1 — the coinsurance trap. A property carries $6,000,000 in hazard coverage on buildings whose replacement cost is $9,000,000, under a policy with an 80 percent coinsurance clause. Required coverage to avoid penalty: $7,200,000. After a $400,000 fire loss, the coinsurance penalty reduces the recovery by roughly a sixth — a real loss of tens of thousands of dollars, in a property whose reserve cannot absorb it. Reviewers are not insurance adjusters, but a certificate showing coverage well below replacement cost draws the question. Get a current replacement cost valuation and match coverage to it.\n\nWorked example 2 — the missing fidelity bond. A management agent handles project funds; the regulatory agreement requires fidelity coverage; the agent carries a blanket bond that does not name the project. This is a finding, and it is also a genuine risk: the most common significant fraud in this industry is misappropriation by someone with routine access to project funds. Verify the bond names or covers the project, at the required amount, and that the certificate is current.\n\nWorked example 3 — the distribution taken from the wrong source. An owner takes a $75,000 distribution in a year in which the surplus cash computation shows $41,000 available. The excess $34,000 is an improper distribution: it must be returned to the project, it is a regulatory agreement violation, and it will appear in the audit and then in the MOR. Compounding factor: if the owner also failed to fund residual receipts, the same act produced two findings. The control is procedural — no distribution is taken until the audited surplus cash computation is complete and reviewed.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: identity-of-interest services. The owner's affiliate provides landscaping to the project at $6,200 per month. Identity-of-interest arrangements are not prohibited, but they are scrutinized: the service must be actually provided, the price must be reasonable relative to market, the relationship must be disclosed, and required approvals must be obtained. The documentation to keep is a contemporaneous market comparison (two or three independent bids), the contract, and evidence of the services performed. Owners who cannot produce a market comparison after the fact usually cannot construct a persuasive one.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: flood maps that changed. FEMA remaps the area and the property, previously outside the special flood hazard area, is now inside it. Nobody at the property learns this until a lender notice arrives eighteen months later. The obligation attaches when the designation changes, not when the owner notices. Add an annual flood zone verification to the insurance renewal checklist. The same discipline applies to changes in required coverage amounts after a rehabilitation increases replacement cost.\n\nWorked example 6 — the naive reading that costs a client. \"Our taxes are being appealed, so they're not really delinquent.\" A pending assessment appeal generally does not suspend the obligation to pay, and unpaid taxes accrue penalties and can become a lien senior to the mortgage. Advise clients to pay under protest where the jurisdiction permits it and to document the appeal, rather than treating an appeal as a deferral. The regulatory agreement asks whether taxes are current, not whether they are contested."}]},{"id":"m83","unit":"u8","number":3,"title":"Organizing and Submitting the Perfect Desk Review Package","subtitle":"","sections":[{"heading":"Creating a Desk Review Document Index","body":"Addendum C of Form 9834 is the Required Documents List. The reviewer is going to work from a list; your client should submit against that same list, in that order, with a table of contents that names every item and states where it is. This is not cosmetic. A reviewer who spends forty minutes hunting for the AFHMP has formed a view of the property's records management before evaluating any of them.\n\nThe structure that works:\n\n• A one-page COVER MEMO (Section 3 covers what goes in it).\n• A TABLE OF CONTENTS keyed to the reviewer's own required documents list, with a tab or bookmark number for each item.\n• Every item present, in list order.\n• For each item that does not exist or does not apply, an N/A PAGE in its position, stating in one line why. Never a gap.\n• Consistent labeling: every document shows what it is, the period it covers, and the date it was prepared or approved.\n• A document control page: who assembled the package, the as-of date for point-in-time documents (rent roll, waiting list), and a contact for questions.\n\nWorked example 1 — the index that answers questions before they are asked. Item 14 on the CA's list is \"current utility allowance analysis.\" The property's analysis is three years old and a new one is in progress. Two ways to submit:\n  (a) Include the 3-year-old analysis under tab 14. The reviewer notes a stale analysis and writes a finding.\n  (b) Include the 3-year-old analysis under tab 14, plus a one-page note: the date of the current analysis, the engagement letter with the firm performing the update, the expected completion date, and the interim step taken. The finding may still be written, but it is now written against a property that knows and is acting, which is the difference between a systemic deficiency and a scheduled remediation.\nThe document is the same. The framing is the entire difference in weight.\n\nWorked example 2 — the point-in-time trap. The rent roll is dated the 1st; the waiting list is printed the 12th; the occupancy report is dated the 20th. During the on-site visit the reviewer finds a household on the rent roll who is not on the occupancy report. Nothing is wrong — the reports are as of different dates — but the reviewer has spent an hour proving that. Fix by choosing ONE as-of date for all point-in-time reports, stating it on the document control page, and noting any material change between that date and submission.\n\nWorked example 3 — the naming convention that saves a rating. Files named \"Scan001.pdf\" through \"Scan046.pdf\" force the reviewer to open each one. Files named \"14_Utility_Allowance_Analysis_2023-06.pdf\" are self-indexing. Where the CA accepts electronic submission, use a numbered naming convention matching the required documents list, and include the index as both a PDF and a plain listing. This costs a junior staffer two hours and buys goodwill that is impossible to buy any other way.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: documents that are too large. A property's waiting list runs 900 applicants and its work order history runs 4,000 records. Do not dump raw exports. Submit a summary with defined fields plus the full data in a usable format, and state in the index that the complete records are available on site and in what form. Reviewers ask for what they need; burying them signals either disorganization or evasion.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: privileged or sensitive material. The package request includes \"all correspondence regarding tenant complaints,\" and one file involves pending litigation and communications with counsel. Do not silently omit it. Identify in the index that an item is withheld, state the general basis, and have the owner's counsel address it directly with the reviewer. Silent omission of a requested item, discovered later, is far more damaging than a disclosed and explained withholding. Note separately that VAWA-related documentation follows its own confidentiality rules (Chapter 17) and is not simply filed with general correspondence.\n\nWorked example 6 — the index as a reusable asset. Build the index once as a template with the standard items, then maintain it continuously. At the next review the property regenerates a current package in days rather than weeks. This is also the artifact that lets a consultant serve more clients: the index, the N/A conventions, and the naming convention travel from engagement to engagement while the contents change."},{"heading":"Common Desk Review Deficiencies — And How to Prevent Them","body":"These are the deficiencies that appear over and over. Each is presented with its root cause and the control that prevents it, because a corrective action response that names a control is worth ten that promise diligence.\n\n1. LATE SUBMISSION. Root cause: the notification letter goes to a general email or an address of record nobody monitors. Control: a designated recipient, a same-day calendar entry with the submission deadline and two interim milestones, and confirmation of receipt requested in writing.\n\n2. INCOMPLETE PACKAGE. Root cause: assembling from memory rather than from the reviewer's list. Control: submit against the list, with N/A pages.\n\n3. RENT ROLL THAT DOES NOT TIE TO TRACS. Root cause: corrections processed in the property software but not transmitted, or transmitted and not reflected in the local system. Control: a monthly three-way reconciliation — rent roll, file, TRACS — with exceptions cleared within the month.\n\n4. STALE POLICIES. Root cause: nobody owns the policy library. Control: a policy register listing each document, its owner, its last revision date, and its next review date, reviewed quarterly.\n\n5. EIV MASTER FILE GAPS. Root cause: the reports are generated for a purpose and then discarded, or a staff transition breaks the routine. Control: a standing monthly and quarterly EIV calendar with a printed log signed by the person who ran each report, kept in the master file.\n\n6. AFHMP NOT PERFORMED. Root cause: the plan was written to be approved, not to be executed. Control: convert the AFHMP into a marketing calendar with dated deliverables, and file the evidence — ad copies, letters, contact logs — against each item as it happens.\n\n7. MISSING PROOF OF TRAINING. Root cause: training happens and nobody keeps the sign-in sheet. Control: a training register per employee with dates, topics, provider, and certificates, updated the day training occurs.\n\n8. CERTIFICATIONS TRANSMITTED LATE. Root cause: a manual reminder process dependent on one person. Control: automated recertification calendars with a monthly queue report reviewed by two people.\n\n9. UNSIGNED OR EXPIRED CONSENT FORMS. Root cause: the HUD-9887/9887-A renewal is treated as a formality. Control: the certification checklist blocks completion until the consent forms are executed and dated.\n\n10. NO EVIDENCE OF SUPERVISORY REVIEW. Root cause: review happens informally. Control: a second signature line on the certification checklist, dated.\n\nWorked example 1 — root cause versus symptom, worked through. Symptom: six late annual recertifications. Naive cause: \"staff were busy.\" Real root cause analysis asks WHEN each certification entered the process. If notices went out on time and tenants returned paperwork late, the root cause is a follow-up gap and the control is a documented escalation at 30 days. If notices never went out, the root cause is the notice generation process and the control is automation plus verification. If notices went out and paperwork came back on time but processing lagged, the root cause is capacity and the control is staffing or workload redistribution. Three different findings, three different corrective actions — and a reviewer can tell which one you actually did.\n\nWorked example 2 — the deficiency that hides another. A package is missing the current management certification. The obvious cure is to produce it. But WHY is it missing? Investigation shows the agent changed 14 months ago and the transition checklist was never completed, which also means the EIV coordinator was never formally reassigned and two former-agent users may still have access. One missing document, three findings behind it. Always ask what a missing document implies about the process that should have produced it.\n\nWorked example 3 — the boundary case: a document that exists in two versions. The property has house rules distributed to tenants in 2019 and a revised set adopted in 2024 that was never distributed. Which is operative? For enforcement purposes, generally the version properly adopted and distributed to tenants with required notice. Submitting the 2024 version alone would misrepresent what tenants received. Submit both with a note explaining the sequence and, more importantly, complete the distribution properly — a rule nobody received cannot fairly be enforced.\n\nWorked example 4 — the ambiguous case: whether to disclose a deficiency you have not yet cured. Some consultants advise never volunteering a problem. That advice is wrong for the desk review, for a specific structural reason: Part I is scored, the reviewer has independent data, and the on-site visit follows. A deficiency the reviewer finds after you had the chance to disclose it damages credibility for the entire review, including on items where you would otherwise get the benefit of the doubt. Disclose, with a remediation plan and a date. Reserve non-disclosure for matters where counsel is involved and says so.\n\nWorked example 5 — the pattern to look for in your own package. Before submitting, read the package as an adversary for thirty minutes and write down every question you would ask. Then answer each one, either by adding a document or by adding a line to the cover memo. Almost every finding a reviewer writes from a desk review package is an answer to a question the package raised and did not answer."},{"heading":"Curing Deficiencies Before Submission","body":"The window between the notification letter and the submission deadline is the single most productive period in the entire MOR cycle. What can be cured in that window should be, and what cannot should be disclosed.\n\nTriage in this order, because this is the order of weight:\n\n1. Items affecting ELIGIBILITY AND SUBSIDY. Missing or expired certifications, ineligible households, EIV Existing Tenant Search omissions, double subsidy, billing for vacated units. These carry money and program integrity.\n2. Items affecting TENANT RIGHTS. VAWA notice distribution, reasonable accommodation requests without documented responses, rejection letters without required content, lease addenda not executed.\n3. SYSTEMIC documentation gaps. EIV master file, training records, policy currency, waiting list documentation.\n4. FILE-LEVEL paperwork. Missing signatures, unfiled documents, unmarked superseded documents.\n5. COSMETIC organization. Tabs, labels, indexing.\n\nWorked example 1 — a 30-day window, worked. Notification received the 5th; package due the 30th; a consultant is engaged the 8th.\n  Days 8-10: run the three-way reconciliation (rent roll / files / TRACS) and pull the EIV report inventory. Identify the money items.\n  Days 10-16: cure eligibility and subsidy items. Complete overdue certifications, transmit corrections, terminate stale assisted units in TRACS, run and file any Existing Tenant Searches that can still be run, quantify any repayment exposure.\n  Days 14-20: tenant rights items. Reconstruct the VAWA distribution record, document accommodation decisions, review the last twelve months of rejection letters for content.\n  Days 16-24: systemic gaps. Rebuild the EIV master file, assemble training records, update policies with revision dates and a policy register.\n  Days 20-27: file-level sweep of the predicted sample (Chapter 3's risk categories).\n  Days 26-29: assemble, index, write the cover memo, internal adversarial read.\n  Day 29: submit with confirmation of receipt. Not day 30 — leave one day for a transmission failure.\n\nWorked example 2 — the cure that must not be faked. A property discovers that 9 households never received the VAWA notice at admission. It cannot un-ring that bell, and back-dating a distribution log is fraud, not remediation. The correct cure: distribute the notices NOW, document the distribution with the actual current date, write a memo explaining what was discovered, when, how, and what control now prevents recurrence, and disclose it in the cover memo. A property that discloses this typically receives a finding with a straightforward corrective action. A property caught back-dating faces a different kind of proceeding entirely. State this to clients bluntly and early: we fix forward, we never fix backward.\n\nWorked example 3 — the correction that must be sequenced. A property finds four income calculation errors. Correcting them requires corrected 50059s, which generate retroactive HAP adjustments, which change tenant rents, which require tenant notice, which may require repayment agreements. Do not transmit corrections piecemeal in the last week before submission. Sequence: compute all corrections, quantify total exposure, prepare tenant notices with proper advance timing, prepare repayment agreements with an affordability analysis, then transmit and disclose the whole package at once with a summary schedule. Partial corrections mid-stream look like an unraveling.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: a cure that creates a tenant hardship right before a review. Correcting an error will raise a household's rent by $180 per month effective the first of next month. Rent increases require proper advance notice; a correction is not an exception to that. Compute the correct effective date under the notice rules, tell the reviewer in the cover memo what the correction is and when it takes effect, and do not shortcut notice to make the number look better on the submission date. A reviewer who sees a compliant correction in progress is satisfied; a reviewer who sees a tenant notice deadline violated to accelerate a correction has a new finding.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: cure or disclose when you cannot tell if it is wrong. A household's asset calculation depends on whether an irrevocable trust is accessible to the family. The property's determination is defensible but not obviously right. Do not silently pick the answer that is better for the property. Document the analysis, state the basis, cite the handbook provision relied on, and note in the cover memo that the treatment involves a judgment. Reviewers cite undocumented judgments; they discuss documented ones.\n\nWorked example 6 — what NOT to spend the window on. Reorganizing the policy binder, printing new tabs, or rewriting the tenant selection plan from scratch when the existing one is merely outdated in two places. These feel productive and score almost nothing. If the window is short, money items first, always."},{"heading":"Electronic vs. Physical Desk Review Packages","body":"Submission format varies by Contract Administrator, and getting it wrong is the most avoidable finding in this business. Confirm the required format in writing when the notification letter arrives; do not assume last cycle's method still applies, particularly if the CA changed.\n\nELECTRONIC SUBMISSION — now the common default. Considerations:\n\n• Portal versus email versus secure file transfer. Portals often have size limits per file and per submission; email attachments frequently bounce on size or are stripped by security filters.\n• File format: PDF, text-searchable where possible. A scanned image PDF is acceptable but a searchable one is materially easier to review.\n• Naming convention keyed to the required documents list.\n• Bookmarks in combined PDFs, or discrete files with an index.\n• Proof of transmission and receipt. Screenshot the confirmation; save the portal receipt; request written acknowledgment.\n• PERSONALLY IDENTIFIABLE INFORMATION. Tenant data transmitted electronically must be handled consistently with HUD's privacy and EIV security requirements. Use the CA's designated secure channel. Emailing unencrypted tenant income data because the portal was slow is a security incident, not a shortcut.\n\nPHYSICAL SUBMISSION — still used by some administrators and for on-site production:\n\n• Three-ring binders with numbered tabs matching the index, not stapled stacks.\n• A duplicate copy retained by the property, identical and dated, so that the property knows exactly what the reviewer has.\n• Trackable delivery with signature. The delivery receipt is the proof of timeliness.\n• For on-site production, a dedicated table and a labeled box, not documents distributed across three offices.\n\nWorked example 1 — the transmission that never arrived. A property emails a 180 MB package on the due date to the reviewer's individual address. The mail server silently drops it. Nobody notices for nine days. The finding is late submission, and the property's evidence — a sent-items entry — proves only that it tried. Control: submit through the designated channel, confirm receipt affirmatively, and if no acknowledgment arrives within one business day, follow up by phone and document the call.\n\nWorked example 2 — the searchable-PDF advantage, quantified. A reviewer working through a 400-page non-searchable scan spends hours locating specific documents. The same package, searchable and bookmarked, is navigable in minutes. This does not change any substantive fact — and it changes the reviewer's experience of the property's competence for two days. Where the property's scanner produces images, run OCR. It is a setting, not a project.\n\nWorked example 3 — the redaction question. Should tenant social security numbers be redacted in a desk review package? Do not improvise. HUD reviewers and CAs are authorized recipients of tenant data for review purposes, and redacting information the reviewer needs creates its own problem. Ask the CA what their protocol requires, follow it, transmit through the secure channel they designate, and document the instruction you were given. The wrong move in either direction — over-redacting a document into uselessness, or emailing unprotected PII to a personal address — is worse than asking.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: hybrid submissions. A CA takes the policy documents electronically but expects tenant files to be produced on site. The property scans everything and offers a link to the reviewer on arrival. If the reviewer's own systems will not open the property's file-sharing platform, or the site's internet is unreliable, the review stalls. Test the actual mechanics before the visit: confirm access works from the reviewer's device or provide a local machine, and keep a printed subset of the sampled files as a fallback.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: what the property keeps. Whatever the format, the property retains an EXACT COPY of what was submitted, dated, with the transmission record. When the report arrives sixty days later and says a document was not provided, the argument is decided by whether the property can produce the submitted package and its receipt. This is a two-minute discipline that resolves an entire category of disputes.\n\nWorked example 6 — the practical build. Maintain the desk review package as a living folder structure on the property's server, organized by the required documents list, updated as documents are created. Then a submission is an export and an index refresh, not a project. Properties that adopt this go from a three-week scramble to a two-day assembly, which is exactly the difference the next section is about."},{"heading":"Maintaining a Continuous Desk Review Readiness Posture","body":"The properties that score well are not the ones that prepare hardest; they are the ones that never stop being ready. Under the 2022 risk-based schedule this matters more than it used to: a Not Troubled property with an Above Average rating may not see a reviewer for 36 months, which is long enough for three staff turnovers and complete process drift — and long enough that the property has no recent practice at being reviewed.\n\nThe readiness system, on a calendar:\n\nMONTHLY\n• Three-way reconciliation: rent roll, tenant files, TRACS. Clear exceptions within the month.\n• EIV monthly report cycle, printed, logged, filed in the master file.\n• Certification queue review: what is due in the next 120 days, and what is overdue.\n• Voucher review before submission: any unit assisted in TRACS but vacant on the rent roll.\n• Work order aging review.\n\nQUARTERLY\n• EIV quarterly report cycle and the user access audit — who has access, is each still employed, is each still authorized.\n• Zero-income household follow-up, if the property's policy commits to a quarterly interval.\n• Sample audit: pull five files at random against Addendum A and score them.\n• Policy register review: anything due for revision.\n• Waiting list integrity check: order, purge status, preference application.\n\nANNUALLY\n• Full self-assessment against Form 9834, all applicable categories.\n• Utility allowance analysis review.\n• AFHMP performance review against the plan's own commitments.\n• Fair housing, VAWA, and EIV security training, with the register updated.\n• Insurance renewal review, including flood zone verification and replacement cost.\n• Financial statement submission on the 90-day clock.\n• Desk review package refresh, so the folder structure is current.\n\nWorked example 1 — the cost comparison that sells the retainer. Reactive model: a 20-hour internal scramble plus 60 consultant hours in the 30 days before each review, plus the corrective action work after a Satisfactory-with-findings result. Continuous model: roughly 6 to 10 staff hours per month on the calendar above, plus a 20-hour annual self-assessment. The continuous model costs more hours in total across three years — and it produces Above Average, which under the matrix means the third year has no review at all. Present both columns to a client with the review interval in the bottom row. That is the argument.\n\nWorked example 2 — the staff transition, which is where readiness actually dies. An occupancy specialist leaves. Everything that lived in her habits — the reminder spreadsheet, the EIV report routine, the follow-up calls at 30 days — stops. Six months later the property has a queue of late certifications and two quarters of missing EIV reports. Control: every recurring task has a written procedure, a calendar entry that belongs to a ROLE rather than a person, and a documented backup. Test it by having the backup perform the task once per quarter.\n\nWorked example 3 — the 36-month gap problem. A property earns Above Average and is next reviewed in three years. In month 30, the manager realizes nobody has run the quarterly self-audit since month 8. The fix is not a heroic sprint at month 34. It is a scheduled 12-month and 24-month internal mock review, calendared the day the last report arrives. Long intervals require MORE self-discipline, not less, and a consultant who sells only pre-review sprints is leaving the most valuable work — and the recurring revenue — on the table.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: readiness at a property in transition. A property is being sold, or the agent is changing. Recall the 6-month trigger: an approved change of ownership or management agent requires a new MOR within 6 months regardless of rating. The readiness posture during a transition should intensify, not relax. The specific deliverables: a dated file inventory at transition, a written record of what was received and what was missing, and immediate reassignment of EIV coordinator and user roles.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: how much readiness is too much. A property spends four hours a week on self-audit and finds nothing for six consecutive months. That is a signal to reduce frequency, not to keep grinding — move the file sample from monthly to quarterly and reinvest the time in the areas that DID produce findings. Readiness should be risk-weighted just like the reviewer's sample. Track where your own audits find things, and audit there more.\n\nWorked example 6 — the artifact that proves readiness. Keep a READINESS BINDER (physical or electronic) containing the monthly reconciliation logs, the quarterly audit results, the annual self-assessment against 9834, and the record of what each found and how it was resolved. When a reviewer sees that a property found and fixed six issues over the past year before anyone asked, the entire posture of the review changes — from \"what is wrong here\" to \"does this system work.\" That shift is worth more than any single document in the package."}]},{"id":"m91","unit":"u9","number":1,"title":"The Complete Tenant File — Structure and Required Documents","subtitle":"","sections":[{"heading":"Why the Tenant File Is the Heart of MOR Compliance","body":"Addendum A of Form HUD-9834 is the Tenant File Checklist, applied file by file across the sample. More MOR findings originate in tenant files than in any other single area, for a structural reason: the tenant file is where eligibility, income, rent, subsidy, lease rights, and fair housing obligations all come to rest in one folder. Everything the property did for that household either shows in the file or, for review purposes, did not happen.\n\nThe governing principle, and the sentence to repeat to every client: IF IT IS NOT DOCUMENTED, IT DID NOT HAPPEN. A reviewer cannot score intent, effort, or a manager's memory. This is not bureaucratic pedantry — it is the only way a third party can verify that federal funds were paid to an eligible household in the correct amount.\n\nWhat a well-built file must be able to prove, in this order:\n\n1. ELIGIBILITY at admission — income within limits, program-specific eligibility (age, disability, student rules), citizenship or eligible immigration status documentation, and that the household was selected consistent with the waiting list and the Tenant Selection Plan.\n2. INCOME AND ASSETS, verified in accordance with the verification hierarchy, for every certification.\n3. RENT AND SUBSIDY, correctly computed and correctly transmitted, with every change supported.\n4. LEASE AND RIGHTS — the current lease, every required addendum, every required notice, and evidence of delivery.\n5. THE HISTORY — an unbroken chronological record from application through the current certification, with no gaps and no unexplained documents.\n\nFile organization. HUD does not mandate a single physical structure, and reviewers will accept any organization they can navigate. What matters is CONSISTENCY: every file in the property built the same way, so a reviewer opening file six knows where to look because of what they saw in file one. The common structure, most-recent-first within each section:\n\n• Section 1 — Current certification: the current HUD-50059, the signed HUD-9887/9887-A, the verification documents supporting it, and the EIV income report with any discrepancy resolution.\n• Section 2 — Prior certifications, chronological.\n• Section 3 — Lease and addenda: current lease, all addenda, house rules acknowledgment, unit transfer documentation.\n• Section 4 — Application and admission: the original application, screening documentation, verification of eligibility, and the admission-stage notices.\n• Section 5 — Correspondence and notices, including rent change notices with evidence of delivery.\n• Section 6 — Inspections and maintenance related to the household.\nNote that VAWA documentation does NOT belong in this file at all — it is kept separately and securely, for reasons covered in Chapter 17.\n\nWorked example 1 — the file that is right and looks wrong. A household's income was calculated correctly, the rent is correct, and TRACS matches. But the file contains three loose verification forms with no certification attached, an unmarked superseded 50059, and documents filed oldest-first in one section and newest-first in another. The reviewer spends twenty minutes reconstructing the sequence and writes a file organization finding. The MONEY was right. The FILE was not, and the file is what is scored.\n\nWorked example 2 — the gap that tells a story. A file shows certifications effective 3/1/22, 3/1/23, and 3/1/25. There is no 3/1/24. Three possibilities: the certification was done and misfiled; the certification was never done and the household went 24 months without recertification; or the household moved out and back in. The reviewer will resolve this from TRACS in ninety seconds. Your job at intake is to run the same check first — pull each sampled household's certification history from TRACS and compare it to the file, looking for exactly this pattern.\n\nWorked example 3 — the document that raises more questions than it answers. A file contains a handwritten note: \"spoke w/ tenant re: son's income — n/a.\" Who spoke, when, about what income, what was concluded, on what basis? An undated, unsigned, uninformative note is worse than no note, because it evidences an inquiry the file cannot show was resolved. Teach the four-element file note: DATE, WHO, WHAT WAS DETERMINED, WHAT DOCUMENT SUPPORTS IT — with initials.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: electronic files. Many properties have moved to electronic tenant files, which is fine and often better. The requirements do not change: the record must be complete, retrievable, and reliable, with the same retention. What DOES change is the failure mode. Electronic files fail through inconsistent naming, documents scanned into the wrong household, missing signature pages when a two-sided form was scanned one-sided, and access controls that let anyone edit anything. Audit an electronic system by pulling five files and checking for exactly those four failures.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: what if the file is right but the tenant disputes it? A household insists they reported a job that the file does not reflect. There is no verification, no interim certification, no note. Even if the property's position is correct, the file cannot prove the report was never made. The control is a documented intake channel for tenant-reported changes — a dated, initialed form or log entry for every report — which protects the property and the tenant equally. Properties without one lose these disputes routinely, and reviewers treat repeated disputes as evidence of a process gap."},{"heading":"Application Package — Required Documents","body":"The application package establishes that the household was ELIGIBLE and was SELECTED PROPERLY. Reviewers examine admissions carefully because an improper admission is not curable by later paperwork — the household is in the unit.\n\nWhat belongs in the admission section:\n\n• The completed, signed and DATED application, with the date AND time of receipt recorded consistently with the waiting list.\n• Form HUD-92006, Supplement to Application for Federally Assisted Housing — the optional contact form on which an applicant may designate a person or organization to be contacted in specified circumstances. It must be OFFERED to every applicant; the applicant may decline, and the declination is documented.\n• Race and ethnicity data collection (form HUD-27061-H), offered to the applicant; if the applicant declines to furnish it, the property records the visual observation as instructed and documents that the applicant declined.\n• Verification of ELIGIBILITY: income and assets verified per the verification hierarchy; family composition; program-specific eligibility such as age or disability for 202/811 or elderly-designated units; student eligibility where applicable.\n• CITIZENSHIP / ELIGIBLE IMMIGRATION STATUS declarations for every household member, with the supporting evidence or the election not to contend eligible status, which triggers proration rules.\n• SOCIAL SECURITY NUMBER documentation for household members as required, or documentation of an applicable exemption.\n• SCREENING documentation: what was checked, against what criteria in the Tenant Selection Plan, and the results — credit, rental history, criminal history where the TSP provides for it, and the EIV EXISTING TENANT SEARCH run before move-in.\n• The HUD-9887 and HUD-9887-A consent forms, signed and dated.\n• Documentation of the offer sequence — that the household was selected from the waiting list in order, subject to any approved preferences and unit-size matching.\n• If the applicant was rejected: the rejection letter with its required content, and the record of any response or meeting.\n\nWorked example 1 — the admission that cannot be defended. A household is admitted from a waiting list with 40 names. The file contains no evidence of position, no record of the applicants skipped or the reason (unit size mismatch, declined offer, unreachable), and the list itself has no time stamps. The income eligibility is fine. The ADMISSION is indefensible: the property cannot show the selection was nondiscriminatory. Findings in this area are serious because they reach fair housing, not just paperwork. Control: at every offer, print or export the list as it stood, and record for each applicant passed over the specific documented reason.\n\nWorked example 2 — the HUD-92006 that was never offered. A file has no 92006 and no declination. The manager says \"we tell everyone about it.\" The requirement is to offer the form and to document the outcome, and the outcome is one of exactly two documented states: a completed form with the designated contact, or a signed declination. There is no third state called \"we mentioned it.\" This is a small item that appears in a very large share of files at properties that have never been coached on it.\n\nWorked example 3 — the Existing Tenant Search, and the case that shows why it matters. The property admits a household on June 1 without running the search. In September, HUD data shows the household received assistance at another property in June and July. Now: a double subsidy to unwind, a repayment, a tenant relationship damaged, and a finding for the missing pre-admission search. Contrast the same fact pattern where the search WAS run: the property sees the other assistance at application, works with the household and the other property to establish a clean move-out date, and the problem never exists. The search takes two minutes and is a pre-move-in gate.\n\nWorked example 4 — the mixed-status boundary case. A household of four: two members are U.S. citizens, one is a lawful permanent resident with documentation, and one elects not to contend eligible immigration status. The household is not ineligible — it is a MIXED-STATUS household and assistance is PRORATED (the mechanics are in Chapter 13). What the admission file must contain is the declaration for every member, the evidence for those contending status, the signed election for the member who does not contend, and the proration calculation. The most common error is not the math; it is a missing declaration for one household member, which leaves the whole determination unsupported.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: screening information that is stale or wrong. A credit report obtained at application shows a judgment that the applicant says was satisfied and removed. The TSP permits rejection for unsatisfied judgments above a threshold. The correct process: give the applicant the required notice of the information relied upon and the opportunity to dispute its accuracy, obtain the updated information, and DOCUMENT the resolution. Rejecting on stale information the applicant was never given a chance to correct is both a TSP violation and a fair housing exposure.\n\nWorked example 6 — the file that proves consistency. Reviewers test screening by pulling several rejections and several admissions and comparing them against the TSP. If two applicants had similar records and one was admitted and one rejected, the file must show what distinguished them. Keep a screening decision summary in each admission and each rejection file: criterion applied, information relied upon, decision, decision-maker, date. That one page converts an unprovable practice into a demonstrable one."},{"heading":"Move-In Documentation and the Initial Lease Package","body":"Move-in is the densest documentation event in the tenancy. Everything executed here governs the relationship for years, and errors here replicate through every subsequent certification.\n\nThe move-in package:\n\n• The HUD MODEL LEASE for the program, in its current form, signed and dated by all adult household members and by the owner/agent, with the correct term and correct rent figures.\n• The initial HUD-50059, signed by the head of household and all adult members as required, with the effective date matching the move-in date.\n• REQUIRED LEASE ADDENDA: the VAWA lease addendum (form HUD-91067), signed by all adult household members; pet rules where the property is subject to pet-ownership requirements; house rules acknowledgment; and any program-specific addendum.\n• LEAD-BASED PAINT disclosure and the EPA pamphlet for pre-1978 construction, with the required signatures.\n• The VAWA NOTICE OF OCCUPANCY RIGHTS (HUD-5380) and the CERTIFICATION FORM (HUD-5382), provided at admission — one of the three required distribution points — with documentation of delivery.\n• The MOVE-IN INSPECTION, signed by the tenant and the property, documenting unit condition. This is the document that decides security deposit disputes two years later.\n• SECURITY DEPOSIT receipt and the account documentation.\n• UTILITY ALLOWANCE documentation and, where applicable, evidence of how utility reimbursement will be paid.\n• Notice of the tenant's obligation to report changes, and of the recertification process, with the initial recertification notice.\n• Keys and access documentation, and emergency contact information.\n\nWorked example 1 — the signature that was never obtained. A household of two adults moves in; only the head of household signs the lease and the 50059. The co-head's signature line is blank. Consequences: the lease may be unenforceable against the unsigned adult; the certification lacks a required signature; and if the household later disputes the income used, the unsigned member never attested to it. Control: a move-in checklist that lists every signature required, by document, with a second-person verification before the file is closed.\n\nWorked example 2 — the effective date mismatch that cascades. The lease term begins June 15; the 50059 shows an effective date of June 1; TRACS is transmitted effective June 1. Now the property billed assistance for June 1-14 on a unit that was not occupied under the lease. Beyond the overbilling, the household's recertification anniversary is now set to June 1 rather than June 15, which propagates for the life of the tenancy. Fix the anniversary at move-in and verify it at the first annual — misaligned anniversaries are one of the most common quiet errors in the industry.\n\nWorked example 3 — the addendum that is missing years later. A property adopted the VAWA lease addendum for new move-ins in 2018 but never executed it for households already in place. In 2026, half the files lack it. The correct remediation: execute the addendum for existing households now, documented with the actual current date, and disclose. Do not backdate. Note also the related but distinct obligation — the HUD-5380 notice and HUD-5382 form at the three trigger points — which is a separate requirement from the lease addendum and is missed independently.\n\nWorked example 4 — the move-in inspection that decides a dispute. A tenant vacates and is charged $840 for damage to flooring. The move-in inspection is unsigned by the tenant and marked only \"good.\" The property will very likely lose the dispute, and if this is a pattern the reviewer will treat it as a lease enforcement and tenant-rights issue rather than a maintenance one. The control: a room-by-room inspection form with condition detail, tenant signature, and dated photographs retained with the file.\n\nWorked example 5 — the boundary case: a household member who moves in later. A household of three is admitted; a fourth member joins in month five with the owner's approval. What must happen: household composition change processed per the property's policy and 4350.3, an interim certification if the change affects income or the certification, verification of the new member's income and eligibility including citizenship status and SSN documentation, the new adult added to the lease, the VAWA addendum executed by the new adult, and the unit size reassessed against occupancy standards. Properties routinely do the interim and forget the lease and addendum.\n\nWorked example 6 — the ambiguous case: what if the tenant refuses to sign something required? A tenant declines to sign the house rules acknowledgment. The rules may still be enforceable if properly adopted and distributed, and the correct response is to document the refusal — date, who witnessed it, and what was provided — rather than to leave a blank. The same applies to the HUD-92006 declination and to any acknowledgment. Documented refusal is a complete record; a blank line is not."},{"heading":"Ongoing File Maintenance During Active Tenancy","body":"Between move-in and move-out, the file has to keep telling a continuous story. The failures here are quieter than admission failures but they accumulate, and they are what a sampled file most often reveals.\n\nWhat has to be added and maintained, continuously:\n\n• Each ANNUAL certification with its verifications, EIV income report, discrepancy resolution documentation, signed 9887/9887-A, and the signed 50059.\n• Each INTERIM certification (form HUD-50059-A where applicable) with the reported change, verification, computation, and tenant notice.\n• The record of the RECERTIFICATION NOTICES sent — the initial notice and the reminder notices at the required intervals — with evidence of delivery.\n• RENT CHANGE NOTICES with proper advance timing and evidence of delivery.\n• Tenant-reported changes as they are received, dated and initialed, whether or not they resulted in a certification.\n• Lease renewals or continuations, and any lease modifications.\n• REASONABLE ACCOMMODATION requests and responses (with medical information handled confidentially — see Chapter 16).\n• Notices of lease violation, cure, and any legal action, with evidence of delivery.\n• Work orders and inspection records relating to the unit where they bear on lease obligations.\n• Live-in aide documentation, where applicable, kept current.\n• Unit transfer documentation.\n\nWorked example 1 — the interim that should have happened and the file that proves it did not. In March a tenant tells the manager her hours increased. The manager notes it on a sticky pad, gets busy, and nothing else happens. In November, EIV shows the increased wages. Now: the interim is late by eight months, the household has been underpaying rent, HAP has been overpaid, a repayment agreement is needed, and the property's own note establishes it knew in March. Two lessons. First, the intake channel must be a dated log, not a sticky note, and every entry must be dispositioned. Second, and counterintuitively, the property's honest March note makes the finding WORSE — which is exactly why the control has to be disposition, not just capture.\n\nWorked example 2 — the notices that were sent and cannot be proven. A property mails recertification reminder notices on schedule but keeps no copies and no mailing record. Six households recertify late. The property's defense — that notice was given and the tenants delayed — is true and unprovable. Control: retain a copy of each notice in the file, with the date and method of delivery recorded; for hand delivery, a signed acknowledgment or a witnessed delivery log. This single practice converts an owner-side finding into a documented tenant-side delay in a meaningful share of cases.\n\nWorked example 3 — the accommodation buried in the wrong place. A tenant requests a transfer to a first-floor unit for a mobility-related reason. The request, the verification of need, the interactive discussion, and the decision are in the manager's email and nowhere else. The property granted the accommodation promptly and correctly, and it will still be cited, because the reviewer asks for the accommodation log and the file and finds nothing. Note the confidentiality point: disability-related documentation should be maintained so that access is limited to those with a need to know, which for many properties means a separate confidential section — while the FACT of the request, the date and the decision belong in the log.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: how long do superseded documents stay? Prior certifications and their verifications remain in the file for the duration of the tenancy — a reviewer routinely traces two or three years back to test consistency. What should NOT remain is ambiguity: superseded documents are marked as superseded with a date and are filed in the historical section, not left in the current section where they compete with the operative document.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: documents obtained for one purpose that reveal another. During an annual recertification the property obtains a bank statement showing a deposit consistent with an unreported settlement. Does the property have to act on information it did not seek? Yes — the certification must reflect the household's actual income and assets as verified, and a verifier who sees inconsistent information must resolve it. The file must show the inquiry and its resolution. Ignoring visible contradictory information in a document the property itself obtained is the least defensible position available.\n\nWorked example 6 — the maintenance habit that prevents most of this. At the close of every certification, a second person runs a five-item check: (1) all required signatures present and dated; (2) verification supports every income and asset figure; (3) EIV report present and any discrepancy resolved in writing; (4) 50059 effective date correct and transmitted; (5) superseded documents marked and filed. Five minutes per certification, performed by someone other than the preparer. This is the highest-yield control in tenant file management, and it is the control most often missing in properties that score Satisfactory instead of Above Average."},{"heading":"Move-Out Documentation and File Retention","body":"Move-out files are the least audited and among the most sampled. The property has no ongoing relationship to surface errors, staff attention moves to the next tenancy, and the documentation obligations do not end when the keys come back.\n\nWhat the move-out file must contain:\n\n• The NOTICE — the tenant's notice to vacate, or the owner's notice of termination with its required content and grounds, and evidence of delivery.\n• Where termination was owner-initiated: the documentation of grounds, any required opportunity to cure, and any legal proceedings.\n• The MOVE-OUT INSPECTION, ideally with the tenant present or with documented opportunity to attend, compared against the move-in inspection, with photographs.\n• The SECURITY DEPOSIT accounting: itemized statement of any deductions provided within the deadline required by state law and the lease, with the refund or the balance billed.\n• The FINAL CERTIFICATION / TERMINATION transmitted in TRACS with the correct effective date.\n• Any special claim documentation where the program permits vacancy, unpaid rent, or damage claims — supported by the required evidence.\n• Forwarding address, final ledger, and any repayment agreement balance and its disposition.\n\nRETENTION. Tenant files are retained for the TERM OF TENANCY PLUS THREE YEARS, and the EIV documents that live in the tenant file follow the same clock — Income Discrepancy and No Income reports are retained in the tenant file for the term of tenancy plus three years, while master-file EIV reports are retained three years. Application and rejection records are retained as required, and state law may impose longer periods for some records. Where litigation is pending or reasonably anticipated, retention obligations extend regardless of the schedule.\n\nWorked example 1 — the termination that was never transmitted. A tenant vacates March 18; the file is boxed; the TRACS termination is entered June 1. Assistance was billed for late March, April and May — an overpayment to repay, plus a finding for untimely processing, plus a rent roll that has been wrong for three months. Control: the move-out checklist's LAST item is the TRACS termination, verified by a second person, and the monthly reconciliation catches any unit assisted in TRACS but vacant on the rent roll.\n\nWorked example 2 — the deposit accounting that ran past the deadline. State law requires an itemized statement within 30 days; the property sends it on day 52 with $610 in deductions. Two exposures: under many state statutes, missing the deadline forfeits some or all deduction rights and can trigger penalties; and the MOR reviewer treats it as a lease compliance and tenant rights issue. Consultants must know the applicable state deadline for each client's jurisdiction — this is one of the few areas where the governing rule is not federal.\n\nWorked example 3 — the abandonment case. A tenant stops paying, the unit appears vacant, and nobody can reach them. The property changes the locks and disposes of the belongings. Depending on the jurisdiction, abandonment has a defined legal process, and skipping it creates liability far beyond a MOR finding. The compliance answer is procedural: follow the state's abandonment process, document each step and each attempt at contact, and process the termination in TRACS on the correct date established by that process — not on the date somebody decided the unit looked empty.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: death of the sole household member. The head of household, living alone, dies. Assistance terminates in accordance with program rules and the lease, and the estate handles personal property under state law. What the file needs: documentation of the date of death, the notice and communications with the estate or next of kin, the final accounting, and the correctly dated termination in TRACS. Note also the EIV Deceased Tenants report, which exists precisely because assistance sometimes continues after death — a property that learns of a death from that report rather than from its own process should treat it as a control failure.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: the household that \"might come back.\" A tenant enters a rehabilitation facility for what is expected to be 60 days. Is this a move-out, a temporary absence, or something else? The property's written policy on extended absence governs, applied consistently, with the facts documented — including the expected duration, communications with the household, and the determination made and when. The unacceptable answer is to leave assistance running for nine months with nothing in the file. Reviewers do not necessarily second-guess a reasonable determination; they always cite an absent one.\n\nWorked example 6 — the retention failure that surfaces years later. A property purges files three years after move-out — but the household's tenancy ended with a repayment agreement that is still being collected, and a fair housing complaint filed 18 months after move-out is pending. Both circumstances extend the obligation. Control: a retention schedule with a HOLD flag, applied when litigation, a complaint, an open repayment balance, or an audit is pending, reviewed before any destruction. And when files ARE destroyed, destroy them securely and document what was destroyed and when — records containing tenant income and identity data cannot simply be put in a dumpster."}]},{"id":"m92","unit":"u9","number":2,"title":"Annual and Interim Certifications — Timing, Process, and Compliance","subtitle":"","sections":[{"heading":"Annual Recertification — Timing and Scheduling","body":"Every assisted household must be recertified at least annually, effective on the ANNIVERSARY of the household's move-in date (or of the established anniversary where a prior transfer or correction set it). The rule sounds simple; the failures are all about the calendar behind it.\n\nThe notice sequence in Handbook 4350.3 REV-1 Chapter 7. Owners must give the household:\n\n• An INITIAL NOTICE at move-in and annually thereafter, explaining the recertification requirement, the tenant's obligation to report, and the consequences of failing to respond.\n• A FIRST REMINDER NOTICE approximately 120 DAYS before the recertification anniversary date.\n• A SECOND REMINDER NOTICE approximately 90 DAYS before.\n• A THIRD REMINDER NOTICE approximately 60 DAYS before, which also functions as the notice of the consequences of non-response.\nThe handbook supplies model notices at Exhibits 7-1 through 7-4. Use them or a version that contains the same required content, and retain copies with proof of delivery.\n\nWhy the 120-day start matters arithmetically: verification takes time. Third-party verification requests can run two to six weeks; EIV data must be pulled and discrepancies resolved; the tenant may need to obtain documents. A property that begins at 60 days is working with no margin, and the certification that misses its effective date is the predictable result.\n\nTHE 15-MONTH CLIFF. If a new recertification is not submitted within 15 months of the previous year's anniversary, HUD terminates the certification, and the owner must repay assistance collected for the three-month period from the date the annual recertification should have been effective through the end of the fifteenth month. This is the hard backstop behind \"late recertifications\" and it converts a paperwork problem into a cash problem.\n\nWorked example 1 — the clean timeline. Move-in September 15, 2024. Anniversary September 1 or September 15 depending on how the anniversary was established at move-in — verify which, because properties get this wrong and then repeat it annually. Assume a September 1 anniversary.\n  First reminder: approximately May 4 (120 days before September 1).\n  Second reminder: approximately June 3 (90 days).\n  Third reminder: approximately July 3 (60 days).\n  Interview and verification requests: May through June.\n  Verifications returned and computation complete: by late July.\n  Tenant signs the 50059: August.\n  Effective date: September 1. Transmitted to TRACS promptly.\n  Rent change notice to the tenant with the required advance notice before September 1.\n\nWorked example 2 — the tenant who does not respond. The property sends all three notices, documented. The tenant does not respond by the anniversary. The property may not simply keep paying assistance indefinitely, and it may not unilaterally invent an income figure. It follows its written policy and the handbook's process for non-response, which culminates in the loss of assistance and potential termination of tenancy, with the required notices. The documentation that decides this case is the three retained notices with delivery evidence. Without them, the property is the party that failed.\n\nWorked example 3 — the certification completed late, and the money. An annual due July 1 is completed and transmitted September 20. The verification shows income rose; the household's TTP should have increased $110 on July 1.\n  July and August were billed at the old figures: HAP overpaid by $110 × 2 = $220.\n  The tenant underpaid by $220 over the same months.\n  Retroactive adjustment reduces a future voucher by $220; the tenant owes $220, collected under an affordable repayment arrangement.\n  Plus a timeliness finding. One late certification, four consequences. Multiply by nine late certifications and the client understands the stakes.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: the tenant responds on time and the employer does not. All notices went out on schedule; the tenant returned everything in June; the third-party employer verification was requested June 5 and never came back. The property follows the verification hierarchy (Section 3) and moves to the next acceptable level of verification, documenting the attempts — the request date, follow-up dates, and the reason for moving down the hierarchy. The certification is completed on time using acceptable lower-tier verification with the attempts documented. What is NOT acceptable is missing the effective date while waiting.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: which anniversary governs after an interim? A household has a September 1 annual anniversary and processes an interim effective March 1. The ANNUAL anniversary does not move — the next annual is still September 1. Properties frequently and incorrectly reset the annual clock to the interim date, which produces certifications that drift and, eventually, a household that goes more than twelve months without an annual. Verify the anniversary against the ORIGINAL move-in (or established) date, not against the most recent certification of any type.\n\nWorked example 6 — the scheduling control that prevents almost all of this. Generate a rolling 150-day certification queue report monthly, showing each household's anniversary, the notices due and sent, verification status, and completion status. Review it with two people. Every late certification in the industry is visible on that report 120 days before it happens."},{"heading":"Interim Certifications — When They Are Required","body":"An interim recertification adjusts the household's rent and assistance between annuals when circumstances change. The rules divide into what the TENANT must report, what the OWNER must process, and what the owner's WRITTEN POLICY controls — and clients confuse the three constantly.\n\nUnder 4350.3 as written, the framework is:\n\n• The tenant must report changes in household composition, and must report income and other changes as required by the lease and by the owner's written policy.\n• The owner MUST process an interim when required by the handbook and the owner's policy — including changes in family composition, and decreases in income that the family reports and that affect the rent.\n• The owner's WRITTEN POLICY must state which changes must be reported, within what time frame, and how the owner will act on them. That policy must be applied UNIFORMLY. Selective processing — running interims that raise rent and skipping those that lower it, or vice versa — is a fair housing and program compliance problem, not merely an inconsistency.\n• Rent DECREASES generally take effect on the first of the month after the change is reported and verified; rent INCREASES require the advance notice specified by the lease and the handbook, which is why a late-processed increase cannot simply be imposed on the next first of the month.\n\nWhere HOTMA changes this: HOTMA establishes standardized interim triggers — an interim is REQUIRED when income DECREASES by 10 percent or more, and when income INCREASES by $2,400 or more annually (adjusted), with owner discretion below those thresholds under a written policy. HUD's multifamily compliance date for the HOTMA income and asset provisions is JANUARY 1, 2027, per Notice H 2025-07 (which superseded the January 1, 2026 date in Notice H 2025-03). Until a property implements, the 4350.3 framework above governs; owners that adopt early must follow the HOTMA rules consistently and process the certifications accordingly. Whichever regime applies, the written policy must MATCH it — a property operating under 4350.3 with a policy citing HOTMA thresholds is deficient, and so is the reverse.\n\nWorked example 1 — the income decrease, timed correctly. A household reports on March 12 that the head lost her job effective March 5. Verification is obtained March 20.\n  The decrease is verified; the interim is processed effective APRIL 1 — the first of the month following the reported and verified change.\n  Annual income drops from $31,200 to $9,600 (unemployment). Adjusted income drops accordingly, and the TTP falls from roughly $720 to roughly $205.\n  The household's rent drops; HAP increases; the 50059-A is transmitted.\n  The file documents the report date, the verification, the computation, the effective date, and the notice to the tenant.\n  Common error: waiting until the annual. A household in this situation cannot pay the old rent, falls into arrears, and the property has created a collection problem out of its own delay.\n\nWorked example 2 — the income increase, and why the effective date is later. The same household's head starts a new job June 1 at $2,600 per month and reports it June 3. Verification completes June 25. The TTP will rise by roughly $500.\n  A rent INCREASE requires the advance notice the lease and handbook require. If the property's notice requirement is 30 days, a notice issued June 27 produces an effective date of August 1, not July 1.\n  Properties that impose the increase on July 1 without proper notice create a lease violation and a tenant dispute — and a reviewer who finds a rent increase implemented without documented notice writes a tenant-rights finding, which is weighted more heavily than a timing error.\n\nWorked example 3 — the composition change with no income effect. A household's adult son moves out. Income does not change materially because he had none. Is an interim required? Household composition changes must be processed — the household's makeup drives dependent deductions, unit size appropriateness, and the certification's accuracy even where the dollar figures do not move much. Process it, document it, and check the unit size against occupancy standards. Properties that skip \"no dollar impact\" composition changes end up with 50059s that do not match the household in the unit, which is exactly what a reviewer compares.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case that creates real hardship. A household reports a $180 monthly increase in October; the annual is in December. Under a policy with a threshold, the increase may not require an interim. Processing one anyway — because \"more compliance is better\" — imposes a rent increase in November that the annual would have captured in December anyway, and does so inconsistently with the written policy. The rule: FOLLOW THE POLICY. Uniform application is the compliance obligation; enthusiasm is not.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: seasonal and irregular income. A household member works seasonally, earning nothing November through March and $3,200 per month April through October. Reporting the \"increase\" each April and the \"decrease\" each November would produce four certifications a year. The correct treatment is at the annual: ANNUALIZE the seasonal pattern using the documented history, so the certification reflects the annual figure rather than the current month. Interims then apply only to changes in the PATTERN — the job ends, the hours change permanently — not to the predictable seasonal swing already built into the annual calculation.\n\nWorked example 6 — the naive reading that produces a systemic finding. \"We process interims when tenants ask us to.\" That is not a policy; it is a practice that guarantees the interims processed will skew toward decreases (which tenants request) and away from increases (which they do not). A reviewer who sees twenty interims, all decreases, at a property with normal wage growth will ask why. The answer must be a written policy applied uniformly, with an EIV-driven check that catches increases the tenant did not report."},{"heading":"Income Verification Hierarchy — The Four Methods","body":"HUD requires verification at the HIGHEST LEVEL AVAILABLE, and requires documentation of why a lower level was used when it was. Handbook 4350.3 REV-1 Chapter 5 establishes the hierarchy; Chapter 9 and 24 CFR 5.233 make EIV use mandatory as part of it.\n\nThe hierarchy, highest to lowest:\n\n1. UP-FRONT INCOME VERIFICATION (UIV) — principally EIV. Mandatory for annual recertifications for existing tenants, used to verify employment and income and to identify discrepancies. EIV is not a substitute for the rest of the process; it is the starting point and the cross-check.\n2. THIRD-PARTY WRITTEN VERIFICATION — written verification direct from the source (employer, benefit agency, bank), including documents the tenant provides that originate from a third party (pay stubs, benefit award letters, bank statements) when direct verification is not obtained. HUD distinguishes between verification received directly from the source and third-party documents provided by the tenant; both are third-party in nature, and the file should show which was obtained and why.\n3. THIRD-PARTY ORAL VERIFICATION — documented in a file note with the date, the person contacted, their title and contact information, what was verified, and who made the call.\n4. TENANT SELF-CERTIFICATION / SELF-DECLARATION — acceptable only when higher levels are unavailable, and only with documentation of the attempts made.\n\nThe rule that produces the most findings: THE FILE MUST SHOW THE ATTEMPT. A self-certification with no record of an attempted third-party verification is a finding even if the self-certified figure is correct.\n\nWorked example 1 — the clean sequence, documented. Certification effective October 1 for a household with one wage earner.\n  July 8: EIV report pulled; shows the employer and quarterly wages consistent with the tenant's report.\n  July 9: third-party written verification of employment mailed to the employer.\n  July 24: no response; second request sent, documented.\n  August 6: still no response; the property obtains four consecutive current pay stubs from the tenant and computes annual income from them, documenting the two failed direct requests.\n  August 8: EIV wage data compared to the pay stub computation; the variance is 3 percent and explained by an overtime pattern; a file note records the comparison and conclusion.\n  The file now shows the hierarchy applied and the reason for each step. This file passes.\n\nWorked example 2 — the file that fails despite a correct number. Same household, and the specialist simply takes four pay stubs at the interview and computes correctly. No EIV comparison in the file, no record of any attempt at direct verification. The income figure is right. The finding is written for failure to document verification at the required level. Clients find this maddening and it is worth being blunt: HUD is verifying the PROCESS, because the process is the only assurance that the numbers are right in the files nobody sampled.\n\nWorked example 3 — the discrepancy that must be resolved, not explained away. EIV shows $34,000 in annual wages; the pay stubs support $28,600. The specialist assumes EIV is stale and uses the pay stubs. Two problems: the assumption is undocumented, and it may be wrong — the difference could be a second job, a bonus, or overtime that will recur. The correct process: identify the source of the difference (contact the employer, review the stubs for the period covered, ask the tenant), determine the correct anticipated annual income, and DOCUMENT the analysis and conclusion. The written resolution memo is the deliverable, and its absence is a finding regardless of which number was used.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: income with no third-party source. A household member does informal childcare for neighbors, paid in cash, roughly $200 per month. There is no employer to verify and no tax return. The hierarchy has an answer: document the attempts, obtain what documentation exists (a log the tenant maintains, statements from the payers where available), and use a self-certification with the supporting detail. What must be in the file: the self-certification, the basis for the amount, the attempts made at higher-level verification, and the specialist's analysis. Reviewers accept documented reasoning about hard cases; they cite blank spaces.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: verification that expires. Verifications are generally usable for a limited period — the common convention is that a verification is current if obtained within 120 days of the certification's effective date, and the HUD-9887/9887-A consent forms are valid for 15 months. A property that verifies in March for a September 1 effective date has verifications that are stale on their face. Sequence the work so verification happens inside the window, and re-verify anything that ages out rather than submitting an old document.\n\nWorked example 6 — the naive reading that costs a rating. \"We use EIV, so our verification is covered.\" EIV is mandatory and it is the first step, not the whole. It does not cover income types outside its data (self-employment, most asset income, child support paid directly, informal income), it can lag, and it does not verify household composition, deductions, or expenses. Files that consist of an EIV printout and a signed 50059 fail. A useful diagnostic at intake: pull three files and ask, for each income and deduction line on the 50059, \"what document in this file supports this number?\" If any line has no answer, the property has a systemic verification problem."},{"heading":"The Tenant Income Certification Form (TIC / HUD 50059)","body":"Form HUD-50059 is the certification document for HUD multifamily assisted housing — the form that carries household composition, income, assets, deductions, TTP, tenant rent, utility allowance or reimbursement, and assistance payment. Form HUD-50059-A is used for interim and certain other transactions such as terminations and unit transfers, depending on the transaction type. The data is transmitted to HUD through TRACS.\n\n(Practitioners who also work in Low-Income Housing Tax Credit properties use \"TIC\" for the tax credit Tenant Income Certification, which is a different document under a different program. On a HUD MOR, the operative document is the 50059. Where a property has both HUD assistance and tax credits, both exist and must be consistent — inconsistency between them is a finding that reviewers on either side can spot.)\n\nWhat must be right on the 50059:\n\n• Household composition — every member, relationship, date of birth, and the citizenship/eligible immigration status coding.\n• Every income source, correctly classified and correctly annualized.\n• Assets — cash value and income, with imputation applied when the threshold is exceeded.\n• Deductions — dependents, elderly/disabled family, medical and disability assistance expenses above the applicable threshold, and child care.\n• The TTP computation and the resulting tenant rent, utility allowance and any utility reimbursement, and the assistance payment.\n• The effective date and transaction type.\n• SIGNATURES — head of household and all adult members as required, and the owner/agent representative, all dated.\n\nWorked example 1 — the four-element TTP check on a completed 50059. A reviewer testing a 50059 recomputes TTP as the GREATEST of: 30 percent of monthly adjusted income; 10 percent of monthly gross income; the welfare rent in as-paid localities; and the minimum rent, which is $25 for most Section 8 project-based programs. If the form shows a TTP that is not the greatest of those, either an input is wrong or the computation is. When auditing files, recompute all four elements rather than checking the one the software used — software configured with a wrong minimum rent or a missing welfare rent element produces consistently wrong TTPs that look internally consistent.\n\nWorked example 2 — the transmission that did not match the file. The file's 50059 shows adjusted income $18,240 and TTP $456. TRACS shows adjusted income $18,240 and TTP $456. The rent roll shows tenant rent $391. Is something wrong? Not necessarily — tenant rent is TTP minus the utility allowance, so a $65 utility allowance reconciles it exactly. This is the reconciliation to teach staff: TTP and TENANT RENT are different numbers, and confusing them is the single most common misreading of a 50059 by people who are otherwise competent.\n\nWorked example 3 — the signature problem that voids the certification's evidentiary value. A 50059 is signed by the head of household but not by the adult co-head, and the owner representative's signature is undated. The computation is correct. The finding is written because the certification is not properly executed, and if the household later disputes the income used, the property cannot show the co-head attested to it. Build the signature check into the second-person review.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: corrections. An error is found in a transmitted 50059. The correction is processed as a corrected certification with the correct effective date, transmitted, and the superseded document is marked as superseded and retained. Two errors properties make: replacing the original in the file (destroying the audit trail) or leaving both unmarked (creating ambiguity). The file must show what was originally certified, what was corrected, when, and why — a one-line correction note with initials does the job.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: the household that disputes a figure at signing. A tenant refuses to sign the 50059 because she disagrees with the treatment of her son's income. Do not \"fix\" the figure to obtain the signature, and do not process the certification as if it were signed. Document the dispute, explain the basis for the treatment with the handbook citation, offer the property's grievance or dispute process, and — if the property's determination is correct — proceed under its written policy for a household that refuses to sign, which typically leads to the loss-of-assistance process. Then verify the determination is actually right, because a tenant's objection is sometimes the first sign of a real error.\n\nWorked example 6 — the audit routine that catches most 50059 errors in five minutes. For any file: (1) recompute all four TTP elements; (2) trace every income line to a verification document; (3) confirm every deduction is supported and correctly computed (including that a percentage threshold was applied once, not per expense); (4) confirm the effective date matches the transaction and the anniversary; (5) confirm signatures and dates; (6) confirm the transmitted figures match the file. Six checks. Run them on five files at any property and you will know within half an hour whether the property has a systemic certification problem."},{"heading":"Common Certification Errors and How to Prevent Them","body":"These are the errors that recur across properties, with the control for each. Present them to clients as a diagnostic list, not a lecture — most managers recognize their own property in three or four of them immediately.\n\n1. LATE PROCESSING. Control: the rolling 150-day queue report, reviewed monthly by two people.\n2. MISSING OR STALE VERIFICATION. Control: the certification checklist blocks completion until every income and deduction line traces to a document dated inside the window.\n3. EIV NOT USED, OR USED WITHOUT DOCUMENTED RESOLUTION. Control: the EIV income report and any discrepancy resolution memo are required checklist items.\n4. INCOME ANNUALIZED INCORRECTLY. The classic errors: using 52 weeks × 40 hours instead of the standard 2,080-hour convention consistently; multiplying a biweekly check by 24 instead of 26; treating a bonus as one-time when it recurs annually; ignoring documented overtime patterns. Control: an annualization worksheet retained in the file showing the method used.\n5. DEDUCTIONS TAKEN WITHOUT SUPPORT, OR THRESHOLDS APPLIED WRONG. Under 4350.3 as written: $480 per dependent; $400 per elderly or disabled FAMILY (not per person); medical and disability assistance expenses deductible only to the extent they exceed 3 percent of ANNUAL income, with the threshold applied ONCE across both categories combined, not to each. Control: a deduction worksheet with the threshold arithmetic shown.\n6. ASSETS MISHANDLED. Under 4350.3 as written, when total cash value exceeds $5,000, count the GREATER of actual income or imputed income at HUD's passbook savings rate. Control: an asset worksheet listing each asset, its cash value, its actual income, and the imputation comparison.\n7. SIGNATURES MISSING OR UNDATED. Control: the second-person signature check.\n8. EFFECTIVE DATES WRONG. Control: verify the anniversary against the original move-in date at every annual, not against the last certification.\n9. TRANSMISSION NOT MATCHING THE FILE. Control: the monthly three-way reconciliation.\n10. NOTICES NOT RETAINED. Control: copies with delivery evidence, in the file.\n\nWorked example 1 — the annualization error, worked all the way to the rent. A tenant is paid biweekly, $1,180 gross per check. The specialist multiplies by 24 (treating biweekly as semi-monthly): $28,320. The correct figure is 26 pay periods: $30,680. The understatement is $2,360.\n  Monthly adjusted income understated by roughly $197.\n  TTP understated by roughly $59 per month.\n  Over a 12-month certification: $708 in tenant underpayment and $708 in HAP overpayment.\n  Across four files with the same error: $5,664 total, plus a systemic finding.\n  The control is not \"be careful.\" It is a worksheet that requires the pay frequency to be stated and the multiplier to match it.\n\nWorked example 2 — the deduction threshold applied twice. An elderly household has $2,100 in unreimbursed medical expenses and $1,400 in disability assistance expenses, with annual income of $18,000. The 3 percent threshold is $540.\n  Wrong: ($2,100 − $540) + ($1,400 − $540) = $1,560 + $860 = $2,420.\n  Right: the threshold applies ONCE across the combined expenses. ($2,100 + $1,400) − $540 = $2,960.\n  The error understates the deduction by $540, overstating adjusted income and overcharging the tenant by roughly $13.50 per month. Note the direction: this error hurts the TENANT, and reviewers cite it just as readily as errors that hurt HUD.\n\nWorked example 3 — the elderly deduction taken per person. A household has an elderly head and an elderly spouse. Under 4350.3 as written the deduction is $400 per FAMILY, not per person. A property taking $800 overstates the deduction, understates adjusted income, and overpays HAP. This is one of the most common single errors in the industry and it is trivially preventable with a worksheet that states \"per family.\"\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: the asset threshold cliff. A household holds $4,980 in assets earning $12 in actual interest. No imputation — count $12. The same household deposits $30 the following week and is at $5,010 at the next certification. Now imputation applies: at a 2 percent passbook rate the imputed figure is $100.20, and because imputed exceeds actual, $100.20 is counted. A $30 deposit changed the counted asset income from $12 to $100.20. Reviewers test this boundary because it is easy to state and easy to miss. (Note: the imputation rate is HUD's published passbook savings rate, which HUD sets annually — for CY2026 it is 0.40 percent. Worked examples in this track use the 2 percent convention for arithmetic consistency with the Occupancy track; in live work, use the published rate for the certification year, and confirm which asset regime — 4350.3 or HOTMA — governs the effective date.)\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: a change reported between the interview and the effective date. The certification is computed in July for a September 1 effective date. In August the tenant reports that her hours are being cut effective September 15. What now? The annual certification reflects income ANTICIPATED for the next 12 months, so a known, verifiable change that will occur during the period should be reflected — either in the annual computation or through a properly processed interim once the change takes effect. What is not acceptable is completing the annual on stale information the property already knows to be wrong. Document the analysis either way.\n\nWorked example 6 — the diagnostic to run at every intake. Pull five files: two recent annuals, one recent interim, one recent move-in, one recent move-out. Run the six-check audit from Section 4 on each. Count the errors and classify them by the ten categories above. In two hours you will have a defensible statement of the property's error rate, the categories that drive it, and the two or three controls that would eliminate most of it — which is both the finding a reviewer would reach and the proposal for the engagement."}]},{"id":"m93","unit":"u9","number":3,"title":"Special Certification Situations","subtitle":"","sections":[{"heading":"Live-In Aide Requirements and Documentation","body":"A live-in aide is defined at 24 CFR 5.403 as a person who resides with one or more elderly persons, near-elderly persons, or persons with disabilities, and who: (1) is determined to be ESSENTIAL to the care and well-being of the person(s); (2) is NOT OBLIGATED for the support of the person(s); and (3) WOULD NOT BE LIVING IN THE UNIT except to provide the necessary supportive services. All three prongs must be satisfied and documented.\n\nThe consequences of live-in aide status, which is why properties get this wrong in both directions:\n\n• The aide's INCOME IS NOT COUNTED in the household's annual income. Not reduced, not partially counted — excluded entirely.\n• The aide is NOT a household member for purposes of deductions. No dependent deduction, and the aide does not make the household eligible for anything.\n• The aide has NO SUCCESSION RIGHTS. On the death or move-out of the qualifying tenant, the aide is not a remaining member of the tenant family and has no right to continue occupancy. This must be documented, and it is the reason properties should have the aide sign an acknowledgment at approval.\n• The household is generally entitled to an ADDITIONAL BEDROOM for the aide under occupancy standards — the aide is counted for unit size but not for income.\n• The aide MAY BE SCREENED. Approval of a live-in aide is not automatic; the owner may screen the aide consistent with the Tenant Selection Plan, including for drug-related and criminal activity, and may deny an aide who fails that screening — while remaining obligated to consider a different aide.\n• A live-in aide request is generally handled as a REASONABLE ACCOMMODATION, which means the interactive process, prompt response, and documentation obligations from Chapter 16 apply.\n\nWhat the file must contain: the written request; verification from a knowledgeable professional that the person needs the supportive services (verifying the NEED, not diagnosing the disability); the property's determination and approval date; the aide's acknowledgment of no succession rights; screening documentation; and the aide's identification on the 50059 in the correct status.\n\nWorked example 1 — the clean case. A 74-year-old tenant with a mobility impairment requests that her nephew move in to provide daily assistance. The nephew earns $41,000 as a warehouse supervisor.\n  Verification from the tenant's physician confirms the need for in-home supportive services.\n  The nephew is screened per the TSP; nothing disqualifying.\n  He signs the acknowledgment that he is a live-in aide with no succession rights.\n  His $41,000 IS NOT COUNTED. The household's income is unchanged.\n  The household moves from a 1-bedroom to a 2-bedroom under the occupancy standards, processed as a UNIT TRANSFER (preserving the recertification anniversary), not as a move-out and move-in.\n  The 50059 shows the aide in live-in aide status, not as a household member.\n  The single most common error in this fact pattern is counting the nephew's income, which would raise the household's TTP by hundreds of dollars per month and is flatly wrong.\n\nWorked example 2 — the prong that fails. A 68-year-old tenant requests live-in aide status for her adult daughter, who has been living in the unit for three years, is unemployed, and whose two children also live there. Analysis: the daughter does not satisfy \"would not be living in the unit except to provide supportive services\" — she was already living there. And the presence of her own children raises the question whether she is part of the household rather than an aide. Correct handling: the daughter and grandchildren are HOUSEHOLD MEMBERS whose income counts and who generate dependent deductions, not a live-in aide with an excluded income. Getting this wrong understates income substantially and produces a repayment.\n\nWorked example 3 — the aide who is obligated for support. A tenant with a disability requests live-in aide status for her spouse. A spouse is generally obligated for support and cannot be a live-in aide; the spouse is a household member whose income counts. Same analysis applies to a parent of a minor. The \"not obligated for support\" prong exists precisely to prevent the exclusion of income from someone in the household's economic unit.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: the aide who starts earning income from the tenant. A family member serves as the live-in aide and is later paid through a state Medicaid home-care program for those services. Two questions arise: whether that payment is income to the aide (whose income is excluded anyway, so it does not change the household calculation), and whether the payment received BY THE TENANT and passed through changes anything. The general treatment is that payments made to a family member for care of a household member with a disability follow specific rules on both the income and expense sides, and the interaction with the disability assistance expense deduction has to be worked carefully. This is a case to document the analysis, cite the provision relied on, and — if genuinely uncertain — put the question to the Contract Administrator in writing. What you must not do is guess silently.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: the aide who becomes the household. A tenant with an approved live-in aide dies. The aide has no succession rights and must vacate. But the aide has lived there four years, has a disability herself, and asks to stay. This is genuinely difficult and it is a place where properties create liability by improvising. The correct steps: apply the rule (no succession rights) consistently; consider any reasonable accommodation request on its own terms, which does not create a tenancy but may affect the timeline; give the notice the lease and program rules require; and — where the aide might qualify for admission on her own — inform her of the application and waiting list process. Consistency plus documentation is the protection; ad hoc mercy for one aide and strict enforcement for another is a fair housing complaint.\n\nWorked example 6 — the annual maintenance nobody does. Live-in aide status is not permanent. At each annual recertification, verify that the arrangement continues, that the qualifying tenant still needs the services, and that the aide still meets all three prongs — and document it. A file that shows an aide approved in 2019 with nothing since is a finding, and it is also how properties end up with an \"aide\" who has quietly become an unauthorized occupant with counted income."},{"heading":"Student Eligibility Rules Under Section 8","body":"The Section 8 student rule at 24 CFR 5.612 is one of the most misapplied provisions in the handbook, because people remember it as \"students can't get Section 8,\" which is not what it says.\n\nThe rule. A student enrolled at an INSTITUTION OF HIGHER EDUCATION is INELIGIBLE for Section 8 assistance if the student:\n• is separately determined not to be eligible, or\n• does not meet one of the exceptions,\nAND the student's PARENTS are not income-eligible.\n\nThe exceptions — a student is NOT subject to the parental income test if the student:\n• is at least 24 years of age; or\n• is a VETERAN; or\n• is MARRIED; or\n• has a DEPENDENT CHILD; or\n• is a person with disabilities receiving assistance as of November 30, 2005; or\n• is otherwise INDEPENDENT of their parents under the criteria in the regulation and HUD guidance — including that the student is not claimed as a dependent by their parents and demonstrates independence (which typically involves documentation such as tax returns and financial independence evidence); or\n• has no parents living, or the parents cannot be located.\n\nSo the analysis is a sequence, and the order matters:\n1. Is the person a student enrolled at an institution of higher education? If no, the rule does not apply at all.\n2. Does the student meet ANY exception? If yes, evaluate eligibility normally.\n3. If no exception applies, BOTH the student's income AND the parents' income must be within limits. If either fails, the student is ineligible.\n\nAlso note the income side: financial assistance in excess of tuition is treated as income for certain students under 24 CFR 5.609, which is a separate question from eligibility. (Where HOTMA is implemented, the treatment of student financial assistance changed — the amount counted is measured against tuition and required fees and charges, with exceptions. Under HUD Notice H 2025-07, the multifamily compliance date for the HOTMA income provisions is January 1, 2027; confirm which regime governs the effective date you are certifying.)\n\nWorked example 1 — the clean ineligibility. A 20-year-old, unmarried, no children, not a veteran, enrolled full time at a state university, applying on his own. His income is $6,400. His parents' combined income is $148,000, well above the applicable limit. He is INELIGIBLE, because no exception applies and the parents are not income-eligible. Document: enrollment verification, the exception analysis showing each exception considered and why it does not apply, and the parental income verification.\n\nWorked example 2 — the exception that flips the result. Same 20-year-old, but he has a two-year-old daughter living with him. He HAS A DEPENDENT CHILD, so the parental income test does not apply. Evaluate his eligibility on his own income like any other applicant. Properties that stop at \"student, under 24, parents wealthy\" and reject him have committed a serious error — potentially a fair housing exposure, since familial status is protected and the rejection turned on a misapplied rule.\n\nWorked example 3 — the case that trips experienced staff. A 26-year-old graduate student. Age 24 or over is an exception, so the parental income test does not apply. Evaluate normally. The mistake is treating \"graduate student\" as more restrictive than undergraduate; the rule turns on the statutory criteria, not on degree level.\n\nWorked example 4 — the existing tenant who enrolls. A 21-year-old member of an assisted household enrolls at a community college. Does the household lose assistance? The student rule applies to the STUDENT'S eligibility for assistance, and the analysis in a household context is different from a lone student applicant — it turns on the household's composition and who is receiving assistance. This is precisely the fact pattern where properties either panic and terminate (wrong, and potentially a serious tenant-rights problem) or ignore it entirely (also wrong). The correct step: perform and DOCUMENT the analysis at the next certification, obtain enrollment status and the facts bearing on each exception, and if the outcome is uncertain, put the question to the Contract Administrator in writing before acting on a household's assistance.\n\nWorked example 5 — the boundary case: \"institution of higher education.\" The rule reaches students at institutions of higher education as defined by the Higher Education Act. A person enrolled in a short-term trade certificate program, a GED program, or an adult education course is not necessarily within the definition. Do not apply the student rule reflexively to anyone taking a class. Obtain the enrollment documentation and determine whether the institution is within the definition — and document the determination.\n\nWorked example 6 — the ambiguous case: independence that is hard to prove. A 22-year-old estranged from her parents cannot obtain their income information and states they have not claimed her as a dependent for three years. The independence criteria require documentation, and the practical file will contain: her tax returns showing she was not claimed, evidence of self-support, a self-certification of estrangement, documented attempts to obtain parental information, and the property's written determination. This is a case where a careful, documented determination protects the property whichever way it goes, and an undocumented one is a finding whichever way it goes.\n\nThe consulting point: student files are sampled disproportionately because reviewers know this rule is misapplied. Any property with a college nearby should have a standing student eligibility worksheet — enrollment, age, veteran status, marital status, dependent children, independence documentation, parental income where required — completed for every student household and re-verified annually."},{"heading":"Mixed-Status Families — Prorated Assistance","body":"A MIXED-STATUS FAMILY is a household in which at least one member is eligible for assistance based on citizenship or eligible immigration status and at least one member is not. Under 24 CFR 5.520, such a family is NOT ineligible — its assistance is PRORATED.\n\nThe framework:\n\n• Every household member must have a DECLARATION of citizenship or eligible immigration status. Members contending eligible immigration status must provide the required documentation, which is verified through the required process.\n• A member may ELECT NOT TO CONTEND eligible status. That election is signed and retained. That member is not eligible, and the family becomes mixed-status rather than ineligible.\n• The assistance is PRORATED based on the ratio of eligible members to total members.\n• A household in which NO member is eligible is not entitled to assistance.\n• There are continued-assistance and deferral provisions applicable to certain households under the statute and regulations; where a household's circumstances suggest one may apply, verify the current rule rather than relying on memory.\n\nWorked example 1 — the proration arithmetic, done cleanly. A family of five: three members are eligible (two citizens and one lawful permanent resident with documentation); two members elect not to contend eligible status.\n  Eligible ratio: 3 ÷ 5 = 0.60.\n  Assume the assistance payment computed for the family as if fully eligible is $840 per month.\n  Prorated assistance: $840 × 0.60 = $504.\n  The family's rent obligation increases by the $336 difference — this is the practical consequence to explain to the household in advance, in writing, before it appears on a rent statement.\n  The file must contain: five declarations, documentation for the three contending members, the signed elections for the two who do not contend, the proration computation, and the notice to the family.\n\nWorked example 2 — the ratio that changes mid-year. The same family. One of the two non-contending members moves out.\n  New ratio: 3 ÷ 4 = 0.75.\n  Prorated assistance: $840 × 0.75 = $630 — an increase of $126 in assistance and a corresponding decrease in the family's rent.\n  This is a household composition change requiring processing, and it produces a rent DECREASE, effective per the interim rules. Properties that fail to process composition changes with \"no income effect\" miss this entirely and overcharge the family for months.\n\nWorked example 3 — the error that makes a household ineligible on paper. A family of four submits declarations for three members; the fourth (a 16-year-old) has no declaration in the file because staff assumed minors were exempt. They are not — a declaration is required for every member. The file cannot support the determination, and the reviewer cannot verify the proration. The finding is written not because the family is ineligible but because the determination is unsupported. Control: the declaration count is checked against the household member count on the 50059 at every certification.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: documentation that is pending verification. A member contends eligible immigration status and provides documentation that is submitted for verification; the verification is pending. There are specific rules governing the treatment of a household while verification is pending, including provision of assistance during the pendency in defined circumstances and the appeal rights that attach to an adverse determination. Do not improvise here: identify the applicable provision, follow it, document each step and date, and give every notice the rule requires. Errors in this area affect a household's housing and carry consequences well beyond a MOR finding.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: the family that does not want to discuss it. Staff may not demand information beyond what the regulation requires, may not apply the process selectively based on national origin, accent, or appearance, and may not condition assistance on documentation the rule does not require. The process is uniform: every applicant and every household member goes through the same declaration process, with the same forms, in the same sequence, and the file shows it. Selective application of citizenship verification is a fair housing violation in a particularly serious form — and reviewers who see declarations completed for some households and not others will look hard at which households.\n\nWorked example 6 — the consulting practice point. Mixed-status proration is arithmetic that software often handles correctly and staff often cannot explain. Test it at intake: ask the specialist to walk through how the prorated figure was derived for a specific household. If the answer is \"the system does it,\" verify the ratio by hand against the declarations in the file. A software configuration error in proration replicates across every mixed-status household at the property."},{"heading":"Minimum Rent, Hardship Exemptions, and Zero Income","body":"Three related situations that reviewers examine closely because they involve the households with the least ability to absorb an error.\n\nMINIMUM RENT. The minimum rent is $25 for most Section 8 project-based programs. It is one of the four elements of the TTP comparison, and it binds only when it is the GREATEST of the four. PHAs administering public housing, the Housing Choice Voucher program and Section 8 Moderate Rehabilitation may set a minimum rent between $0 and $50.\n\nTHE HARDSHIP EXEMPTION (24 CFR 5.630). Because the minimum rent can require payment from a family with no income, it is paired with a hardship exemption:\n• The family requests the exemption in writing.\n• The owner or PHA must SUSPEND the minimum rent beginning the month FOLLOWING the request, while the determination is made. Suspension is not discretionary.\n• The determination establishes whether a qualifying hardship exists and whether it is TEMPORARY or LONG-TERM.\n• During the suspension/review period the family may not be evicted for nonpayment of the minimum rent.\n• LONG-TERM hardship: the exemption continues while the hardship lasts, and suspended amounts are not owed.\n• TEMPORARY hardship: the minimum rent is suspended for 90 days; afterward the family resumes paying, and the suspended amounts become payable under a reasonable repayment agreement.\n• Qualifying hardships include loss of eligibility for or awaiting a determination on a federal, state or local assistance program; an income decrease from changed circumstances including loss of employment; imminent eviction for inability to pay the minimum rent; a death in the family; and other circumstances determined by HUD or the PHA.\n\nTHE CRITICAL LIMIT: the hardship exemption applies ONLY to the minimum rent element. It does not touch 30 percent of adjusted income, 10 percent of gross income, or the welfare rent. Suspending the minimum rent removes one candidate and leaves the greatest of the remaining three.\n\nWorked example 1 — the exemption that changes the TTP, but not to zero. A household's TTP is $25 because the minimum rent binds. Their other elements are: 30 percent of monthly adjusted income = $3.00; 10 percent of monthly gross = $5.00; no welfare rent. They request an exemption on March 12.\n  Suspension effective April 1 — the month following the request.\n  With the minimum rent removed, TTP = $5.00, not $0.00.\n  If TEMPORARY: suspension covers April, May and June; the $60 in suspended minimum rent becomes payable in July under an affordable repayment agreement, and no eviction for that $60 during the review period.\n  If LONG-TERM: the $5.00 TTP continues while the hardship lasts and nothing is owed for the exempted months.\n\nWorked example 2 — the request that changes nothing. A household's TTP is $190 because the WELFARE RENT binds. They request a minimum rent hardship exemption. Removing the $25 minimum leaves $82.50, $31.50 and $190.00. TTP = $190.00, unchanged. The request is properly processed and properly produces no relief. Explaining this BEFORE the family files is a kindness; explaining it afterward is a complaint.\n\nWorked example 3 — the zero-income household with a pending application. A household has no income and has applied for SSDI; the determination is pending. Without an exemption: (a) $0.00, (b) $0.00, (d) $25.00 → TTP $25.00. The household requests an exemption on the ground that it is awaiting a determination of eligibility for a federal assistance program — an expressly qualifying hardship. Suspension effective the following month: TTP $0.00. Here the exemption does reach zero, but only because the other elements are themselves zero.\n\nZERO INCOME HOUSEHOLDS. A household reporting no income is not suspicious by itself — it is common and legitimate. What reviewers examine is whether the property VERIFIED and MONITORED it:\n• A zero-income certification signed by the household.\n• The EIV No Income Reported report, run and reviewed.\n• Documentation of how the household meets basic expenses — food, transportation, utilities — obtained without demanding information the rules do not permit, and recorded as a documented conversation.\n• Follow-up at the interval the property's written policy commits to (commonly quarterly), actually performed and documented.\n• Prompt processing of an interim when income begins.\n\nWorked example 4 — the policy that created its own finding. A property's written policy commits to quarterly zero-income reviews. The property has three zero-income households and performed the reviews once in eighteen months. The finding is written against the property's OWN policy. The lesson generalizes: never commit in writing to a frequency you will not perform. If quarterly is unrealistic, write semiannual and do it.\n\nWorked example 5 — the boundary case: income that starts and is not reported. A zero-income household's member starts work in May; nothing is reported; EIV surfaces it in the fourth quarter. The property owes an interim, a recomputation, and a repayment analysis — and the zero-income monitoring interval is exactly the control that should have caught it. When a client has zero-income households, the quarterly check plus the EIV New Hires cycle is the pairing that prevents this.\n\nWorked example 6 — the ambiguous case: a household with income that is entirely excluded. A household's only receipts are payments that are excluded from annual income. Their annual income for HUD purposes is zero, but they are not \"zero income\" in the ordinary sense. Document the classification carefully — the income exists, is verified, and is excluded by a cited provision — because a file that simply shows \"zero income\" for a household with visible resources invites exactly the wrong inference. The distinction between \"no income\" and \"no COUNTABLE income\" should appear in the file note."},{"heading":"Unit Transfers, Household Composition Changes, and VAWA Emergency Transfers","body":"Three transaction types that share one failure mode: they are processed as move-outs and move-ins when they should not be, or not processed at all.\n\nUNIT TRANSFERS. A household moving to a different unit in the same property, under the same HAP contract, is a TRANSFER. Processing it as a termination plus a new admission produces: a reset recertification anniversary, a spurious termination and admission in TRACS, a possibly unnecessary re-determination of eligibility, security deposit confusion, and a rent roll that misrepresents tenure. Transfers arise for occupancy standards (household grew or shrank), accessibility needs, VAWA emergency transfers, and property-initiated moves for rehabilitation.\n\nHOUSEHOLD COMPOSITION CHANGES. Additions and departures must be reported and processed per the lease and the owner's written policy. Every addition requires: owner approval per the policy, verification of the new member's income and eligibility, citizenship/immigration declaration, SSN documentation as required, addition to the lease, execution of the VAWA lease addendum by any new adult, a recomputation, and an occupancy standards check. Every departure requires processing, a recomputation, and — for deductions — a check of whether the departure changes deduction eligibility.\n\nVAWA EMERGENCY TRANSFERS. Covered in depth in Chapter 17, but they intersect certification processing here. A tenant qualifies for an emergency transfer if they (1) reasonably believe they are threatened with imminent harm from further violence if they remain in the unit, OR (2) were a victim of sexual assault that occurred on the premises within the 90 days preceding the request. Every covered housing provider must maintain a written EMERGENCY TRANSFER PLAN and make it available on request; form HUD-5383 is the Emergency Transfer Request.\n\nWorked example 1 — the transfer done right. A household of two in a 1-bedroom has a child. Under the occupancy standards they qualify for a 2-bedroom, and one is available.\n  Process as a UNIT TRANSFER. The recertification anniversary is PRESERVED.\n  A composition change certification adds the child: new dependent, a $480 dependent deduction under 4350.3 as written, recomputed TTP.\n  The lease is amended or a new lease executed for the new unit as the property's practice requires, with all addenda.\n  Security deposit is transferred or adjusted per policy, documented.\n  Move-in inspection for the new unit; move-out inspection for the old.\n  TRACS reflects a transfer, not a termination and admission.\n\nWorked example 2 — the same facts done wrong, with the damage traced. Staff process a move-out and a move-in.\n  The anniversary resets from March 1 to the transfer date of August 15.\n  TRACS shows a termination and a new admission, so the household's tenure appears to restart — which affects any tenure-dependent analysis and looks like turnover in the property's statistics.\n  A full eligibility re-determination may have been performed unnecessarily, and if the household's income has risen above the applicable limit for NEW admissions, staff may wrongly conclude the household is ineligible. This is the dangerous part: continued occupancy and initial eligibility are different tests, and a mishandled transfer can produce a wrongful termination of assistance.\n  Fixing it requires corrected certifications and TRACS corrections, and the anniversary must be restored.\n\nWorked example 3 — the composition change that changes a deduction. A household consists of an elderly head (age 71) and her adult daughter. The head is the qualifying elderly member and the household receives the elderly/disabled family deduction. The head moves to a nursing facility permanently and the daughter, age 44 with no disability, remains as the sole household member.\n  The elderly/disabled family deduction NO LONGER APPLIES, because no member is elderly or a person with a disability.\n  Adjusted income rises by the deduction amount; TTP rises accordingly.\n  This is processed as a composition change with an interim certification, and the resulting RENT INCREASE requires proper advance notice before it takes effect.\n  Also required: a determination of the daughter's status as a remaining member of the tenant family under the property's policy and program rules, and an occupancy standards check on the unit size.\n  Properties routinely process the composition change and forget the deduction, leaving the household with an incorrect (too low) rent that must later be corrected retroactively.\n\nWorked example 4 — the VAWA emergency transfer, and the certification mechanics behind it. A survivor requests an emergency transfer under the two-prong test and submits HUD-5383. The property's Emergency Transfer Plan governs the process. On the certification side: an internal transfer is a UNIT TRANSFER preserving the anniversary; if the transfer is to another property, the mechanics differ and the plan should describe them. If the household is bifurcated — the perpetrator removed from the lease — and the removed member was the one whose eligibility qualified the household for assistance, the remaining household gets a 90-DAY PERIOD to establish eligibility or find alternative housing, with a possible extension of up to 60 ADDITIONAL DAYS. Critically, VAWA documentation does NOT go in the tenant file: it must be kept in a separate, secure location and may not be entered into any shared database.\n\nWorked example 5 — the boundary case: a transfer that changes the subsidy math. A household transfers from a unit with a $95 utility allowance to one with a $41 allowance because the utility configuration differs. TTP does not change (it depends on income, not the unit), but TENANT RENT does: tenant rent is TTP minus the utility allowance, so tenant rent rises by $54 while the household's total housing cost is theoretically unchanged. Explain this to the household BEFORE the transfer, in writing. A household that transfers and then receives a rent statement $54 higher with no explanation files a complaint, and the complaint is about communication, not compliance.\n\nWorked example 6 — the ambiguous case: an unauthorized occupant who has become permanent. A household's adult nephew has stayed \"temporarily\" for eleven months. Is he a household member? Under the lease and the property's written policy, an occupant beyond the permitted guest period is either approved as a household member — with income counted, screening applied per the policy, and the lease amended — or is an unauthorized occupant subject to lease enforcement. What is not acceptable is a third state in which the property knows and does nothing. That third state is the one reviewers find, and it produces both an income finding (unreported income in the unit) and a lease enforcement finding. Note the interaction with VAWA and with reasonable accommodation: before enforcing, confirm that the occupancy is not connected to a protected circumstance, because enforcement in those cases has its own rules."}]},{"id":"m94","unit":"u9","number":4,"title":"HOTMA Changes to Certifications — What Changes January 1, 2027","subtitle":"","sections":[{"heading":"HOTMA Overview — The Compliance Date, and Why It Keeps Moving","body":"The Housing Opportunity Through Modernization Act of 2016 (HOTMA) rewrote how income and assets are determined for HUD-assisted housing. SECTION 102 changes the definition of income and reduces the frequency of income reviews; SECTION 104 sets asset limits for eligibility and continued occupancy and changes how assets and certain investments are treated. HUD's implementing final rule was published in 2023 and implemented for Multifamily through Notice H 2023-10.\n\nTHE COMPLIANCE DATE — verify this before you teach it, because it has moved three times.\n\n• Notice H 2023-10 originally set full compliance for Multifamily at JANUARY 1, 2025.\n• Notice H 2025-03 (May 2025) extended it to JANUARY 1, 2026.\n• Notice H 2025-07 (published December 2025) further revised Section 6.2 of the prior notice and set the date at JANUARY 1, 2027.\n\nAs of the date this chapter was written, JANUARY 1, 2027 is the operative Multifamily compliance date. Two cautions for a working consultant. First, this applies to HUD MULTIFAMILY programs — the Office of Public and Indian Housing programs have their own implementation track, so a client with both a PBRA property and a voucher-based operation may be on two different clocks. Second, verify the current date before every engagement. A consultant who quotes a superseded compliance date in a written deliverable has published an error with the client's name on it.\n\nWhat this means operationally, right now:\n\n• Until a property implements HOTMA, the 4350.3 REV-1 framework governs its certifications — the $5,000 asset threshold, the $480 dependent deduction, the $400 elderly/disabled family deduction, and the 3 percent medical/disability threshold, as taught throughout this track.\n• Owners MAY implement early. HUD's guidance contemplates owners who adopt HOTMA before the compliance date calculating incomes and rents manually and entering the results into TRACS 202D using the rent override function. Early adopters must apply the HOTMA rules consistently, not selectively.\n• MORs conducted AFTER the compliance date will identify HOTMA-related deficiencies as findings requiring corrective action. Before that date, the finding a reviewer can write is against whichever regime the property has adopted and whether its written policies match its practice.\n• THE POLICY-PRACTICE MATCH IS THE LIVE ISSUE TODAY. A property operating under 4350.3 with a Tenant Selection Plan that recites HOTMA thresholds is deficient. So is the reverse.\n\nWorked example 1 — the property that half-implemented. A regional operator updated its Tenant Selection Plans to HOTMA language in 2024 in anticipation of the original January 1, 2025 date, then never changed its certification practice when the date moved. Two years later its TSPs describe a $50,000 asset self-certification threshold while its 50059s apply the $5,000 imputation rule. Both documents cannot be right. The reviewer's finding is not about HOTMA at all — it is that the written policy does not describe actual practice, which is a Category-level finding about management systems. Cure: pick a regime, state it explicitly in the policy with an effective date, and align every document to it.\n\nWorked example 2 — the client who wants to implement early. A well-run 220-unit property asks whether to adopt HOTMA now. The honest analysis: early adoption means manual computation with TRACS rent overrides, staff working two rule sets during any transition, and a genuine risk of inconsistency across certifications — against the benefit of being ready and of avoiding a mass transition at the deadline. For most properties the better plan is a STAGED READINESS approach: update policies with a stated future effective date, train staff now, build the worksheets now, run parallel calculations on a sample of files to find surprises, and switch on the compliance date. Say clearly which you recommend and why.\n\nWorked example 3 — the transition arithmetic that will surprise clients. Under 4350.3 the elderly/disabled family deduction is $400 and the dependent deduction is $480. Under HOTMA these are indexed; the CY2026 figures published by HUD are $550 for the elderly/disabled family deduction and $500 per dependent. For an elderly household with two dependents, deductions rise from $400 + $960 = $1,360 to $550 + $1,000 = $1,550 — a $190 increase in deductions, roughly $4.75 per month less rent. Meanwhile the medical expense threshold rises from 3 percent to 10 percent of annual income, which for the same household with $18,000 of income moves the floor from $540 to $1,800 — potentially a $1,260 REDUCTION in the deductible amount, roughly $31.50 per month MORE rent. For medically expensive households the second effect dominates the first. Show clients both directions; the phase-in and hardship provisions in Section 4 exist precisely because of this.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: certifications straddling the date. An annual recertification with an effective date of December 1, 2026 is computed in October 2026 under the pre-HOTMA rules. An annual with an effective date of January 1, 2027 is computed in November 2026 — under which rules? The rule that governs is the one in effect for the CERTIFICATION'S EFFECTIVE DATE, not the date the work was done. Properties should identify every certification with an effective date on or after the compliance date and process it under the new rules, which in practice means the transition begins in the fall preceding the date. Build the client's transition calendar backward from the first affected effective date.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case worth flagging to the client in writing. Because the compliance date has moved repeatedly, any deliverable you produce that recites HOTMA figures or dates should carry an explicit \"verified as of [date], confirm before relying\" line and a citation to the notice number. This is not defensive boilerplate; it is the professional standard in an area where the operative date changed three times in three years."},{"heading":"HOTMA Asset Rules — Self-Certification, Imputation, and the Asset Ceiling","body":"HOTMA's asset provisions are the most consequential day-to-day change for certification staff, and they run in two directions: some things get easier, and one thing gets much harder.\n\nWHAT GETS EASIER — the self-certification threshold. Where a family's net family assets are at or below the threshold, the owner may accept the family's DECLARATION of the asset value and self-certification of anticipated income from those assets, rather than obtaining third-party verification. The statutory figure is $50,000, ADJUSTED ANNUALLY FOR INFLATION; the amount published by HUD for CY2026 is $52,787. The self-certification may be used for three years, with full third-party verification required at least every third year.\n\nWHAT GETS EASIER — imputation. Under 4350.3 as written, imputed income is calculated whenever net family assets exceed $5,000, at HUD's passbook savings rate. Under HOTMA the imputation threshold rises to the same indexed figure — $52,787 for CY2026 — so the great majority of households will have no imputed asset income at all. When imputation does apply, it uses HUD's published passbook rate; for CY2026 that rate is 0.40 percent.\n\nWHAT GETS HARDER — the asset ceiling. HOTMA imposes an ELIGIBILITY LIMIT that does not exist under 4350.3. A family is generally ineligible for assistance if:\n• its NET FAMILY ASSETS EXCEED the limit — $100,000 statutory, indexed, published as $105,574 for CY2026; or\n• the family OWNS REAL PROPERTY SUITABLE FOR OCCUPANCY as a residence.\nBoth prongs carry exceptions, and the exceptions are where the real work is.\n\nReal property exceptions include, among others: property the family is actively offering for sale; property that does not meet the disability-related needs of household members (physical accessibility, proximity to transit, needed space); property owned by a household that includes a victim of domestic violence, dating violence, sexual assault or stalking; and property co-owned with a non-household member who lives there. Asset exclusions under HOTMA include categories that were treated differently before — retirement accounts, certain ABLE account balances, and certain irrevocable trusts among them.\n\nENFORCEMENT AGAINST EXISTING TENANTS. The limit cannot be waived, but enforcement against current participants may be DELAYED, with HUD's rule permitting a grace period of up to 6 MONTHS under the provider's policy. This must be written into the property's policies, applied uniformly, and documented per household.\n\nWorked example 1 — the self-certification in practice. A household reports a checking account of $1,940, a savings account of $6,100 and no other assets. Total net family assets: $8,040.\n  Under 4350.3 today: assets exceed $5,000, so imputation applies. At the 2 percent convention used throughout this track, imputed income is $160.80; actual interest is $22; count the greater, $160.80.\n  Under HOTMA: $8,040 is far below the $52,787 threshold, so the family may DECLARE the assets and SELF-CERTIFY the income, no third-party verification is required, and no imputation applies. Counted asset income: the actual anticipated income, $22.\n  The difference in counted income is $138.80 annually — small in rent terms, but the process difference is enormous: two verification requests and a wait, versus a signed declaration.\n\nWorked example 2 — the ceiling that disqualifies. An applicant household holds $118,000 in a brokerage account and $9,400 in cash. Under HOTMA, net family assets of roughly $127,400 exceed the CY2026 limit of $105,574, and the household is ineligible for admission. Two follow-ups that matter. First, check the EXCLUSIONS before concluding — if a substantial portion were held in an excluded retirement account, the countable figure could fall below the ceiling. Second, note that this household would have been ELIGIBLE under 4350.3 as written (which has no asset ceiling at all, only imputation). This is the single biggest substantive change HOTMA makes at admission, and consultants must make sure clients' Tenant Selection Plans and application processes are built to test it on the compliance date, not after.\n\nWorked example 3 — the real property prong, applied carefully. An applicant owns a house in another state, inherited from a parent, currently listed with a broker at market price. The house is \"suitable for occupancy\" in the ordinary sense. But property ACTIVELY OFFERED FOR SALE is an exception. The file needs the listing agreement, evidence of active marketing, and a follow-up mechanism at the next certification. Compare a second applicant who owns a house occupied by a non-household-member co-owner: a different exception applies. And a third who owns a two-story house with no accessible entry while a household member uses a wheelchair: the disability-needs exception is the relevant one, documented with the verification of need. Same prong, three different analyses, three different documents.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: the existing tenant who inherits. A long-term tenant inherits $140,000. Under HOTMA the household exceeds the asset ceiling. It cannot be waived — but enforcement against a current participant may be delayed up to 6 months under the provider's written policy, and the policy must be applied uniformly. The practical sequence: process the interim per the property's policy, apply the written grace period, notify the household clearly and in writing of the consequence and the timeline, and document every step. The wrong handling — quietly ignoring it, or terminating immediately with no notice — creates a serious problem in either direction.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: assets held for someone else. A household member is the custodian of a $61,000 account held for a niece who is not a household member, and is also the trustee of an irrevocable trust. Whether an asset is the FAMILY'S depends on ownership, access and control, not on whose name is on the envelope. Document the instrument, the family's actual access, and the determination with its basis. This is exactly the kind of judgment reviewers will not second-guess when it is documented and will always cite when it is not.\n\nWorked example 6 — the consulting deliverable. For every client, build a HOTMA asset worksheet now: net family assets computed, the exclusion checklist applied, the ceiling test, the real property test with its exception analysis, and the self-certification eligibility test — with the indexed figures stated and dated. When the compliance date arrives, the worksheet is already in use and staff have been running it for a year."},{"heading":"HOTMA Streamlined (Triennial) Income Determination for Fixed-Income Families","body":"HOTMA Section 102 reduces the frequency of full income examinations for families whose income is predominantly FIXED. This is a genuine administrative saving, and it is also a place where properties create findings by taking the shortcut without meeting its conditions.\n\nThe mechanism:\n\n• Where 90 PERCENT OR MORE of a family's unadjusted income consists of FIXED INCOME, the owner may use a STREAMLINED INCOME DETERMINATION in the intervening years, conducting a FULL third-party income examination at least EVERY THREE YEARS.\n• In the intervening years, the owner applies a COST-OF-LIVING ADJUSTMENT (or the applicable current interest rate) to each specific source of fixed income, using a publicly available figure or third-party-generated documentation the tenant provides.\n• The family must CERTIFY both that 90 percent or more of unadjusted income is fixed income AND that the sources of fixed income have not changed from the previous year.\n• Fixed income means sources that do not vary in the ordinary course — Social Security and SSI, most pensions and annuities, certain disability and long-term benefit payments, and similar recurring payments.\n• A parallel simplification applies to assets: where net family assets are at or below the indexed threshold, the family's declaration and self-certification may be accepted, with full third-party verification at least every three years.\n\nWhat does NOT change: the ANNUAL RECERTIFICATION still occurs. The streamlining is in the VERIFICATION METHOD, not in the existence of the certification. A property that reads this as \"we only recertify these households every three years\" has misread it and will fail on the 15-month rule and on the annual certification requirement itself.\n\nWorked example 1 — the qualifying household, three years running. An 79-year-old tenant receives $1,486 per month in Social Security and $110 per month from a small pension. No other income. Fixed income is 100 percent of unadjusted income.\n  YEAR 1 (full examination): third-party verification of both sources; income $19,152; certification completed normally.\n  YEAR 2 (streamlined): the family certifies that 90 percent or more of income remains fixed and the sources have not changed. The owner applies the published Social Security COLA to the $1,486 and the applicable adjustment to the pension, documents the source of each COLA figure, and completes the annual certification. No third-party verification requests.\n  YEAR 3 (streamlined): the same process, with that year's COLA.\n  YEAR 4: FULL third-party examination again.\n  The saving is real — two rounds of verification requests eliminated for this household — and the file must show the family's certification, the COLA source, and the arithmetic for each streamlined year.\n\nWorked example 2 — the household that does not qualify, and why staff get it wrong. A tenant receives $1,240 per month in Social Security ($14,880) and works part time earning $4,800. Total unadjusted income $19,680. Fixed income share: $14,880 ÷ $19,680 = 75.6 percent. This is BELOW 90 percent, so the streamlined determination is not available and a full examination is required every year. Staff who eyeball a household as \"mostly Social Security\" without computing the ratio will apply the shortcut to households that do not qualify. Build the ratio into the worksheet.\n\nWorked example 3 — the boundary case at the threshold. A tenant receives $18,000 in Social Security and $1,900 in interest and dividends that vary year to year. Fixed share: $18,000 ÷ $19,900 = 90.45 percent — just above the line. It qualifies this year. If the variable income rises to $2,100 next year, the share falls to 89.6 percent and it does not. This is why the family's annual certification about the 90 percent figure matters: the test must be re-applied every year, and the file must show it was.\n\nWorked example 4 — the failure mode that produces a repayment. A property applies the streamlined determination to a qualifying household in year 2. During that year the tenant's pension is converted to a different annuity paying $340 more per month, which the tenant does not report because \"nothing changed, it's still my pension.\" The certification the family signed said the sources had not changed. Two years later the full examination catches it, and the household has been under-charged for roughly 24 months. The control: the annual certification language must be specific about what \"changed\" means, and the property should still pull the EIV data every year — the streamlined determination reduces third-party verification requests, it does not switch off EIV monitoring.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: mid-cycle changes. A streamlined-year household reports a new income source in month 7. What now? A new source means the certification's premise no longer holds. Process the change per the interim rules, obtain verification of the new source, and reset the household's position in the three-year cycle per the property's written policy. Write that policy BEFORE the first streamlined year, because the question will arise and an ad hoc answer is a finding.\n\nWorked example 6 — the practice-management point. The streamlined determination is the strongest efficiency argument in HOTMA for elderly and disabled properties, where a large share of households are 100 percent fixed income. For a 150-unit Section 202 property with 120 qualifying households, eliminating two of every three rounds of third-party verification is a substantial reduction in staff hours. Quantify it for the client — hours per certification times households times two-thirds — and pair it with the cost of the transition work. That is a business case, not a compliance lecture."},{"heading":"HOTMA Income Exclusions, Deductions, and Interim Triggers","body":"Section 102 changes what counts as income, what is deducted from it, and when a certification must be redone mid-year. These are the changes that will show up on every 50059 after implementation.\n\nDEDUCTIONS. Under HOTMA the fixed deduction amounts are INDEXED to inflation and published annually. The amounts HUD published for CY2026 are:\n• Dependent deduction: $500 per dependent (from the $480 in 4350.3 as written).\n• Elderly/disabled FAMILY deduction: $550 (from $400). Still per family, not per person.\n• Earned income exclusion for dependent full-time students: $500.\nAlways use the figure published for the certification's EFFECTIVE-DATE YEAR, not the figure you memorized.\n\nTHE MEDICAL AND DISABILITY EXPENSE THRESHOLD. HOTMA raises the threshold from 3 PERCENT to 10 PERCENT of annual income for unreimbursed health and medical care expenses and for disability assistance expenses. Because this can sharply reduce deductions for households with high medical costs, it comes with relief:\n• A PHASE-IN: families deduct expenses exceeding 5 percent of annual income in the first year, 7.5 percent in the second, and the full 10 percent thereafter. To qualify for the phased relief a family must have been receiving a deduction because expenses exceeded 3 percent of annual income as of the applicable date.\n• A separate FINANCIAL HARDSHIP exemption is available where the general standard would create hardship.\nThe phase-in is a HARDSHIP EXEMPTION with conditions, not an automatic schedule for everyone. Read the conditions before promising a client's residents relief.\n\nASSET INCOME AND EXCLUSIONS. Imputation applies only above the indexed threshold (CY2026: $52,787), at HUD's published passbook rate (CY2026: 0.40 percent). Certain assets are excluded from net family assets, including retirement accounts, certain ABLE account balances, and certain irrevocable trusts. Non-necessary personal property is included only above the indexed threshold.\n\nINTERIM RECERTIFICATION TRIGGERS. HOTMA standardizes what was largely owner policy under 4350.3:\n• An interim is REQUIRED when income DECREASES by 10 PERCENT OR MORE.\n• An interim is REQUIRED when income INCREASES by $2,400 OR MORE annually (an indexed figure).\n• Below those thresholds, the owner has discretion under a WRITTEN POLICY applied uniformly.\n\nWorked example 1 — the medical threshold, both directions, one household. An elderly household: annual income $18,000, unreimbursed medical expenses $2,400, two dependents.\n  Under 4350.3 as written: 3 percent floor = $540. Medical deduction = $2,400 − $540 = $1,860. Elderly deduction $400. Dependents 2 × $480 = $960. Total deductions $3,220. Adjusted income $14,780. TTP at 30 percent = $369.50/month.\n  Under HOTMA at the FULL 10 percent threshold: floor = $1,800. Medical deduction = $2,400 − $1,800 = $600. Elderly deduction $550. Dependents 2 × $500 = $1,000. Total deductions $2,150. Adjusted income $15,850. TTP = $396.25/month.\n  The household pays $26.75 more per month, despite the larger fixed deductions, because the medical floor moved by $1,260.\n  Under the PHASE-IN at 5 percent: floor = $900. Medical deduction = $1,500. Total deductions $3,050. Adjusted income $14,950. TTP = $373.75 — nearly the old figure.\n  Show clients all three columns. The middle column is what happens without the relief; the third is what the relief is worth; and the conversation about which residents qualify for the phase-in is the reason to run the analysis before the compliance date rather than after.\n\nWorked example 2 — the interim trigger arithmetic. A household's annual income is $26,400. The head's hours are cut, reducing income to $23,400.\n  Decrease: $3,000 ÷ $26,400 = 11.4 percent — AT OR ABOVE 10 percent, so an interim is REQUIRED.\n  Compare a decrease to $24,600: $1,800 ÷ $26,400 = 6.8 percent — below the threshold, so the owner's written policy governs whether an interim is processed.\n  Now the increase side: the same household's income rises by $2,600 annually. That EXCEEDS $2,400, so an interim is REQUIRED. An increase of $1,900 does not require one; the policy governs.\n  Note the asymmetry that clients find surprising: decreases are measured in PERCENT, increases in DOLLARS. A high-income household's $2,400 increase is a small percentage and still triggers; a low-income household's 10 percent decrease may be a few hundred dollars and still triggers.\n\nWorked example 3 — the exclusion that changes an eligibility answer. A household holds $96,000 in a 401(k) and $14,000 in savings. Under HOTMA, retirement accounts are excluded from net family assets, so countable net family assets are roughly $14,000 — well under the CY2026 ceiling of $105,574 and under the $52,787 self-certification threshold. Without applying the exclusion, staff would compute $110,000 and wrongly deny the household. This is the error to train against: apply the exclusions BEFORE testing the ceiling.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: which year's figures. A certification with an effective date of March 1, 2027 is computed in January 2027. Use the figures published for the year of the EFFECTIVE DATE. Because HUD publishes the adjusted amounts annually, properties must update their worksheets and software tables every January, and must verify that software vendors have done the same. A software table left at last year's dependent deduction produces a wrong deduction on every certification until someone notices.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: the household that qualifies for hardship relief but does not ask. The phase-in relief and the financial hardship exemption generally operate on request, with conditions. A household whose medical deduction is about to fall by $1,260 may not know to ask. The compliant and decent practice is the same: notify affected households in writing before implementation, explain the change and the relief that may be available, document the notice, and document each request and determination. A property that quietly implements the full 10 percent threshold and never mentions the relief will be technically defensible and will generate complaints — and reviewers ask how households were notified.\n\nWorked example 6 — the readiness test. Before the compliance date, run every current household through both rule sets and produce a two-column report: current TTP and post-HOTMA TTP. Sort by the size of the increase. The households at the top of that list are the ones who need notice, relief analysis, and a conversation. That report is the single most useful HOTMA deliverable a consultant can produce, and it takes a data export and a spreadsheet."},{"heading":"Policy, Tenant Selection Plan, and System Updates Under HOTMA","body":"HOTMA is not only a calculation change; it is a documentation change. Every written instrument that describes how the property determines income, assets and eligibility has to be revised, and reviewers will compare the documents to the practice.\n\nWHAT MUST BE UPDATED:\n\n• THE TENANT SELECTION PLAN. It must state the asset limitation and how it is applied at admission, the real property restriction and its exceptions, the self-certification threshold and process, the interim recertification triggers and the owner's discretionary policy below them, the streamlined determination policy for fixed-income families, the grace period policy for existing tenants over the asset ceiling, and the hardship exemption processes.\n• CERTIFICATION PROCEDURES AND WORKSHEETS. Asset worksheets, deduction worksheets with the correct thresholds, the fixed-income ratio test, and the annualization conventions.\n• THE HOUSE RULES AND LEASE-RELATED DOCUMENTS, to the extent they describe reporting obligations that HOTMA changes.\n• TENANT NOTICES. The notice describing what households must report and when has to match the new triggers.\n• SOFTWARE. Deduction tables, thresholds, the passbook rate, imputation logic, and the interim trigger logic. Verify with the vendor in writing, and TEST rather than trusting.\n• TRAINING RECORDS. Every certification staff member trained on the new rules, dated, documented.\n• EIV AND VERIFICATION PROCEDURES, which change in emphasis: less third-party asset verification below the threshold, and the every-third-year full verification cycle to track.\n\nWorked example 1 — the TSP revision that is actually complete. A 180-unit property's revised TSP adds: a section stating the asset limitation with the indexed figure and the date it was published; the real property restriction with each exception listed and the documentation required for each; the self-certification threshold, the three-year full-verification cycle, and who signs what; the interim triggers with the exact percentage and dollar figures and the owner's written discretionary policy below them; the streamlined determination policy including the 90 percent test and how COLAs are sourced; the grace period for existing tenants over the ceiling, stated as a uniform period; and the hardship exemption request process with timelines. Then, critically, an EFFECTIVE DATE and a revision history. A TSP without an effective date during a regime transition is unusable as evidence of anything.\n\nWorked example 2 — the software failure that produces a systemic finding. A property's vendor updates deduction tables but not the imputation threshold, so the software continues imputing above $5,000 after implementation. Every certification with assets between $5,000 and $52,787 now overstates income. At a property with 90 such households, that is 90 wrong certifications, a systemic finding, and a mass correction with retroactive HAP adjustments. The control is a TEST PROTOCOL: before go-live, run five constructed test cases through the software — a household just below the self-certification threshold, one just above, one with excluded retirement assets, one at the medical phase-in, and one triggering an interim by the dollar test — and verify each result by hand.\n\nWorked example 3 — the notice obligation nobody plans for. Implementation will change rents for some households, in both directions. Rent INCREASES require the advance notice the lease and handbook require, and a mass implementation means a mass notice cycle. Plan it: identify affected households, compute the change, schedule notices to meet the advance requirement before the effective date, and staff the resulting questions. A property that implements on January 1 and mails notices on December 28 has created a lease violation for every household whose rent went up.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: the property that has not decided. A client asks whether it can simply keep operating under 4350.3 past the compliance date \"until things settle.\" No. After the compliance date, full compliance is mandatory and MORs will record HOTMA-related deficiencies as findings requiring corrective action. What a client CAN do is sequence the transition so that certifications with effective dates on or after the date are processed under the new rules, which requires starting the work months earlier. Put the transition calendar in writing with the client's sign-off.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: a partially implemented portfolio. An operator with fourteen properties implements at eight and defers at six because of staffing. During the transition the organization is running two rule sets, and the risk is not the rules — it is staff moving between properties. Controls: each property's operative regime stated in a one-page cover sheet at the front of the certification procedures; worksheets color-coded or clearly labeled by regime; and no staff member working certifications at both a converted and an unconverted property in the same week without a supervisory check. This sounds excessive until you have seen a specialist apply a $52,787 threshold at a property still on $5,000.\n\nWorked example 6 — the consulting engagement this creates. HOTMA transition work is a defined, scopeable, deadline-driven project: policy revision, worksheet build, software test protocol, staff training, the two-column TTP impact report from Section 4, the notice campaign, and a post-implementation file audit at 30 and 90 days. Price it as a fixed-fee project per property with a portfolio discount, deliver it in the twelve months before the compliance date, and note that the post-implementation audit is where the follow-on retainer comes from — because the first ninety days after a regime change is when the errors that a reviewer will find are actually created."}]},{"id":"m101","unit":"u10","number":1,"title":"Income Calculation — Getting It Right Every Time","subtitle":"","sections":[{"heading":"Annual Income — What to Count and What to Exclude","body":"ANNUAL INCOME is defined at 24 CFR 5.609 and elaborated in Handbook 4350.3 REV-1 Chapter 5. It is all amounts, monetary or not, that go to or are received on behalf of the family head, spouse or co-head, or any other family member — anticipated to be received FROM SOURCES OUTSIDE THE FAMILY during the 12 months following the certification's effective date — unless specifically excluded.\n\nFour features of that definition do all the work, and each one produces its own class of error:\n\n1. IT IS PROSPECTIVE. Annual income is a projection of the NEXT twelve months, not a record of the last twelve. History is evidence of the future, not the answer.\n2. IT IS GROSS. Income is counted before taxes, before garnishments, before insurance and retirement withholding. What the tenant takes home is irrelevant.\n3. IT IS FROM OUTSIDE THE FAMILY. Money moving between household members is not income; money coming in from outside is.\n4. IT IS EVERYTHING NOT EXCLUDED. The exclusions are a defined list. If a receipt is not on the list, it counts, no matter how unfair that feels.\n\nWHAT COUNTS — the recurring categories: wages, salaries, overtime, tips, commissions, bonuses and other employment compensation; net income from a business or profession; interest, dividends and other net income from real or personal property; the full amount of periodic Social Security, SSI, annuity, insurance policy, retirement fund, pension, and disability or death benefit payments; unemployment and disability compensation, worker's compensation and severance; welfare assistance; periodic and determinable allowances such as alimony and child support ACTUALLY RECEIVED or reliably anticipated; regular contributions or gifts from persons not residing in the unit; and all regular pay, special pay and allowances of a member of the armed forces, with a specific exception for hostile-fire pay.\n\nWHAT IS EXCLUDED — the categories that generate the most questions: income of children under 18 (earned income only — unearned income of a minor, such as SSI paid on behalf of a child, IS counted); payments received for the care of foster children or foster adults; lump-sum additions to family assets such as inheritances, insurance payments, capital gains and settlements for personal or property losses (note the distinction between a lump sum, which is an ASSET, and a periodic payment, which is INCOME); amounts received specifically for reimbursement of medical expenses; income of a live-in aide; the earned income of a full-time student 18 or older who is a dependent, above a threshold amount; adoption assistance above a threshold; student financial assistance under the applicable rules; certain amounts under federal statutory exclusions; and hostile-fire pay.\n\nWorked example 1 — the projection rule, worked correctly. A tenant started a job on March 1 at $19.50 per hour, 40 hours per week, with a certification effective July 1. She has four months of history.\n  Correct method: project forward. $19.50 × 2,080 hours = $40,560 annually.\n  Wrong method A: annualize year-to-date earnings including the pre-employment months of zero, producing roughly $13,520 and understating income by $27,040.\n  Wrong method B: use last year's W-2 from a different job at a different wage.\n  The file should show the wage, the hours, the multiplier used, and the source document. If hours vary, use the documented average and say so.\n\nWorked example 2 — gross versus net, with the arithmetic. A pay stub shows gross $1,846.15 biweekly, net $1,312.40 after taxes, health insurance and a 401(k) contribution.\n  Count: $1,846.15 × 26 = $47,999.90, rounded per the property's convention.\n  Do NOT count $1,312.40 × 26 = $34,122.40.\n  The difference — nearly $14,000 — would understate TTP by roughly $345 per month. Staff who \"use the number the tenant actually gets\" are making a $4,000-per-year error per household, and it is one of the most common errors reviewers find.\n\nWorked example 3 — inside versus outside the family. A household has an adult son who pays his mother $300 per month toward household expenses.\n  That $300 is NOT income — it is a transfer between household members, and the son's own income is already counted.\n  Now change one fact: the son does not live in the unit and sends his mother $300 per month. Now it IS income — a regular contribution from a person not residing in the unit — and $3,600 is counted.\n  Same $300, opposite treatment, and the distinguishing fact is residence.\n\nWorked example 4 — the minor's income, both directions. A household has a 16-year-old who earns $7,800 at a part-time job and also receives $9,048 per year in SSI on her own behalf.\n  The $7,800 in EARNED income is EXCLUDED — she is under 18.\n  The $9,048 in SSI is UNEARNED income and IS COUNTED in full.\n  Staff who learn \"children's income doesn't count\" exclude both and understate income by $9,048, which at 30 percent is roughly $226 per month of rent. This single misunderstanding is worth auditing for at every property with families.\n\nWorked example 5 — the boundary case: lump sum or periodic? A tenant receives a $42,000 settlement from a personal injury claim. Excluded from income as a lump-sum addition to family assets — but it becomes an ASSET, and asset rules then apply. Change the facts: the same claim is settled for $700 per month for six years. Now it is a PERIODIC payment and it IS income — $8,400 per year. The character of the payment, not its source, determines the treatment. A third variation: a $42,000 lump sum representing DELAYED periodic payments (for example, a retroactive award of a benefit) has its own treatment rules that must be applied specifically. Document which category you concluded and why.\n\nWorked example 6 — the ambiguous case reviewers love. A tenant's mother pays the tenant's car insurance directly to the insurer, $148 per month. Nothing passes through the tenant's hands. Is it income? The definition reaches amounts received ON BEHALF OF a family member, and regular contributions or gifts from persons outside the household are included. A regular, ongoing payment of a household expense by an outside party is generally counted. A one-time gift is not a regular contribution. The file must show the analysis: what is paid, how often, by whom, since when, and the conclusion. Staff who avoid asking about these arrangements are not avoiding the problem — they are avoiding the documentation of a problem the reviewer will ask about directly."},{"heading":"Adjusted Income — Deductions That Reduce Tenant Rent","body":"ADJUSTED INCOME is annual income minus the allowable deductions at 24 CFR 5.611 and Handbook 4350.3 Chapter 5. Under 4350.3 as written — the regime governing most Multifamily properties until the HOTMA compliance date of January 1, 2027 — there are five:\n\n1. DEPENDENT DEDUCTION: $480 per dependent. A dependent is a household member who is under 18, a person with a disability, or a full-time student — and who is NOT the head, spouse, co-head, foster child, or live-in aide.\n2. ELDERLY / DISABLED FAMILY DEDUCTION: $400 PER FAMILY, where the head, spouse or co-head is at least 62 or is a person with a disability. Per family, not per person.\n3. MEDICAL EXPENSE DEDUCTION: unreimbursed medical expenses of an ELDERLY OR DISABLED FAMILY, deductible to the extent they exceed 3 PERCENT of ANNUAL income.\n4. DISABILITY ASSISTANCE EXPENSE DEDUCTION: unreimbursed attendant care and auxiliary apparatus expenses that enable a family member (including the person with the disability) to work, deductible above the same 3 percent threshold and CAPPED at the amount of employment income enabled.\n5. CHILD CARE DEDUCTION: reasonable, unreimbursed child care for children UNDER 13, necessary to enable a family member to work, seek work, or further their education — with the work-related portion CAPPED at the income earned as a result.\n\nTHE THREE RULES THAT PRODUCE MOST DEDUCTION FINDINGS:\n• The 3 percent threshold is computed on ANNUAL income, not adjusted income.\n• The threshold is applied ONCE, across medical and disability assistance expenses COMBINED — not to each category separately.\n• The elderly/disabled deduction is per family; the dependent deduction is per dependent.\n\n(Where HOTMA is implemented: the deductions are indexed — CY2026 published figures are $500 per dependent and $550 for the elderly/disabled family deduction — and the medical/disability threshold rises to 10 percent with phased hardship relief. Use the figures for the certification's effective-date year and the regime that governs it.)\n\nWorked example 1 — the full deduction computation. A household: head age 68, spouse age 66, a 15-year-old grandchild in their custody, and a 20-year-old grandchild who is a full-time student. Annual income $22,400. Unreimbursed medical expenses $3,900. No child care, no disability assistance expenses.\n  Dependents: the 15-year-old (under 18) and the 20-year-old (full-time student) = 2 × $480 = $960.\n  Elderly/disabled family deduction: $400 — ONE deduction, even though both head and spouse are elderly.\n  Medical: 3 percent of $22,400 = $672. Deduction = $3,900 − $672 = $3,228.\n  Total deductions: $960 + $400 + $3,228 = $4,588.\n  Adjusted income: $22,400 − $4,588 = $17,812.\n  Monthly adjusted income: $1,484.33. Thirty percent: $445.30.\n\nWorked example 2 — the threshold applied twice, and the harm it does. Same household, plus $1,800 in disability assistance expenses for the spouse.\n  WRONG: ($3,900 − $672) + ($1,800 − $672) = $3,228 + $1,128 = $4,356.\n  RIGHT: ($3,900 + $1,800) − $672 = $5,700 − $672 = $5,028.\n  The wrong method understates the deduction by $672, overstates adjusted income by the same amount, and overcharges the household by roughly $16.80 per month. Note the direction: this error harms the TENANT, and reviewers cite it exactly as readily as errors that harm HUD.\n\nWorked example 3 — the child care cap, worked. A single parent pays $6,400 per year for licensed care for a 7-year-old so she can work. She earns $9,100 in that job.\n  The deduction is limited to the earned income enabled: $6,400 is less than $9,100, so the full $6,400 is deductible.\n  Change the facts: she earns $4,800. Now the work-related child care deduction is capped at $4,800, and $1,600 is not deductible.\n  Change again: half the care hours enable WORK and half enable her to attend school. The portion enabling education is not subject to the earned-income cap in the same way; separate the hours, apply the cap only to the work-related portion, and document the split. Reviewers test exactly this, because it is where properties either over-deduct (ignoring the cap) or under-deduct (applying the cap to the education portion).\n\nWorked example 4 — the dependent who is not a dependent. A household consists of a head, her spouse, and her 19-year-old son who works full time and does not attend school.\n  The son is NOT a dependent — he is over 18, not a student, and not a person with a disability. No $480 deduction.\n  His income IS counted in full, because he is 18 or over.\n  Properties that take a dependent deduction for every child under some assumed age overstate deductions and understate rent. The definition is the test, not the family relationship.\n\nWorked example 5 — the boundary case: expenses that are reimbursed or will be. A tenant presents $4,200 in medical bills, of which $1,650 will be reimbursed by insurance. Only UNREIMBURSED expenses are deductible: $2,550, less the threshold. The common error is deducting the billed amount. Ask specifically what insurance, Medicare, Medicaid or a supplemental policy will pay, and verify. Also note that amounts received specifically to reimburse medical expenses are excluded from income — the two rules work together and staff frequently apply neither.\n\nWorked example 6 — the ambiguous case: anticipated versus historical medical expenses. A household documents $5,100 in medical expenses over the past year, but $3,400 of that was a one-time surgery that will not recur. Annual income is $19,000. Medical expenses are anticipated for the coming twelve months, like income. The defensible computation projects recurring costs — prescriptions, premiums, regular treatment — and includes non-recurring items only where they are anticipated. Here, if recurring costs are $1,700 and no further surgery is expected, the deduction is $1,700 − $570 = $1,130, not $5,100 − $570 = $4,530. Document the projection method. Properties that mechanically deduct last year's total over-deduct systematically, and reviewers who see a large medical deduction ask what it is composed of."},{"heading":"Calculating Total Tenant Payment and HAP","body":"TOTAL TENANT PAYMENT is the GREATEST of four elements:\n  (a) 30 percent of MONTHLY ADJUSTED income;\n  (b) 10 percent of MONTHLY GROSS (annual) income;\n  (c) the WELFARE RENT — in as-paid localities, the portion of welfare assistance specifically designated for housing;\n  (d) the MINIMUM RENT — $25 for most Section 8 project-based programs.\n\nThen:\n  TENANT RENT = TTP − UTILITY ALLOWANCE (if the tenant pays utilities directly).\n  If the UTILITY ALLOWANCE EXCEEDS the TTP, tenant rent is $0 and the difference is a UTILITY REIMBURSEMENT paid to the tenant (or the utility company).\n  HAP = GROSS RENT (contract rent + utility allowance) − TTP.\n\n\"Greatest,\" not \"average,\" not \"whichever the software picked.\" Recompute all four every time you audit a file.\n\nWorked example 1 — (a) 30 percent of adjusted income binds. Family of four, annual income $28,800, two dependents, no other deductions. Contract rent $1,010; utility allowance $95.\n  Adjusted income: $28,800 − $960 = $27,840. Monthly: $2,320.00.\n  (a) 30% of $2,320.00 = $696.00\n  (b) 10% of ($28,800 ÷ 12 = $2,400) = $240.00\n  (c) welfare rent: n/a\n  (d) minimum rent: $25.00\n  TTP = $696.00.\n  Tenant rent = $696.00 − $95 = $601.00.\n  Gross rent = $1,010 + $95 = $1,105. HAP = $1,105 − $696 = $409.00.\n\nWorked example 2 — (b) 10 percent of GROSS income binds. An elderly household, annual income $24,000, with $18,180 in total deductions (large unreimbursed medical expenses). Contract rent $940; utility allowance $0 (owner pays utilities).\n  Adjusted income: $24,000 − $18,180 = $5,820. Monthly: $485.00.\n  (a) 30% of $485.00 = $145.50\n  (b) 10% of ($24,000 ÷ 12 = $2,000) = $200.00\n  (c) n/a  (d) $25.00\n  TTP = $200.00 — element (b) binds.\n  This is the case staff most often get wrong, because deductions have pushed adjusted income low enough that the 10 percent floor takes over. Software configured to compute only 30 percent of adjusted income produces $145.50 and a $54.50 monthly error, every month, for every household in this posture.\n  Tenant rent = $200.00 (no utility allowance). HAP = $940 − $200 = $740.00.\n\nWorked example 3 — (c) the WELFARE RENT binds. A family of four in an as-paid locality receives TANF of $6,600 per year, of which $190 per month is specifically designated for housing. Three dependents. Contract rent $875; utility allowance $110.\n  Adjusted income: $6,600 − (3 × $480 = $1,440) = $5,160. Monthly: $430.00.\n  (a) 30% of $430.00 = $129.00\n  (b) 10% of ($6,600 ÷ 12 = $550) = $55.00\n  (c) welfare rent = $190.00\n  (d) $25.00\n  TTP = $190.00 — element (c) binds.\n  Tenant rent = $190.00 − $110 = $80.00. Gross rent = $985. HAP = $985 − $190 = $795.00.\n  The welfare rent element applies only in AS-PAID localities, where the welfare agency designates a housing portion and adjusts it based on actual housing costs. Most jurisdictions are not as-paid. A property in a non-as-paid jurisdiction should have element (c) permanently n/a — and if a file shows a welfare rent in such a jurisdiction, something is misconfigured.\n\nWorked example 4 — (d) the MINIMUM RENT binds. A single tenant with $600 in annual income from occasional day labor. No deductions. Contract rent $760; utility allowance $84.\n  Adjusted income: $600. Monthly: $50.00.\n  (a) 30% of $50.00 = $15.00\n  (b) 10% of ($600 ÷ 12 = $50) = $5.00\n  (c) n/a  (d) $25.00\n  TTP = $25.00 — element (d) binds.\n  Note that (a) and (b) both produced real, non-zero figures. The minimum rent is not a default for zero-income families; it is a floor that outranks small computed amounts.\n  Tenant rent = $25.00 − $84 = negative → tenant rent $0.00, and a UTILITY REIMBURSEMENT of $59.00 is owed to the tenant. Gross rent = $844. HAP = $844 − $25 = $819.00.\n\nWorked example 5 — the HARDSHIP EXEMPTION applied to Example 4. The tenant loses even the day labor and requests a minimum rent hardship exemption in writing on March 12.\n  The minimum rent is SUSPENDED beginning April 1 — the month following the request — while the determination is made.\n  With element (d) removed: (a) $0.00, (b) $0.00, (c) n/a → TTP = $0.00.\n  Tenant rent $0.00; utility reimbursement rises to the full $84.00; HAP = $844 − $0 = $844.00.\n  If the hardship is TEMPORARY, the suspension runs 90 days and the suspended minimum rent becomes payable afterward under a reasonable repayment agreement; the family cannot be evicted for that amount during the review period. If LONG-TERM, the exemption continues while the hardship lasts and nothing is owed.\n  Critical limit: the exemption removes ONLY element (d). Had (a) been $58.00, the TTP after suspension would be $58.00, not zero.\n\nWorked example 6 — the UTILITY REIMBURSEMENT case in a normal-income household. A household's TTP computes to $67.00; the utility allowance for the unit size and utility configuration is $148.\n  Tenant rent = $67 − $148 = negative → tenant rent $0.00.\n  Utility reimbursement = $148 − $67 = $81.00 per month, paid to the tenant (or to the utility provider under the property's arrangement).\n  Gross rent = contract rent $915 + $148 = $1,063. HAP = $1,063 − $67 = $996.00.\n  Three things reviewers check here: that the reimbursement is actually PAID, on the required schedule; that it appears correctly on the voucher; and that the tenant ledger reflects it. Properties with only a handful of reimbursement households frequently never build the process, and a computed-but-unpaid reimbursement is both a finding and a debt to the tenant.\n\nWorked example 7 — the audit routine. For any file, recompute (a), (b), (c) and (d), take the greatest, subtract the utility allowance, and reconcile to the rent roll and TRACS. If any of the four elements is missing from the property's worksheet or software output, you have found a systemic issue rather than a file issue — which is a different finding and a different corrective action."},{"heading":"Self-Employment Income — The Net Income Approach","body":"Self-employment income is counted as NET INCOME from the operation of a business or profession — gross receipts minus ordinary and necessary business expenses. It is the single most error-prone income category, because it is the one place where HUD's rules and the tenant's tax return diverge, and staff reflexively trust the tax return.\n\nThe framework:\n\n• Start with GROSS RECEIPTS for the anticipated 12 months.\n• Subtract ORDINARY AND NECESSARY BUSINESS EXPENSES actually incurred to produce the income.\n• DO NOT subtract items that reduce taxable income but are not cash expenses of producing income — the two that matter most are DEPRECIATION and any deduction for the owner's own compensation or draw.\n• Business EXPANSION or CAPITAL EXPENDITURES are not deductible as operating expenses; principal payments on business loans are not deductible (interest generally is).\n• Amounts withdrawn from the business by the family are not a separate item of income if the net income is already counted — but withdrawals in excess of net income can implicate asset rules.\n• Verify with the documents that exist: tax returns and schedules (Schedule C, Schedule E, Schedule F, partnership or S-corporation returns), profit-and-loss statements, business bank statements, and where the business is new or informal, a self-certification supported by whatever records exist plus a documented attempt at higher-level verification.\n\nWorked example 1 — the depreciation adjustment, worked. A tenant operates a house-cleaning business. Her Schedule C shows:\n  Gross receipts: $38,400\n  Supplies: $2,100\n  Vehicle expense (actual costs, documented): $4,800\n  Insurance: $900\n  Depreciation on her van: $5,200\n  Net profit per Schedule C: $25,400\n  HUD computation: add BACK depreciation, because it is not a cash expense of producing income.\n  $25,400 + $5,200 = $30,600 counted as annual income.\n  The difference is $5,200 — roughly $130 per month in TTP at 30 percent. Properties that simply copy the Schedule C net profit understate income on nearly every self-employed household, because virtually every Schedule C has depreciation.\n\nWorked example 2 — the tenant who pays herself a salary. A tenant operates a small landscaping S-corporation. The return shows officer compensation of $28,000 and net business income after that of $6,400.\n  Do not count only the $6,400, and do not count only the $28,000. The family's income includes the compensation received AND the net income of the business attributable to the family — here roughly $34,400 in total, subject to the same add-back analysis for depreciation and non-cash items.\n  The error to avoid in both directions: counting the salary twice (once as wages and once inside net income), or counting only one component. Lay the return out line by line and document what was counted from where.\n\nWorked example 3 — the new business with no history. A tenant began selling handmade goods online four months ago. There is no tax return. Records consist of platform payout statements and a notebook of material costs.\n  Compute from what exists: four months of documented gross receipts, annualized with a documented method; documented material and shipping costs subtracted; a self-certification of the projection, with the attempts at higher-level verification noted.\n  Then SCHEDULE A REVIEW: note in the file that the projection will be re-examined at the next certification against actual results. A documented estimate that is revisited is defensible; an estimate that becomes permanent because nobody looked again is not.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: the business that loses money. A tenant's Schedule C shows a net LOSS of $3,200 after adding back depreciation. Does the household's other income get reduced by the loss? No — a business loss does not offset other income for HUD purposes. Count the business income as ZERO and count wages, benefits and other income in full. Properties that net a business loss against wages understate income, sometimes dramatically, and the error is invisible unless someone reads the return.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: is it a business or a job? A tenant drives for a rideshare platform and receives a 1099. Rideshare and gig work is self-employment, so the net income approach applies — gross platform earnings minus documented ordinary and necessary expenses, with depreciation added back if a mileage or depreciation deduction was taken on a return. The common error is treating a 1099 like a W-2 and counting the gross platform payments with no expense recognition, which OVERSTATES income and overcharges the household. Document the expense analysis and the method used, and be consistent across households — one gig worker treated as self-employed and another as a wage earner at the same property is an inconsistency that reviewers catch.\n\nWorked example 6 — the verification that fails and what to do. A tenant refuses to provide a tax return, saying it is private. The consent forms authorize obtaining information needed to determine eligibility and rent, and the property cannot certify income it cannot verify. Sequence: explain the requirement and the consent already signed; request alternatives (profit-and-loss statement, business bank statements, platform statements); document each request and the response; and if no adequate documentation is provided, follow the written policy for a household that fails to provide required information, which leads to the loss-of-assistance process. What you may not do is invent a number, and what you should not do is accept a bare self-certification when higher-level documentation plainly exists."},{"heading":"Income Inclusions and Exclusions — The Most Common Mistakes","body":"A diagnostic list. Each item is an error that appears repeatedly in real files, with the correction and the control.\n\n1. USING NET INSTEAD OF GROSS. Correction: count gross. Control: the annualization worksheet requires the gross figure and the source document.\n2. ANNUALIZING WITH THE WRONG MULTIPLIER. Weekly × 52, biweekly × 26, semi-monthly × 24, monthly × 12, hourly × 2,080 for full-time. Control: the worksheet states pay frequency and the multiplier must match it.\n3. EXCLUDING A MINOR'S UNEARNED INCOME. Earned income of a member under 18 is excluded; unearned income such as SSI paid on the child's behalf is COUNTED.\n4. IGNORING RECURRING OVERTIME, TIPS, BONUSES OR COMMISSIONS. If the pattern is documented and expected to continue, it is anticipated income. Control: obtain enough pay history to establish the pattern and document the method.\n5. COUNTING A LUMP SUM AS INCOME. Lump-sum additions to family assets are excluded from income and treated as ASSETS. Periodic payments are income. Delayed periodic payments have their own treatment.\n6. MISSING CHILD SUPPORT OR ALIMONY. Amounts actually received or reliably anticipated are counted. A court-ordered amount that is documented as unpaid is not counted merely because it was ordered — document the verification of what is actually received.\n7. COUNTING A LIVE-IN AIDE'S INCOME. Excluded entirely.\n8. NETTING A BUSINESS LOSS AGAINST OTHER INCOME. Not permitted; count business income as zero.\n9. FORGETTING THE FULL-TIME STUDENT EARNED INCOME RULE. The earned income of a dependent full-time student 18 or over is counted only up to a threshold amount; the excess is excluded. (Under HOTMA the applicable figure is indexed — the CY2026 published amount is $500.)\n10. TREATING REIMBURSEMENTS AS INCOME. Amounts received specifically to reimburse medical expenses are excluded — and the same expenses, if unreimbursed, are what the medical deduction is built on. Do not count the reimbursement AND deduct the expense.\n\nWorked example 1 — the overtime pattern, decided correctly. A maintenance worker's last 26 pay stubs show base pay of $1,120 biweekly plus overtime that appears in 19 of 26 periods, averaging $186 when it appears.\n  Naive approach A: count base only — $29,120. Understates.\n  Naive approach B: count the highest-overtime stub annualized — overstates.\n  Defensible approach: total gross across the documented period and annualize, or compute base ($1,120 × 26 = $29,120) plus documented average overtime (19 × $186 = $3,534) = $32,654, documenting the method and the pay periods used.\n  Then ask the employer whether the overtime pattern is expected to continue; if the employer states it is ending, document that and project accordingly.\n\nWorked example 2 — child support that is ordered and not paid. A court order requires $450 per month. The tenant states she has received nothing in fourteen months.\n  Do not count $5,400 because it was ordered.\n  Do document: the order, the tenant's certification of non-receipt, and verification from the state disbursement unit or child support agency where obtainable — that record is usually available and is the strongest evidence.\n  If partial payments are received irregularly, count the documented actual pattern and note the method.\n  Re-examine at each certification, because collection status changes.\n\nWorked example 3 — the student earned income threshold. A household includes a 19-year-old dependent who is a full-time student and earns $9,400.\n  Under 4350.3 as written, only the amount up to the applicable threshold is counted and the excess is excluded, because he is a DEPENDENT and a FULL-TIME STUDENT.\n  Change one fact: he is not a full-time student. Now his entire $9,400 is counted as an adult household member's income, and he is not a dependent for the deduction either.\n  Change another: he is a full-time student but is the HEAD of household. The dependent-student rule does not apply to a head, spouse or co-head — count in full.\n  Three facts, three different answers, and the worksheet should ask all three questions explicitly.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: income that starts mid-year. A tenant will begin a job on October 15; the certification is effective September 1. Annual income anticipates the next twelve months, so the anticipated wages from October 15 forward are part of the projection for that period. Compute the partial-year amount for the certification period and document the method — or, where the property's policy and the facts make it more accurate, complete the certification on current income and process an interim when the job begins, consistent with the written policy and the interim rules. Either approach can be defensible; an undocumented choice is not.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case that requires a decision, not avoidance. A tenant receives irregular cash payments from a relative outside the household: $200 in January, nothing until June, $500 in June, $150 in September. Regular contributions from persons not residing in the unit are income; genuinely sporadic gifts are not. Here the pattern is irregular in both timing and amount. The defensible treatment documents the facts, states the conclusion and the reasoning, and — critically — applies the same reasoning at every household in the property. What produces findings is not the judgment; it is the absence of one, or its inconsistent application.\n\nWorked example 6 — the intake diagnostic. Pull five files with varied household types (a wage household, an elderly fixed-income household, a self-employed household, a household with a minor receiving SSI, and a household with a full-time student). For each line of income on the 50059, ask: what document supports it, what multiplier was used, and which inclusion or exclusion provision was applied. In under two hours you will know which of the ten errors above the property is making, and the answer is almost never \"none.\""}]},{"id":"m102","unit":"u10","number":2,"title":"Assets — Definition, Calculation, and HOTMA Changes","subtitle":"","sections":[{"heading":"What Counts as a Household Asset","body":"Assets are items of value a family owns. They matter for two reasons: the INCOME they produce (actual or imputed) is part of annual income, and — once HOTMA is implemented — the total value can make a household ineligible.\n\nThe key measure is CASH VALUE: the market value of the asset MINUS reasonable costs that would be incurred in converting it to cash. Not market value; not what the tenant paid; not the balance on a statement if converting it would cost something.\n\nWHAT IS AN ASSET:\n• Cash held in checking and savings accounts, certificates of deposit, and money market accounts.\n• Stocks, bonds, mutual funds, Treasury bills and similar securities.\n• Equity in real property or other capital investments — market value minus the outstanding debt against it minus reasonable costs of sale.\n• Revocable trusts, and amounts the family can access from a trust.\n• IRAs, Keogh accounts, and similar retirement accounts, under the 4350.3 framework, at the amount the family can access.\n• Lump-sum receipts that were excluded from income — inheritances, insurance settlements, capital gains, lottery winnings paid in a lump sum.\n• Personal property held as an INVESTMENT — coin or stamp collections, art, gems, antique cars held for investment value.\n• Cash value of whole life insurance policies (not term policies, which have none).\n• Assets DISPOSED OF for less than fair market value within the past two years (Section 4).\n\nWHAT IS NOT AN ASSET:\n• Necessary personal property — furniture, clothing, a car used for transportation, ordinary household goods.\n• The value of an interest in Indian trust land.\n• Term life insurance policies (no cash value).\n• Assets that are part of an active business (the business's net income is counted instead).\n• Assets not accessible to the family and which produce no income — the classic example being a genuinely irrevocable trust the family cannot reach.\n• Equity in property for which the family does not have the legal right to sell or otherwise convert to cash.\n\nWorked example 1 — cash value versus market value, worked. A tenant owns a rental house.\n  Market value: $184,000\n  Outstanding mortgage: $121,000\n  Estimated costs of sale (broker commission and closing costs at roughly 8 percent): $14,720\n  CASH VALUE: $184,000 − $121,000 − $14,720 = $48,280.\n  The asset income is separate: net rental income (rents minus operating expenses, mortgage interest, taxes and insurance — not principal) is counted as actual income from the asset.\n  The error to avoid: using $184,000 as the asset value. That overstates the asset by $135,720 and, in a HOTMA world, could push a household over the eligibility ceiling that it does not actually exceed.\n\nWorked example 2 — the retirement account under the current framework. A 58-year-old tenant has $61,000 in a 401(k). Under 4350.3 as written, what counts is the amount the family CAN ACCESS. If withdrawal is permitted subject to a 10 percent penalty and taxes, the accessible amount net of the penalty is the cash value analysis to document. If the plan genuinely does not permit withdrawal while employed, the family cannot access it and the analysis differs. What the file must show is the plan's actual terms — not an assumption. (Under HOTMA, retirement accounts are excluded from net family assets, which is a substantial change; confirm which regime governs the effective date you are certifying.)\n\nWorked example 3 — the whole life policy. A tenant has a whole life policy with a $50,000 death benefit and a $7,400 cash surrender value, less a $600 surrender charge.\n  Asset cash value: $6,800.\n  The death benefit is NOT the asset — the family cannot access $50,000.\n  A term policy in the same household has no cash value and is not an asset at all.\n  This is a small but frequent error, and it runs in the direction of overstating assets and overcharging tenants.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: the car. A tenant owns a 2019 sedan worth $14,000 used to get to work. NOT an asset — necessary personal property. The same tenant also owns a 1968 muscle car worth $46,000 that is stored and driven twice a year. That IS an asset — personal property held as an investment. The distinguishing question is not the value but the purpose. Document the determination and the basis for it, because it will be questioned. (Note that under HOTMA, non-necessary personal property is included only above an indexed threshold — $52,787 for CY2026 — which changes this analysis substantially on implementation.)\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: joint accounts. A tenant is a joint owner on her elderly father's checking account with a $32,000 balance; her father lives elsewhere and the arrangement exists so she can pay his bills. Is it the family's asset? The analysis is ACCESS AND OWNERSHIP: legally she can withdraw the funds, which argues for inclusion; practically the funds are her father's, which argues against. The defensible file documents the account terms, the source of the funds, the family's certification about ownership and use, and the property's determination with its reasoning. Reviewers do not second-guess documented reasoning here; they cite blank spaces. If the amount is large enough to change eligibility under HOTMA, get the determination in writing from the Contract Administrator.\n\nWorked example 6 — the asset nobody asks about. A household certifies \"no assets\" for six consecutive years while receiving direct-deposited Social Security. Every direct deposit implies an account. The verification process must ask about accounts specifically, and a certification of zero assets in a household with direct deposit should prompt a follow-up. Reviewers spot this pattern quickly, and it reads as a verification process that accepts whatever the tenant says."},{"heading":"The $5,000 Rule and Passbook Rate Imputation","body":"Under Handbook 4350.3 as written — the framework governing most Multifamily properties until the HOTMA compliance date of January 1, 2027 — asset income is computed with a two-branch test:\n\n• If total CASH VALUE of all family assets is $5,000 OR LESS: count the ACTUAL income earned from the assets. No imputation.\n• If total cash value EXCEEDS $5,000: count the GREATER of (a) actual income earned, or (b) IMPUTED income, computed as total cash value × HUD's PASSBOOK SAVINGS RATE.\n\nNote carefully: the threshold test uses TOTAL cash value of ALL assets combined, not asset by asset. And when imputation applies, it is applied to the TOTAL, not just the excess over $5,000.\n\nA note on the rate. HUD publishes a passbook savings rate; for CY2026 the published rate is 0.40 percent. The worked examples in this track use a 2 percent convention for arithmetic clarity and for consistency with the HUD Occupancy track's examples. In live work, use the rate HUD has published for the certification's effective-date year, and confirm which regime — 4350.3 or HOTMA — governs that date. The MECHANICS below do not change with the rate; only the multiplier does.\n\nWorked example 1 — below the threshold. A family holds a checking account with an average balance of $1,750 earning $18 in annual interest, and no other assets.\n  Total cash value $1,750 ≤ $5,000 → count ACTUAL income: $18.\n  No imputation. An owner who imputed anyway would count $35 at the 2 percent convention — wrong in the family's favor here, but wrong nonetheless, and wrong in a way that will be inconsistent across the property.\n\nWorked example 2 — above the threshold, imputed wins. A family holds $9,455 in assets earning $150 in actual income.\n  Cash value $9,455 > $5,000 → compare.\n  Actual: $150.00. Imputed: $9,455 × 0.02 = $189.10.\n  Count $189.10 — the greater.\n\nWorked example 3 — above the threshold, ACTUAL wins. A family holds $42,000 in a CD ladder earning $1,470 annually.\n  Actual: $1,470.00. Imputed: $42,000 × 0.02 = $840.00.\n  Count $1,470.00 — the greater. The comparison runs both ways, and staff who assume imputation always applies above the threshold understate income for high-yield holdings.\n\nWorked example 4 — the cliff, and why reviewers test it. A family holds $4,980 earning $12.\n  Count $12 — below the threshold.\n  The family deposits $30. Now $5,010 > $5,000.\n  Actual: $12.00. Imputed: $5,010 × 0.02 = $100.20. Count $100.20.\n  A $30 deposit changed counted asset income from $12 to $100.20, a difference of $88.20 annually and roughly $2.21 per month in TTP. The cliff is easy to state and easy to miss, which is exactly why it appears on reviews.\n\nWorked example 5 — the non-interest-bearing account. A family holds $12,300 in a checking account that pays no interest, plus $1,000 in cash at home.\n  Total cash value $13,300 > $5,000.\n  Actual income: $0.\n  Imputed: $13,300 × 0.02 = $266.00. Count $266.00.\n  This is the case owners most often miss, because there is no statement line showing income. A zero-yield balance still generates imputed income once the family is above the threshold.\n\nWorked example 6 — the boundary case: which balance to use. A tenant's checking account fluctuates between $400 and $3,100 across the month. Use the AVERAGE balance over a representative period (commonly six months of statements) for checking accounts, and the CURRENT balance for savings-type accounts, consistent with the property's written method — and apply the method consistently. Say the family's average checking balance is $1,040 and its savings balance is $4,300. Total is $5,340, which EXCEEDS $5,000, and imputation applies: $106.80 at the 2 percent convention. Using the day-of balance of $1,470 instead of the average would give $5,770 and $115.40 — the same branch, a different number, and a finding if the method is not documented.\n\nWorked example 7 — the ambiguous case: assets held by a minor. A household's 12-year-old has a $6,800 custodial account funded by a grandparent. The child is a household member, and the asset belongs to the child. Include it in total family assets and apply the test to the household total. Note the interaction with income rules: the child's EARNED income is excluded, but ASSET income (actual or imputed) attributable to the household is counted — these are different rules and staff frequently conflate them.\n\nWorked example 8 — what changes under HOTMA. The imputation threshold rises to the indexed figure ($52,787 for CY2026), meaning the great majority of households will have NO imputed asset income; families at or below the threshold may DECLARE assets and SELF-CERTIFY the income; and a new eligibility CEILING appears at the indexed $105,574 (CY2026) plus the real property restriction. When you audit a file, the first question is which regime governed its effective date, and the second is whether the property applied that regime consistently."},{"heading":"Verifying Assets — Before and After HOTMA","body":"Asset verification follows the same hierarchy as income verification: highest level available, with documentation of why a lower level was used. What differs is that assets are easier to under-verify, because a family that does not mention an account creates no paper trail at all.\n\nUNDER 4350.3 AS WRITTEN:\n• Third-party verification of account balances and asset income — verification forms sent to financial institutions, or third-party documents provided by the family such as statements.\n• For real property: documentation of market value (assessment, appraisal, or a documented market analysis), the mortgage balance, and an estimate of the costs of sale.\n• Six months of statements is the common convention for establishing an average checking balance; a current statement for savings-type accounts.\n• A family's certification of assets is used where higher-level verification is unavailable, WITH the attempts documented.\n• The family certifies to ALL assets, including any disposed of for less than fair market value within the past two years.\n\nUNDER HOTMA (Multifamily compliance date January 1, 2027):\n• Where net family assets are at or below the indexed threshold ($52,787 for CY2026), the family may DECLARE the value and SELF-CERTIFY the anticipated income — no third-party verification required.\n• That self-certification may be relied on for three years, with FULL third-party verification at least every third year.\n• Above the threshold, third-party verification is required.\n• The ELIGIBILITY CEILING and the REAL PROPERTY restriction must be tested at admission, which requires questions the current application may not ask.\n\nWorked example 1 — the verification that was never attempted. A file contains a family certification that assets total $3,200, with no bank statements and no verification requests. The certification may be accurate. The finding is written because the hierarchy was not applied and no attempt was documented. Control: the certification checklist requires either third-party documentation or a documented attempt plus the reason for accepting a lower level.\n\nWorked example 2 — the statement that reveals another asset. A verification of a savings account arrives showing the balance requested — and, on the same statement, a transfer of $9,000 to an account at another institution. The property cannot ignore what it has been handed. The correct step: ask about the second account, obtain verification, and document the inquiry. Ignoring visible evidence of an unreported asset in a document the property itself obtained is the least defensible posture available.\n\nWorked example 3 — the real property valuation done properly. A household reports owning a parcel of land inherited from a parent.\n  Obtain: the county assessment (with a note about the relationship between assessed and market value in that jurisdiction), any recent appraisal, evidence of any mortgage or lien, and an estimate of costs of sale.\n  Compute cash value: market value minus debt minus costs of sale.\n  Determine income: is the land rented, farmed, or producing anything? If not, actual income is zero, and the cash value goes into the imputation test.\n  Document the valuation method. Two properties valuing the same parcel differently is a defensible outcome; a valuation with no stated method is not.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: the account the family says is closed. A family reports closing a savings account that held $18,000 six months ago and states the funds were spent on a family member's medical care. Two threads: verify the closure and, more importantly, determine whether this is a DISPOSAL of assets for less than fair market value (Section 4) — spending money on legitimate expenses is not a disposal, but gifting or transferring it is. Document the disposition, obtain what evidence exists, and record the determination. This is a fact pattern with an innocent explanation most of the time and a rule that requires you to ask anyway.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: HOTMA self-certification with contrary information. Under HOTMA a family may self-certify assets below the threshold — but if the owner has information CONTRARY to the certification, accepting it uncritically is not defensible. Suppose a family self-certifies $6,000 in assets while the EIV income report shows substantial interest and dividend income, or the file contains a recent statement showing $80,000. The self-certification option does not require the owner to ignore what it knows. Document the conflict, resolve it, and note the resolution. Reviewers will look specifically for whether the self-certification option was applied as a shortcut around known facts.\n\nWorked example 6 — the transition control every client will need. Beginning with certifications effective on or after the compliance date, the asset questions on the application and the recertification packet must change: the ceiling test, the real property questions with their exception categories, the self-certification declaration, and the three-year full-verification tracker. Build the tracker as a simple field on the household record — \"last full asset verification: [date]\" — because three years from implementation, nobody will remember which households are due, and a missed third-year verification is a clean, easily written finding."},{"heading":"Disposal of Assets Below Fair Market Value","body":"A family that gives away or sells an asset for less than its fair market value cannot thereby reduce its counted assets. Under 4350.3 as written, assets DISPOSED OF FOR LESS THAN FAIR MARKET VALUE within the TWO YEARS preceding the certification are counted as if the family still held them — specifically, the DIFFERENCE between the cash value the asset would have had and the amount actually received is included in total family assets.\n\nThe mechanics:\n• The look-back is two years from the certification's effective date.\n• The amount included is the DIFFERENCE (imputed asset value), not the whole asset.\n• Disposals in the normal course of business, foreclosures, bankruptcy, separation or divorce settlements where the family receives important consideration not measurable in dollars, and assets disposed of as a result of certain circumstances are treated differently — the rule targets transfers made to reduce countable assets, not every transaction.\n• There is a threshold below which the rule is not applied, and the family certifies at each certification whether any such disposal occurred.\n• The imputed amount rolls OFF once the two years have passed.\n\nWorked example 1 — the classic case, worked. Fourteen months before the certification's effective date, a tenant transferred a $28,000 certificate of deposit to her adult daughter for $1.\n  Cash value at disposal: $28,000. Amount received: $1.\n  Included in family assets: $27,999.\n  If the family's other assets total $2,600, total assets are $30,599, which exceeds $5,000, and imputation applies to the TOTAL — $611.98 at the 2 percent convention.\n  This continues until the disposal is more than two years old, at which point the $27,999 drops out and total assets return to $2,600 (below the threshold, actual income only).\n  Set a calendar note in the file for the roll-off date. Properties that forget continue counting the disposal indefinitely and overcharge the household — an error that harms the tenant and that reviewers cite.\n\nWorked example 2 — the disposal that is not a disposal. A tenant sold her car for $6,200, which was within the range of its market value, and spent the proceeds on rent, medical bills and a funeral.\n  This is a sale at fair market value, not a disposal below it. Nothing is imputed for the transfer.\n  Spending money is not disposing of assets below fair market value — the family simply has fewer assets now, which is a legitimate change in circumstances.\n  The file should document the sale, the amount received, and the disposition of the proceeds, because a $6,200 swing invites the question.\n\nWorked example 3 — the partial disposal. A tenant sold a parcel of land worth $40,000 to a cousin for $22,000.\n  Difference: $18,000, included in family assets for two years.\n  Note that this is the case people miss, because a sale for real money looks legitimate. The test is fair market value, not whether money changed hands.\n  Documentation: the deed, the sale price, and the basis for the market value determination.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: the divorce settlement. A tenant transferred her interest in a jointly owned home to her former spouse as part of a divorce settlement, receiving no cash. Was this a disposal below fair market value? Generally, transfers as part of a separation or divorce settlement where the family receives important consideration not measurable in dollars are treated differently from a gift. Document the settlement agreement, the consideration, and the determination. This is a case where a reflexive application of the two-year rule produces a wrong and quite painful result for the household.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: the transfer that predates everything. An applicant discloses at admission that she transferred $60,000 to her son three years ago. Three years exceeds the two-year look-back, so nothing is imputed. But note two things. First, verify the DATE, because a transfer 22 months ago and one 26 months ago are treated differently and the family's recollection may be imprecise. Second, under HOTMA, the household would also face the asset ceiling test at admission — which the disposed-of asset does not affect once outside the look-back, but which the family's REMAINING assets certainly do.\n\nWorked example 6 — the control that makes this manageable. Include an explicit, dated question at every certification: \"In the past two years, has any household member given away, sold for less than its value, or transferred any asset?\" with a signature. Then, when a disposal is disclosed, record it on a schedule with the date, the asset, the value, the amount received, the imputed difference, and the ROLL-OFF DATE. That schedule is a two-column addition to the file that prevents both the failure to count and the failure to stop counting."},{"heading":"Real Property as an Asset — Post-HOTMA Treatment","body":"Real property is the highest-stakes asset category, because it involves the largest dollar amounts and — after HOTMA implementation — because owning it can make a household INELIGIBLE rather than merely raising its rent.\n\nUNDER 4350.3 AS WRITTEN. Real property is an asset valued at CASH VALUE — market value minus outstanding debt minus reasonable costs of conversion. Income from the property is the NET income it produces (rents minus operating expenses, taxes, insurance and mortgage INTEREST; principal payments are not an expense). If the property produces no income, cash value still feeds the imputation test.\n\nUNDER HOTMA. Two changes matter.\n• The ASSET CEILING: net family assets exceeding the indexed limit ($105,574 for CY2026) generally make a family ineligible, subject to exclusions and the continued-occupancy grace period (up to 6 months for current participants under a written, uniformly applied policy).\n• The REAL PROPERTY RESTRICTION: a family that OWNS REAL PROPERTY SUITABLE FOR OCCUPANCY as a residence is generally ineligible — subject to exceptions that do most of the work in practice. The exception categories include property actively offered for sale; property that does not meet the disability-related needs of household members; property owned by a household that includes a victim of domestic violence, dating violence, sexual assault or stalking; and property co-owned with a person who is not a household member and who resides there.\n\nWorked example 1 — cash value with an underwater property. A tenant owns a house worth $142,000 with a $155,000 mortgage.\n  Cash value: $142,000 − $155,000 = negative → treat as ZERO. Assets cannot be negative, and a negative value does not offset other assets.\n  Income: if it is rented for $1,100 per month with $1,240 in monthly expenses including interest, taxes and insurance, net income is negative; count zero, and do not offset other income.\n  Under HOTMA the REAL PROPERTY prong is still in play regardless of equity — the question is whether the family owns property suitable for occupancy, not whether it has equity in it. This is the trap: a household with zero countable asset VALUE can still be ineligible under the ownership prong unless an exception applies.\n\nWorked example 2 — the inherited house, three ways.\n  (a) The family inherited a house and is actively marketing it through a broker at market price. The \"actively offered for sale\" exception applies. File: listing agreement, marketing evidence, follow-up at the next certification.\n  (b) The family inherited a two-story house with no accessible entrance, and a household member uses a wheelchair. The disability-needs exception is the relevant one. File: verification of the need, documentation of the property's characteristics, and the determination.\n  (c) The family inherited a one-third interest in a house occupied by a sibling who is not a household member. The co-ownership exception is the relevant one. File: the deed, evidence of the sibling's residence, and the determination.\n  Same underlying fact — the family owns real property — and three different exceptions with three different evidence packages. Do not build a single \"real property\" form; build an exception checklist.\n\nWorked example 3 — the rental property that produces income, computed fully. A household owns a duplex.\n  Market value $210,000; mortgage balance $96,000; costs of sale at 8 percent = $16,800.\n  Cash value: $210,000 − $96,000 − $16,800 = $97,200.\n  Annual rents received: $19,200. Operating expenses: $4,100. Property taxes: $2,900. Insurance: $1,450. Mortgage INTEREST: $4,700. Mortgage PRINCIPAL: $2,600 (not deductible).\n  Net income counted: $19,200 − $4,100 − $2,900 − $1,450 − $4,700 = $6,050.\n  Under 4350.3, compare actual ($6,050) to imputed ($97,200 × 0.02 = $1,944) and count the greater: $6,050.\n  Under HOTMA, the household's countable net family assets include the $97,200; combined with other assets it must be tested against the ceiling, AND the real property prong must be analyzed with its exceptions.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: property the family cannot sell. A tenant holds a remainder interest in a house occupied by a life tenant, or holds property subject to a legal restriction preventing sale. Equity in property for which the family does not have the legal right to sell or convert to cash is generally not counted as an asset. Document the instrument creating the restriction. Under HOTMA, the ownership prong requires its own analysis — the question there is whether the family owns property suitable for occupancy, and a remainder interest in a house someone else occupies for life is a genuinely difficult case that should be documented and, where the stakes are high, put to the Contract Administrator in writing.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case with real human stakes. A long-term elderly tenant inherits her late brother's house in another state. It is habitable. She cannot afford its taxes and upkeep, does not want to move, and has begun the process of listing it but has not signed a listing agreement. Under HOTMA she is at risk on the ownership prong. The right consulting sequence: explain the rule and the exceptions clearly and immediately; help her document the steps toward listing so the \"actively offered for sale\" exception can be established; apply the property's written grace period policy for current participants; notify her in writing of the timeline and the consequence; and document every step. What creates liability is silence — a household that learns of the consequence only when the termination notice arrives has a legitimate grievance about process even where the substantive rule is clear.\n\nWorked example 6 — the practice point. Real property questions must be on the application and on every recertification form after implementation, phrased to elicit ownership of ANY real property interest anywhere, including inherited and co-owned interests. Most current application forms ask about \"income from real estate,\" which does not surface a vacant inherited parcel producing nothing. Rewriting that one question is a small deliverable with disproportionate value, and it belongs in the HOTMA transition package from Chapter 10."}]},{"id":"m103","unit":"u10","number":3,"title":"EIV — Mandatory Use, Required Reports, and Security Compliance","subtitle":"","sections":[{"heading":"EIV Overview — What It Is and Why It Is Mandatory","body":"The Enterprise Income Verification system is HUD's web-based system providing employment and income data drawn from federal sources — principally wage and unemployment data reported to the National Directory of New Hires, and Social Security and SSI benefit data from the Social Security Administration.\n\nUSE IS MANDATORY. 24 CFR 5.233 requires processing entities to use HUD's EIV system IN ITS ENTIRETY as a third-party source to verify tenant employment and income information during annual and streamlined reexaminations of family composition and income, and to reduce administrative and subsidy payment errors. The operational requirements are in Handbook 4350.3 REV-1 Chapter 9 and the EIV housing notices. Failure to use EIV in its entirety may result in sanctions and the assessment of disallowed costs associated with any resulting incorrect subsidy or tenant rent calculations, or both.\n\nTwo words in that regulation carry the weight: IN ITS ENTIRETY. It is not enough to print an income report for each household at recertification. The system's monitoring reports must be run, reviewed, acted on and retained. Most EIV findings on MORs are not about income verification at all — they are about the monitoring reports and the security requirements.\n\nWhere EIV sits in the verification process:\n• It is the FIRST step and the CROSS-CHECK, not the whole verification. It does not cover self-employment, most asset income, informal income, child support paid directly between parties, or household composition; it can lag; and it must be reconciled against other verification rather than substituted for it.\n• EIV data is used to identify DISCREPANCIES that must be investigated and resolved in writing.\n• EIV must be used at admission through the EXISTING TENANT SEARCH, before move-in.\n\nWHAT EIV IS NOT: it is not a substitute for tenant interviews, not a basis for taking adverse action without giving the tenant an opportunity to contest the information, and not a source of data that may be shared outside the authorized purposes.\n\nWorked example 1 — the property that \"uses EIV\" and fails anyway. A 140-unit property prints an EIV income report for every household at each annual recertification, files it, and does nothing else. No monthly or quarterly monitoring reports, no master file, no user access audit, no discrepancy resolution memos, no Existing Tenant Searches. The property genuinely believes it is compliant. The MOR produces a cluster of EIV findings, none of which concern the income reports it has been dutifully printing. Teach the distinction explicitly at intake: EIV compliance has THREE legs — verification use, monitoring reports, and security administration — and most properties do the first and neglect the other two.\n\nWorked example 2 — the timing that matters. A property pulls the EIV income report on the day it completes the certification, months after the interview. If EIV shows an employer the tenant did not disclose, the property now has to reopen the verification, potentially delaying the certification past its effective date. Pull EIV EARLY — at the start of the certification process, alongside the notice cycle — so any discrepancy has time to be resolved before the effective date. This single sequencing change eliminates a large share of late certifications at properties that are otherwise organized.\n\nWorked example 3 — the boundary case: data that is not yet available. A household admitted in April has no EIV income data in June because the certification has not been in the system long enough for the data match to populate. This is normal system behavior, not noncompliance — but the file must contain a dated note stating why the report is absent and when it will be obtained. Undocumented absence looks identical to a failure to use EIV.\n\nWorked example 4 — the ambiguous case: EIV shows an employer the tenant denies. EIV reports an employer with wages of $18,400; the tenant says she has never worked there and suspects identity theft. Do NOT take adverse action on EIV data alone. The process: give the tenant the opportunity to contest, document her statement, attempt verification directly with the reported employer, advise her of the process for correcting federal records if the data is wrong, and document each step and its outcome. Complete the certification on the best-verified information with the analysis in writing. Then set a follow-up date. A file showing a documented, reasoned process is compliant even if the underlying facts stay murky.\n\nWorked example 5 — the consequence clients understand. A property with systemic EIV deficiencies faces more than a rating problem: disallowed costs for incorrect subsidy calculations, repayment obligations, and — because EIV carries federal privacy obligations — security findings with a different character than ordinary paperwork deficiencies. When scoping an engagement, price the EIV workstream separately and treat it as the highest-yield area per hour, because it is both heavily weighted and quickly fixable."},{"heading":"The Required EIV Reports — Timing, Use, and Retention","body":"EIV's monitoring reports are where most findings live. Practice on frequency varies somewhat by guidance version and by Contract Administrator expectations, so build the client's calendar from the current handbook chapter and notices — but the report set and its purposes are stable.\n\nTHE REPORTS AND WHAT EACH IS FOR:\n\n• INCOME REPORT — the household-level report showing wages, unemployment and Social Security/SSI data. Used at each annual recertification (and at interims per policy) as part of verification. Retained in the TENANT FILE.\n• INCOME DISCREPANCY REPORT — flags differences between income reported on the certification and income in federal data. Each flagged household must be investigated and the resolution documented in writing. Retained in the TENANT FILE.\n• EXISTING TENANT SEARCH — run for each applicant BEFORE move-in, to identify whether the applicant is already receiving assistance elsewhere. Results retained with the admission documentation.\n• NEW HIRES REPORT — identifies household members with new employment reported to the National Directory of New Hires, providing a list of households with new hire information in the period following the report date. Used to catch employment the household has not reported. Retained in the MASTER FILE.\n• MULTIPLE SUBSIDY REPORT — identifies households appearing to receive assistance at more than one location. Retained in the MASTER FILE.\n• DECEASED TENANTS REPORT — identifies household members reported deceased. Retained in the MASTER FILE.\n• IDENTITY VERIFICATION REPORTS (FAILED EIV PRE-SCREENING and FAILED VERIFICATION) — identify household members whose identity data did not verify against SSA records, which prevents income data from being returned. Commonly run monthly. Retained in the MASTER FILE.\n• NO INCOME REPORTED REPORT — identifies households for whom no income is reported in federal data, used alongside zero-income monitoring. Retained in the TENANT FILE.\n\nRETENTION — the distinction that produces findings. Master-file reports (New Hires summary, Multiple Subsidy, Deceased Tenants, identity verification reports) are retained for three years. Tenant-file reports (Income, Income Discrepancy, No Income Reported) are retained in the tenant file for the TERM OF TENANCY PLUS THREE YEARS. Reports must be destroyed securely when their retention ends.\n\nWorked example 1 — building the calendar. For a 160-unit property, a workable EIV calendar looks like:\n  MONTHLY: identity verification reports (failed pre-screening / failed verification), reviewed and corrected; a printed log entry signed by whoever ran them, filed in the master file.\n  QUARTERLY: New Hires, Multiple Subsidy, Deceased Tenants, and the zero-income cross-check against No Income Reported; each with a log entry and documentation of any follow-up.\n  AT EACH CERTIFICATION: the household Income Report and the Income Discrepancy Report, with a written resolution for any discrepancy.\n  AT EACH APPLICATION, PRE-MOVE-IN: the Existing Tenant Search.\n  ANNUALLY: user access audit and security awareness training.\n  Confirm the frequencies against the current handbook chapter and any CA-specific expectations, and put them in the property's written EIV procedures.\n\nWorked example 2 — the failed identity verification that hides income. A household member's name was entered as \"Roberto Nunez-Vega\" while SSA records show \"Roberto Nunez Vega.\" The identity verification fails, so NO income data returns for that member. The property's income report looks clean — because the system returned nothing, not because the member has no income. Staff read the blank as \"no wages.\" Six months later the member's wages surface another way and there is an eighteen-month underpayment. This is why the identity verification reports are run monthly and corrected immediately: an unverified member is an invisible member.\n\nWorked example 3 — the New Hires report used properly. The quarterly New Hires report shows a new employer for a household member. Sequence: verify the employment, determine the anticipated income, apply the interim recertification rules and the property's written policy, process any required interim with proper notice for an increase, and document the whole chain from report to certification. Now the counter-example: a property that runs the report, sees the entry, and waits until the annual. If the annual is nine months away, the household underpays for nine months and the property has a written record showing it knew. Running the report and not acting is worse than not running it.\n\nWorked example 4 — the Multiple Subsidy hit. The report shows a household member also appearing at a property 300 miles away. Investigate before acting: it may be a data artifact, a member who moved out and was never terminated at the other property, or an actual double subsidy. Contact the other property, document the inquiry and the response, determine the correct facts, and resolve — which may mean a termination and a repayment at one location. Document everything, because a double-subsidy resolution can become contentious and the file is the record.\n\nWorked example 5 — the boundary case: the Deceased Tenants report. The report lists a household member who died four months ago; the property was never notified. Two immediate items: correct the certification and the assistance from the appropriate date, and examine WHY the property did not know. If the deceased member was the sole household member, assistance has been paid on an unoccupied unit — a repayment. If a remaining household member continued in occupancy without reporting the death, the composition change was unreported and a recomputation is required. Either way the report did its job and the property's own process did not.\n\nWorked example 6 — the ambiguous case: which reports must a property run when a CA expects more than the handbook states? Some Contract Administrators expect specific frequencies or additional documentation. Where the CA's expectation exceeds the handbook, meeting it is generally the practical course — and the correct response is to write the CA's expectation into the property's own EIV procedures, so that practice, policy and expectation align. What produces findings is running reports at a frequency nobody wrote down and nobody can prove."},{"heading":"EIV Income Discrepancy Resolution — The Process","body":"The Income Discrepancy Report compares income reported on the household's certification to income in federal data and flags differences beyond a tolerance. A flag is not a finding of fraud, an accusation, or a basis for action by itself. It is a question the file must answer.\n\nTHE PROCESS, in order:\n\n1. REVIEW the report promptly after each certification — many properties operate on a review within 30 days of certification, which is a defensible standard to write into the procedures.\n2. ANALYZE the discrepancy before contacting anyone. Most flags have innocent explanations: a job that ended before the certification period, a job that started after, a different pay period covered, a one-time bonus, a member whose identity verification failed, or a timing lag in the federal data.\n3. If analysis does not resolve it, VERIFY — contact the employer or agency, obtain documentation, and interview the tenant.\n4. DETERMINE the correct income for the certification period.\n5. If the certification was wrong, CORRECT it: corrected 50059, transmitted, with the resulting retroactive adjustment computed.\n6. If a tenant-side underpayment resulted, establish a REPAYMENT AGREEMENT on affordable terms, documented.\n7. WRITE THE RESOLUTION MEMO: what the discrepancy was, what was reviewed, whom you contacted and when, what was determined, and what action followed — dated and initialed, filed with the report in the tenant file.\n\nTHE RULE THAT PRODUCES THE MOST FINDINGS: the memo is the deliverable. A correctly resolved discrepancy with no memo scores the same as an unresolved one.\n\nWorked example 1 — the innocent explanation, documented. The report flags a household with $7,800 more in federal wage data than the certification shows. Investigation: the wages are from an employer the tenant left in February; the certification effective June 1 correctly projected only the new job's income. Resolution memo: \"Discrepancy of $7,800 relates to employment at [employer] which ended 2/14. Separation confirmed by employer contact 6/18 with [name/title]. Certification correctly reflects only current employment at [employer], verified by third-party written verification dated 5/22. No correction required.\" Dated, initialed, filed. This takes four minutes and closes the item permanently.\n\nWorked example 2 — the real discrepancy, worked to the dollar. The report flags a household. Investigation shows a second job the tenant did not report, beginning eight months before the certification, paying $1,150 per month.\n  Annual income understated by $13,800.\n  Adjusted income understated by the same amount; TTP understated by roughly $345 per month.\n  The error existed for the eight months since the certification: $2,760 in tenant underpayment and $2,760 in HAP overpayment.\n  Actions: corrected certification transmitted; HAP adjustment absorbed on a future voucher; repayment agreement with the household, tested for affordability (rent plus repayment generally not exceeding roughly 40 percent of monthly adjusted income); resolution memo filed; and a review of whether the property's process should have caught it earlier — specifically whether the New Hires report was run and reviewed during those eight months. If it was not, the property has a second finding, and if it was and no action followed, a worse one.\n\nWorked example 3 — the discrepancy that reveals a property error, not a tenant error. The report flags a household because the certification counted $9,048 of a minor's SSI as excluded. The tenant did nothing wrong; the specialist misapplied the minor-income rule. The correction runs in the same mechanical way, but the framing and the corrective action are entirely different: there is no repayment agreement premised on tenant nondisclosure, the household must be notified of a correction it did not cause, and the corrective action is training plus a worksheet change. Reviewers pay attention to whether a property distinguishes its own errors from tenant errors — properties that treat every discrepancy as a tenant problem are visible immediately.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: a discrepancy the tenant contests. The tenant denies the reported income and provides a written statement. The property cannot take adverse action on unverified federal data over the tenant's documented objection without further verification. Contact the reported employer; if the employer confirms, share the basis with the tenant and proceed; if the employer does not confirm, or cannot be reached, document that and complete the certification on the best-verified information, noting the unresolved element and a follow-up date. What is not acceptable is either accepting the tenant's word without inquiry or acting on the data without giving the tenant the opportunity to contest.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: a small discrepancy. The report flags a $410 annual difference. Investigating costs staff time; the rent effect is roughly $10 per month. Some properties adopt a materiality threshold below which they document a brief review and move on. That is defensible ONLY if the threshold is written into the procedures, is applied uniformly, and the brief review is still documented. An undocumented informal practice of ignoring small flags is a finding, and it is also how a genuinely significant discrepancy gets missed because it happened to look small at first glance.\n\nWorked example 6 — the audit routine. Pull the last four quarters of Income Discrepancy Reports and count: how many households were flagged, how many have a written resolution in the tenant file, and how many resolutions actually state a determination rather than merely noting the flag. That ratio is the property's EIV discrepancy compliance rate, and it is one number that predicts an entire category of MOR findings."},{"heading":"EIV Security Requirements and Authorized User Management","body":"EIV carries federal privacy obligations. Its security requirements are not administrative housekeeping — they are the area where a MOR finding most closely resembles a genuine breach of federal information security rules, and they are heavily weighted for that reason.\n\nTHE REQUIREMENTS:\n\n• ROLE-BASED ACCESS. Each user is assigned a role appropriate to their job, and access is limited to the properties and functions they need. A coordinator administers user access for the organization.\n• SIGNED ACCESS AUTHORIZATION AND RULES OF BEHAVIOR. Every user signs the required access request and the Rules of Behavior before receiving access, and those documents are retained.\n• ANNUAL SECURITY AWARENESS TRAINING for every user, documented with dates.\n• IMMEDIATE TERMINATION OF ACCESS on separation, transfer, or change of duties.\n• PERIODIC USER ACCESS RECERTIFICATION — the coordinator reviews the user list and confirms each user's continued need. Build this at least annually; more frequently is better.\n• PHYSICAL AND ELECTRONIC SAFEGUARDS. EIV printouts secured in locked storage; screens not visible to tenants or visitors; no EIV data sent by unencrypted email; no EIV data stored on unsecured shared drives or personal devices.\n• LIMITED DISCLOSURE. EIV data may be used only for authorized purposes and disclosed only to those authorized. It is not shared with other landlords, not given to tenants' family members, and not used for non-program purposes.\n• SECURE DESTRUCTION at the end of the retention period, documented.\n• A written EIV POLICY covering all of the above.\n\nWorked example 1 — the terminated user, and why it is weighted heavily. An occupancy specialist resigns in March. Her EIV access is still active in September. Even if she never logged in, the property has maintained active federal system access for a person with no authorization, for six months. The finding is a security finding. The control is procedural and cheap: EIV access termination joins the offboarding checklist alongside keys, badges and email, with the coordinator's written confirmation retained. Ask for that checklist at every intake.\n\nWorked example 2 — the printout on the desk. During the on-site review, an EIV income report for another household is sitting face-up on the leasing desk in a lobby where tenants sign in. That is an unauthorized disclosure risk observed directly by the reviewer. Controls: EIV printouts leave locked storage only when in active use, are never left unattended, are shredded when no longer needed, and staff use privacy screens. Reviewers do notice this; it is one of the few EIV items visible without opening a file.\n\nWorked example 3 — the email that should not have been sent. A regional manager emails a spreadsheet of EIV income data to a colleague's personal address to work on it over the weekend. This is a security incident regardless of intent. The property's written policy must address transmission explicitly, staff training must cover it, and the incident — if it occurred — should be handled under the organization's incident procedures rather than quietly. A property that discloses and remediates an incident is in a far better position than one where the reviewer discovers the practice is routine.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: shared credentials. A property has one EIV login used by three staff members \"because it's easier.\" This defeats every accountability control in the system: no individual attribution, no ability to terminate one person's access, and a signed Rules of Behavior that means nothing. It is also the kind of practice that turns a single incident into an organizational finding. Each user gets their own credentials, always, with no exceptions for temporary staff.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: the third-party compliance vendor. A property engages an outside firm to process certifications, and the firm wants EIV access. Whether and how a vendor may be granted access depends on the vendor's relationship to the owner/agent and HUD's rules on who may be an authorized user — this is not a question to answer by assumption. Establish the arrangement in writing, follow the coordinator's process for user authorization, ensure the vendor's staff sign the Rules of Behavior and complete the training, and document it all. And if the vendor is NOT an authorized user, the property may not simply email them EIV data instead.\n\nWorked example 6 — the retention-and-destruction gap. A property has EIV master file reports going back nine years in a filing cabinet. Retention for master-file reports is three years; keeping federal income data beyond its retention period without a reason is itself a problem, and destruction must be secure and documented. Build a destruction schedule with a hold flag for pending matters — the same structure as tenant file retention — and record what was destroyed and when.\n\nWorked example 7 — the security self-audit that takes an hour. Print the user list. For each user: still employed? still in a role requiring access? signed access authorization and Rules of Behavior on file? training current within twelve months? Then walk the office: is EIV data in locked storage, are screens shielded, is there a shredder, and is the master file secured? Then read the written EIV policy and ask whether it describes what you just saw. Most properties fail at least two items on that list, and every one of them is fixable the same week."},{"heading":"Building an EIV Compliance System","body":"EIV compliance fails for a predictable reason: the tasks are recurring, distributed, and easy to defer, and nothing breaks visibly when they are skipped. The remedy is a system with named owners, a calendar, and evidence.\n\nTHE COMPONENTS:\n\n1. A WRITTEN EIV POLICY AND PROCEDURE covering: verification use, the report calendar with frequencies, discrepancy resolution steps and timeframes, the Existing Tenant Search as a pre-move-in gate, security requirements, user administration, retention and destruction, and the specific person (by ROLE, not name) responsible for each.\n2. THE REPORT CALENDAR, integrated into the property's operating calendar rather than kept separately.\n3. THE MASTER FILE — organized by report type and period, with a running log showing who ran each report, when, what it showed, and what follow-up occurred.\n4. THE TENANT FILE INTEGRATION — the income report and any discrepancy resolution memo required by the certification checklist before a certification can be closed.\n5. USER ADMINISTRATION RECORDS — the user list, access authorizations, Rules of Behavior, training records, and the periodic access recertification.\n6. THE SELF-AUDIT — quarterly, against the property's own procedures, documented with what was found and fixed.\n\nWorked example 1 — the log that carries the whole system. A single spreadsheet with columns: Report | Period | Date Run | Run By | Items Identified | Action Taken | Date Completed | Verified By. Every report, every period, one row. Print it quarterly, sign it, and file it at the front of the master file. When a reviewer asks \"how do you monitor EIV?\", the answer is one document that proves twelve months of practice. Properties that cannot produce a log describe their process verbally, and a verbal description of a monitoring process is precisely what a finding is written about.\n\nWorked example 2 — the role-based assignment that survives turnover. Do not write \"Maria runs the quarterly reports.\" Write \"the Occupancy Specialist runs the quarterly reports by the 10th of the month following quarter end; the Community Manager verifies and signs the log by the 20th.\" When Maria leaves, the task belongs to a position that still exists. Then test it: have the backup run one quarter's reports.\n\nWorked example 3 — the certification checklist gate. Add two items to the certification checklist that BLOCK completion: \"EIV income report obtained and reviewed — date ___\" and \"Income discrepancy: none / resolved (memo attached) — date ___.\" A certification cannot be closed without them. This converts an easily deferred task into a hard dependency, and it is the single most effective EIV control at the file level.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: a small property with one staff member. At a 24-unit property with a single manager, the \"second person verifies\" control is not available on site. Alternatives: the regional or asset manager performs the verification remotely on a quarterly cycle, the owner's bookkeeper signs the log, or an outside consultant performs a quarterly review. What matters is that SOMEONE other than the person running the reports looks at the log, and that the arrangement is written down. A control that cannot be staffed should be replaced with one that can, not left in the policy as fiction.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: what to do about historical gaps. A property discovers it has no master file reports for the past two years. It cannot recreate history. What it CAN do: run the current reports immediately, begin the log, document the gap and its discovery date in a memo, state the control now in place, and disclose it in the desk review package. Every part of that is better than an empty file with no explanation, and it converts a \"no system\" finding into a \"system installed, gap acknowledged\" finding — a real difference in weight.\n\nWorked example 6 — the consulting engagement. EIV remediation is the most cleanly scopeable engagement in MOR consulting: a written policy, a report calendar, a master file build, a user access audit, a discrepancy resolution backlog cleanup, staff training, and a quarterly review retainer. It is bounded, it is fixable in weeks rather than months, it is heavily weighted on the MOR, and it produces visible artifacts the client can see. For a first engagement with a new client, it is often the best possible starting point — you deliver something concrete quickly and earn the standing to raise the harder problems.\n\nWorked example 7 — the metric to report to the client. Four numbers, quarterly: percentage of certifications closed with an EIV income report and documented discrepancy resolution; percentage of required monitoring reports run and logged on schedule; number of active users versus number of authorized, currently employed, currently trained users; and days elapsed from discrepancy identification to documented resolution. Those four numbers tell an owner more about EIV compliance than any narrative, and a trend line across four quarters is the argument for renewing the retainer."}]},{"id":"m111","unit":"u11","number":1,"title":"Waiting List Management — Rules, AFHMP, and Best Practices","subtitle":"","sections":[{"heading":"HUD Waiting List Requirements","body":"The waiting list is where fair housing compliance is proven or lost. Handbook 4350.3 REV-1 Chapter 4 governs waiting list and tenant selection procedures for the Multifamily assisted portfolio, and 24 CFR Part 5, Subpart F supplies the regulatory framework for Section 8 project-based admissions — including income eligibility and targeting at 24 CFR 5.653 and owner preferences at 24 CFR 5.655.\n\nThe core requirements:\n\n• MAINTAIN A WRITTEN WAITING LIST for the property, in a form that establishes the ORDER of applicants.\n• Record for each applicant: name, DATE AND TIME of application, household size, unit size(s) sought, any preference claimed and its verification status, any need for an accessible unit or accessibility feature, contact information, and current status (active, housed, withdrawn, removed and why).\n• SELECT IN ORDER, subject to unit size matching, approved preferences, income targeting, and accessible unit requirements.\n• DOCUMENT EVERY DEVIATION from strict order — why each applicant was passed over.\n• Apply the property's TENANT SELECTION PLAN, uniformly.\n• RETAIN the list and application records as required, including rejected and withdrawn applications, so that selection decisions can be reconstructed.\n\nINCOME TARGETING. For Section 8 project-based assistance, of the assisted units that become available for occupancy in any fiscal year, NOT LESS THAN 40 PERCENT must be leased to families that are EXTREMELY LOW INCOME at the time of admission (24 CFR 5.653). Properties must track this by fiscal year, not by calendar year and not by feel.\n\nACCESSIBLE UNITS. Accessible units must be offered first to households needing the accessibility features, consistent with Section 504 requirements and the property's own written procedures — which requires a way to identify those households on the list.\n\nWorked example 1 — the list that cannot prove its own order. A property maintains its list in a word processing document, sorted alphabetically, with application dates but no times. Two applicants applied the same day for the same unit size; one was housed. The property cannot demonstrate why. Even where the decision was innocent, the record cannot support it, and an unprovable order is treated as an absent one. Controls: date AND time capture at the moment of application; a system that logs changes; and a preserved snapshot of the list as it stood at each offer.\n\nWorked example 2 — the deviation that is fine, documented, and the same deviation undocumented. A 2-bedroom becomes available. The list, in order, shows:\n  #1 — single applicant, seeking 1-bedroom → passed over: unit size mismatch.\n  #2 — family of four seeking 2-bedroom → OFFERED, declined in writing (moved out of area).\n  #3 — family of three seeking 2-bedroom → unreachable after three documented attempts over ten days.\n  #4 — family of three seeking 2-bedroom → OFFERED and accepted.\n  This is a clean file: the reviewer can reconstruct every step. Now remove the notations. The same events produce a file showing that applicants 1, 2 and 3 were skipped for no stated reason and #4 was housed. Identical conduct; one is compliant and one is a serious finding. The work is not in the decision, it is in the notation, and the notation takes thirty seconds per applicant.\n\nWorked example 3 — income targeting arithmetic, tracked properly. A 90-unit Section 8 property fills 14 assisted units during its fiscal year.\n  40 percent of 14 = 5.6 → at least 6 of the 14 admissions must be extremely low income at admission (round up; do not round down to 5).\n  The property admits 4 extremely low income households and 10 very low income households.\n  It has MISSED the targeting requirement, and the failure is visible in its own data.\n  The control: a running fiscal-year admissions log with an ELI/VLI column and a percentage computed after every admission — not a count done at year end when nothing can be corrected. When the property is behind, the next selections must be made from the list with targeting in view, consistent with the TSP.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: preferences and their proof. A property has an approved residency preference and applies it. Preferences are permissible under 24 CFR 5.655 within its limits, but must be in the TSP, must be applied consistently, and must be documented — including the verification of each claimed preference. Note the fair housing dimension: residency preferences can have different effects on different populations depending on the demographics of the drawn area, which is one reason the AFHMP analysis and the preference need to be considered together, not separately.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: an applicant who is unreachable. A property calls once, gets voicemail, and moves on. That is not a documented attempt at contact; it is one call. A defensible practice specifies the method and number of attempts in the TSP (for example: two phone attempts and one written notice, over a stated period, with the written notice sent to the address of record), performs them, and records each attempt with date and outcome. Then — and this is the part that gets missed — the practice must accommodate applicants with known communication needs, such as an applicant who has requested communications in an accessible format.\n\nWorked example 6 — the practice point at intake. Ask for three things: the current list export, the last twelve months of admissions with their source position on the list, and the TSP. Reconcile the admissions to the list. If you cannot reconstruct why each admitted household was selected when it was, neither can the reviewer, and you have found the property's most serious exposure in the first hour of the engagement."},{"heading":"Waiting List Openings, Closings, and Purges","body":"Opening, closing and purging a waiting list are legitimate management actions. They become findings when they are done without written procedures, without notice, or in ways that fall unevenly on protected populations.\n\nCLOSING A LIST. A property may close its waiting list when the list is long enough that new applicants have no realistic prospect of housing within a reasonable period. What is required: the criteria for closing stated in the TSP; a decision made on those criteria; public notice of the closing consistent with the AFHMP and the property's marketing practice; and consistent treatment of applications received after the closing date (returned, not quietly discarded).\n\nOPENING A LIST. Reopening requires notice designed to reach the populations the AFHMP identifies as least likely to apply — not merely a sign in the leasing office window. Document the outreach performed: publications used, dates, organizations contacted, and copies of the notices.\n\nPURGING A LIST. Periodic purges keep the list accurate. What is required: purge criteria and frequency in the TSP; notice to each applicant at the address of record with a clear deadline and a clear statement of the consequence of not responding; a reasonable response period; documented removals with dates; accommodation of applicants with known communication or disability-related needs; and retention of the purge notices, responses and removal decisions.\n\nWorked example 1 — the purge that created a fair housing exposure. A property mails update letters with a 30-day response deadline and removes non-responders. One removed applicant had previously requested, in writing, that communications be sent in large print because of a vision impairment; the purge notice went out in standard print. The purge procedure was reasonable; its execution as to that applicant was not. Control: the list carries a communication-needs field, and every mass mailing is filtered through it. This is a five-minute system change that prevents a category of complaint.\n\nWorked example 2 — the purge with no record. A property purges and the list drops from 340 to 96 names. The reviewer asks what happened to 244 applicants. The property has the current list and nothing else — no notices, no responses, no removal log. Nothing improper may have occurred, and nothing can be shown. Control: every purge produces a purge FILE — the notice template, the mailing list, the date mailed, responses received, and the removal decisions with dates — retained with the waiting list records.\n\nWorked example 3 — the closed list that kept taking applications. A property closes its list but continues accepting applications from walk-ins \"just in case,\" holding them in a drawer. When the list reopens, those applications are entered with their original dates, ahead of applicants who followed the published process. This is exactly the kind of informal accommodation that produces a discrimination finding, because the people who benefit are the people who happened to walk in and be told about the drawer. Either the list is open to everyone or it is closed to everyone.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: how long is \"reasonable\" for a response period? The rules do not supply a universal number; the TSP does, and the standard is reasonableness. A 10-day response window on a first-class mailing is aggressive and will disproportionately remove applicants who travel, are hospitalized, or have unstable mail. Thirty days is common and defensible. What matters more than the number is that the period is stated in the TSP, is applied uniformly, and accounts for the population served — a senior property should think hard about anything shorter than 30 days.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: partial opening. A property with a long general list wants to open only for 3-bedroom applicants because it has turnover in large units and none in small ones. That is a rational management decision, and it is permissible if it is in the TSP, is applied uniformly, and is publicized appropriately. The trap is the fair housing dimension: opening only for large units affects families with children differently from other households, and familial status is protected. Here the effect favors families, but the analysis is the same in either direction. Document the business reason and the outreach, and be able to state the reasoning if asked.\n\nWorked example 6 — the consulting deliverable. Build the client a WAITING LIST OPERATIONS MEMO: when the list opens and closes and on what criteria, how notice is given and to whom, purge frequency and procedure with the notice template attached, the communication-needs field and how it is used, and the records retained for each action. Two pages, cross-referenced to the TSP. Most properties have never written any of this down, which is why the reviewer's questions produce improvised answers."},{"heading":"The Affirmative Fair Housing Marketing Plan (AFHMP)","body":"The AFHMP — form HUD-935.2A — is the property's written commitment to market affirmatively: to reach populations LEAST LIKELY TO APPLY for the housing without special outreach, and to do so in a way that furthers fair housing rather than merely avoiding discrimination.\n\nWhat the plan contains: the property's marketing area and its demographics; identification of the group or groups least likely to apply; the specific outreach the property will conduct — publications, community organizations, media, methods — to reach them; the marketing staff and training; the accessibility of the marketing itself; and the property's fair housing poster and logo obligations.\n\nTwo operating facts that clients routinely miss:\n• The plan must be KEPT CURRENT and reviewed and updated on the schedule HUD requires — commonly described as a review every five years, and sooner when circumstances change materially (a demographic shift in the market area, a change in the property's population, a change in ownership or the addition of program layers). Verify the current requirement for the specific program and property.\n• The plan is a COMMITMENT MEASURED AGAINST ITS OWN TERMS. A reviewer does not ask whether the property markets; it asks whether the property did what its plan says it does.\n\nWorked example 1 — the plan that is current and unperformed. The AFHMP identifies a least-likely-to-apply population and commits to: quarterly advertising in a named Spanish-language newspaper; outreach letters twice yearly to three named community organizations; and materials available in Spanish. In two years the property has done none of it. The finding is written against the plan's own terms. The cure is one of two things: PERFORM the marketing, with dated evidence — tear sheets, invoices, letters sent, contact logs, translated materials; or AMEND the plan to what the property will actually do and obtain approval. Never leave a plan on file describing outreach nobody performs.\n\nWorked example 2 — the market that changed and the plan that did not. A plan written in 2013 identifies the least-likely-to-apply group based on census data from that era. The market area's demographics have shifted substantially since. The plan is stale in substance even if it is technically on file. Recommend an update built on current data: pull the current demographic profile of the market area, compare it to the property's applicant and resident profile, identify the gap, and design outreach against the gap. This analysis is a genuine consulting deliverable and it takes an afternoon with public data.\n\nWorked example 3 — the property with chronic vacancy and thin marketing. A property runs 89 percent occupancy with eleven names on its list. This combination draws two lines of inquiry: an occupancy and financial question about vacancy loss, and a fair housing question about whether the property is marketing affirmatively at all. Here the AFHMP matters MORE, not less: a property that cannot fill units and cannot show outreach to least-likely-to-apply populations is exposed on both fronts. The remedy is a marketing calendar built from the AFHMP with dated deliverables and evidence filed against each.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: word-of-mouth referrals. A property fills most vacancies from resident referrals. Nothing prohibits referrals, and they are often a sign of a well-liked property. But a property that fills exclusively from an existing resident network tends to replicate the existing resident population — which is the precise mechanism the affirmative marketing requirement exists to counteract. The analysis to run: compare the demographics of the applicant pool and recent admissions to the market area. If the property's population is materially less diverse than its market area, the referral pipeline is doing something the AFHMP is supposed to counterbalance, and the outreach commitments need to be real.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: accessible and effective communication in marketing. The AFHMP contemplates that marketing be accessible. Practically, this means the property's advertising, application materials and website should be usable by people with disabilities and available in alternative formats on request, and the fair housing logo and poster obligations must be met. A property with an inaccessible online application portal has a marketing problem, a Section 504 problem, and an applicant pool skewed by both. Test the portal; do not assume the vendor handled it.\n\nWorked example 6 — the evidence file that makes this easy. Create an AFHMP EVIDENCE BINDER organized by the plan's own commitments: one tab per committed activity, with dated evidence filed as it occurs. When the reviewer asks \"show me your affirmative marketing,\" the answer is a binder that maps one-to-one onto the plan. Building it takes an hour; maintaining it takes ten minutes a month; and it converts the most commonly failed item in this area into a demonstrably passed one."},{"heading":"Tenant Selection — From Waiting List to Lease","body":"Selection is the sequence from \"next on the list\" to \"signed lease,\" and every step must be documented well enough that a stranger can reconstruct it.\n\nTHE SEQUENCE:\n1. Identify the next applicant(s) by list order, filtered for unit size, accessibility need, approved preferences, and income targeting posture.\n2. Make the OFFER, documented — date, method, unit, terms, response deadline.\n3. Record the RESPONSE — accepted, declined (with reason if given), or no response after documented attempts.\n4. Verify ELIGIBILITY: income and assets per the verification hierarchy; program-specific eligibility (age, disability, student rules); citizenship/immigration declarations for all members; SSN documentation; and the EIV EXISTING TENANT SEARCH before move-in.\n5. SCREEN per the TSP: credit, rental history, criminal history where the TSP provides for it — applied consistently, with the applicant given required notices and the opportunity to dispute the accuracy of information relied on.\n6. DECIDE. If approved, proceed to the move-in package (Chapter 11). If rejected, issue a compliant rejection letter and preserve the record.\n7. UPDATE the list to reflect the outcome, and preserve the snapshot.\n\nWorked example 1 — the offer and the declination, done right. An applicant is offered a 2-bedroom on the 8th, by phone, confirmed by letter the same day, with a response deadline of the 15th. She declines on the 11th because the unit is on the third floor and she cannot manage stairs.\n  This declination is a flag, not merely a \"no.\" A stairs-related declination may signal a need for an accessible unit or a reasonable accommodation, and the property's response should include informing her of the accessible unit process and her right to request an accommodation, and recording the accessibility need on the list.\n  What the file shows: the offer, the response, the accommodation discussion, and her position on the list going forward.\n  What a careless file shows: \"declined.\" And the next time a first-floor unit becomes available, nobody knows.\n\nWorked example 2 — the consistency test reviewers actually run. The reviewer pulls five rejections and five admissions and compares them against the TSP criteria.\n  Applicant A was rejected for two late payments in the last twelve months.\n  Applicant B, admitted, had three late payments in the same period.\n  The TSP says \"a pattern of late payments may be grounds for rejection.\"\n  The property now must explain the distinction, and \"may\" is doing a lot of work. Either the criterion needs to be objective (a stated number within a stated period), or every deviation needs a documented, non-discriminatory basis. Vague criteria are not lenient; they are indefensible.\n  Control: a SCREENING DECISION SUMMARY in every file — criterion applied, information relied upon, decision, decision-maker, date.\n\nWorked example 3 — the rejection letter with required content. A compliant letter states the specific reason for rejection; identifies the information relied upon and its source; informs the applicant of the right to dispute the accuracy and completeness of that information; explains how to request a meeting to discuss the rejection and the timeframe; and is issued promptly. Compare \"your application has been denied based on our screening criteria,\" which tells the applicant nothing, prevents meaningful response, and leaves the property unable to demonstrate consistent application later.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: an applicant whose screening result relates to a disability. An applicant's rental history shows a prior eviction that the applicant states arose from behavior related to a psychiatric disability now being treated, and she requests a reasonable accommodation in the screening process — asking the property to consider mitigating information. The property is not required to abandon its criteria, but it IS required to consider a request for an accommodation in the application of those criteria, engage in the interactive process, and make an individualized assessment. Blanket refusal to consider mitigating circumstances is where properties get into serious trouble. Document the request, the information considered, and the decision with its basis.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: the applicant who is over income at admission. An applicant reaches the top of the list; income verification shows income above the applicable limit for admission. The applicant is not eligible for admission — and this is a different test from CONTINUED occupancy, which is why a household already in place with rising income is treated differently. Document the computation, the limit applied and its effective date, and issue the rejection with its required content. Then check the file for the most common error in this area: using an outdated income limit table. Income limits are published annually; a property applying last year's table is making a systematic error.\n\nWorked example 6 — the practice point. The selection file for every admission should be reconstructable in five minutes: list position, offer, response, verifications, screening, decision, and move-in. Build a one-page ADMISSION SUMMARY as the cover sheet of the admission section — it is the single cheapest defense against a fair housing inquiry and it also happens to be exactly what a MOR reviewer needs to see."},{"heading":"Waiting List Technology and Record-Keeping","body":"Whether the list lives in property management software, a database, or a spreadsheet, the compliance requirements are identical: the ORDER must be provable, the HISTORY must be preserved, and the ACCESS must be controlled. What changes with technology is the failure mode.\n\nWHAT ANY SYSTEM MUST DO:\n• Capture date AND time of application at the moment of application.\n• Preserve a snapshot of the list as it stood at each selection — either through an audit log or through periodic exports retained as records.\n• Log changes: who moved, removed, or reordered an applicant, when, and why.\n• Record preference claims and their verification, accessibility needs, and communication needs.\n• Support the purge process with retained notices and removal records.\n• Restrict who can modify the list.\n• Produce an export the property can hand a reviewer.\n\nFAILURE MODES BY MEDIUM:\n• SPREADSHEETS: silent overwriting (no history), sorting that destroys order, no access control, and versions proliferating across desktops and email. If a client uses a spreadsheet, the minimum controls are a monthly dated export retained as a record, edit access limited to named staff, and a change log tab that is actually used.\n• PROPERTY MANAGEMENT SOFTWARE: the order and history are usually good; the failures are configuration (a preference set up wrong applies to everyone), unlogged manual overrides, and staff working around the system in a side spreadsheet.\n• PAPER: durable and provable if kept in order and never re-sorted, but slow to purge, hard to search, and vulnerable to a single physical loss. Paper lists should be photocopied or scanned periodically as a backup record.\n\nWorked example 1 — the spreadsheet that lost its own history. A property maintains its list in a spreadsheet on a shared drive. Applicants are removed by deleting rows. When the reviewer asks how many applicants were removed in the past year and why, there is no answer, because the removals left no trace. The property is not accused of anything and cannot demonstrate anything. Control: never delete — change STATUS and record the reason and date, and retain monthly exports so the list's state at any point can be shown.\n\nWorked example 2 — the software configuration error that ran for two years. A property's software was configured with a preference that was intended to apply only to a specific set of units and instead applied property-wide. Every selection for two years was ordered by a preference the TSP did not authorize in that scope. The individual staff decisions were all \"follow the system.\" The finding is systemic, and the remediation involves reconstructing the correct order for past selections to determine whether anyone was harmed. Control: test the configuration against the TSP with constructed cases at setup and after every software update, and re-test annually.\n\nWorked example 3 — the side spreadsheet. Site staff maintain the official list in the software and a \"working list\" in a spreadsheet because the software is slow. Over time the working list diverges. When the reviewer asks for the waiting list, staff produce the software list; when asked how the last five admissions were selected, the answers match the spreadsheet. This is a serious problem because it looks like concealment even when it is only convenience. Control: one list, in one place. If the software is unusable, fix or replace it — a workaround that becomes the real system is a finding waiting to be written.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: online applications. An online portal captures date and time automatically and reduces transcription error — real advantages. It also raises three issues: accessibility of the portal for applicants with disabilities; access for applicants without internet or devices, which the property must accommodate (a paper alternative and assistance available on request); and data security for applicant information. A portal that is the ONLY way to apply can skew the applicant pool in ways that matter under fair housing analysis. Offer a documented alternative and say so in the marketing.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: how long to keep waiting list records. Retain applications, the list, purge records and rejection documentation long enough to reconstruct selection decisions and to meet applicable retention requirements — and longer where a complaint, litigation or audit is pending. Because fair housing complaints can be filed some time after the events, a property that purges its rejection files at the earliest permissible moment can find itself unable to defend a selection it made correctly. Advise clients to retain selection records conservatively, with a hold process for pending matters and secure destruction thereafter.\n\nWorked example 6 — the intake test that takes ten minutes. Ask the client to produce: (a) the waiting list as it stood on a specific date six months ago; (b) the list of applicants removed in the last twelve months with reasons; (c) the audit log or change history. A property that can produce all three has a compliant system. A property that can produce none has a finding it does not know about yet, and the fix — status codes instead of deletions, monthly retained exports, restricted edit access — costs almost nothing and can be implemented the same week."}]},{"id":"m112","unit":"u11","number":2,"title":"Tenant Selection Plan — HOTMA Requirements and Best Practices","subtitle":"","sections":[{"heading":"What the TSP Must Contain","body":"The Tenant Selection Plan is the property's written rulebook for admissions. Handbook 4350.3 REV-1 Chapter 4 requires it, and it is a desk review document, an on-site document, and the yardstick against which every admission and rejection is measured. When a reviewer asks \"why was this applicant rejected?\", the only acceptable answer begins \"our Tenant Selection Plan says.\"\n\nWHAT A COMPLETE TSP CONTAINS:\n\n• The property's programs and the eligibility requirements of each, with income limits and how they are applied.\n• How applications are taken, dated and time-stamped, and how the waiting list is maintained.\n• Waiting list opening, closing and purge criteria and procedures.\n• Any PREFERENCES, their authority, their terms, how they are verified, and how they interact with list order and income targeting.\n• Income targeting: the 40 percent extremely low income requirement for Section 8 project-based admissions under 24 CFR 5.653 and how the property tracks and meets it.\n• Unit size / occupancy standards, and how households are matched to units.\n• Accessible unit procedures — how accessible units are offered first to households needing the features, and the transfer provision.\n• SCREENING CRITERIA, stated objectively: credit, rental history, criminal history within the limits of law and HUD requirements, and any other criteria — with the sources checked and how each is applied.\n• The rejection process, the content of rejection notices, and the applicant's right to respond and to a meeting.\n• Reasonable accommodation and modification procedures, including in the application process.\n• VAWA protections in admission and the required notices.\n• Live-in aide policy.\n• Student eligibility rules and how they are applied.\n• Verification procedures and the hierarchy.\n• Interim reporting requirements and the owner's discretionary policy.\n• Nondiscrimination statement and the property's fair housing commitments.\n• Effective date and revision history.\n\nWHERE HOTMA CHANGES THE TSP: on implementation (Multifamily compliance date January 1, 2027 per Notice H 2025-07), the TSP must additionally address the asset limitation and how it is applied at admission; the real property restriction and its exceptions; the asset self-certification threshold and the three-year full-verification cycle; the standardized interim triggers and the owner's discretionary policy below them; the streamlined determination policy for fixed-income families; the continued-occupancy grace period for existing tenants over the asset ceiling; and the hardship exemption processes.\n\nWorked example 1 — the TSP that is missing one section and produces three findings. A property's TSP does not address live-in aides. Consequences: an aide request is handled ad hoc; a second request is handled differently; and the reviewer, finding two inconsistent decisions and no written standard, writes a TSP deficiency, an inconsistency finding, and — because live-in aide requests are typically reasonable accommodation requests — a fair housing observation. One missing section, three exposures. Audit the TSP against a checklist of required contents at every engagement.\n\nWorked example 2 — the TSP that describes a practice nobody follows. The plan says applications are accepted only on Tuesdays and Thursdays from 9 to 4. In practice staff accept applications whenever someone walks in. That informality is kind and it is a finding, because the written standard and the practice diverge, and because it means some applicants were told the office was closed to applications while others were not. Fix in whichever direction is honest: change the practice, or change the plan.\n\nWorked example 3 — the subjective criterion. A TSP states the property may reject applicants who \"do not appear likely to be good residents.\" That is unenforceable, indefensible, and an invitation to a fair housing complaint, because it permits any decision and explains none. Every criterion must be stated so that two different staff members applying it to the same facts reach the same result. Rewrite as objective, verifiable standards with defined lookback periods and defined sources.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: how much detail is too much? A TSP that specifies an exact numeric credit score cutoff is objective but may be over-restrictive relative to the property's population and may have effects the property has not analyzed; a TSP that says \"credit will be considered\" is unusable. The workable middle states what is checked, what specific patterns are disqualifying (for example, unpaid debt to a prior landlord above a stated amount within a stated period), what mitigating evidence will be considered, and how an applicant may dispute the accuracy of the information. Objective, but with a documented path for individualized consideration.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: multiple program layers. A property has Section 8 PBRA, LIHTC, and a state loan program with its own occupancy requirements. The TSP must reconcile them — where requirements conflict, the more restrictive generally governs for that element, and the plan should say so explicitly rather than leaving staff to guess. Layered properties produce the most confused TSPs in the industry, and the fix is a table: requirement, source program, how applied here.\n\nWorked example 6 — the revision discipline. Every TSP should carry an effective date, a revision history table, and a stated review cycle (annually is a sensible default, plus whenever a rule changes). During a regime transition like HOTMA, an undated TSP is worse than useless as evidence. When you revise, keep the superseded version with its dates — the property may need to show what governed an admission made two years ago."},{"heading":"Criminal History Screening — HUD Guidance and Fair Housing","body":"Criminal history screening is the highest-risk area of tenant selection, because it sits at the intersection of legitimate safety concerns, a statutory framework, and well-established fair housing exposure under a disparate impact theory.\n\nWHAT THE LAW REQUIRES (mandatory denials). A limited set of denials is required rather than discretionary:\n• Persons subject to a LIFETIME SEX OFFENDER REGISTRATION requirement under a state sex offender registration program are prohibited from admission (24 CFR 5.856).\n• Persons convicted of manufacturing or producing METHAMPHETAMINE on the premises of federally assisted housing must be denied (24 CFR 5.854).\nThese are the categories where the owner has no discretion. Everything else is discretionary within the framework.\n\nWHAT THE LAW PERMITS (discretionary denials). Owners may deny admission for drug-related criminal activity, violent criminal activity, and other criminal activity that would adversely affect the health, safety or right to peaceful enjoyment of other residents — within the standards at 24 CFR 5.852 through 5.855, and consistent with the property's TSP. The regulations expressly contemplate that owners MAY consider MITIGATING CIRCUMSTANCES, including evidence of rehabilitation, and may require the applicant to furnish it.\n\nWHAT FAIR HOUSING LAW ADDS. HUD's Office of General Counsel guidance on the application of the Fair Housing Act to the use of criminal records (issued in 2016) sets out the analysis: because criminal justice involvement is not evenly distributed across racial and ethnic groups, a policy that screens on criminal records can have a DISPARATE IMPACT even without discriminatory intent. Under that framework:\n• A BLANKET BAN on anyone with any criminal record is very difficult to defend.\n• ARRESTS THAT DID NOT RESULT IN CONVICTION do not prove that underlying conduct occurred and generally may not be the basis for denial.\n• A policy must be tailored — the conduct considered, the lookback period, and the relationship to a substantial, legitimate, nondiscriminatory interest in resident safety.\n• Individualized assessment and consideration of mitigating factors are the practical protection.\n\nA NOTE ON PENDING CHANGE. HUD published a proposed rule, \"Reducing Barriers to HUD-Assisted Housing\" (April 10, 2024), that would require individualized assessment, narrow the criminal records that may be considered to those relevant to health and safety, and require full consideration of mitigating factors. VERIFY ITS CURRENT STATUS before advising a client — a proposed rule is not law, and rules in this area have moved. Build the client's policy so that it already reflects individualized assessment and documented mitigating-factor consideration, which is both defensible under current law and consistent with the direction of the proposal.\n\nWorked example 1 — the blanket ban, rewritten. A TSP states: \"Any applicant with a felony conviction will be denied.\" Problems: it sweeps in a 30-year-old conviction unrelated to safety, it ignores mitigating evidence, and it is squarely the kind of policy the disparate impact analysis targets.\n  Rewrite: \"The property will consider convictions for [defined categories of conduct bearing on the safety of residents, staff and property] occurring within [defined lookback period appropriate to the conduct]. Arrests not resulting in conviction will not be the basis for denial. The property will conduct an individualized assessment and will consider mitigating information, including the nature and severity of the conduct, the time elapsed, the applicant's age at the time, evidence of rehabilitation, and the circumstances. Applicants will be given the specific information relied upon and an opportunity to dispute its accuracy and to present mitigating information before a final decision.\"\n  That version is defensible, administrable, and produces a record.\n\nWorked example 2 — the arrest record. A background check returns three arrests over eight years and one conviction (a misdemeanor unrelated to safety, six years ago). Staff deny \"based on criminal history.\"\n  The arrests cannot properly support the denial; an arrest is not proof that conduct occurred.\n  The single old misdemeanor almost certainly does not meet a tailored standard tied to resident safety.\n  The denial is both a TSP problem and a fair housing exposure. And the letter, which said only \"criminal history,\" gave the applicant no way to respond and gave the property no record of what it actually relied on.\n\nWorked example 3 — the individualized assessment, done well. An applicant has a conviction for a violent offense seven years ago. He provides: completion of a treatment program, four years of continuous employment with a supervisor's letter, three years of stable tenancy with a landlord reference, and a letter from a parole officer.\n  The property conducts and DOCUMENTS an individualized assessment: the nature and severity of the conduct, time elapsed, evidence of rehabilitation, and the specific risk to residents.\n  Whatever the outcome, the property has a defensible record. If it approves, it has documented why. If it denies, it has documented that it considered the mitigating evidence and why it concluded the risk remained.\n  Compare a property that denies with \"does not meet criminal screening criteria\" and no assessment. Same applicant, same outcome, entirely different exposure.\n\nWorked example 4 — the mandatory denial applied correctly. An applicant is subject to a lifetime sex offender registration requirement in his state. Admission is prohibited. Here there is no individualized assessment and no discretion — and the property must be careful to verify that the applicant is actually subject to a LIFETIME registration requirement, not merely a registration requirement of some duration, because the two are different and the mandatory bar reaches only the lifetime category. Document the verification and its source.\n\nWorked example 5 — the boundary case: household members and guests. An applicant is admissible; her adult son, who will live in the unit, has a recent conviction within the property's criteria. The screening applies to household members, and the property must decide on the household as constituted. Options include denial of the household as proposed, or admission of the household without that member if the applicant chooses to proceed without him — which must be documented and must be genuinely the applicant's choice, not a condition manufactured by the property. Any subsequent occupancy by the excluded member is a lease issue with its own process.\n\nWorked example 6 — the ambiguous case: criminal history related to domestic violence. An applicant's record includes an arrest or conviction arising from an incident in which she was the survivor of domestic violence — a common pattern, including dual arrests and charges arising from defensive conduct. VAWA protections bar denial of admission on the basis of the applicant's status as a survivor, and the property must not use a criminal record that is itself a product of the victimization as the basis for denial. This requires staff to ask enough to understand the context without demanding proof the rules do not permit, and to document the analysis. Train for it specifically: it is one of the places where a mechanical screening process produces both a fair housing violation and a VAWA violation in a single decision."},{"heading":"Income Targeting and Eligibility Limits","body":"Income rules at admission run in two directions: a CEILING (the household must be income-eligible) and a TARGET (a share of admissions must go to the lowest-income households). Properties track the first and forget the second.\n\nINCOME LIMITS. Eligibility is determined against HUD's published income limits for the area and household size, effective for the applicable year. For Section 8 project-based assistance, admission is generally limited to households at or below the applicable very low income limit, with the exceptions provided in the regulations and the handbook for certain admissions at the low income limit. Program-specific rules apply to Section 202, Section 811, BMIR and Section 236 properties. Verify the limit that applies to the program, the area and the household size — and use the CURRENT published table.\n\nINCOME TARGETING. For Section 8 project-based assistance, of the assisted units that become available for occupancy in any fiscal year, NOT LESS THAN 40 PERCENT must be leased to EXTREMELY LOW INCOME families at admission (24 CFR 5.653). Extremely low income is defined by reference to the published limits.\n\nADMISSION VERSUS CONTINUED OCCUPANCY. These are different tests. A household whose income rises above the limit AFTER admission is not thereby made ineligible to remain; program rules govern continued occupancy and, where income rises enough, the household may pay a higher share or cease receiving assistance while remaining in the unit, depending on the program. Confusing these two tests produces wrongful terminations — one of the more serious errors a property can make.\n\nWorked example 1 — the outdated table. A property applies the prior year's income limits for four months after the new limits are published. Two households were rejected as over-income who would have been eligible under the current table, and one was admitted who was not eligible under the old table but is under the new one. The rejections are the serious problem: applicants were denied housing on a superseded standard. Control: a calendar entry for the annual income limit publication, a documented update to the property's tables and software, and a note of the effective date in the TSP file.\n\nWorked example 2 — the targeting shortfall caught in time. A property's fiscal year is July 1 to June 30. By March it has filled 11 assisted units, 3 of them extremely low income.\n  Requirement: at least 40 percent of units filled in the year.\n  If the year ends with 14 total admissions, at least 6 must be ELI. The property has 3 with roughly 3 admissions expected.\n  It must make the remaining selections with targeting in view, consistent with its TSP — which is exactly why the TSP must state HOW targeting is achieved, so that selecting for targeting is a written, uniformly applied procedure rather than an ad hoc override of list order.\n  Contrast the property that computes this in July, after the year closed. Nothing can be done, and the finding is written.\n\nWorked example 3 — the income limit boundary. A household of three has verified annual income of $41,880. The applicable very low income limit for a three-person household is $41,900. The household is eligible — by $20. Two lessons: the computation has to be right to the dollar, because a $200 annualization error changes the answer; and the file must show the limit used, its effective date, the household size, and the source. A file showing only \"eligible\" cannot survive a challenge in either direction.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: household size changes between application and move-in. A household of four is verified at application; a member moves out before move-in, leaving three. The applicable income limit changes with household size, and so may the unit size. Re-verify and re-determine at move-in rather than relying on the application-stage determination, and document the change. Properties that process the admission on stale composition data produce a certification that is wrong on day one.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: anticipated income at admission for a household with a job starting later. An applicant will start a job in six weeks at a wage that would still leave the household eligible, but a bonus structure could push it over. Annual income anticipates the next twelve months. Compute on the best available verified information, document the basis and the assumptions, and note the follow-up — an interim will address it if the actual income differs materially. Do not deny on a speculative maximum, and do not certify on a figure you know to be understated.\n\nWorked example 6 — the tracking artifact. Build the client a fiscal-year ADMISSIONS LOG: date, unit, household size, annual income, income category (ELI / VLI / LI), and a running ELI percentage. One row per admission, updated at admission. It answers the targeting question at any moment, it is the document a reviewer wants, and it makes the year-end failure impossible to miss in advance."},{"heading":"Screening Criteria — Credit and Rental History","body":"Credit and rental history screening is permitted, useful, and routinely done in ways that cannot be defended. The requirements are the same as for any criterion: objective, in the TSP, applied consistently, based on accurate information the applicant can dispute, and open to individualized consideration where the law requires it.\n\nWHAT A DEFENSIBLE SCREENING PROCESS LOOKS LIKE:\n• The criteria are in the TSP, stated objectively, with defined lookback periods.\n• The sources checked are named (consumer reporting agency, prior landlords, court records) and are checked for every applicant in the same way.\n• The applicant is told what information was relied on and given the opportunity to dispute its accuracy and completeness — and where a consumer report is used, applicable consumer reporting law imposes its own notice obligations.\n• Mitigating information is considered where required, and the consideration is documented.\n• Every decision is recorded on a screening decision summary: criterion, information relied on, decision, decision-maker, date.\n• The process accommodates disability-related requests, including requests to consider mitigating information as a reasonable accommodation.\n\nWorked example 1 — the inconsistency that writes itself. Applicant A is rejected for a $640 unpaid balance owed to a prior landlord. Applicant B is admitted with a $900 balance owed to a prior landlord, because the leasing agent \"knew the landlord and knew it was a dispute.\" The agent may well have been right. The record shows two applicants treated differently on the same criterion with no documented basis. Control: if a mitigating explanation is accepted, WRITE IT DOWN — what was explained, what was verified, and why the decision differed. An undocumented exception is indistinguishable from arbitrary treatment.\n\nWorked example 2 — the credit criterion nobody can apply. A TSP says \"applicant must demonstrate satisfactory credit.\" Two staff members will apply that differently on the same file, and neither decision can be explained to a reviewer. Rewrite to something administrable: for example, unpaid collections owed to prior housing providers or utility providers above a stated amount within a stated period, with defined exclusions (medical debt is a common and sensible exclusion) and a documented path to present mitigating information. Objective does not mean harsh; it means repeatable.\n\nWorked example 3 — the rental history check that gets it backwards. A property calls only the CURRENT landlord. A landlord who wants a tenant to leave has an incentive to give a glowing reference; a landlord who has been paid on time for four years has no incentive to say anything at all. A defensible process contacts prior landlords over a defined period, asks the same questions each time from a written form, records the responses, and notes when a landlord did not respond. And it applies the same effort to every applicant — a property that calls three references for some applicants and none for others has a consistency problem regardless of outcomes.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: no credit history at all. An applicant has no credit file — common for young applicants, recent immigrants, survivors of financial abuse, and people who have lived without credit. \"No credit history\" is not bad credit, and treating it as disqualifying can produce a disparate impact on protected groups. The TSP should say what happens: alternative evidence of payment history (rent receipts, utility payment records, a statement from a prior landlord), or admission without a credit-based rejection. Decide the policy in advance rather than improvising at the counter.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: an eviction record that tells an incomplete story. A court record shows an eviction filing three years ago. Filings are not judgments, cases are dismissed, some are resolved by agreement, and in some jurisdictions filings remain visible regardless of outcome. Relying on a filing alone risks rejecting an applicant who prevailed. The defensible practice looks at the DISPOSITION, gives the applicant the information and the chance to explain, and considers mitigating circumstances — including circumstances related to domestic violence, where VAWA protections apply and a filing arising from the applicant's victimization may not be the basis for denial.\n\nWorked example 6 — the consulting deliverable that pays for itself. Build the client a SCREENING MATRIX: one row per criterion, with columns for the source checked, the objective standard, the lookback period, the mitigating factors considered, the required notice, and the documentation retained. Attach it to the TSP. Then pull the last ten screening decisions and score them against the matrix. The gap between the matrix and the last ten decisions is your finding, your training agenda, and your evidence that the engagement was worth the fee."},{"heading":"Rejection Notices and the Applicant Response Process","body":"When an applicant is rejected, the notice and the response process are what stand between a routine decision and a fair housing complaint. Handbook 4350.3 sets out the requirements, and the practical standard is this: the applicant must be able to understand why, to check whether the information was right, and to ask the property to reconsider.\n\nWHAT THE REJECTION NOTICE MUST DO:\n• State the SPECIFIC reason for the rejection — not a category, the reason.\n• Identify the information relied upon and its SOURCE, so the applicant can verify it.\n• Inform the applicant of the right to dispute the ACCURACY AND COMPLETENESS of that information.\n• Explain how to request a MEETING to discuss the rejection, and the timeframe for doing so (commonly a stated number of days from the notice).\n• Be issued promptly, in writing, to the address of record — in an accessible format where a communication need is known.\n• Preserve the applicant's rights under fair housing law, Section 504 and VAWA, including the right to request a reasonable accommodation in the process.\n\nWHAT THE MEETING IS. A prompt, documented opportunity for the applicant to respond — to present corrected information, mitigating circumstances, or a request for accommodation. It is conducted by someone who can actually reconsider the decision, ideally not the person who made it. The property documents who attended, what was presented, what was considered, and the outcome, and it communicates the result in writing.\n\nWHAT THE FILE MUST HOLD AFTERWARD: the application, the screening documentation, the decision summary, the rejection notice with proof of delivery, any request for a meeting, the meeting record, the final decision, and any accommodation request and its disposition.\n\nWorked example 1 — the notice that does everything wrong, rewritten.\n  Wrong: \"Dear Applicant: Your application for residency has been denied. Sincerely, Management.\"\n  What is missing: the reason, the information relied on, the source, the dispute right, the meeting process, and any accessibility consideration. The applicant cannot respond and the property cannot later prove what it did.\n  Right: \"Your application dated March 4 for a two-bedroom unit has been denied. The reason is an unpaid balance of $640 owed to [prior housing provider], reported by [source] in a report dated March 11, which falls within the criterion in Section [x] of our Tenant Selection Plan (unpaid balances owed to a prior housing provider exceeding $500 within the preceding 36 months). You have the right to dispute the accuracy and completeness of this information. You may request a meeting to discuss this decision by contacting [name, phone, email, address] within 14 days of the date of this letter. If you need this notice or the meeting in an accessible format, or need any accommodation related to a disability, contact [name] and we will provide it. A copy of the information relied upon is enclosed.\"\n\nWorked example 2 — the meeting that changes the outcome. The applicant produces a receipt and a letter from the prior landlord showing the $640 was paid two months before the report was pulled. The property verifies, reverses the decision, and documents the sequence. This is a good outcome and it is also the SYSTEM working: the notice made the error visible, the meeting corrected it, and the file proves it. A property whose notices never produce a correction is probably not producing notices that let applicants respond.\n\nWorked example 3 — the meeting that does not change the outcome, done properly. An applicant meets and presents mitigating circumstances that do not overcome the criterion. The property documents what was presented, states in writing that it considered the information, gives the reason the decision stands, and preserves the record. The applicant is not satisfied — but the file shows an individualized process. That is the difference between a defensible denial and an indefensible one.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: an accommodation request during the rejection process. An applicant with a disability requests, as a reasonable accommodation, that the property consider information about circumstances related to her disability that bear on the screening result. The property is not required to abandon its criteria, but it must consider the request, engage in the interactive process, make an individualized assessment, and document it. Treat any such request as an accommodation request formally: log it, respond promptly, and document the analysis. A rejected applicant whose accommodation request went unanswered is the fact pattern most likely to become a complaint.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: the applicant who does not respond. The notice is sent, the deadline passes, nothing happens. The file is complete if it shows the notice, its delivery, the deadline, and the absence of a response. Where the property knows of a communication need — a request for large print, a language access issue, or a documented disability affecting communication — silence is not a safe assumption and the property should make and document a further attempt in the appropriate format.\n\nWorked example 6 — the audit that predicts a complaint. Pull the last ten rejections. For each ask: does the notice state a specific reason; does it identify the information and source; does it convey the dispute right and the meeting process; is there proof of delivery; and is there a screening decision summary showing the criterion applied? Then compare across the ten for consistency. Most properties fail on specificity and on proof of delivery. Both are fixable with a template and a mailing log, and both are the difference between defending a decision and settling one."}]},{"id":"m113","unit":"u11","number":3,"title":"Fair Housing, Section 504, and Reasonable Accommodations","subtitle":"","sections":[{"heading":"The Seven Federal Fair Housing Protected Classes","body":"The Fair Housing Act (Title VIII of the Civil Rights Act of 1968, as amended, 42 U.S.C. 3601 et seq.) prohibits discrimination in housing on the basis of SEVEN protected characteristics:\n\n• RACE\n• COLOR\n• RELIGION\n• NATIONAL ORIGIN\n• SEX\n• FAMILIAL STATUS (households with children under 18, pregnant persons, and persons in the process of securing legal custody)\n• DISABILITY (handicap, in the statutory language)\n\nRace, color, religion, national origin and sex came from the 1968 Act; familial status and disability were added by the Fair Housing Amendments Act of 1988.\n\nWHAT IS PROHIBITED extends well past refusing to rent: different terms, conditions or privileges; different services or facilities; discriminatory statements, advertising or notices; steering; misrepresenting availability; refusing to make reasonable accommodations or permit reasonable modifications; failure to design and construct covered multifamily dwellings built for first occupancy after March 13, 1991 in accordance with the Act's accessibility requirements; harassment; and retaliation against a person for exercising fair housing rights or assisting others in doing so.\n\nTWO THEORIES OF LIABILITY, and both matter for a MOR-adjacent practice:\n• DISPARATE TREATMENT — treating a person differently because of a protected characteristic. Intent matters.\n• DISPARATE IMPACT — a neutral policy that has a significantly greater adverse effect on a protected group, unless it is necessary to achieve a substantial, legitimate, nondiscriminatory interest that cannot be served by a less discriminatory alternative. Intent is not required.\n\nSEX, SEXUAL ORIENTATION AND GENDER IDENTITY — an area a consultant must handle carefully and currently. HUD has enforced the Fair Housing Act's prohibition on sex discrimination as encompassing discrimination because of sexual orientation and gender identity, following the reasoning of the Supreme Court's decision in Bostock v. Clayton County. Separately, HUD's Equal Access Rule at 24 CFR 5.105(a)(2) requires that HUD-assisted housing be made available without regard to actual or perceived sexual orientation, gender identity or marital status, and 24 CFR 5.106 addresses equal access in accordance with gender identity in certain Community Planning and Development programs. NOTE: this area is in active rulemaking — HUD published a proposed rule on April 28, 2026 (Docket No. FR-6518-P-01) that would revise the Equal Access Rule, including removing the regulatory definitions of \"gender identity\" and \"sexual orientation\" from 24 CFR Part 5 and adding a definition of \"sex,\" with a comment period that closed June 29, 2026. As of the writing of this chapter that proposal had not been finalized. VERIFY THE CURRENT STATE OF THE REGULATION before advising a client, and do not assume that either the prior guidance or the proposal represents current law without checking.\n\nSTATE AND LOCAL LAW frequently adds protected classes — source of income, age, marital status, military or veteran status, and others. A property complies with the most protective applicable law. Never advise a client on fair housing without knowing the state and locality.\n\nWorked example 1 — familial status, the most commonly violated class in ordinary practice. A property's house rules state: \"Children must be supervised by an adult at all times in common areas\" and \"No bicycles or play equipment on the grounds.\" Both rules single out families with children. The first may be defensible if narrowly tailored to a genuine safety concern for a specific hazard (a pool, for instance) and applied as a safety rule rather than a blanket restriction on children's presence; a general \"at all times\" supervision rule applied to all common areas restricts children's use of the property and is a classic familial status violation. The second, applied to children's bicycles but not to adults' property, is differential treatment. Review every house rule with the question: does this rule burden families with children more than other households, and if so, what is the specific, legitimate, narrowly tailored reason?\n\nWorked example 2 — steering, which staff do without knowing the word. An applicant with two small children asks about availability. The leasing agent says, \"We have a unit on the third floor and one on the ground floor near the playground — you'd probably prefer the ground floor with the kids.\" Well intentioned; it is steering. The agent has directed a household toward particular units on the basis of familial status. The rule: describe ALL available units and their features, and let the household choose. Train staff to state availability neutrally and to let the applicant volunteer preferences.\n\nWorked example 3 — disparate impact, worked. A property adopts a minimum income requirement of three times the CONTRACT rent for all applicants, including those receiving Section 8 assistance whose share of the rent is a fraction of that figure. The rule is neutral on its face and effectively excludes the assisted households the property exists to serve, with predictable effects along protected lines. Analysis: is there a substantial, legitimate, nondiscriminatory interest? Ability to pay is legitimate. Is this rule necessary to serve it, or is there a less discriminatory alternative? Applying the income multiple to the TENANT'S PORTION of rent serves the same interest with far less impact. The alternative exists, so the policy is vulnerable — and in many jurisdictions it also violates source-of-income protections.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: religious accommodation and house rules. A resident asks to affix a religious item to her doorframe, contrary to a house rule prohibiting attachments to unit exteriors. This is not a disability accommodation, but the analysis is similar in structure: is the rule serving a genuine interest, is there a way to permit the practice without defeating that interest, and is the rule being applied evenly (are holiday decorations permitted?). A property that allows seasonal decorations while refusing a religious item is treating residents differently on the basis of religion. Document the decision and apply the rule evenly.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: a resident complaint about another resident. A resident complains that a neighbor's family gatherings are loud and adds comments about the neighbor's national origin. The property must address the NOISE under its lease and house rules, uniformly, and must not act on the discriminatory framing. It must also consider whether the complaining resident's conduct is itself harassment of a protected person — a property that facilitates resident-on-resident harassment can face liability. Document what was reported, what was investigated, what was found and what was done. Handling this well is a judgment call; handling it by ignoring it is not defensible.\n\nWorked example 6 — the intake question that finds most problems. Ask for the house rules, the application form, the marketing materials and the last three rejection letters, and read all of them with the seven classes in mind. In most engagements you will find at least one rule that burdens families with children, one marketing statement that describes an ideal resident, or one letter that gives no reason. All three are cheap to fix before a reviewer or a tester finds them."},{"heading":"Reasonable Accommodations for Tenants with Disabilities","body":"A REASONABLE ACCOMMODATION is a change, exception or adjustment to a rule, policy, practice or service that may be necessary for a person with a disability to have an equal opportunity to use and enjoy a dwelling, including public and common use spaces. The obligation arises under the Fair Housing Act and, for recipients of federal financial assistance, under Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act.\n\nTHE FRAMEWORK:\n• A request may be made by the person with a disability or by someone acting on their behalf, ORALLY OR IN WRITING. A property may not require a specific form as a condition of considering a request — it may offer a form as a convenience.\n• The property must respond PROMPTLY. Unreasonable delay is treated as a denial.\n• The property engages in an INTERACTIVE PROCESS: discussing the request, the disability-related need, and possible alternatives that would be effective.\n• VERIFICATION: if the disability and the need are OBVIOUS or already known, no verification may be requested. If not obvious, the property may request verification that the person has a disability and that the requested accommodation is needed because of it — from a reliable third party in a position to know. The property may NOT ask for a diagnosis, medical records, or the nature or severity of the condition.\n• The property may deny only if the accommodation would impose an UNDUE FINANCIAL AND ADMINISTRATIVE BURDEN or would FUNDAMENTALLY ALTER the nature of the provider's operations — and must then discuss whether an alternative would be effective.\n• Every request and its disposition must be DOCUMENTED, with disability-related information kept confidential and access limited.\n\nCOMMON ACCOMMODATIONS: an assigned accessible parking space; permission to keep an assistance animal notwithstanding a no-pet policy; a live-in aide; a transfer to a ground-floor or accessible unit; a change in the rent payment date to align with benefit receipt; communications in an accessible format; permission for a third party to receive notices; an extension of time to respond to a notice; and consideration of mitigating information in screening.\n\nWorked example 1 — the assistance animal, the most common request in the portfolio. A resident in a no-pet property requests to keep a dog as an assistance animal for a psychiatric disability.\n  An assistance animal is not a pet, and pet rules, pet deposits and pet fees do not apply.\n  The disability is not obvious, so the property may request verification of the disability and of the disability-related need for the animal from a reliable third party in a position to know — following HUD's assistance animal guidance (FHEO-2020-01).\n  The property may NOT ask for a diagnosis, may not require specific training or certification for the animal, may not require a particular breed, and may not charge a pet deposit.\n  The property MAY require that the animal not pose a direct threat based on individualized, objective evidence of the specific animal's conduct, and may hold the resident responsible for damage the animal causes, on the same terms as any resident damage.\n  Documentation: the request, the verification, the decision, the date, and any conditions — filed in the accommodation log with the disability-related information kept confidential.\n\nWorked example 2 — the accommodation that is denied correctly. A resident requests that the property build a private fenced yard for her assistance animal, at the property's expense. Analysis: is the request necessary for equal opportunity to use and enjoy the dwelling, and is it reasonable? A fenced private yard at a multifamily property is a substantial structural change likely to impose an undue financial and administrative burden and to fundamentally alter the property's common areas. The correct handling is not a flat \"no\": engage in the interactive process, explore alternatives (a designated relief area, an assigned nearby parking or entrance, a leash-and-schedule arrangement), document the analysis and the alternatives offered, and put the decision in writing with the reasons. A denial that follows an interactive process and offers alternatives is defensible; a denial by letter with no discussion is not.\n\nWorked example 3 — the parking accommodation and the reserved space problem. A resident with a mobility impairment requests a reserved space near her entrance. The property has open parking and worries about \"setting a precedent.\" Precedent is not a defense; each request is individualized. Reserving a specific space for a resident with a disability-related need is a classic reasonable accommodation and is ordinarily granted. Implementation matters: sign the space, notify residents of the policy generally (without identifying the resident's disability), and have an enforcement mechanism, because an unenforced reserved space is not an accommodation.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: the request that arrives during an eviction. A resident facing termination for lease violations — repeated late payments — requests, as an accommodation, a change in the rent due date to the third of the month, when her disability benefit arrives. The timing does not defeat the request. The property must consider it. A change in due date is typically a minimal administrative adjustment and is frequently granted; if the property's position is that the pattern of nonpayment exceeds a due-date problem, it must still consider the request, document the analysis, and be able to show it engaged. Properties that reflexively refuse to consider accommodations once legal action starts create their most serious exposures.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: verification that overreaches in both directions. A resident submits a letter from an online service that has no treatment relationship, purchased for a fee. The property may consider whether the source is a reliable third party in a position to know — HUD's guidance addresses documentation from the internet specifically. The property may seek verification from a source that actually knows the person. What the property may NOT do is refuse all consideration, demand a diagnosis, or impose a blanket rule that only a physician's letter is acceptable. Document the analysis. This is the single most common area where property staff either capitulate to inadequate documentation or overreach into medical information, and both errors are findings.\n\nWorked example 6 — the accommodation log, and why it is the deliverable. Build a log with: date of request, requester (identified by unit), the accommodation requested, whether the disability and need were obvious, verification requested and received (with the substance kept confidential), the interactive process steps, the decision, the date of decision, and any conditions. Then measure two things: the time from request to decision, and the consistency of decisions on similar requests. Reviewers ask for the log. Complaints turn on those two measures."},{"heading":"Reasonable Modifications for Tenants with Disabilities","body":"A REASONABLE MODIFICATION is a STRUCTURAL change to the premises — a grab bar, a ramp, a widened doorway, lowered cabinets, a roll-in shower, a visual doorbell. The distinction from an accommodation is not academic, because WHO PAYS depends on which framework applies.\n\nTHE TWO FRAMEWORKS:\n• Under the FAIR HOUSING ACT, a housing provider must PERMIT a tenant to make reasonable modifications at the TENANT'S EXPENSE, where the modification is necessary for the person with a disability to have full enjoyment of the premises. The provider may, where reasonable, condition permission on the tenant's agreement to restore the interior to its prior condition at the end of the tenancy (reasonable wear and tear excepted), and may in appropriate circumstances require a reasonable escrow arrangement for the restoration cost.\n• Under SECTION 504 of the Rehabilitation Act, a RECIPIENT of federal financial assistance must make structural modifications to units and common areas as a reasonable accommodation AT THE RECIPIENT'S EXPENSE, unless doing so would impose an undue financial and administrative burden or fundamentally alter the program.\n\nFor HUD-ASSISTED multifamily properties, which are recipients of federal financial assistance, THE SECTION 504 FRAMEWORK IS ORDINARILY THE OPERATIVE ONE. A property that tells an assisted resident \"you may install a grab bar at your own expense\" is applying the wrong framework and has likely just committed a Section 504 violation.\n\nWorked example 1 — the grab bar, decided correctly. A resident with a mobility impairment requests grab bars in the bathroom.\n  The property is a recipient of federal financial assistance.\n  Under Section 504, the property makes the modification at its own expense unless it can demonstrate an undue financial and administrative burden.\n  A grab bar installation costing a few hundred dollars will not meet the undue burden standard at a property of any size.\n  The property installs, documents the request, the verification (if the need was not obvious), the decision, the work order, the completion date, and the cost.\n  The WRONG handling: \"Approved — please arrange installation at your expense and restore at move-out.\" That sentence is the violation.\n\nWorked example 2 — the roll-in shower and the undue burden analysis, done properly. A resident requests conversion of a tub to a roll-in shower, estimated at $9,400.\n  Undue burden is evaluated in light of ALL resources available to the program or activity — not the unit's rent, and generally not the site budget in isolation. The analysis considers the cost, the property's and the recipient's overall financial resources, the availability of other funding (including whether a reserve for replacement release could fund it), and the effect on operations.\n  It also considers ALTERNATIVES: is there a vacant or soon-available accessible unit at the property that would meet the need, offered with a transfer at the property's expense? An effective alternative can satisfy the obligation.\n  Whatever the outcome, the property documents the analysis with figures. An undue burden claim asserted without a documented financial analysis will not survive scrutiny.\n\nWorked example 3 — the modification the tenant wants to make personally. A resident wants to install a specific brand of smart lock and lever handles, and wants to do the work himself through a contractor he knows. Even under the Section 504 framework, the property has a legitimate interest in the workmanship, code compliance, permits, and the condition of its property. The workable approach: the property performs the work as its obligation, meeting the disability-related need, using an equivalent product; or, where the resident prefers his own contractor, the property documents an agreement covering scope, code compliance, permits, insurance and inspection. What the property may not do is use \"we have standards\" as a way to refuse the modification entirely.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: modifications to COMMON areas. A resident requests a ramp at a building entrance with three steps. This is a common-area modification and it reaches beyond one household — it also implicates the property's program accessibility obligations under Section 504 generally (Section 4). Treat it as both: respond to the individual request AND evaluate whether the property's obligation to make its programs accessible has been met at that entrance. A property that ramps one entrance in response to one request and never examines the rest of its site has answered the request and not the obligation.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: restoration. Under the FHA framework, restoration can be required in appropriate cases; under Section 504, where the recipient pays for the modification, requiring the tenant to restore makes little sense and would generally be improper. But a mixed situation arises where a resident makes a personal, aesthetic modification alongside a disability-related one. Separate them in the documentation: the disability-related modification is the property's obligation and is not subject to restoration; a purely personal alteration is governed by the lease. Muddling the two produces a move-out dispute and, worse, a record suggesting the property charged a resident for an accommodation.\n\nWorked example 6 — the practice point. Every property should have a written MODIFICATION PROCEDURE distinguishing the two frameworks, stating that the property is a recipient of federal financial assistance and that structural modifications needed as an accommodation are made at the property's expense subject to the undue burden analysis, and describing how requests are received, verified, evaluated, funded, performed and documented. Most properties' policies are copied from private-market templates and say the opposite. Reading a client's modification policy is a two-minute check that finds a serious problem with surprising frequency."},{"heading":"Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act","body":"Section 504 (29 U.S.C. 794) prohibits discrimination on the basis of disability in any program or activity receiving federal financial assistance. HUD's implementing regulations are at 24 CFR Part 8. For HUD-assisted multifamily housing, Section 504 imposes obligations that go beyond the Fair Housing Act.\n\nTHE CORE OBLIGATIONS:\n\n• PROGRAM ACCESSIBILITY. The recipient's programs and activities, viewed in their entirety, must be readily accessible to and usable by persons with disabilities. This reaches the leasing office, community rooms, laundry, mail areas, parking, and the paths connecting them — not only dwelling units.\n• NEW CONSTRUCTION (24 CFR 8.22). In newly constructed multifamily projects, a MINIMUM OF 5 PERCENT of the total dwelling units (or at least one unit, whichever is greater) must be accessible to persons with MOBILITY impairments, and an ADDITIONAL 2 PERCENT (or at least one unit) must be accessible to persons with HEARING OR VISION impairments. Common areas and public spaces must be accessible.\n• SUBSTANTIAL ALTERATION (24 CFR 8.23). Where alterations are undertaken to a project with 15 OR MORE UNITS and the cost of the alterations is 75 PERCENT OR MORE of the replacement cost of the completed facility, the new construction requirements of 8.22 apply. Other alterations must, to the maximum extent feasible, make the altered portion accessible, until 5 percent of the units in the project are accessible.\n• REASONABLE ACCOMMODATION AND STRUCTURAL MODIFICATION at the recipient's expense, subject to undue financial and administrative burden (Section 3).\n• EFFECTIVE COMMUNICATION. The recipient must furnish appropriate auxiliary aids and services where necessary for effective communication — which includes accessible formats for notices and, in appropriate circumstances, interpreters for meetings such as a recertification interview or a grievance meeting.\n• ACCESSIBLE UNIT OCCUPANCY. Accessible units must be offered first to households needing the features, with procedures and a lease provision permitting a required transfer when a qualifying household needs the unit.\n• ADMINISTRATIVE REQUIREMENTS. Recipients with 15 or more employees must designate a person responsible for coordinating 504 compliance and adopt grievance procedures; recipients are required to provide notice of nondiscrimination and to have conducted a self-evaluation.\n\nWorked example 1 — the 5 percent and 2 percent computation. A newly constructed 84-unit assisted property.\n  Mobility-accessible: 5 percent of 84 = 4.2 → at least 5 units (round up; the requirement is a minimum).\n  Hearing/vision-accessible: an additional 2 percent of 84 = 1.68 → at least 2 units.\n  Common areas and public spaces accessible.\n  Then the operational question the MOR will actually reach: are those units OCCUPIED by households needing the features, and can the property prove the offer sequence? A property can meet its construction obligation and fail its occupancy obligation.\n\nWorked example 2 — the substantial alteration threshold, applied. A 40-unit property undertakes a rehabilitation costing $3.4 million; the replacement cost of the completed facility is $4.1 million.\n  $3.4M ÷ $4.1M = 83 percent, which is 75 percent or more, and the project has 15 or more units.\n  The new construction requirements of 8.22 apply — 5 percent mobility and an additional 2 percent hearing/vision, plus accessible common areas.\n  Change the facts: alterations cost $1.6 million, or 39 percent. The substantial alteration trigger is not met, and the \"other alterations\" rule applies instead — altered portions accessible to the maximum extent feasible until 5 percent of units are accessible.\n  This is a computation to run at the PLANNING stage of any rehabilitation, not after. A property that discovers the trigger during construction has a very expensive problem, and a consultant who spots it in the scoping documents has earned a year of fees in one meeting.\n\nWorked example 3 — effective communication in an ordinary transaction. A resident who is deaf comes in for an annual recertification interview. The property's obligation is effective communication, and the appropriate auxiliary aid depends on the complexity of the communication and the person's usual method. A written exchange may suffice for a simple matter; a recertification interview involving income, deductions and legal obligations may require an interpreter. Ask the person what they need, provide it, document the request and what was provided, and do not charge for it. Properties that hand a deaf resident a pen and paper for a 45-minute recertification interview are not communicating effectively and will be told so.\n\nWorked example 4 — the accessible unit occupied by a household that does not need it. Common, and the answer turns entirely on documentation. If the file shows the unit was offered first to qualified households on the waiting list, was declined, and was leased with the required transfer provision, the property is compliant. If there is no such record, the finding is a 504 finding, and 504 findings are serious. Control: the waiting list carries an accessibility-need field, every accessible unit vacancy triggers a documented offer sequence, and every accessible unit lease contains the transfer provision.\n\nWorked example 5 — the boundary case: undue burden at a small property. A 16-unit Section 202 property receives a request for a $22,000 modification. Undue burden is assessed against all resources available to the program or activity, which is broader than the site's operating cash. The analysis should consider the owner's resources, reserve balances and the possibility of a HUD-approved reserve release (form HUD-9250), other funding sources, and alternatives such as transferring the resident to an accessible unit at the property's expense. Document all of it. A small property is not automatically excused, and a bare assertion of poverty is not an analysis.\n\nWorked example 6 — the ambiguous case: the 504 coordinator at a property managed by a large agent. The requirement to designate a coordinator applies to recipients with 15 or more employees; at a property managed by an agent with hundreds of employees, the practical answer is that the coordinator function exists at the organizational level and that site staff know who it is and how to reach them. What produces findings is not the org chart, it is that nobody on site can answer \"who is your 504 coordinator?\" Post it, put it in the notice of nondiscrimination, and put it in the accommodation procedure."},{"heading":"Fair Housing Training, Documentation, and Complaint Response","body":"Fair housing compliance is a system, and the three parts that a reviewer can actually inspect are TRAINING, DOCUMENTATION, and COMPLAINT RESPONSE.\n\nTRAINING. Everyone who touches applicants or residents — leasing, occupancy, maintenance, management, and contracted staff — needs periodic fair housing training covering the seven protected classes and applicable state and local classes, accommodations and modifications, assistance animals, effective communication, VAWA, and the property's own procedures. Annual is the sensible default. Keep a training register per employee: date, topic, provider, duration, and certificate.\n\nDOCUMENTATION. The artifacts that prove the system works:\n• The accommodation and modification LOG, with dates and outcomes.\n• The waiting list with accessibility-need and communication-need fields.\n• Screening decision summaries and rejection notices with the required content.\n• The AFHMP and its evidence file.\n• Posted notices: the fair housing poster, the notice of nondiscrimination, the 504 coordinator, the accommodation contact, and the grievance procedure.\n• House rules reviewed against fair housing standards, with a revision date.\n• Complaint records.\n\nCOMPLAINT RESPONSE. Complaints arrive in three forms and each needs a defined path:\n1. INTERNAL — a resident or applicant complains to the property. Log it, acknowledge it promptly in writing, investigate, document what was found, communicate the outcome, and preserve everything.\n2. AGENCY — a HUD FHEO complaint or a state or local agency complaint. This goes to counsel and ownership immediately. Do not let site staff respond informally.\n3. TESTING — a fair housing organization conducts paired testing. Nobody knows it happened until the results appear. This is the single strongest argument for scripting and training telephone and walk-in inquiries, because testers capture exactly those interactions.\n\nWorked example 1 — the training record that does not exist. A property trained its staff on fair housing at a management conference eighteen months ago. There is no sign-in sheet, no certificate, no agenda. For review purposes the training did not occur. Control: the training register is updated THE DAY training happens, with the agenda and certificate attached. This is a five-minute habit that converts a real activity into provable compliance.\n\nWorked example 2 — the maintenance technician nobody trained. Fair housing training at most properties covers leasing staff. Maintenance staff enter units, interact with residents daily, and make comments. A technician who tells a resident with a disability \"you people always want something changed\" has created liability that no amount of leasing-office training prevents. Train maintenance, and train on the specific scenarios they encounter: entering a unit, responding to a request, encountering an assistance animal, and what to do when a resident raises an accommodation need (answer: pass it to the office the same day, in writing, because a request made to ANY staff member is a request).\n\nWorked example 3 — the complaint handled well. A resident complains in writing that her accommodation request has gone unanswered for six weeks. Correct sequence: acknowledge within a few days; locate the request; determine why it stalled; make a decision on the request promptly; communicate it in writing; document the root cause (in most cases: the request was made verbally to a staff member who did not recognize it as a request); and change the process — for instance, a standing instruction that any staff member receiving any request writes it into the log the same day. The complaint is now evidence of a functioning system rather than of a broken one.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: an accommodation request the property cannot grant, from a resident who is upset. A resident demands a specific unit that is occupied. The property cannot evict another household to satisfy the request. What it must do: engage in the interactive process, identify what disability-related need the specific unit would meet, determine whether an alternative would be effective (a comparable unit, a modification to the current unit, a transfer when a unit becomes available with a documented priority), document the analysis, and respond in writing. Residents rarely file complaints about outcomes they understand; they file complaints about silence.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: an allegation against a staff member. A resident alleges a leasing agent made a comment about her national origin. The agent denies it. There is no recording and no witness. The property cannot resolve the factual dispute — but it can and must respond: document the allegation, interview both parties, document the investigation, take appropriate action (which may be retraining and monitoring rather than discipline where facts are unresolved), communicate to the resident that the matter was taken seriously and what was done, and preserve the record. An unresolved allegation with a documented investigation is a defensible posture. An unresolved allegation with no record is not.\n\nWorked example 6 — the self-audit that finds what a tester would. Once a year, run the property's own inquiry process: call the leasing line and ask about availability as an ordinary caller; then have someone else call and mention children, or an assistance animal, or ask about accessible units. Compare what was said. Then walk in and observe the posted notices, the accessibility of the office, and the application materials. This exercise costs two hours and finds the exact interactions a paired test would capture — and unlike a paired test, it produces a training agenda instead of a complaint.\n\nWorked example 7 — the consulting frame. Fair housing is the one area where a MOR finding is the least of the client's problems: the same facts support an administrative complaint, a private lawsuit, and — for a recipient of federal financial assistance — potential enforcement consequences. Say that plainly to clients when scoping. It changes the priority they assign to a $1,200 training engagement."}]},{"id":"m121","unit":"u12","number":1,"title":"VAWA 2022 — Current Requirements for HUD-Assisted Housing","subtitle":"","sections":[{"heading":"VAWA Overview — Who Is Protected and What Is Prohibited","body":"The Violence Against Women Act housing protections apply to survivors of DOMESTIC VIOLENCE, DATING VIOLENCE, SEXUAL ASSAULT, and STALKING in covered housing programs — which include Section 8 project-based rental assistance, Section 202, Section 811, public housing, Housing Choice Vouchers, and other HUD-covered programs. The protections apply regardless of sex, gender identity or sexual orientation: the statute's name is historical, and the coverage is not limited to women.\n\nWHAT IS PROHIBITED. A covered housing provider may not:\n• DENY ADMISSION to an applicant because that applicant is or has been a victim of domestic violence, dating violence, sexual assault or stalking.\n• EVICT or TERMINATE ASSISTANCE on the basis of that victimization.\n• Treat the incidents of violence as serious or repeated lease violations by the victim, or as good cause for eviction of the victim.\n• COERCE, INTIMIDATE, THREATEN, INTERFERE WITH, or RETALIATE against a person for exercising VAWA rights or assisting others in exercising them.\n\nWHAT VAWA DOES NOT DO. It does not immunize a survivor from eviction for lease violations unrelated to the abuse, and it does not prevent a provider from evicting a tenant who poses an ACTUAL AND IMMINENT THREAT to other tenants or staff — a demanding standard, evaluated on the specific facts, and one that requires consideration of less drastic alternatives.\n\nMOBILITY. A survivor who holds a Section 8 Housing Choice Voucher must be permitted to MOVE WITH CONTINUED ASSISTANCE where the conditions in the rules are met. For project-based programs, the parallel mechanism is the emergency transfer (Section 4).\n\nWHAT VAWA 2022 ADDED. The 2022 reauthorization expanded the COMPLIANCE REVIEW process itself. HUD's monitoring now examines as separate line items: notification compliance (whether the required notices were provided at the required times), documentation-acceptance compliance (whether self-certification was accepted as the rules require), confidentiality compliance, emergency transfer compliance, and anti-retaliation compliance. Practically, this means a property cannot satisfy a reviewer by saying \"we're aware of VAWA\" — each element is tested separately. VAWA 2022 also added the National Housing Trust Fund as a covered program by statute, where it had previously been covered by HUD regulatory action.\n\nTHE CENTRAL WORKED EXAMPLE — build this one for every client, because it shows the real mechanics.\nA Section 202 elderly property. The household consists of the head of household, age 74 — the member whose age qualifies the household for the elderly family deduction — and his spouse. After an altercation constituting domestic violence, the head of household moves out; the survivor remains as the sole remaining household member. She is 58 and is not a person with a disability.\n  Step 1 — VAWA protections attach. The survivor may not be evicted or have assistance terminated because of the violence. If the perpetrator's removal is accomplished through the lease, that is a BIFURCATION (Section 5).\n  Step 2 — Household composition changes. The property processes the composition change under its written policy and 4350.3.\n  Step 3 — The DEDUCTION CHANGES. The elderly/disabled family deduction applied because the head was 62 or older. With that member gone and no remaining member elderly or disabled, the deduction NO LONGER APPLIES. Adjusted income rises by the deduction amount, and the household's TTP rises accordingly.\n  Step 4 — An INTERIM RECERTIFICATION is processed to reflect the composition change and the resulting figures.\n  Step 5 — Because the result is a RENT INCREASE, the property gives the required ADVANCE NOTICE before the increase takes effect. The correction does not get to skip the notice rules because it arises from a VAWA situation.\n  Step 6 — Because the qualifying member for ELIGIBILITY has left, the property must also determine whether the remaining household member is eligible in her own right for the Section 202 program, and if the removed member was the one whose eligibility qualified the household, the 90-DAY period described in Section 5 applies.\n  Step 7 — All VAWA documentation is kept SEPARATELY AND SECURELY, not in the tenant file.\n  This example is worth walking through with a client line by line, because it shows that VAWA protections and ordinary certification mechanics operate simultaneously: the survivor keeps her housing, and her rent may still go up, and the property still owes her proper notice.\n\nWorked example 2 — the lease violation that is not the survivor's. A survivor's abuser damages the unit door during an incident and neighbors complain about noise. The property issues a lease violation notice to the survivor. Analysis: incidents of the violence may not be treated as lease violations by the victim or as good cause for eviction of the victim. Damage caused by the abuser during the abuse is part of the incident. The correct handling is to repair, document, consider bifurcation to remove the perpetrator, and provide the required VAWA notice and certification form with any notice of eviction or termination. Note that the property may still address the tenant's lease obligations for damage in appropriate cases — but reflexively charging the survivor for damage inflicted on her is precisely what the statute is aimed at.\n\nWorked example 3 — the boundary case: the actual and imminent threat exception. A property believes a tenant who is a survivor also poses a genuine danger to other residents, based on specific conduct unrelated to her victimization. The exception exists, and it is narrow: the threat must be actual and imminent, evaluated on the specific facts, and the provider should consider whether less drastic alternatives would address it — a transfer, a modification of conduct, or a bifurcation. Document the facts, the analysis, the alternatives considered, and involve counsel. Invoking this standard casually is how a provider converts a manageable situation into litigation.\n\nWorked example 4 — the ambiguous case: the survivor who is also the leaseholder's abuser according to the other party. Both household members allege victimization. The provider is not a court and cannot adjudicate. What it can do: provide the notice and certification form to both, apply the protections to both, avoid taking sides in the documentation, consider whether an emergency transfer request from either satisfies the criteria, and involve counsel before any bifurcation. The provider's obligation is process, not fact-finding.\n\nWorked example 5 — the retaliation trap. A survivor requests an emergency transfer. The property, frustrated by the administrative work, begins strict enforcement of house rules against her that it does not enforce against others. That is retaliation, it is separately prohibited, and it is now a separately reviewed compliance line item under VAWA 2022. Train staff that once a person has asserted VAWA rights, every subsequent action toward that household must be documented and must be consistent with how comparable households are treated."},{"heading":"HUD Form 5380 — The Notice of Occupancy Rights and When It Must Be Issued","body":"Form HUD-5380, \"Notice of Occupancy Rights under the Violence Against Women Act,\" is the notice that tells applicants and tenants what VAWA protections they have and how to assert them. It must be provided at THREE SPECIFIC TRIGGER POINTS:\n\n1. When an applicant is DENIED admission or assistance.\n2. At ADMISSION.\n3. With any NOTICE OF EVICTION or NOTICE OF TERMINATION OF ASSISTANCE.\n\nForm HUD-5382, the certification form (Chapter 17, Section 3), must be provided at the SAME three points.\n\nTHE MOST COMMON COMPLIANCE ERROR IS DISTRIBUTION AT THE WRONG TIMES. Properties frequently distribute at annual recertification — which is not one of the three required points — while failing to distribute at DENIAL, which is. Distributing at recertification is not itself a violation, and some providers do it as a practice. But it is not a substitute for the required points, and the pattern to look for is a property that over-distributes at the easy point and under-distributes at the hard one.\n\nTest all three points separately at every engagement:\n• Pull the last twelve months of REJECTION files. Is HUD-5380 and HUD-5382 documented as provided with each denial? This is the point that fails most often, because rejection is handled by leasing staff working from a letter template that nobody updated.\n• Pull recent MOVE-IN files. Are both forms documented as provided at admission?\n• Pull the last twelve months of TERMINATION and EVICTION notices. Were both forms provided with each?\n\nDocumentation of delivery matters as much as delivery. A signed acknowledgment, a delivery log, or a mailing record — something that lets the property prove the forms went out.\n\nWorked example 1 — the quiz item that catches nearly everyone. A property provides HUD-5380 and HUD-5382 to every household at every annual recertification, and to every new resident at move-in. It does not provide them with rejection letters. Is the property compliant? NO. Two of the three required points are covered (admission is; recertification is not a required point at all); the DENIAL point is missed entirely. The property is doing more work than required in one place and none in another. This is the single most common VAWA notification error in the industry.\n\nWorked example 2 — the termination notice that omits the forms. A property issues a notice of lease termination for nonpayment. The forms are not included, because staff associate VAWA with domestic violence situations and this is \"just a nonpayment case.\" The requirement is not conditioned on the provider's belief about whether violence is involved — the forms go with ANY notice of eviction or termination of assistance. And the reason for the rule is exactly this case: the household receiving a nonpayment notice may be in a situation the property knows nothing about, and the notice is how she learns she has rights. Control: the forms are attached to the notice TEMPLATE, so they cannot be omitted.\n\nWorked example 3 — the boundary case: a denial that is not a rejection letter. An applicant is determined ineligible during the verification stage and the property simply stops processing without issuing anything. Beyond the tenant selection problem (Chapter 15 — a rejection requires a compliant notice), the VAWA trigger attaches to denial of admission or assistance. Where the property has effectively denied, it must issue the rejection notice AND the VAWA forms. \"We just never got back to them\" fails on both counts.\n\nWorked example 4 — the ambiguous case: language and accessibility. HUD publishes the VAWA forms in multiple languages. A property serving a substantial limited-English-proficient population should provide the forms in a language the recipient can understand, consistent with the property's language access obligations, and must provide them in an accessible format where a communication need is known. A notice of rights the recipient cannot read has not accomplished its purpose, and this is exactly the kind of gap a reviewer probes when the property's resident population and its document set do not match.\n\nWorked example 5 — the practice point. Build a VAWA DISTRIBUTION LOG with three sections — denials, admissions, terminations/evictions — each row showing the date, the household or applicant, the forms provided, and the method of delivery. Cross-check it monthly against the rejection log, the move-in log, and the notice log. Any row in those logs without a corresponding VAWA distribution entry is a gap you can close before a reviewer finds it.\n\nWorked example 6 — remediation when the gap is historical. A property discovers that no VAWA forms were provided with rejections for two years. It cannot fix the past. What it does: install the control immediately (forms attached to the rejection template); document the discovery, its date and scope; distribute the current forms where there is an ongoing relationship (existing tenants, applicants still on the list); and disclose the gap with the remediation in the desk review package. Never back-date a distribution log. A documented, disclosed, remediated gap is a manageable finding; a fabricated record is a different category of problem entirely."},{"heading":"HUD Form 5382 — Self-Certification and the Documentation Standard","body":"Form HUD-5382, \"Certification of Domestic Violence, Dating Violence, Sexual Assault, or Stalking,\" is the form on which a survivor may certify their status in order to invoke VAWA protections.\n\nTHE DOCUMENTATION STANDARD — this is the rule staff most often get wrong:\n• A housing provider MAY request documentation when a tenant or applicant asserts VAWA protections.\n• If documentation is requested, the individual may satisfy the request by submitting HUD-5382 ALONE — the self-certification is sufficient.\n• The individual may alternatively submit other forms of documentation: a record from a police officer, court, or administrative agency; or documentation signed by a victim service provider, attorney, or medical professional from whom the individual has sought assistance, along with the individual's signed statement.\n• THE CHOICE OF WHICH FORM OF DOCUMENTATION TO SUBMIT BELONGS TO THE INDIVIDUAL. The provider may not demand a police report or medical records instead of the self-certification.\n• The ONE EXCEPTION: where the provider receives CONFLICTING information — for example, documentation from two households each claiming protections regarding the same incident, or information that conflicts with the certification — the provider may request third-party documentation, and must give the individual the opportunity to provide it within the timeframe the rules specify.\n• A provider may also choose to provide protections WITHOUT any documentation at all. Nothing requires a provider to request documentation.\n\nTIMEFRAME. When documentation is requested, the individual must generally be given at least 14 BUSINESS DAYS to provide it, and the provider may extend that period. Failure to provide documentation within the period can allow the provider to proceed, but the provider should document the request, the period given, and any extension.\n\nWorked example 1 — the well-intentioned violation. A resident discloses domestic violence and asks for help. The property manager says, \"I'll need a police report so we have something in the file.\" That single sentence is a VAWA violation: it demands a specific form of documentation the individual is not required to provide, and it may deter a survivor who has legitimate reasons not to involve police. The correct sentence: \"Here is a notice of your rights and a certification form. You may use this form, or if you prefer, documentation from a police officer, court, agency, victim service provider, attorney, or medical professional. It is your choice which to provide, and you have at least 14 business days.\"\n\nWorked example 2 — the conflicting information case, handled properly. Two members of the same household each submit HUD-5382 regarding the same incident, each identifying the other as the perpetrator. This is the classic conflicting-information scenario. The provider may request third-party documentation from each, must give the required time period, and must not resolve the conflict by simply believing whoever spoke first. Document the requests, the responses, and the analysis, and involve counsel before any adverse action against either household member. The provider is not required to make a finding about who is telling the truth; it is required to follow a process.\n\nWorked example 3 — the boundary case: documentation that arrives late. A survivor is given 14 business days and provides the certification on day 19. The provider may extend the period, and in most circumstances extending is both permissible and sensible — the population involved has legitimate reasons for delay. What matters is that the property's practice is consistent and documented. A provider that accepted late documentation from one household and refused it from another has an inconsistency problem on top of everything else.\n\nWorked example 4 — the ambiguous case: the disclosure that is not a request. A resident mentions to a maintenance technician that her ex-partner has been showing up and she is frightened. She does not ask for anything. Does anything trigger? Formally, VAWA protections are invoked by the individual, and the provider's obligations around documentation attach when protections are asserted. Practically, the right response is to make sure she knows her rights exist: provide the HUD-5380 notice and the HUD-5382 form, tell her about the emergency transfer plan and how to request one, and document that the information was provided — without pressing her to certify anything. Then, critically, keep that documentation SEPARATELY (Section 4), not in her tenant file.\n\nWorked example 5 — the certification that arrives after a termination notice. A property issues a termination notice for repeated disturbances. The resident responds with HUD-5382 certifying that the disturbances arose from domestic violence. The property must consider the protections: incidents of violence may not be treated as lease violations by the victim or as good cause for eviction of the victim. The appropriate steps are to halt the termination as to the survivor, consider bifurcation to remove the perpetrator, document the analysis, and — if the property believes an actual and imminent threat exists — apply that narrow standard with counsel. Proceeding with the termination without analyzing the certification is a straightforward violation and one that the 2022 documentation-acceptance compliance review is designed to catch.\n\nWorked example 6 — the training point. Every staff member who might receive a disclosure — leasing, maintenance, management, and after-hours staff — needs to know exactly three things: do not ask for details, do not demand police reports or medical records, and get the person the notice and the certification form plus the emergency transfer information the same day. Everything else can be handled by the office. Scripting those three points takes twenty minutes of training and prevents the most common violations in this area."},{"heading":"The Emergency Transfer Plan and VAWA Confidentiality","body":"THE EMERGENCY TRANSFER PLAN. Every covered housing provider must have a WRITTEN emergency transfer plan and must make it available upon request. Form HUD-5383 is the Emergency Transfer Request form.\n\nTHE TWO-PRONG ELIGIBILITY TEST. A tenant qualifies for an emergency transfer if the tenant:\n(1) REASONABLY BELIEVES they are THREATENED WITH IMMINENT HARM from further violence if they remain in the unit; OR\n(2) is a victim of SEXUAL ASSAULT that occurred ON THE PREMISES within the 90 DAYS preceding the transfer request.\nEither prong is sufficient. Note the difference in structure: prong one is about the tenant's reasonable belief going forward; prong two is a factual test about a past event with a location requirement and a 90-day window.\n\nWHAT THE PLAN MUST DESCRIBE: how a tenant requests a transfer; the documentation the provider will require (consistent with the self-certification standard); how the provider will handle internal transfers (to another unit at the property) and external transfers (to another property, including any partnerships or referral arrangements); what the provider will do when no unit is immediately available, including how the tenant's request is prioritized; the confidentiality protections; and that the tenant may also seek other assistance.\n\nVAWA CONFIDENTIALITY — a distinct and stricter obligation than ordinary file confidentiality:\n• Information a survivor submits, and the fact that a person is a survivor, may not be entered into any SHARED DATABASE.\n• It may not be disclosed to any other entity or individual, except where the tenant requests or consents in writing, where required for use in an eviction proceeding as permitted, or where otherwise required by applicable law.\n• Per HUD's occupancy guidance, VAWA documentation should be kept in a SEPARATE, SECURE LOCATION — NOT filed in the regular tenant file. The reason is concrete: other adult household members can typically access the tenant file, and the perpetrator may be one of them.\n\nThis is the point clients most often miss, because it cuts against everything else they have been taught. In every other area of MOR compliance the instruction is \"put it in the file.\" For VAWA it is the opposite.\n\nWorked example 1 — the confidentiality failure that endangers someone. A property files a survivor's HUD-5382 in her tenant file. Her husband, a co-head on the lease, comes to the office and asks to review the file — a request the property routinely grants to leaseholders. The certification is in it. This is not a paperwork finding; it is a disclosure that can have physical consequences. Control: a separate, locked VAWA file, accessible to a named limited set of staff, with a log of access; the tenant file contains NO reference to VAWA status. If a cross-reference is operationally necessary, it must not itself reveal the status.\n\nWorked example 2 — the shared database problem. A management company uses a portfolio-wide property management system with resident notes visible to staff at all its properties. A site manager enters \"VAWA transfer — DV situation\" in the notes field. That is an entry in a shared database and it is prohibited. Control: written instruction that VAWA information is never entered in the system, plus a check of the notes fields during the compliance audit. Software vendors do not solve this; policy and training do.\n\nWorked example 3 — the emergency transfer with no unit available, handled properly. A survivor requests an internal transfer under prong one. No unit is available.\n  The plan must state what happens: the request is documented and prioritized for the next appropriate available unit; the provider assists the tenant in identifying other options, which may include external transfer arrangements or referrals; the provider documents each step and communicates with the tenant.\n  What the plan cannot do is stop at \"we will transfer when a unit is available\" with no further obligation.\n  Note the certification mechanics from Chapter 13: an internal transfer is processed as a UNIT TRANSFER, preserving the recertification anniversary — not as a move-out and move-in.\n\nWorked example 4 — LEASE BIFURCATION, the mechanism and the 90-day clock. Bifurcation allows the provider to split the lease to REMOVE the perpetrator while the survivor remains housed. The mechanics that matter:\n• The provider may bifurcate the lease to evict, remove or terminate assistance to the person who committed the violence, without evicting or terminating assistance to the survivor and other household members.\n• If the removed member was the one whose ELIGIBILITY qualified the household for the assistance, the remaining household members are given a period of 90 DAYS to establish eligibility in their own right or to find alternative housing, and the provider may extend that period by UP TO 60 ADDITIONAL DAYS.\n• Bifurcation follows applicable federal, state and local law for terminating the tenancy of the removed member — it is not a self-executing administrative act, and counsel should be involved.\nReturn to the central example in Section 1: the Section 202 property where the head of household leaves. If his departure is accomplished by bifurcation and his age was what qualified the household, the survivor has 90 days (extendable by up to 60) to establish her own eligibility — while simultaneously the elderly deduction disappears and her rent rises. Those two consequences arrive together, and a client who has walked through this example once will handle the real situation far better.\n\nWorked example 5 — the boundary case: prong two's 90-day window. A tenant reports a sexual assault that occurred in the laundry room 104 days ago and requests an emergency transfer. Prong two does not apply — it requires the assault to have occurred on the premises within the preceding 90 days. But prong ONE may well apply if she reasonably believes she is threatened with imminent harm from further violence if she remains. Evaluate both prongs, always, and document both analyses. A provider that denies on prong two without considering prong one has applied half the test.\n\nWorked example 6 — the ambiguous case: a transfer request that is really an accommodation request, or both. A survivor with a disability requests a transfer citing both safety and accessibility. Process it under BOTH frameworks — the emergency transfer plan and the reasonable accommodation procedure — because the protections and the obligations differ and the tenant should get the benefit of both. Document each analysis separately, keeping the VAWA documentation in the secure file and the accommodation documentation in the confidential disability file, and make sure neither file reveals the contents of the other."},{"heading":"VAWA Implementation — Training, Documents, and Ongoing Compliance","body":"VAWA compliance is now reviewed as five separate elements — notification, documentation acceptance, confidentiality, emergency transfer, and anti-retaliation. Build the client's system to produce evidence for each.\n\nTHE DOCUMENT SET:\n• HUD-5380, Notice of Occupancy Rights — current version, in the languages and formats the property's population needs.\n• HUD-5382, Certification form — same.\n• HUD-91067, the VAWA LEASE ADDENDUM — signed by all adult household members at lease signing, and executed for existing households that lack it.\n• HUD-5383, Emergency Transfer Request form.\n• The property's written EMERGENCY TRANSFER PLAN, available on request.\n• The property's written VAWA procedures, covering distribution, documentation, confidentiality, transfers, bifurcation, and retaliation.\n• The VAWA DISTRIBUTION LOG (Section 2).\n• The separate, secure VAWA FILE with an access log.\n\nTHE TRAINING. Every employee who may receive a disclosure — including maintenance, after-hours, and contracted staff — needs, at minimum: what the four covered forms of violence are; that a survivor may self-certify; that police reports and medical records may not be demanded; that VAWA information never goes in the tenant file or a shared database; that the emergency transfer plan exists and how to hand someone the request form; and that retaliation is prohibited. Document the training with dates and attendance.\n\nTHE PERIODIC AUDIT — quarterly is a workable cadence:\n1. Pull rejections, admissions, and termination/eviction notices for the period; verify VAWA form distribution for each.\n2. Verify HUD-91067 execution for all move-ins in the period, and track remediation for existing households.\n3. Inspect the secure VAWA file: is it separate, is it secured, is access logged, and does the tenant file contain no VAWA references?\n4. Spot-check the property management system's notes fields for prohibited entries.\n5. Review any emergency transfer requests: dates, actions, outcomes, timeframes.\n6. Confirm the emergency transfer plan is current and available on request.\n\nWorked example 1 — the addendum gap and its remediation. A property has executed HUD-91067 for all move-ins since 2019 but never for households admitted before that. Roughly 60 percent of files lack it. Remediation: execute now with the actual current date; document the discovery and scope in a memo; disclose in the desk review package; and — because this is a document requiring signatures from residents — plan the logistics (a mailing with return instructions, plus in-person execution at the next recertification interview for non-responders). Do not back-date. Do not treat it as satisfied by the HUD-5380 notice; the notice and the lease addendum are different obligations.\n\nWorked example 2 — the anti-retaliation element, made auditable. Retaliation is the hardest of the five elements to evidence, because you are proving a negative. The practical approach: once a household has asserted VAWA rights, flag the household (in the SECURE file, not the shared system) and require that any subsequent adverse action toward that household — a lease violation notice, a fee, a denial of a request — be reviewed by a supervisor before issuance, with the comparison to how similar households were treated documented. That review record is the evidence that the action was not retaliatory.\n\nWorked example 3 — the boundary case: a survivor who asks the property to keep her abuser out. A survivor asks the property to bar her ex-partner, who is not a household member, from the property. Providers can generally address trespass and can enforce guest and no-trespass policies, and a court protective order strengthens the position considerably. What the property should do: ask whether a protective order exists and offer to keep a copy in the secure file if she wishes; apply its trespass and guest policies as it would for any resident's request; document. What it should not do is promise physical security it cannot deliver, or condition any assistance on her obtaining an order.\n\nWorked example 4 — the ambiguous case: information the property learns from a third party. A neighbor tells the manager that a resident is being abused. The resident has not asserted anything. The property's obligations around documentation are triggered by the individual's assertion, and the property should not initiate an investigation into a resident's private life or record the neighbor's report in the tenant file. What it can do: ensure the resident has the notice and certification form and knows about the emergency transfer plan, keep any resulting documentation in the secure file, and take no action premised on the third-party report. Confidentiality protections extend to the fact of victimization however the property learns of it.\n\nWorked example 5 — the desk review posture. VAWA items appear in the desk review package: the notices used, the emergency transfer plan, the written procedures, and evidence of the lease addendum. Assemble them as a labeled section with the distribution log summary — and, importantly, WITHOUT any individual survivor's documentation. A property that submits a survivor's certification form as \"evidence of compliance\" has demonstrated a confidentiality violation in the act of trying to prove compliance. Prove the SYSTEM with policies, logs and templates; never with a survivor's records.\n\nWorked example 6 — the consulting engagement. VAWA remediation is compact and high-value: a document set refresh, a written procedure, the lease addendum campaign, the secure file build, the distribution log and its integration into the rejection/admission/termination workflows, a staff training session, and a quarterly audit. It takes days, not months. And because the 2022 reauthorization made each element a separate review line, a client who has all six artifacts walks into the review able to answer five questions that most properties cannot answer at all."}]},{"id":"m122","unit":"u12","number":2,"title":"Leases, HAP Contracts, and Tenant Protections","subtitle":"","sections":[{"heading":"The HUD Model Lease — Required Provisions","body":"HUD-assisted multifamily properties use the HUD MODEL LEASE for their program. The lease is not a document the owner drafts; it is a document the owner executes, in the current HUD form, with only the additions HUD permits. A property using its own lease, or an old version, or one with unauthorized modifications, has a finding that reaches every household at the property simultaneously.\n\nWHAT THE MODEL LEASE ESTABLISHES:\n• Parties, unit, term, and the rent — with the tenant's portion stated and the mechanism by which it changes when the certification changes.\n• The utility allowance and any utility reimbursement.\n• The security deposit and its terms.\n• The tenant's obligations: use as a residence, household composition and reporting, maintenance, conduct, access for the owner, and the obligation to supply information for recertification.\n• The owner's obligations: maintenance, services, utilities where applicable, and quiet enjoyment.\n• Termination provisions — the grounds, the notice, and the procedures, which are more protective than ordinary state landlord-tenant law.\n• Attachments and addenda.\n\nREQUIRED ADDENDA AND ATTACHMENTS, which is where properties actually fail:\n• The VAWA LEASE ADDENDUM, form HUD-91067, signed by all adult household members.\n• HOUSE RULES, where the property has them, properly adopted and attached.\n• PET RULES where applicable — properties designated for elderly persons or persons with disabilities are subject to specific pet ownership rules, and the pet rules must be consistent with them. Note that ASSISTANCE ANIMALS are not pets and are not governed by pet rules (Chapter 16).\n• LEAD-BASED PAINT disclosure and the EPA pamphlet for target housing built before 1978, with the required signatures and retention.\n• Any program-specific attachment required by HUD.\n\nWorked example 1 — the modified lease. A property added a clause requiring tenants to pay a $75 fee for any maintenance call determined to be \"tenant-caused,\" and another requiring 60 days' notice to vacate. Neither is in the model lease. Analysis: the model lease governs, and unauthorized modifications are not enforceable as HUD-approved terms; the fee provision may also conflict with the property's obligations, and a notice requirement inconsistent with the model lease creates a conflict the property will lose. Remediation: remove the provisions, notify tenants, refund fees charged under an unauthorized clause, and — if the property wants a legitimate policy on tenant-caused damage — implement it through the mechanisms the lease actually provides.\n\nWorked example 2 — the missing signature that surfaces at eviction. A household of two adults; only one signed the lease. Two years later the property seeks to terminate for lease violations by the non-signing adult. The property's position is weak: it is trying to enforce a lease against someone who never signed it, and its own file shows the omission. Beyond the litigation risk, it is a certification problem — the 50059 also required that adult's signature. Control: a move-in checklist listing every signature by document, verified by a second person before the file is closed.\n\nWorked example 3 — the boundary case: house rules that conflict with the lease. House rules prohibit guests staying more than seven consecutive days; the lease and the property's TSP address guests differently; and the property has a live-in aide in residence. Three problems: an internal conflict, a rule that may be unenforceable as written, and a fair housing exposure if applied to a live-in aide or a care provider. House rules must be consistent with the lease, must be adopted properly, and must yield to reasonable accommodation obligations. Read them side by side with the lease at every engagement — twenty minutes, and it finds something almost every time.\n\nWorked example 4 — the lead-based paint file that cannot be produced. A property built in 1971 cannot locate lead disclosure forms for eleven households. The disclosure obligation is federal, separate from HUD occupancy rules, carries its own penalties, and requires retention. Remediation: execute disclosures now with current dates, document the discovery, install the control (the disclosure is a blocking item on the move-in checklist), and disclose the gap. Do not back-date. And check the property's records for the underlying lead inspection or evaluation documentation, because the disclosure references what the owner knows.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: the pet rules at a mixed property. A property has some units designated for elderly persons and persons with disabilities and some that are not. Pet rules and the rules governing pet ownership may apply differently across the property. Getting this wrong produces either unlawful restriction or unenforceable rules. Map the designations to the units, state the applicable rules for each, and — separately and prominently in the written policy — state that assistance animals are not pets and are governed by the accommodation process, so that staff do not run an accommodation request through the pet rules.\n\nWorked example 6 — the audit routine. Pull five leases from different years. Check: current model lease form; all adult signatures with dates; owner signature; correct rent, utility allowance and effective date; VAWA addendum executed; house rules attached and acknowledged; pet rules where applicable; lead disclosure for pre-1978 properties. Then compare the rent on the lease to the current 50059 and the rent roll. Five leases, fifteen minutes, and the answer tells you whether the property has a systemic lease problem or an isolated one."},{"heading":"The HAP Contract — Owner Obligations","body":"The Housing Assistance Payments contract is the agreement between the owner and HUD (or the Contract Administrator acting for HUD) under which assistance is paid. It is the source of most of the obligations a MOR tests, and owners routinely have never read it.\n\nWHAT THE HAP CONTRACT OBLIGATES THE OWNER TO DO:\n• Lease the assisted units only to ELIGIBLE families, selected in accordance with HUD requirements.\n• Comply with the occupancy requirements — eligibility, certification, rent computation — as prescribed by HUD.\n• Maintain the units and the project in DECENT, SAFE AND SANITARY condition (physical condition now assessed under NSPIRE).\n• Comply with EQUAL OPPORTUNITY and nondiscrimination requirements.\n• Maintain BOOKS AND RECORDS and permit HUD or its designee to inspect them — the basis for the MOR itself.\n• Submit required financial and operational reports.\n• Bill accurately, and refund or credit amounts received in error.\n• Comply with the terms governing rent adjustments, contract term, and renewal.\n\nCONTRACT TERM AND RENEWAL. Most project-based Section 8 contracts are renewed under the Multifamily Assisted Housing Reform and Affordability Act (MAHRA) framework, with renewal options and rent-setting mechanisms defined by HUD's Section 8 renewal policy. Rent adjustments at renewal are typically taken either by the OPERATING COST ADJUSTMENT FACTOR (OCAF), published annually by HUD, or through a BUDGET-BASED adjustment prepared under Handbook 4350.1 Chapter 7 procedures — with comparability and other requirements depending on the renewal option.\n\nWorked example 1 — the obligation owners forget: refunding amounts received in error. A property discovers it billed assistance for a unit for four months after the tenant vacated. The HAP contract obligates repayment; the discovery does not create the obligation, it merely reveals it. Owners who treat repayment as negotiable are misreading the contract. The consulting advice is the same every time: compute it, correct it, disclose it, and repay it — the cost of doing so is fixed, and the cost of being found not to have done so is not.\n\nWorked example 2 — the renewal notice that was missed. Section 8 contract renewals and rent adjustment requests run on deadlines — commonly a requirement to submit the renewal or adjustment request a set number of days before the anniversary (120 days is a common convention; confirm the requirement for the specific contract and renewal option). A property that misses the window can lose a year of an adjustment it was entitled to. This is not a MOR finding so much as a management failure with a direct dollar cost, and it is one of the easiest things for a consultant to prevent: build a contract calendar at intake with the anniversary date, the submission deadline, and the responsible person.\n\nWorked example 3 — OCAF versus budget-based, decided with arithmetic. A property's expenses rose sharply because of an insurance renewal.\n  OCAF for the year applied to the eligible portion of the contract rent yields an increase of roughly $31 per unit per month.\n  A budget-based adjustment, if approved on the documented figures, would yield roughly $58 per unit per month.\n  At 120 assisted units, the difference is $27 × 120 × 12 = $38,880 per year.\n  Against that: the preparation cost, the processing time, the scrutiny of every budget line, and the risk that the approved amount is lower than requested.\n  Decide deliberately with the numbers in front of the owner. What is not defensible is taking OCAF every year by default and then operating at a deficit that feeds the risk classification and shortens the MOR cycle.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: units under contract versus units at the property. A 150-unit property has 96 units under the HAP contract. The obligations that flow from the HAP contract attach to the assisted units, while the regulatory agreement (and, for insured properties, the mortgage) reaches the whole project — and fair housing law reaches everything. Properties get into trouble by assuming that unassisted units are outside every requirement. Map it explicitly for the client: which requirements attach to the contract, which to the regulatory agreement, and which to the property as a whole.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: an owner who wants to opt out. An owner contemplates not renewing at the end of the contract term. There are notice requirements to tenants and to HUD, enhanced voucher implications for residents, and state law overlays in some jurisdictions. This is legal work, not consulting work, and the correct advice is to get counsel involved early — but the consultant's contribution is real: the tenant notice obligations, the certification and TRACS mechanics of a contract expiration, and the records the property will need are all things you can prepare while counsel handles the rest.\n\nWorked example 6 — the intake habit. Ask for the HAP contract and every renewal amendment at the start of every engagement, and read the term, the rent schedule, the number of assisted units by size, and the adjustment mechanism. Half of what a MOR tests is in that document, and a surprising number of owners cannot produce it — which is itself a records finding and, more practically, means nobody at the property knows what they agreed to."},{"heading":"Lease Terminations — Process and Documentation","body":"Termination of tenancy in HUD-assisted multifamily housing is more constrained than ordinary landlord-tenant practice. The requirements come from the model lease, HUD Handbook 4350.3 Chapter 8, program regulations, and — layered on top — state and local law. Where they differ, the more protective requirement generally governs the tenant.\n\nTHE GROUNDS. Termination generally requires GOOD CAUSE: material noncompliance with the lease (including repeated minor violations that disrupt the livability of the project or interfere with others' rights), material failure to carry out obligations under state landlord-tenant law, or other good cause as defined in the rules. Nonpayment of rent is material noncompliance. Criminal activity and drug-related criminal activity have their own provisions.\n\nTHE NOTICE. The notice must:\n• State the specific date the tenancy will terminate.\n• State the REASONS with enough specificity that the tenant can prepare a defense — dates, conduct, and lease provisions.\n• State that the tenant has an opportunity to respond to management (and, for many terminations, the right to a meeting).\n• Advise the tenant of the right to defend in a judicial proceeding.\n• Be served in the manner the lease and applicable law require, with proof of service.\n• Be accompanied by the VAWA notice (HUD-5380) and certification form (HUD-5382) — required with any notice of eviction or termination of assistance.\n• Meet the required notice PERIODS, which vary by ground (nonpayment has a shorter period than most other grounds; certain drug and criminal activity grounds have their own rules), and which are set by the lease, the handbook and state law together.\n\nWorked example 1 — the notice that is too vague to enforce. A notice states: \"You are in violation of your lease due to disturbances. Your tenancy will terminate in 30 days.\" A court will very likely find this insufficient: the tenant cannot tell which incidents, when, or which lease provision. Rewrite: \"On March 3 at approximately 11:40 p.m., and again on March 19 at approximately 12:15 a.m., loud amplified music from your unit was the subject of complaints from residents of units 210 and 214, and was verified by staff. This conduct violates paragraph [x] of your lease and paragraph [y] of the House Rules, which prohibit noise that interferes with other residents' peaceful enjoyment. You received a written warning on March 6, a copy of which is enclosed.\"\n\nWorked example 2 — the termination that VAWA stops. A property issues a termination notice for repeated disturbances. The tenant responds with HUD-5382 certifying the disturbances arose from domestic violence perpetrated against her. Incidents of violence may not be treated as lease violations by the victim or as good cause for eviction of the victim. The property must halt the termination as to her, consider bifurcation to remove the perpetrator, and document the analysis. The correct posture is not reluctance — properties that resist here generate exactly the enforcement attention VAWA 2022's compliance review was designed to produce.\n\nWorked example 3 — the nonpayment case with a repayment agreement in the background. A household falls behind because of an unaffordable repayment agreement (Chapter 12: rent plus repayment should generally not exceed roughly 40 percent of monthly adjusted income). The property terminates for nonpayment. Two problems: the arrears may be attributable to an agreement the property should not have made, and if the underlying certification error was the property's, the equities are worse still. Before terminating for nonpayment where a repayment agreement exists, re-run the affordability analysis, restructure if it fails, and document. This is both the right thing and the defensible thing.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: a termination where a disability may be involved. A tenant with a mental health disability has accumulated housekeeping violations. The property has issued notices and is ready to terminate. Before proceeding: has the tenant requested an accommodation, and has anyone considered whether one is appropriate? A request may be made at any time, including after termination proceedings begin, and reflexively refusing to consider one at that stage is a serious exposure. The correct sequence documents the violations, documents any accommodation request and its analysis, considers whether an accommodation would enable compliance, and proceeds only after that record exists.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: termination of ASSISTANCE versus termination of TENANCY. These are different. A household may lose assistance — for failure to recertify, for exceeding an applicable limit under program rules, for failure to provide required information — while remaining a tenant paying a different rent, depending on the program and the circumstances. Staff who conflate them either terminate a tenancy they had no grounds to terminate, or fail to terminate assistance when the rules require it. Make the distinction explicit in the property's written procedures, with separate notice templates for each — and note that BOTH require the VAWA forms.\n\nWorked example 6 — the documentation package for any termination. The file should hold: the incidents with dates and evidence; prior warnings and their delivery; the notice with proof of service; the VAWA forms provided with the notice; any tenant response and the meeting record; any accommodation request and its analysis; the decision; and, where applicable, the judicial proceeding record. If any element is missing, the termination is weaker in court and the file is a finding on the MOR — the same gaps hurt in both forums, which is a point worth making to clients who see compliance and litigation as separate concerns."},{"heading":"House Rules — Adoption, Modification, and Enforcement","body":"House rules are the property's operational rules of the road. They are permitted, they are useful, and they are one of the most common sources of fair housing exposure in the portfolio, because they are usually written by well-meaning people without a fair housing review.\n\nTHE REQUIREMENTS:\n• House rules must be REASONABLE and related to the safety, care and cleanliness of the building or the safety, comfort and convenience of residents.\n• They must be CONSISTENT WITH THE LEASE and with HUD requirements. They cannot add obligations the lease does not support or contradict lease terms.\n• They must be properly ADOPTED and DISTRIBUTED to residents, with the notice the lease requires for changes.\n• They must be APPLIED UNIFORMLY, and must yield where a reasonable accommodation requires an exception.\n• They must not discriminate in purpose or effect against any protected class.\n\nWorked example 1 — the familial status audit, rule by rule. Take a typical set of house rules and test each:\n  \"Children may not play in the parking lot.\" — Reasonable as a safety rule if it prohibits PLAY IN THE PARKING LOT by anyone, phrased neutrally; singling out children is the problem, not the safety concern.\n  \"Children must be accompanied by an adult at all times outside the unit.\" — A classic familial status violation. It restricts children's use of the property in a way no adult faces.\n  \"No bicycles, scooters, or toys in common areas.\" — Applied to children's property while adults' items are tolerated, this is differential treatment. Written neutrally and applied to everyone's personal property, it may be defensible.\n  \"Quiet hours 10 p.m. to 7 a.m.\" — Neutral and reasonable.\n  \"Residents under 18 may not use the laundry room.\" — Restricts a facility by age; hard to defend as anything but familial status discrimination.\n  Rewriting rules to be conduct-based rather than person-based fixes most of this: prohibit the CONDUCT that creates the concern, applicable to everyone.\n\nWorked example 2 — the rule that collides with an accommodation. House rules prohibit any animals. A resident requests an assistance animal. The rule yields — assistance animals are not pets, and the accommodation obligation overrides the rule. The house rules should say so explicitly, so that staff encountering the request do not process it as a pet exception, charge a pet deposit, or apply breed restrictions. One sentence in the house rules prevents a category of violation.\n\nWorked example 3 — the rule enforced against some and not others. A property has a rule against storing items on balconies. Enforcement notices over two years went to seven households; a walk of the property shows fourteen balconies with stored items. The rule is fine; the enforcement is selective, and selective enforcement is where discrimination claims live. Control: enforcement driven by a documented, periodic inspection applied to all units, with notices issued to every household in violation, tracked on a log.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: adopting or changing house rules. Changes require the notice the lease provides and should be documented — the proposed change, the notice to residents, any resident comment where the process provides for it, the adoption, and the distribution with proof. A property that changes a rule by taping a sign in the lobby has not adopted anything enforceable. And a rule adopted properly but never distributed to households admitted afterward will fail when enforced against them.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: rules about guests. Guest limits are permissible and are also the rules most likely to cause trouble, because they intersect with household composition, live-in aides, care providers, and family caregiving patterns that vary across cultures. A defensible rule states a clear numeric limit tied to a legitimate concern (unauthorized occupancy), specifies how longer stays are approved, and states explicitly that live-in aides and care providers approved through the accommodation process are not guests. Then it must be enforced uniformly, which requires a record of approvals.\n\nWorked example 6 — the deliverable. Perform a HOUSE RULES REVIEW at every engagement: read each rule against the lease, against fair housing standards with all seven protected classes in mind, and against the accommodation obligation; note which rules are person-based rather than conduct-based; check the adoption and distribution record; and pull the enforcement log to test uniformity. It takes an hour, it produces a concrete redline the client can adopt, and it addresses the area where a MOR finding and a fair housing complaint are most likely to arise from the same document."},{"heading":"Tenant Grievance and Complaint Procedures","body":"Assisted properties need a defined way for residents to raise problems and get an answer. Requirements vary by program — some programs and some regulatory agreements impose specific grievance procedures, Section 504 requires recipients with 15 or more employees to adopt grievance procedures for disability discrimination complaints, and the model lease and HUD guidance address the tenant's opportunity to respond to notices. Confirm what applies to the specific property, and then build a procedure that meets the highest applicable standard.\n\nWHAT A WORKABLE PROCEDURE CONTAINS:\n• How a resident raises a concern — in writing, verbally, or through a form, with the property required to accept any of them.\n• Who receives it and within what time it is acknowledged.\n• How it is investigated and by whom.\n• An opportunity for the resident to present information, in person where the matter warrants it.\n• A written response with the outcome and the reasons.\n• An escalation path to someone above the person who made the initial decision.\n• Accessibility: accommodations for residents with disabilities and language access where needed.\n• Documentation: a log, and a file for each matter.\n• A statement that using the procedure will not result in retaliation.\n\nWorked example 1 — the procedure that exists only on paper. A property's grievance procedure is in a binder; residents have never been told about it; there is no log; and complaints are handled by whoever hears them. A resident later files a fair housing complaint alleging she raised an issue repeatedly and was ignored. The property cannot show what she raised, when, or what was done. The procedure did not help because it was never operative. Controls: post the procedure, include it in the move-in package, reference it in the annual notice, and log every complaint the same day it is received.\n\nWorked example 2 — the complaint that is actually an accommodation request. A resident complains that the trash area is too far from her building and she has difficulty carrying bags because of a health condition. Handled as a grievance, this goes into a queue about trash logistics. Handled correctly, it is a potential REASONABLE ACCOMMODATION request, which carries a promptness obligation and an interactive process. Train intake staff to spot the distinction: any complaint that references a disability, a health condition, or difficulty doing something others can do should be routed to the accommodation process as well as logged as a complaint.\n\nWorked example 3 — the complaint about another resident. A resident complains about a neighbor's conduct. The procedure must handle it without turning into resident-versus-resident arbitration: investigate the CONDUCT against the lease and house rules, take the appropriate enforcement step if warranted, respond to the complainant about what was done (without disclosing another household's private information), and document. Note the fair housing dimension from Chapter 16: if the complaint carries a discriminatory framing, or if the conduct complained of appears to be harassment of a protected person, the property has its own obligations and cannot simply mediate.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: a grievance about a rent calculation. A resident disputes her TTP. This is not really a grievance in the general sense; it is a certification dispute with its own path — explain the computation with the handbook basis, re-verify if there is any doubt, correct if wrong, and if the property's determination stands, document the explanation given. The two most useful things here: never adjust a figure merely to end a dispute, and always treat a resident's objection as a possible signal of a genuine error before defending the number.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: an anonymous complaint. A note is slipped under the office door alleging that a staff member is demanding favors from residents. Anonymity limits investigation and does not permit inaction. Document the receipt, escalate to ownership and, given the nature of the allegation, to counsel; investigate what can be investigated (records, other residents' experiences, patterns); and take appropriate protective steps. Allegations of this nature are not grievance-log matters — they are escalation matters, and the procedure should say which categories bypass the ordinary path.\n\nWorked example 6 — the metric to report. Two numbers, quarterly: the number of complaints received and the median days from receipt to written response. A property with zero complaints is not a property with no problems; it is usually a property where residents have no functioning way to raise them, and that is what a reviewer or a fair housing tester will eventually surface. A property with complaints, prompt responses, and a documented log is demonstrating exactly the management capacity the MOR exists to assess."}]},{"id":"m123","unit":"u12","number":3,"title":"Financial Management — Budgets, RFR, Audits, and HAP Billing","subtitle":"","sections":[{"heading":"Operating Budget Management","body":"The operating budget is the property's plan expressed in dollars. On a MOR it is examined through Addendum B where the Contract Administrator's scope includes financial review; where it does not, HUD asset management reviews the same material independently. Either way, the discipline is the same.\n\nWHAT GOOD BUDGET MANAGEMENT LOOKS LIKE:\n• An annual budget prepared on documented assumptions — occupancy, rent levels, contract rent adjustments, known cost increases, and the required reserve deposit.\n• Budget-to-actual comparison, monthly or at least quarterly, with variance explanations written at the time.\n• Explicit funding of the reserve for replacement deposit as a non-discretionary line.\n• A capital plan that connects to the reserve and to the property's actual condition.\n• Documentation supporting any budget-based rent adjustment request, prepared under Handbook 4350.1 Chapter 7 procedures, with form HUD-92547-A as the budget worksheet where applicable.\n\nWorked example 1 — the occupancy assumption that guarantees a variance. A budget projects 97 percent occupancy at a property that has run 89 percent for two years with eleven names on its waiting list.\n  Revenue overstated: at 120 units and an average gross rent of $1,050, eight points of occupancy is roughly 9.6 units × $1,050 × 12 = $120,960 per year.\n  Every month will show an unfavorable revenue variance nobody can explain, because the explanation is the assumption itself.\n  The fix runs through the occupancy department, not the spreadsheet: the AFHMP, outreach, application processing time, and unit turnaround. Then budget to what the property can actually achieve, and treat improvement as a plan with milestones rather than an assumption.\n\nWorked example 2 — the variance that invites the wrong questions. Maintenance payroll budgeted at $148,000; actual $211,000; no explanation on file. The reviewer's next questions are not about payroll — they are about whether costs are properly allocated to this project, whether identity-of-interest entities are involved, and whether the project is being charged for services it did not receive. A one-paragraph variance note written when the variance occurred (\"contracted landscaping terminated in March; work brought in-house; offsetting reduction in contract services line\") answers all of it. Variance explanation is cheap; its absence is expensive.\n\nWorked example 3 — the budget that hides deferred maintenance. A property reports favorable variances in maintenance and capital lines three years running while NSPIRE scores decline and the capital needs assessment grows. Under-spending is not a strength here; it is operating results propped up by underinvestment, and it shows up in physical condition, in the reserve analysis, and eventually in the risk classification — which under the 2022 rule shortens the MOR cycle. Read variances in both directions and say so to owners who present under-budget as a win.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: a mid-year cost shock. An insurance renewal comes in 42 percent above budget, an increase of $58,000 annually. Options: absorb it and accept a deficit; pursue a budget-based rent adjustment for the next contract year; reduce discretionary spending; or examine coverage structure with a broker (while keeping coverage adequate — see Chapter 8's coinsurance example). The wrong response is to fund it by skipping reserve deposits, which converts an operating problem into a regulatory agreement violation. Rank the options with the owner and document the decision.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: allocating shared costs. An owner operates three properties with a shared maintenance crew and a shared administrative office. Allocations must be reasonable, consistently applied, documented, and supportable — time records for shared labor, a defensible basis for administrative allocation (unit count, square footage, or documented effort), and no allocation of costs that benefit only another property. Auditors test this and so do reviewers where the financial review is in scope. Build the allocation methodology in writing once, apply it consistently, and revisit it annually.\n\nWorked example 6 — the deliverable. A one-page BUDGET DISCIPLINE MEMO: the assumptions behind each major line with their sources, the variance threshold that triggers a written explanation (a percentage and a dollar floor), who writes explanations and by when, and the review cadence. Most properties have a budget and no process around it; the process is what a reviewer sees."},{"heading":"Reserve for Replacement — Funding, Withdrawals, and Planning","body":"The Reserve for Replacement is funded by required monthly deposits, held under HUD's control, and released only with HUD approval for capital items. It is one of the most reliably deficient areas in the portfolio.\n\nTHE MECHANICS:\n• The regulatory agreement (and for many properties the HAP contract or its renewal terms) sets the required monthly deposit, which HUD may adjust — and adjustments are frequently missed when they occur.\n• Funds are held in an interest-bearing account under HUD's control.\n• Releases are requested on FORM HUD-9250, \"Funds Authorizations,\" which covers both Reserve for Replacement and Residual Receipts releases, with supporting documentation.\n• Records of releases are retained — HUD's guidance contemplates retention of HUD-9250 authorizations for the current year and the previous three years.\n• Reserve balances and deposit activity appear in the audited financial statements and are reconciled.\n\nWorked example 1 — the deposits that stopped, with the arithmetic. Required deposit $4,180 per month. Deposits made through August 2024; stopped for four months during a cash shortage; resumed January 2025.\n  Shortfall: $4,180 × 4 = $16,720.\n  Consequences: a compliance finding, a regulatory agreement default in substance, likely restriction on distributions, and a note in the audited statements.\n  Remediation: fund the arrears, document the catch-up, and disclose.\n  Control: the deposit is an automated transfer on a fixed date, reconciled monthly by someone other than the person who initiates it.\n\nWorked example 2 — the release request that gets rejected, and the one that does not. A property submits HUD-9250 for $62,000 for roofing on two buildings with a single proposal and no scope detail. Common rejection reasons: insufficient documentation of need, no evidence of competitive procurement where expected, items that are operating rather than capital expenses, work already performed without prior approval, or an amount that would leave the reserve at an unacceptable level.\n  The prepared request states: the item and its age and condition (with the inspection or engineer's report), the scope of work, the procurement process and the bids received, the amount requested, the reserve balance before and after, and the completion timeline. It takes an afternoon to assemble and converts a rejection into an approval.\n\nWorked example 3 — the categorization error, worked. A property charges $9,400 of turnover painting and carpet cleaning to the reserve.\n  Painting and cleaning on turnover are ordinarily OPERATING expenses. Carpet REPLACEMENT on a defined useful-life cycle may be a capital item.\n  The effect is double: the reserve intended for capital needs is depleted, and operating results look better than they are.\n  Control: a written capitalization policy stating what is capital and what is operating, with dollar thresholds and useful-life references, applied consistently and reviewed by the accountant.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: emergency work before approval. A boiler fails in February and is replaced immediately for $38,000; the HUD-9250 follows. Emergency work is a real category and after-the-fact approvals do happen — and the property's position depends entirely on contemporaneous documentation: the failure, the health and safety consequence, the attempt to contact HUD or the Account Executive, the procurement done under the circumstances, and prompt submission afterward. A property that documents in real time is usually fine; a property that submits six months later with one invoice is not.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: compliance versus adequacy. A property makes every deposit on time and holds $211,000 against a capital needs assessment showing $1.4 million over ten years. It is COMPLIANT and INADEQUATE. The MOR tests the deposit requirement; asset management and the risk classification test adequacy. This distinction is a consulting opportunity: a compliant-but-underfunded reserve is the evidence-based argument for a deposit increase or a budget-based rent adjustment, and framing it that way converts a compliance engagement into a strategic one the owner will pay more for.\n\nWorked example 6 — residual receipts, the account owners forget. Where the regulatory agreement requires it, surplus cash above allowable distributions goes to a residual receipts account, also under HUD control and also released on HUD-9250. An owner who takes distributions without correctly computing surplus cash creates BOTH an improper distribution and a residual receipts deficiency — two findings from one act. Compute surplus cash from the audited statements annually, before any distribution.\n\nWorked example 7 — the reserve planning artifact. Build a ten-year RESERVE PROJECTION: opening balance, annual deposits, interest, planned withdrawals by component with expected years, and closing balance by year. When the projection goes negative in year six, the owner has a concrete, dated problem to solve — and a document to attach to a deposit increase or rent adjustment request. Without it, \"the reserve seems low\" is an opinion."},{"heading":"Audits — Requirements, Findings, and Responses","body":"The annual audit is both an obligation and an early-warning system. Under HUD's uniform financial reporting standards at 24 CFR Part 5, Subpart H, covered owners must submit financial information annually, NO LATER THAN 90 DAYS AFTER FISCAL YEAR END, electronically through FASSUB — for most assisted and insured properties, audited financial statements with the auditor's opinion, supplemental schedules, the surplus cash computation, footnotes, owner certifications, and reports on internal control and on compliance.\n\nWHAT REVIEWERS DO WITH THE AUDIT:\n• Confirm TIMELY submission, which is visible in HUD's system independent of anything the owner submits.\n• Read the OPINION. A qualified opinion, going-concern emphasis, or disclaimer is a serious signal.\n• Read the FINDINGS in the compliance and internal control reports, which frequently overlap MOR territory.\n• Compare the statements to operations: occupancy and vacancy loss, HAP revenue against TRACS, reserve deposits against the required schedule, and distributions against surplus cash.\n\nWorked example 1 — the deadline arithmetic clients get wrong. A December 31 fiscal year end is due by roughly March 31. A June 30 fiscal year end is due by roughly September 28. Owners with multiple properties on different fiscal years apply one deadline to all of them and miss the others. Build a fiscal-year calendar for the whole portfolio at intake. (HUD has at times granted filing extensions; treat any extension as specific to the year and property it was granted for and retain the written evidence.)\n\nWorked example 2 — the audit finding that becomes a MOR finding six months later. The compliance report states that \"tenant files tested did not consistently contain evidence of third-party income verification.\" The MOR reviewer reads it during the desk review and now has an owner-acknowledged pattern before opening a file. Two consequences: read the audit FIRST at every intake, because it tells you where the property is weak; and remediate audit findings on their own schedule rather than leaving them to be rediscovered.\n\nWorked example 3 — the reconciliation that ends an inquiry before it starts. HAP revenue per the audited statements is $1,412,880; the sum of TRACS vouchers for the period is $1,398,240 — a $14,640 difference. Possible causes: year-end timing, adjustment vouchers processed in a later period, special claims, or an actual billing error. The reviewer's concern is not the difference but whether the owner can EXPLAIN it. Prepare a one-page reconciliation with the statements. A prepared reconciliation ends the conversation; an unprepared one starts an inquiry that will reach the tenant files.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: which properties file what. Submission requirements differ by program, by whether the property is insured, and by the size and nature of the federal assistance received. A 12-unit property under one program may face different requirements than a 200-unit insured property. Do not generalize from the last client — confirm from the regulatory agreement, the HAP contract and current HUD guidance, and if you cannot confirm, get the owner's accountant on the call rather than guessing. Saying \"I need to confirm that\" is a better professional look than being wrong in writing.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: an audit finding the owner disputes. The auditor reports a material weakness in internal control over cash receipts; the owner believes the auditor misunderstood the process. The audit is what it is — but the owner's RESPONSE, included in the reporting package, is the owner's opportunity to state its position and its corrective action. Write that response carefully: acknowledge what is accurate, state the correction and the control implemented, and where the owner disagrees, state the basis factually and without heat. That response will be read by the MOR reviewer, the Account Executive, and possibly a lender.\n\nWorked example 6 — the going-concern paragraph and the MOR cycle. An auditor includes a substantial-doubt emphasis based on negative working capital and deferred maintenance. This rarely appears as an occupancy finding, but it feeds the property's RISK CLASSIFICATION, and a Troubled classification caps the reward for even a Superior MOR at 24 months instead of 36. Tell clients this explicitly, because otherwise a property with excellent files cannot understand why it did not get the longest review interval."},{"heading":"HAP Billing and TRACS — Accuracy and Timeliness","body":"HAP billing is where occupancy compliance becomes money. The owner transmits certification data (the 50059 family) to TRACS and bills monthly through the voucher process; HUD or the Contract Administrator pays against the voucher. The reviewer compares vouchers to certifications to the rent roll to the tenant files, and every mismatch is a thread to pull.\n\nWHAT REVIEWERS EXAMINE:\n• TIMELINESS of certification transmission, which is visible in TRACS.\n• Assistance billed for units or households not entitled to it — a move-out not terminated, a mishandled transfer, an ineligible household.\n• ADJUSTMENTS and SPECIAL CLAIMS, and whether they carry the required support.\n• REPAYMENT activity: amounts owed by tenants, agreements in place, and amounts owed back to HUD.\n• Consistency between utility reimbursements on the voucher and the tenant ledgers.\n\nWorked example 1 — the late certification and its two consequences. An annual effective July 1 is transmitted September 12.\n  Consequence one: a timeliness finding.\n  Consequence two: July and August were billed at the OLD assistance amount. If the recertification reduced assistance, the property was overpaid for two months and the correction reduces a future voucher; if it increased assistance, the property was underpaid and must claim the difference.\n  A pattern of retroactive adjustments is a finding in its own right, because it evidences a process that routinely misses effective dates.\n\nWorked example 2 — the move-out that kept paying, with the control. A tenant vacates March 18; the termination is transmitted effective June 1.\n  Assistance was billed for late March, April and May — an overpayment to be repaid, and depending on the facts it may implicate special claim rules for the vacancy.\n  Control: the move-out checklist ENDS with the TRACS termination, verified by a second person; and the monthly reconciliation compares occupied units on the rent roll to assisted units in TRACS, with any unit assisted-but-vacant investigated the same week.\n\nWorked example 3 — the income error traced all the way through. A household's annual income was understated by $3,600 because a second job was never counted; the error persisted 14 months.\n  TTP understated by roughly $90 per month (30 percent of $3,600 ÷ 12).\n  Tenant underpayment: $1,260. HAP overpayment: $1,260.\n  The property repays HUD $1,260 AND pursues $1,260 from the tenant under an affordable repayment agreement — and absorbs it if the tenant cannot pay.\n  Show a client this arithmetic once and file-level accuracy stops being an abstraction.\n\nWorked example 4 — the special claim that fails for documentation. A property submits a vacancy claim following a move-out. Claims must meet the program's specific conditions and carry the required documentation — the circumstances of the vacancy, the property's efforts to fill the unit, the timeframes, and the amounts. A claim submitted with a bare assertion is denied, and a pattern of denied or unsupported claims draws attention to the billing process generally. Build a claim checklist per claim type and assemble the support at the time of the event, not at submission.\n\nWorked example 5 — the boundary case: corrections that cross fiscal years. A correction discovered in February relates to a certification effective the prior August, spanning two fiscal years. The correction is still processed on its correct effective date; the accounting treatment and the reconciliation to the audited statements need to account for the period. Coordinate with the accountant BEFORE transmitting a large retroactive correction so that the statements and the vouchers tell the same story. Reviewers reconcile them.\n\nWorked example 6 — the monthly voucher review, which is the whole control in one habit. Before submitting each voucher, run four checks: (1) every assisted unit in TRACS is occupied per the rent roll; (2) every occupied assisted unit has a current certification; (3) every certification effective this month was transmitted; (4) utility reimbursements on the voucher tie to the tenant ledgers. Fifteen minutes per month. It catches nearly every billing error before it becomes a repayment, and the signed check-off sheet is exactly the evidence a reviewer wants to see."},{"heading":"Distributions, Identity of Interest, and Regulatory Agreement Compliance","body":"The regulatory agreement governs how project funds may be used and what the owner may take out. Violations here are not paperwork problems; they are defaults, and they carry consequences beyond the MOR.\n\nTHE CORE OBLIGATIONS:\n• PROJECT FUNDS FOR PROJECT PURPOSES. Funds may be used only for the project's operations and obligations.\n• DISTRIBUTIONS only from SURPLUS CASH, only in permitted amounts, only at permitted times, and only when the property is in compliance (including reserve funding and, where applicable, physical condition standards).\n• BOOKS AND RECORDS maintained and made available.\n• No unapproved ENCUMBRANCES, transfers of project property, changes in ownership, or changes in management agent.\n• IDENTITY-OF-INTEREST arrangements disclosed and, where required, approved — with services actually provided at reasonable cost.\n\nWorked example 1 — the improper distribution, quantified. An owner takes a $75,000 distribution in a year in which the surplus cash computation shows $41,000 available.\n  Excess: $34,000, which must be returned to the project.\n  It is a regulatory agreement violation, it appears in the audit, and it flows into the MOR.\n  Compounding factor: if the owner also failed to fund residual receipts where required, the same act produced two findings.\n  Control: no distribution is taken until the audited surplus cash computation is complete and reviewed, with a written approval step.\n\nWorked example 2 — the identity-of-interest contract, done defensibly. An owner affiliate provides landscaping at $6,200 per month. The arrangement is permitted, and its defensibility rests on four things: the relationship is DISCLOSED; the services are ACTUALLY PROVIDED (with evidence — schedules, logs, invoices tied to work); the price is REASONABLE relative to market (with two or three independent bids obtained contemporaneously); and required approvals are obtained. Owners who cannot produce a contemporaneous market comparison rarely construct a persuasive one after the fact. Get the bids when the contract is signed, and re-bid periodically.\n\nWorked example 3 — the expense that is not a project expense. A property's books show $18,400 in \"consulting fees\" paid to an entity owned by a principal, with no contract, no scope, and no deliverable. This is the fact pattern that turns a financial review into an enforcement referral. Whatever the underlying reality, the absence of a contract, scope and evidence of services makes it indefensible. The advice to clients is simple and worth repeating: every payment from project funds needs a contract, a scope, evidence the service was delivered, and a reasonable price — and payments to related parties need all four in writing before the money moves.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: an owner advance and its repayment. An owner advances $60,000 to cover an operating shortfall and later repays itself from operations. Owner advances and their repayment are governed by the regulatory agreement and by surplus cash rules, and repayment outside those rules is an improper distribution by another name. Document the advance when it is made — the amount, the purpose, the terms — and confirm the repayment mechanism before repaying. Retroactively characterizing a withdrawal as \"repayment of an advance\" with no contemporaneous record is not persuasive.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: a change in ownership structure. Principals reorganize into a new single-purpose entity as mortgagor, with HUD approval through the previews/APPS process. Is this a \"change of ownership\"? Treat it as yes for the scheduling rule from Chapter 1 — a new MOR within 6 months of an approved ownership or management agent change — and ask in writing at the time of approval. Also confirm what the change requires under the regulatory agreement and the mortgage: consents, certifications, and updated management documents. The cost of preparing for a review that does not come is a fraction of the cost of being surprised by one that does.\n\nWorked example 6 — the taxes-and-insurance discipline that belongs here. Delinquent real estate taxes are a default under most regulatory agreements, and a pending assessment appeal generally does not suspend the obligation to pay. Where the jurisdiction permits, pay under protest and document the appeal. Likewise the insurance requirements — including the fidelity bond covering those who handle project funds, and flood coverage where the property is in a special flood hazard area, verified annually because FEMA remaps. Both are cheap to verify at intake and both produce serious consequences when missed.\n\nWorked example 7 — the compliance calendar deliverable. Build the client a REGULATORY AGREEMENT CALENDAR: financial statement due date; distribution timing and the surplus cash computation that precedes it; reserve deposit schedule and any adjustment; insurance renewals with the flood zone verification; tax due dates; contract anniversary and rent adjustment submission deadline; and management agreement and certification renewals. One page, one owner per item, reviewed quarterly. Most of the financial findings in this unit are calendar failures rather than judgment failures, and a calendar is the cheapest control in this book."}]},{"id":"m131","unit":"u13","number":1,"title":"Your MOR Consulting Services — What to Offer and How to Package Them","subtitle":"","sections":[{"heading":"The MOR Consulting Market — Why It Exists and Why Now","body":"The market for MOR consulting exists because of a permanent structural mismatch: the compliance obligations at an assisted property are extensive, technical, and constantly changing, while the staff responsible for them are typically one or two people who also lease units, handle resident issues, and answer the phone. Nobody at a 120-unit property has time to track a HOTMA compliance date that moved three times.\n\nFour forces make the market larger right now than it was five years ago:\n\n1. THE RISK-BASED MOR SCHEDULE. Under the 2022 final rule, a Not Troubled property scoring Above Average or Superior is reviewed within 36 months rather than 12. That changes the value proposition from \"pass your review\" to \"be reviewed a third as often,\" which is a quantifiable operational saving rather than a soft benefit.\n2. THE HOTMA TRANSITION. A mandatory regime change in income and asset determination, with a compliance date of January 1, 2027 for Multifamily under Notice H 2025-07, requiring policy rewrites, worksheet builds, software testing, staff training and resident notices at every property in the country. That is a deadline-driven project with a defined scope.\n3. VAWA 2022's EXPANDED COMPLIANCE REVIEW, which turned a general awareness obligation into five separately examined elements.\n4. STAFF TURNOVER. Occupancy specialists carry a body of knowledge that leaves with them, and the properties least able to absorb turnover are the ones most likely to experience it.\n\nWHO BUYS. Understand the three buyer types, because they buy differently:\n• THE OWNER, who cares about risk, cost and asset value. Sells on consequences: repayment exposure, enforcement, the review cycle.\n• THE MANAGEMENT AGENT (regional or corporate), who cares about portfolio consistency and about not being replaced. Sells on standardization and defensibility.\n• THE SITE MANAGER, who cares about surviving the next review and about not being blamed. Sells on relief and on making them look good — and remember that the site manager is often the person who first says your name to someone with a budget.\n\nWorked example 1 — the value calculation that closes an owner. A 140-unit Section 8 property, Not Troubled, currently Satisfactory with recurring findings.\n  Current state: MOR every 24 months. Each cycle costs roughly 120 internal staff hours (preparation, the visit, the response), plus disruption, plus the corrective action work.\n  Target state: Above Average, MOR every 36 months.\n  Over six years: three reviews instead of four — one entire cycle eliminated. At a loaded internal cost of, say, $55 per hour, 120 hours is $6,600 per cycle in staff time alone, before counting the corrective action work, the risk of repayment, and the management attention.\n  Your engagement: a $14,000 first-year program (assessment, remediation, mock MOR, training) and a $9,000 annual retainer.\n  The owner's calculus is not \"does this pay for itself in saved staff hours\" — it is that plus reduced repayment exposure plus a defensible position with HUD plus one fewer disruption every six years. Put all four in the proposal, with the schedule matrix from Chapter 1 reproduced.\n\nWorked example 2 — the buyer who is not the owner. A regional manager for a 9-property portfolio wants help but has no budget authority. The mistake is to spend three meetings persuading her. The correct move: help her build the internal case — a one-page memo she can send upward showing the current review posture of each property, the repeat findings across the portfolio, the HOTMA transition deadline, and a scoped proposal with a portfolio price. You are not selling to her; you are equipping her to sell for you. That memo is free and it is the highest-conversion document in this business.\n\nWorked example 3 — the property that cannot pay. A struggling 60-unit property with an Unsatisfactory rating badly needs help and has no money. Options: a smaller scoped engagement targeting only the highest-weighted items (EIV, certification timeliness, the corrective action response); payment terms spread across the corrective action period; or a decline. What you should NOT do is a full-scope engagement at a discount that leaves you resentful and the client under-served. Say what you can do for the money available, in writing, and be specific about what is excluded.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: the client who wants a guarantee. An owner asks you to guarantee an Above Average rating. You cannot, and you should say so plainly: the rating depends on a reviewer's judgment, on the CA's conventions, on conditions you do not control, and on the client's own execution. What you CAN commit to is scope and deliverables — a defined assessment, a defined remediation list, a mock review against Form 9834, a training session, and a written readiness report. Sell the process, never the outcome. Consultants who promise outcomes in this field end up in disputes they cannot win.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: the client whose real problem is not compliance. You are engaged for MOR prep and discover the property has one occupancy specialist for 200 assisted units, chronic turnover, and a regional manager who has not visited in a year. No amount of file cleanup fixes that. The honest engagement says so: deliver the compliance work you were hired for, AND deliver a staffing and capacity analysis with the arithmetic (certifications per year × hours per certification against available hours). Clients rarely fire the consultant who tells them the truth with numbers attached; they frequently stop calling the one who cleans files and says nothing.\n\nWorked example 6 — the market you can actually reach. Start where you have standing: the state's affordable housing association membership list, the PBCA's published list of assisted properties, HUD's public property data, and the owners and agents you already know. A first-year practice does not need a national brand; it needs eight properties within driving distance and a reputation among the regional managers who talk to each other constantly."},{"heading":"Core Service Offerings — The MOR Prep Suite","body":"Define your services as PRODUCTS with names, fixed scopes, deliverables and prices. Hourly-and-see-what-happens is how consultants end up underpaid and clients end up disappointed.\n\nTHE SUITE:\n\n1. COMPLIANCE ASSESSMENT (the entry product). A defined-scope review producing a written report: file sample audit results by error category, EIV compliance status across all three legs, policy currency review, waiting list and AFHMP review, prior MOR analysis with repeat findings, and a prioritized remediation list. Deliverable: a written report and a two-hour presentation. Typical scope: 25 to 40 hours depending on size.\n2. REMEDIATION PROJECT. Executing the fixes: policy rewrites, EIV master file build, certification corrections, file reconstruction, notice and log systems. Scoped from the assessment findings.\n3. MOCK MOR. A full simulation against Form 9834, including a file sample using the reviewer's risk-weighting logic, a staff interview simulation, a document production test, and a written report in the reviewer's own format with a predicted rating range.\n4. STAFF TRAINING. Modular: certification mechanics, EIV, fair housing and accommodations, VAWA, waiting list and TSP, and the interview preparation session.\n5. DESK REVIEW PACKAGE PREPARATION. Index build, document assembly, cover memo, and the adversarial read.\n6. ON-SITE REVIEW SUPPORT. Presence during the review, exit conference participation, and the 48-hour written summary.\n7. CORRECTIVE ACTION PLAN DEVELOPMENT. The post-review response with exhibits (Section 3).\n8. HOTMA TRANSITION PROJECT. Policy revision, worksheet build, software test protocol, the two-column TTP impact report, notice campaign, training, and post-implementation audits at 30 and 90 days.\n9. ONGOING RETAINER. Monthly or quarterly: file sampling, EIV log review, policy watch, and a standing question line.\n\nWorked example 1 — the assessment scoped honestly. A 96-unit property.\n  File sample: 25 files selected on the reviewer's risk-weighting logic (recent move-ins, interims, zero-income, live-in aides, prior-finding files, EIV discrepancies, recent move-outs) — 12 hours.\n  EIV review across verification, monitoring reports and security — 4 hours.\n  Policies: TSP, house rules, EIV, VAWA documents, AFHMP, accommodation procedure — 5 hours.\n  Waiting list and admissions reconciliation — 4 hours.\n  Prior MOR analysis and repeat-finding table — 2 hours.\n  Report writing and presentation — 8 hours.\n  Total 35 hours. Price it as a fixed fee, state the file sample size in the engagement letter, and state explicitly what is NOT included (correcting the errors found is a separate project).\n\nWorked example 2 — the productized training menu. Rather than \"we do training,\" offer: a 3-hour Certification Mechanics workshop; a 2-hour EIV Compliance session; a 2-hour Fair Housing and Accommodations session; a 90-minute VAWA session; and a 90-minute MOR Interview Preparation session. Each with an agenda, materials, a sign-in sheet you provide, and a certificate. The sign-in sheet and certificate are not administrative overhead — they are the artifact the client needs for the MOR training record, which means your deliverable is worth more than the session itself.\n\nWorked example 3 — the mock MOR priced as the flagship. For a 140-unit property: 30 sampled files against Addendum A, a full document production test against Addendum C, four staff interviews, a physical walk of common areas and five units focused on management systems, and a written report in Form 9834 format with a predicted rating range and a prioritized 90-day action list. Roughly 45 to 60 hours. This is your highest-value product because it is the closest thing to the real event, and because the report is a document the client can hand to their owner.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: what to do when the assessment finds something serious. Your file review uncovers what appears to be systematic overbilling — assistance paid on units vacant for months, across several units. This is beyond a compliance finding. Stop, document what you found factually, present it to the client in writing with a recommendation to involve counsel and to self-disclose, and be clear that you are not the person who decides. Note in your engagement letter, from the beginning, what you will do if you find potential fraud — it is far easier to have that conversation at signing than at discovery.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: scope creep with a sympathetic face. You are engaged for an assessment; the site manager asks you to \"just fix\" a few files while you are there, then a few more. Two problems: you are now doing remediation inside an assessment budget, and you are auditing your own work when the assessment report is written. Handle it with a sentence prepared in advance: \"I can fix those under a remediation engagement — let me note them on the list and send you a short scope tomorrow.\" Then actually send it the next day. Consultants lose more money to friendly scope creep than to bad pricing.\n\nWorked example 6 — the packaging that sells best. Bundle the entry: ASSESSMENT + PRIORITIZED PLAN + a half-day training, at a fixed fee, delivered in three weeks. It is small enough to approve without a committee, concrete enough to evaluate, and it produces the document that justifies the remediation project. Roughly two-thirds of well-run assessments convert to remediation work, which is the actual business."},{"heading":"Corrective Action Plan Consulting — A High-Value Niche","body":"The corrective action response after a Below Average or Unsatisfactory rating is the best-defined engagement in this business: a hard deadline, an anxious client, a specified deliverable, and a decision-maker who is paying attention.\n\nWHY IT IS VALUABLE:\n• The deadline is external and immovable, so the decision to hire happens fast.\n• The scope is defined by the findings list rather than negotiated.\n• The client's alternative — writing it themselves — has already failed once, which is why they have this rating.\n• The work product is visible to the owner, the Account Executive and the Contract Administrator, which is where your next referral comes from.\n\nWHAT THE ENGAGEMENT INCLUDES:\n1. Read the report, the prior report, the owner's prior responses, and the exit conference notes.\n2. Triage the findings: factually wrong (appeal candidates), curable now, curable with time, and uncurable-but-controllable.\n3. Determine ROOT CAUSE for each — the process failure, not the person.\n4. Execute or supervise the CURE, with evidence.\n5. Draft the response, finding by finding, with the structure from Chapter 2: finding restated, concurrence, root cause, correction, corrective action, scope of review, evidence, responsible person, completion date.\n6. Assemble the exhibits.\n7. Coordinate any APPEAL — remembering the 30 calendar day deadline from the report/transmittal date under Handbook 4350.1 Chapter 6, with decisions typically due back within 45 days.\n8. Deliver on time, with a transmission record.\n\nWorked example 1 — the eleven-finding engagement, scoped and priced. A 180-unit property, Below Average, eleven findings, 30-day response deadline.\n  Days 1-3: document review, triage, and a written work plan with the client's obligations listed.\n  Days 3-14: cure execution — certifications corrected, EIV master file built, policies revised, training delivered, logs started.\n  Days 10-20: evidence assembly, exhibit preparation.\n  Days 15-24: drafting, finding by finding.\n  Days 24-27: client review and revision.\n  Day 28: submission with confirmation.\n  Roughly 80 to 110 hours. Price as a FIXED FEE with a defined exhibit list and a stated client-obligation list (\"client provides the following documents by day 5\"). Hourly billing on a fixed deadline punishes you for the client's delays.\n\nWorked example 2 — the appeal decision made correctly. Of the eleven findings, two are wrong: one duplicates another and one cites a file that was not in the sample. Nine are valid. The right structure: file the appeal on the two within 30 days, and submit corrective action documentation on all nine on their own due dates, because corrective action deadlines are not tolled by an appeal. Explain this to the client explicitly — many owners believe an appeal pauses everything, and that belief is how a curable finding becomes a failure to correct.\n\nWorked example 3 — the response that must not overstate. A draft says \"all 148 files have been reviewed and corrected.\" The property actually reviewed 148 files and corrected 31; 9 corrections are pending tenant signatures. Write what is true, with the numbers: \"148 of 148 active files reviewed (log at Exhibit 3-B). 31 required correction; 22 corrected and transmitted as of [date] (Exhibit 3-C); 9 pending tenant signature with follow-up scheduled and a completion date of [date].\" Precision reads as control. Overstatement, discovered at a follow-up review, is the single fastest way to convert a recoverable situation into an unrecoverable one — and it puts your name on the document.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: a due date you cannot meet. A finding requires 100 percent file review in 30 days at a property with two occupancy staff. Request an extension IN WRITING BEFORE the due date, with a specific alternative schedule and an interim deliverable, and document the response. Extensions requested in advance are routinely granted; extensions requested after the fact are a second finding. Put the extension request in your work plan on day 3, not day 27.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: the client who wants you to write something you disagree with. The owner wants the response to blame the prior management agent for everything. Some of that may be true and relevant — a documented takeover baseline is legitimate evidence. What is not acceptable is a response that attributes to the prior agent findings the current agent caused, or that omits current-period failures. Your name is not on the submission, but your work is, and the CA and the AE will read the next one too. Write what the evidence supports, tell the client where the evidence does not support their preferred framing, and let them decide with that on the record.\n\nWorked example 6 — the follow-through that creates the retainer. Deliver the response, then propose the obvious next step: a 90-day verification review confirming the corrective actions are still operating, and a mock MOR before the next review, which is now within 12 months because of the rating. The client has just experienced the cost of failing; this is the moment they are most willing to invest in not repeating it. Propose it in the same meeting where you deliver the response, in writing, with a price."},{"heading":"Pricing Strategy and Engagement Structures","body":"Price on VALUE and SCOPE, not on hours. The hours matter for your own planning; the client is buying a defined outcome and a defined deliverable.\n\nTHE THREE STRUCTURES AND WHEN EACH FITS:\n• FIXED FEE — the default for defined-scope projects: assessments, mock MORs, corrective action plans, HOTMA transitions, training. The client knows the cost; you keep the efficiency gains from your own systems.\n• RETAINER — monthly or quarterly for ongoing readiness: file sampling, EIV log review, policy watch, question line, quarterly report. Predictable revenue for you, predictable budget for them, and it is where a practice becomes a business rather than a series of projects.\n• HOURLY — reserved for genuinely open-ended work: an unfolding enforcement matter, litigation support, or a discovery phase where scope truly cannot be defined. Always with an estimate and a not-to-exceed.\n\nPRICING INPUTS: property size and number of assisted units; number of programs layered; current compliance posture (an Unsatisfactory property is more work, not less); travel; the deadline (a 30-day turnaround is worth more than a 90-day one); and your own delivery efficiency.\n\nWorked example 1 — a fixed-fee schedule you can defend. For a single-property engagement, illustrative:\n  Compliance Assessment: 60-100 units, $6,500; 101-200 units, $8,500; 201+ units, quoted.\n  Mock MOR: 60-100 units, $9,000; 101-200 units, $12,000.\n  Corrective Action Plan: from $9,500 depending on finding count, with a stated finding-count band and a per-finding adder above it.\n  HOTMA Transition (per property): $7,500, with a portfolio rate of $4,500 per property at five or more.\n  Training: $1,800 per half-day session on site, $900 remote.\n  Quarterly retainer: $2,200 per quarter per property; $1,600 per property per quarter at four or more.\n  The numbers will vary by market — what matters is that you HAVE a schedule, that the bands are stated, and that you do not re-derive a price in every conversation.\n\nWorked example 2 — the portfolio deal, structured. An agent with 11 properties wants everything. Rather than 11 separate engagements, structure a program: a portfolio assessment (all 11, with a full file sample at 4 and a reduced sample at 7, stated explicitly), a portfolio-wide policy set built once and localized per property, one training program delivered regionally, and a quarterly retainer per property. You gain enormous efficiency from building policies once; price to share part of that gain with the client and keep part. Be explicit in the engagement letter about the reduced sample at 7 properties — an undisclosed reduction in coverage is the one thing that will damage a portfolio relationship permanently.\n\nWorked example 3 — the retainer that actually gets renewed. A retainer renews when the client can see what it produced. Deliver a quarterly report with four numbers: file sample error rate by category; percentage of required EIV reports run and logged on schedule; days from accommodation request to decision; and open items from the prior quarter with status. Add a one-paragraph regulatory update relevant to that property. Four numbers and a paragraph, every quarter, and the renewal conversation answers itself.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: the client who wants an hourly cap on a fixed-fee project. \"We'll do the fixed fee but cap it at 60 hours.\" That is a fixed fee with the upside removed — you carry the overrun risk and forfeit the efficiency benefit. Decline politely and explain the structure: \"The fixed fee is the price for the defined scope, whether it takes 45 hours or 75. If you'd prefer hourly with a not-to-exceed, here is that version.\" Give them the real alternative rather than accepting a hybrid that only works against you.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: pricing a rescue. A property is 12 days from a desk review deadline with no package assembled. Premium pricing is legitimate — you are displacing other work and absorbing schedule risk. State it plainly (\"expedited engagements are quoted at 1.4x the standard schedule\") rather than inflating the estimate quietly. And be honest about what 12 days can accomplish: file-level and EIV items and the response posture, not two years of reconstruction. Put the limits in the engagement letter. A rescue engagement with unrealistic expectations produces an unhappy client even when you do excellent work.\n\nWorked example 6 — payment terms that protect you. For fixed-fee projects: 50 percent on signing, 50 percent on delivery. For corrective action work with a hard deadline: 50 percent on signing, non-refundable, because you are reserving capacity. For retainers: quarterly in advance. For distressed clients: shorter cycles and smaller amounts rather than a large receivable. The most common financial failure in a young consulting practice is not underpricing; it is doing $30,000 of work for a client who cannot pay and discovering it at the invoice."},{"heading":"Legal and Business Structure for Your Consulting Practice","body":"The practice needs a structure that limits liability, sets expectations, and survives a dispute. None of this is exotic, and all of it is cheaper to do before the first engagement than after the first problem.\n\nTHE ENTITY. Most solo practitioners form an LLC. It provides liability separation if respected — separate bank account, no commingling, contracts in the entity's name, and the required filings. Consult an attorney and an accountant in your state for the entity and tax election that fits; the choice interacts with self-employment tax and with how you plan to grow.\n\nINSURANCE:\n• PROFESSIONAL LIABILITY (errors and omissions) — the essential coverage. You are advising clients on determinations that affect federal subsidy and people's housing; if your advice is wrong and produces a repayment, E&O is what stands between you and the loss. Many clients will require evidence of it.\n• GENERAL LIABILITY — commonly required to be on site.\n• CYBER LIABILITY — you will handle tenant income data, and that is exactly the exposure this coverage exists for.\n• Workers' compensation if you have employees, per state law.\n\nTHE ENGAGEMENT LETTER — the single most important document in your practice. It must state:\n• SCOPE, in specifics: what is included, and a short list of what is NOT.\n• DELIVERABLES and dates.\n• FEE and payment terms.\n• CLIENT OBLIGATIONS: documents to be provided, by when, and access to staff. Most consulting failures are caused by client delay, and this clause is how you address it without a fight.\n• CONFIDENTIALITY, in both directions.\n• DATA HANDLING: how you receive, store, transmit and destroy tenant data.\n• THE DISCLAIMER: that you are not providing legal advice, that compliance determinations rest with the owner, and that you do not guarantee any rating or outcome.\n• What you will do if you discover potential fraud or a matter requiring counsel.\n• Termination, and how work in progress is handled.\n\nWorked example 1 — the data-handling clause you will be glad you wrote. You receive tenant files containing income data and social security numbers. Your clause commits you to: receiving data only through the client's designated secure channel or your own encrypted transfer; storing it encrypted, on devices you control, not on personal devices; limiting access to named personnel; not entering tenant data into any shared system; returning or securely destroying data at the end of the engagement and certifying it; and notifying the client promptly of any suspected incident. This is both good practice and a competitive differentiator — say it in the proposal, because your competitors mostly do not.\n\nWorked example 2 — the disclaimer that matters most. A client asks whether they may terminate a household's assistance. You are not their lawyer, and this is a legal question with statutory notice requirements, VAWA implications, fair housing implications and state law overlays. Your engagement letter says you do not provide legal advice; your practice must match the letter. The right answer is: here is the compliance framework and what the file needs to support any path, and this specific decision needs counsel. Consultants who drift into legal advice do so gradually and discover the problem only when something goes wrong.\n\nWorked example 3 — the scope exclusion that prevents a dispute. Your assessment engagement letter includes: \"This engagement does not include correction of errors identified, preparation or transmission of corrected certifications, representation before HUD or the Contract Administrator, legal advice, or preparation of financial statements.\" Six lines. Every one of them prevents an argument later about what the fee covered.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: conflicts. You are engaged by a management agent to assess a property; three months later the owner asks you to assess the agent's performance. That is a conflict, and the fact that both are your clients does not resolve it. Disclose to both, decline one, or obtain informed written consent — and think hard before taking the second engagement even with consent, because the evaluative work will damage one relationship regardless of what you find. Keep a client list and check it before every new engagement.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: what you owe a client after the engagement ends. Six months after a completed engagement, a client emails a question about a HOTMA figure. Answer it — briefly, accurately, and with a note that the compliance date and figures should be verified against current guidance. A five-minute answer maintains a relationship worth thousands. What you should not do is drift into unbilled ongoing advisory work; when the questions become regular, that is the moment to propose the retainer, and the fact that they are asking is the evidence that they need it.\n\nWorked example 6 — the records you keep. Keep your own engagement records: the engagement letter, the work plan, your working papers, the deliverables as delivered, and the correspondence. If a client's MOR later goes badly and questions arise about what you advised, your working papers are the record. Retain them for a period your attorney recommends, store them securely, and destroy client TENANT data on the schedule your engagement letter states — those are two different retention questions and should be handled separately.\n\nWorked example 7 — the professional development obligation. This field changes: HOTMA dates move, the Equal Access Rule is in active rulemaking, criminal screening rules have a pending proposal, and forms get revised. Budget time and money annually for industry association membership, a recognized compliance credential, and a systematic way to monitor HUD notices and Federal Register activity. A consultant whose knowledge is two years old is a liability to clients, and in this specific field two years is enough for three of the figures in this course to have changed."}]},{"id":"m132","unit":"u13","number":2,"title":"The MOR Prep Workflow — Delivering Results","subtitle":"","sections":[{"heading":"The Pre-Engagement Discovery Process","body":"Discovery decides whether the engagement succeeds. Its purpose is to learn enough to scope accurately, price correctly, and set expectations that the delivery can actually meet.\n\nTHE DISCOVERY DOCUMENT REQUEST — send this before the first substantive conversation:\n• The last TWO MOR reports, the owner's written responses, and any follow-up correspondence.\n• The current Tenant Selection Plan, house rules, and EIV written procedures.\n• The AFHMP with evidence of approval.\n• The rent roll and a current waiting list export.\n• The last audited financial statements and the compliance/internal control reports.\n• The HAP contract and any renewal amendments; the regulatory agreement.\n• The certification schedule showing every household's anniversary and current certification.\n• The organizational chart for the property and the region, with tenure of the occupancy staff.\n• The property's NSPIRE score history.\n• Any notice of an upcoming review.\n\nTHE DISCOVERY INTERVIEW — the questions that predict the engagement:\n1. When is your next MOR due, and how did you compute that date? (Most clients cannot answer the second half, which tells you they do not know the schedule matrix.)\n2. Has there been, or is there pending, any change in ownership or management agent? (The 6-month trigger.)\n3. What were your last MOR's findings, and what changed in your PROCESS as a result?\n4. Who processes certifications, how long have they been in the role, and who backs them up?\n5. Walk me through what happens when a tenant reports an income change.\n6. Show me your EIV master file.\n7. How many certifications are currently past their effective date?\n8. What is your fiscal year end, and when were the financial statements submitted?\n9. Are you operating under 4350.3 or have you implemented HOTMA, and does your TSP match?\n\nWorked example 1 — the discovery that reprices the engagement. A prospect describes \"a few file issues\" and wants a light assessment. Discovery reveals: the last MOR was Below Average; the corrective action response promised a monthly EIV review log that stopped after three months; the occupancy specialist started six weeks ago; and eleven certifications are past their effective dates. This is not a light assessment. Reprice before quoting, and explain WHY with the specifics — a client who sees the reasoning usually accepts the higher number, and a client who is quoted low and then billed high never forgives it.\n\nWorked example 2 — the answer that tells you everything. Ask question 3 — what changed in your process after the last findings — and listen. \"We retrained the staff\" means nothing changed. \"We added a second-signature check on every certification and a monthly queue report reviewed by the regional manager, and here is the log\" means a great deal. The difference predicts the next rating better than any file sample.\n\nWorked example 3 — the discovery that finds the deadline nobody mentioned. Question 2 reveals that HUD approved a new management agent four months ago. Under the 2022 rule, a new MOR is due within 6 months of an approved ownership or agent change regardless of the prior rating. The client believed their next review was 20 months away. You have just changed the entire engagement — and demonstrated your value before signing anything. Ask this question at every intake, without exception.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: the client who will not provide documents. A prospect wants a proposal but will not send the prior MOR report \"until we have an agreement.\" You can scope from public information and a conversation, but say plainly what that means: the proposal will be a range, with a scoping call in the first week to confirm, and a stated mechanism for adjusting if the posture is materially different from what was described. What you must not do is quote a firm fixed fee for a property you know nothing about.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: conflicting accounts. The regional manager describes a well-run property; the site manager, alone in the room later, describes chaos. Both are speaking honestly from their vantage. Resolve it with documents rather than impressions: pull five files, look at the EIV master file, and check the certification queue. Ten minutes of evidence settles what an hour of conversation cannot — and it also tells you something important about the organization's internal visibility, which belongs in your findings.\n\nWorked example 6 — the discovery deliverable that wins the work. Convert discovery into a one-page PRIOR REVIEW POSTURE summary: rating history, repeat findings, unkept commitments from the prior response, narrative warnings, the computed next-review due date with the matrix reasoning, and the three highest-risk areas. Deliver it with the proposal, at no charge. It costs two hours, it demonstrates competence rather than asserting it, and it is the document the regional manager forwards to the person who signs."},{"heading":"The File Review Protocol","body":"File review is the core technical work. Doing it systematically — the same way, every time, with a scored instrument — is what makes your findings defensible and your engagements profitable.\n\nSELECTING THE SAMPLE. Mirror the reviewer's risk weighting rather than sampling randomly:\n• All move-ins in the last 12 months.\n• All interim certifications in the last 12 months.\n• All households reporting zero income.\n• All households with live-in aides.\n• All files cited in the prior MOR.\n• All households appearing on the EIV Income Discrepancy Report.\n• All move-outs in the last 12 months.\n• A random selection of remaining files to fill out the sample.\nAt a 96-unit property this typically produces 25 to 30 files, which is close to what a real reviewer will sample and, more importantly, close to WHICH files they will sample.\n\nTHE INSTRUMENT. Build a scored checklist mirroring Addendum A, with a row per requirement and a column per file. Score each item pass, fail, or not applicable, and record the specific defect for each fail. The output is a matrix that produces both a per-file result and a per-REQUIREMENT error rate — and the per-requirement rate is what identifies systemic problems.\n\nTHE SIX-CHECK CERTIFICATION AUDIT for each file:\n1. Recompute all four TTP elements and confirm the greatest was used.\n2. Trace every income and asset line to a verification document dated inside the window.\n3. Confirm every deduction is supported and computed correctly, with any percentage threshold applied once rather than per expense.\n4. Confirm the effective date matches the transaction type and the household's anniversary.\n5. Confirm signatures — all required adults, the owner representative, all dated.\n6. Confirm the transmitted figures match the file and the rent roll.\n\nWorked example 1 — the error rate that changes the recommendation. A 28-file sample produces 19 defects. Sorted by requirement rather than by file:\n  EIV discrepancy resolution memo missing: 11 of 14 applicable files (79 percent).\n  Verification outside the 120-day window: 4 of 28 (14 percent).\n  Missing co-head signature: 2 of 28 (7 percent).\n  Dependent deduction taken for a non-dependent: 1 of 28.\n  Annualization multiplier error: 1 of 28.\n  The headline is not \"19 errors.\" It is that ONE requirement accounts for more than half of them and has a 79 percent failure rate — a systemic control gap with a one-line fix (a blocking checklist item). Reporting by requirement rather than by file is what converts an audit into a recommendation.\n\nWorked example 2 — the file that looks fine and is not. Every document is present, signed and dated; the computation appears correct. Then check 6: the transmitted figures do not match. TRACS shows an adjusted income $1,440 higher than the file, because a correction was transmitted and the corrected 50059 was never filed. The file is complete and WRONG, and it would pass a checklist-only review. The reconciliation to TRACS is the check that catches this class of error, and it is the one most consultants skip because it requires system access. Ask for read access at engagement start.\n\nWorked example 3 — the boundary case: a file with a defensible judgment you would have made differently. A household's asset treatment turns on whether a trust is accessible. The property concluded it is not; you might have concluded otherwise. Is this a finding? If the file documents the instrument, the family's access, and the reasoning, it is a documented judgment, and your report should say so — noting the issue, the basis, and that a reviewer might reach a different conclusion, with a recommendation to seek written guidance from the CA. Flagging judgments as judgments, rather than scoring them as errors, is how you stay credible with clients who know their files.\n\nWorked example 4 — the ambiguous case: an error that harms the tenant. You find a medical expense threshold applied twice, overcharging a household by $16.80 per month for 14 months — $235. Fixing it means a corrected certification, a refund or credit to the tenant, and a HAP adjustment in the other direction. Some clients resist because the correction costs them money and the tenant has not complained. Your position should be firm and stated in writing: the certification must be correct, the error is documented in your report, and leaving it uncorrected after discovery is materially worse than the original error. This is one of the places where a consultant's professional standing is actually tested.\n\nWorked example 5 — the working papers you must keep. For each file reviewed: the checklist with scores, the specific defect noted for each fail, your recomputation worksheet where you recomputed TTP, and a note of anything you flagged as a judgment. These are your evidence if a client later disputes a finding, and they are the raw material for the next engagement's comparison. Store them securely — they contain tenant data — and handle them under the data clause in your engagement letter.\n\nWorked example 6 — the efficiency that makes the work profitable. A disciplined reviewer with a good instrument does a straightforward file in 15 to 20 minutes and a complex one in 40. Twenty-eight files is roughly 10 hours, not 25. The efficiency comes from three things: the instrument (no thinking about what to check), the sequence (all files' TTP recomputations in one pass, all signature checks in another), and read access to TRACS and the rent roll so reconciliation does not require a request per file. Build the instrument once; it is the asset that makes a solo practice viable."},{"heading":"Staff Training as a Compliance Tool","body":"Training is where a consultant's work becomes durable. Files you fix stay fixed for one cycle; a staff member who understands why stays fixed for years — and the training record is itself a MOR item.\n\nDESIGN PRINCIPLES:\n• Train against the property's ACTUAL findings, not a generic curriculum. If the error rate is in EIV discrepancy documentation, spend forty minutes on that and five on the history of the program.\n• Use the property's OWN files (de-identified) as the worked examples. Nothing holds attention like recognizing the property's own errors.\n• Teach the WHY. Staff who understand that a missing resolution memo is what a reviewer scores — not whether the number was right — remember the memo.\n• Give them ARTIFACTS: a worksheet, a checklist, a one-page reference. Training without a tool decays in a month.\n• Document it: agenda, sign-in sheet, duration, materials, and a certificate per attendee.\n\nTHE MODULES THAT MATTER MOST, in the order that produces results:\n1. CERTIFICATION MECHANICS — annualization, the four TTP elements, deductions and thresholds, effective dates, and the six-check audit as a self-check.\n2. EIV — the three legs, the report calendar, the discrepancy resolution memo, and security.\n3. FAIR HOUSING AND ACCOMMODATIONS — the seven classes, what a request looks like and what to do with it, assistance animals, and the confidentiality rules.\n4. VAWA — the three distribution points, self-certification, confidentiality, and the emergency transfer plan.\n5. INTERVIEW PREPARATION — the reviewer's likely questions and how to answer honestly and well.\n\nWorked example 1 — the training built from the audit. Your file review found a 79 percent failure rate on EIV discrepancy documentation. The session: 10 minutes on why the memo is the deliverable, 20 minutes walking through four of the property's own discrepancies (de-identified) and drafting the memo for each together, 10 minutes on the resolution memo template you provide, and 10 minutes installing it as a blocking item on the certification checklist. Fifty minutes, one error category, and a measurable outcome you can verify next quarter.\n\nWorked example 2 — the maintenance training everyone skips. Maintenance staff enter units daily and receive requests, complaints and disclosures. A 45-minute session covering: what an accommodation request sounds like and that it goes to the office the same day in writing; that assistance animals are not pets and are not their call; what to do if a resident discloses domestic violence (hand her the notice and form, say nothing else, tell the manager); privacy around what they see in units; and work order documentation. This session prevents more liability per minute than any other training in the suite, and almost no property has run it.\n\nWorked example 3 — the interview preparation session, run as a rehearsal. Do not lecture. Ask the actual questions — \"walk me through an annual recertification,\" \"what do you do when income goes up mid-year,\" \"how do you use EIV,\" \"what happens when someone is rejected,\" \"how do you handle a reasonable accommodation request\" — and let staff answer. Then coach the answers. Two patterns emerge every time: a staff member who describes a practice that contradicts the written policy (a finding waiting to be spoken), and a staff member who guesses rather than saying \"let me get the procedure.\" Both are fixable in one session.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: training a staff member who is the problem. During the session it becomes clear one specialist is making most of the errors and is resistant. Training will not fix this, and it is not your call to make. What you do: keep the session professional, do not single anyone out, and report to the client factually — the error distribution by preparer if you have it, without characterization. \"Files prepared between March and September show an error rate of 42 percent against 9 percent for other periods\" is a fact the client can act on. \"Your specialist is careless\" is an opinion that gets you into a personnel matter you have no standing in.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: staff who ask you to help them do something you think is wrong. In a session someone asks, \"what do we do when the regional manager tells us not to process an interim because it will raise the rent?\" The honest answer serves everyone: the written policy governs, interims must be processed as the rules and policy require, selective processing is a compliance and fair housing problem, and this needs to be raised with the regional manager — and you will raise the general issue in your report, without attributing it. Then actually do so. Staff will tell a consultant things they will not put in an email; treat that trust carefully and use it to fix the system.\n\nWorked example 6 — the follow-through that proves the training worked. Ninety days after the session, sample five certifications completed since. Compare the error rate on the trained items to the pre-training baseline. Report both numbers. If the rate dropped, you have hard evidence of value and the retainer conversation is easy. If it did not, you have discovered something more important — the training was not the constraint, and the real problem is capacity, supervision or systems."},{"heading":"Mock MOR — Your Most Valuable Pre-Assessment Tool","body":"The mock MOR is the flagship deliverable: a simulation of the real review, conducted against Form HUD-9834, producing a report in the reviewer's own structure with a predicted rating range.\n\nTHE PROTOCOL:\n1. SCOPE CONFIRMATION. Which CA conducts the property's reviews, is Addendum B in scope, and what sampling convention do they use? Build the mock to match.\n2. DESK REVIEW SIMULATION. Request the required documents on the CA's own list, with a deadline, and evaluate what comes back — completeness, organization, timeliness, and whether it ties to TRACS and the rent roll. How the client responds to YOUR request predicts how they will respond to the real one.\n3. FILE REVIEW against Addendum A, using the risk-weighted sample.\n4. DOCUMENT PRODUCTION TEST. On site, ask for twelve specific documents and time how long each takes to produce. A property that needs 25 minutes to find its AFHMP will need 25 minutes in front of the reviewer.\n5. STAFF INTERVIEWS, conducted as a reviewer would.\n6. PHYSICAL WALK of common areas and a few units, focused on management systems: work orders, preventive maintenance records, accessible unit features, posted notices, and EIV data security in the office.\n7. EXIT CONFERENCE, run as a rehearsal, with the client's team practicing note-taking and factual correction.\n8. THE REPORT, in Form 9834 structure, with findings, a predicted rating range, and a prioritized action list with dates and owners.\n\nWorked example 1 — the predicted rating range, stated responsibly. Never predict a single rating. Write: \"Based on 19 defects across 28 files, a systemic EIV documentation gap, current policies, and two repeat findings from the 2024 report, we estimate this property would currently be rated SATISFACTORY, with a realistic risk of BELOW AVERAGE if the reviewer weights the repeat findings heavily. With the corrective actions in Section 4 completed and evidenced, ABOVE AVERAGE is achievable at the next review.\" A range with reasoning is credible and useful. A single confident prediction is neither, and it will eventually be wrong in front of a client.\n\nWorked example 2 — the document production test that changes behavior. You ask for twelve documents on site: current TSP, AFHMP with approval, EIV master file for the last four quarters, HUD-91067 for a specific unit, the emergency transfer plan, the accommodation log, the last three rejection letters, the reserve deposit record, the current management certification, the last audit's compliance report, house rules with distribution evidence, and the certification queue report. Time each. The report includes the times. Nothing motivates a client to build an index like seeing \"Emergency Transfer Plan — not located, 22 minutes\" in writing.\n\nWorked example 3 — the mock that finds a problem no file review would. During the staff interview, the assistant manager says the property \"usually waits until the annual\" for income increases. Nothing in the files shows it, because the increases were never processed. This is a systemic issue discovered by conversation, not by document, and it is exactly what a real reviewer's interviews are for. It also reframes the file review: the absence of interims is now a finding rather than a non-event.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: a mock that finds something requiring immediate disclosure. Your mock uncovers assistance billed on two units vacant for five months. This is not a \"put it in the report\" item. Tell the client the day you find it, in writing, with the recommendation to correct and disclose, and with a note that the exposure grows with each month it continues. Then continue the engagement. A consultant who buries a live billing problem in a report delivered three weeks later has a serious problem of their own.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: how hard to be. A mock that finds nothing is worthless, and a mock that catastrophizes destroys the client's trust in your judgment. Calibrate by scoring against the actual instrument and reporting rates, not impressions: \"11 of 14 applicable files lacked a documented EIV discrepancy resolution\" is neither harsh nor kind, it is a measurement. Reserve strong language for genuinely serious matters — billing integrity, fair housing, VAWA confidentiality — so that when you use it, the client knows it means something.\n\nWorked example 6 — the timing that maximizes value. Run the mock 90 to 120 days before the expected review. That is enough time to cure file-level and EIV items, revise policies, run the training, and rebuild the desk review package — and not so early that the property drifts before the real event. If the client can only afford one engagement, this is the one to sell, because it produces the assessment, the action list, and the training agenda in a single deliverable.\n\nWorked example 7 — the follow-up that closes the loop. Thirty days after the mock, run a verification pass on the action list: what was completed, what has evidence, and what slipped. Two hours, delivered as a one-page status memo. It is the single best predictor of the real outcome, and it is what turns a mock MOR client into a retainer client."},{"heading":"Post-MOR Corrective Action Management","body":"The engagement does not end when the report arrives; that is when the most consequential work begins. Post-MOR management is the phase where ratings are actually improved for the NEXT cycle.\n\nTHE SEQUENCE:\n1. DAY 0 — the report arrives. Calendar the transmittal date, the 30-day appeal deadline for a Below Average or Unsatisfactory rating (Handbook 4350.1 Chapter 6), and every corrective action due date.\n2. DAYS 1-5 — read the report against your mock findings and against the exit conference summary. Identify anything in the report that was never raised. Triage findings into: factually wrong, curable now, curable with time, uncurable-but-controllable.\n3. DAYS 3-15 — draft the appeal if one is warranted; decide by day 15 whether to file.\n4. DAYS 1-25 — execute the cures with evidence.\n5. DAYS 20-28 — draft the response, assemble exhibits, client review.\n6. BY THE DUE DATE — submit with a transmission record.\n7. DAY 30-90 — verify the corrective actions are still operating. This is the step nobody does and the reason findings recur.\n8. ONGOING — compute the next review date from the risk/score matrix and build the readiness calendar backward from it.\n\nWorked example 1 — the verification pass that prevents a repeat finding. A property's corrective action promised a monthly EIV report log signed by the Community Manager. At day 90, you check: the log exists for month one and month two, and month three is blank because the manager was on leave and the backup was never trained. The corrective action is failing quietly, four months after it was submitted, and eleven months before the next review would discover it. Two hours of verification just prevented the single heaviest category of finding — a repeat.\n\nWorked example 2 — the finding that recurs because the root cause was never addressed. The 2023 report cited late annual recertifications; the response said \"staff will be retrained\"; the 2025 report cites late annual recertifications. The root cause was never identified — it was a single-point-of-failure manual reminder process, and retraining does not change a process. Your post-MOR work should force the root cause question for every finding: what in the PROCESS allowed this, and what control now prevents it? A corrective action that names a control can be verified at day 90; one that names an intention cannot.\n\nWorked example 3 — the arithmetic that motivates the client. A property rated Below Average is now on a 12-month cycle. Getting to Satisfactory next cycle moves it to 24 months; getting to Above Average moves it to 36. Over six years, the difference between staying Below Average and reaching Above Average is roughly six reviews versus two. Present that as the goal of the post-MOR engagement — not \"fix the findings,\" but \"change the review interval\" — and the retainer justifies itself in the same conversation.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: corrective actions that require money the client does not have. A finding requires bringing a stale utility allowance analysis current, and the property has no budget for the study. Options: obtain a quote and schedule the work with a documented commitment date; perform an interim analysis using an acceptable methodology while the full study is procured; or request an extension with a specific plan. What does not work is silence. Communicate the constraint and the plan before the due date, in writing, and document the response.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: a finding the client refuses to cure. An owner declines to fund the staffing the corrective action realistically requires. You cannot compel them. What you can do: state the risk in writing, with specifics — the next review is within 12 months, this finding will recur, a recurring finding after a Below Average rating is the profile that draws increased monitoring or a Performance Improvement Plan. Then document that you advised it. Clients are entitled to make their own decisions; consultants are obligated to make sure the decision is informed and that the advice is on the record.\n\nWorked example 6 — the transition into the retainer, done naturally. Deliver the corrective action response. In the same meeting, present a one-page plan for the next 12 months: the day-90 verification pass, quarterly file sampling and EIV log review, a policy watch (HOTMA implementation is coming), the training refresh, and a mock MOR 90 days before the next review date — which you compute in front of them from the matrix. Price it as a quarterly retainer. The client has just lived through the cost of an unprepared review; this is the single moment in the relationship when the value of continuous readiness is self-evident.\n\nWorked example 7 — the metrics to carry forward. For every client, maintain a running scorecard: MOR rating history; findings by category with repeat flags; file sample error rate by requirement, quarter over quarter; EIV report completion rate; days from accommodation request to decision; and certifications completed on time. Six measures. They make your value visible, they make drift detectable, and at the next review they are the evidence that the property's compliance system works — which, as Chapter 2 explains, is the actual question the reviewer is trying to answer."}]},{"id":"m133","unit":"u13","number":3,"title":"Growing Your Practice — Clients, Marketing, and Revenue","subtitle":"","sections":[{"heading":"Finding Your First Clients","body":"The first five clients are the hardest and they are almost never won by marketing. They are won by proximity, credibility and specificity.\n\nWHERE THE FIRST CLIENTS ACTUALLY COME FROM:\n• People who already know your work — former colleagues, former employers, and the regional managers you dealt with in a prior role.\n• State and regional affordable housing associations: membership directories, chapter meetings, and their training calendars.\n• The PBCA and HUD published data identifying assisted properties in your area, which lets you build a real prospect list rather than guessing.\n• Owners and agents you can reach through an accountant, an attorney, or a lender who serves the same market.\n• Properties with a public compliance problem — a new agent, a recent ownership change, a rating you learn about through the industry grapevine.\n\nTHE OUTREACH THAT WORKS AND THE OUTREACH THAT DOES NOT. A generic letter offering \"compliance consulting services\" is deleted. A specific, informed message is answered.\n\nWorked example 1 — the outreach message that gets a reply. To a regional manager at a nine-property agent:\n  \"I work with HUD-assisted properties on MOR readiness. Two things prompted this note. First, the HOTMA compliance date for Multifamily moved again — it is now January 1, 2027 under Notice H 2025-07 — which means the policy and worksheet work is a 2026 project, not a 2027 one. Second, under the 2022 risk-based schedule, properties rated Above Average or Superior at Not Troubled are reviewed every 36 months instead of 24, so moving a property up a level removes a full review cycle. If it is useful, I can send a one-page summary of what the HOTMA transition involves at the property level — no charge, no obligation. If you would rather see the work first, I can put together a prior-review posture summary for one of your properties from the last two MOR reports.\"\n  Why it works: it demonstrates current knowledge with specifics, it names a deadline the recipient has to deal with anyway, and it offers something concrete and free that requires almost nothing from them.\n\nWorked example 2 — the free deliverable that converts. The single best free offer in this business is the PRIOR REVIEW POSTURE summary from Chapter 20: rating history, repeat findings, unkept commitments from the prior response, narrative warnings, and the computed next-review date with the matrix reasoning. Two hours of your time. It requires the client to send you two documents, which is a small commitment that starts the relationship, and it produces a document that shows competence rather than claiming it. Roughly half the prospects who accept it will discuss a paid engagement.\n\nWorked example 3 — the speaking engagement that generates a pipeline. State association chapter meetings and regional training events need presenters and generally do not pay. Present something specifically useful — \"The HOTMA transition: a property-level checklist,\" or \"Reading your MOR report: what the findings actually mean\" — with a handout carrying your contact information. One well-attended 45-minute session in front of thirty property managers is worth more than a year of cold outreach, because the audience is exactly the population that recommends consultants to owners.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: the prospect who wants free work indefinitely. A regional manager sends questions weekly, uses your answers, and never engages. This is common and it is your own fault if it continues. The fix is a friendly boundary applied the third or fourth time: \"Happy to keep answering quick questions — for anything that needs me to look at documents or files, let me put together a small scoped engagement. Here's what that would look like.\" Then send it. Either the relationship becomes commercial or it stops consuming your time.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: taking work you are not fully qualified for. A prospect asks for help with a program layer you have not worked with — a state agency's requirements on a property with tax credits and HOME funds. Options: decline; take it and disclose the limits explicitly in the engagement letter, scoping your work to the HUD side; or partner with someone who has the missing expertise and disclose that arrangement. What you must not do is imply competence you do not have. In a small industry, the reputational cost of one over-reached engagement lasts longer than the fee.\n\nWorked example 6 — the first-year target that is realistic. Eight to twelve single-property engagements or two to three portfolio relationships is a realistic and sufficient first year. That is roughly one new client per month, which is achievable through the channels above without any advertising spend. Resist the temptation to build a website, a brand and a content calendar before you have delivered five engagements — the deliverables you build during those engagements are what you will eventually market with, and they will be better than anything you could invent in advance."},{"heading":"Marketing Your Practice — Positioning and Messaging","body":"Positioning is the decision about what you are known for. In a field this technical, a narrow, credible position beats a broad one.\n\nTHE POSITIONING CHOICES:\n• BY SERVICE — \"MOR readiness and corrective action\" is a strong, specific position.\n• BY PROGRAM — Section 202 and 811 properties, or properties layering HUD assistance with tax credits, are real specializations with distinct bodies of knowledge.\n• BY SITUATION — \"properties recovering from a Below Average or Unsatisfactory rating,\" or \"new management agents taking over assisted properties,\" both of which have urgency and a defined trigger.\n• BY GEOGRAPHY — knowing one state's PBCA conventions in detail is genuinely valuable to owners in that state and is difficult for a national firm to match.\nThe strongest first position is usually service plus geography: MOR readiness for HUD-assisted properties in a defined region whose CA conventions you know cold.\n\nTHE MESSAGE. Lead with the client's problem, not your credentials. The problems that motivate action, in order: an upcoming review; a bad rating; a change of ownership or agent (with its 6-month trigger); a HOTMA deadline; and staff turnover in the occupancy role.\n\nWorked example 1 — the positioning statement, three versions.\n  Weak: \"We provide comprehensive affordable housing compliance consulting services to owners and managers nationwide.\"\n  Better: \"We help HUD-assisted multifamily properties prepare for and recover from Management and Occupancy Reviews.\"\n  Strongest: \"We help HUD-assisted properties in [state/region] move from Satisfactory to Above Average — which under HUD's risk-based schedule means being reviewed every 36 months instead of 24. We know how [the state's PBCA] samples files and scores reports.\"\n  The third version tells an owner exactly what you do, what it is worth, and why you specifically.\n\nWorked example 2 — the content that demonstrates rather than asserts. If you publish anything, publish tools: a HOTMA transition checklist, an EIV report calendar template, a rejection letter content checklist, a mock MOR file sample selection guide. Property managers download tools and remember who made them. Nobody remembers a post about the importance of compliance. And every tool you publish is a piece of work product you already built for a client — the marginal cost is an hour of editing.\n\nWorked example 3 — the case study that respects confidentiality. \"A 140-unit Section 8 property rated Below Average in 2024, with eleven findings including systemic EIV documentation gaps and six late annual recertifications. We conducted an assessment, rebuilt the EIV master file and report calendar, corrected 31 certifications, delivered two training sessions, and prepared the corrective action response. The property was rated Above Average at its next review, moving it from a 12-month to a 36-month review cycle.\" No client name, no identifying detail, real numbers. That paragraph does more than any amount of adjective-laden marketing copy, and it is honest.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: what you may not say. You may not imply HUD endorsement, use HUD's seal or logo, suggest that you can influence a rating through relationships, or use client tenant data in marketing in any form. You also should not overstate credentials — if you hold an industry certification, name it precisely; if you do not, say what your experience is instead. In a field where your buyers deal with federal compliance daily, overstatement is detected quickly and remembered permanently.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: whether to name clients. Some clients will happily serve as references; others will not want it known they hired a compliance consultant, particularly after a bad rating. Ask, in writing, and honor the answer. A short list of named references you can offer privately to a serious prospect is worth more than a logo wall, and it avoids the awkwardness of a prospect noticing that their competitor's name is on your website.\n\nWorked example 6 — the marketing budget that makes sense in year one. Association membership and one or two conference registrations; a simple one-page website with your positioning, services, and contact information; business cards; and the time to build two or three genuinely useful tools. That is the whole list. Paid advertising does not work in a market this small and this relationship-driven, and the money is better spent on a compliance credential and on the software or subscriptions that make your delivery faster."},{"heading":"Building a Referral Network","body":"In a market this small, referrals are the primary growth mechanism. The people who refer are the people who see compliance problems and are not positioned to solve them.\n\nTHE REFERRAL SOURCES THAT ACTUALLY PRODUCE:\n• CPAs who audit HUD-assisted properties. They read the same files you do, they write findings, and they are explicitly not permitted to fix what they audit.\n• Attorneys who handle affordable housing transactions, evictions or fair housing matters.\n• Lenders and servicers in the HUD-insured space.\n• Software vendors serving assisted housing — their implementation staff see broken processes constantly.\n• Regional and state association staff, who get asked \"do you know someone who does this?\" regularly.\n• OTHER CONSULTANTS with a different specialty — a physical/NSPIRE consultant, a tax credit specialist, an energy consultant. They meet your clients and you meet theirs.\n• Former clients and former colleagues.\n\nWHAT MAKES A REFERRAL SOURCE ACTUALLY REFER: they must know precisely what you do, trust that you will not embarrass them, and have a specific trigger that reminds them of you. \"Compliance consulting\" is not a trigger. \"When you see a compliance finding in an audit, or a property that just changed agents, that's when to think of me\" is.\n\nWorked example 1 — the auditor relationship, built correctly. Meet the CPAs in your market who audit assisted properties. Explain the trigger explicitly: \"When your compliance report cites tenant file documentation or EIV, that's a finding the client has to remediate and you can't do it for them — that's when I'm useful.\" Then, when they refer, do three things: respond the same day, keep the auditor informed at a level the client authorizes, and tell the auditor how it turned out. A referral source who learns the outcome refers again; one who hears nothing assumes it went badly.\n\nWorked example 2 — the reciprocal arrangement with a NSPIRE consultant. You do not do physical inspections; they do not do occupancy. Your clients need both and each of you is asked about the other constantly. Formalize it lightly: mutual introductions, a joint one-page overview of the two review types (which is genuinely useful to clients who confuse them), and an agreement on how referrals are handled. Be careful about referral FEES — disclose any fee arrangement to the client, and check your state's rules and your own professional standards; in many cases a clean mutual-referral relationship with no money changing hands is simpler and more durable.\n\nWorked example 3 — the referral that goes badly, handled well. A CPA refers a client who turns out to be unresponsive, does not provide documents, and disputes the invoice. Tell the referring CPA — factually, without complaint — that the engagement did not go well and why in general terms, before they hear a distorted version. Referral sources fear exactly this outcome, and a consultant who handles it transparently gets referred again. One who lets the source be surprised does not.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: a referral into a conflict. An attorney refers an owner whose management agent is already your client, in a dispute about the agent's performance. Decline promptly and explain why in general terms. This is a moment where declining well is worth more than the fee: both the attorney and the agent learn that you take conflicts seriously, which is exactly the quality that makes people comfortable referring sensitive work.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: how much to give away to a source. A software vendor asks you to run a free webinar for their client base. Analysis: you get access to a targeted audience and a piece of content you can reuse; they get value for their clients. That is a fair trade if the audience is genuinely your market and if you can present something useful rather than a sales pitch. What is not a fair trade is unpaid custom work for the vendor's clients under the guise of a partnership. Be specific about what you are giving and what you expect.\n\nWorked example 6 — the maintenance habit. Keep a short list of your referral sources and contact each one a few times a year with something useful, not with a request: a note about the HOTMA date change, a copy of a checklist you built, a comment on a rule that affects their clients. Fifteen minutes each, three times a year. Referral networks decay silently, and the consultants who stay busy in slow periods are the ones who kept the relationships warm when they were not busy."},{"heading":"Scaling Your Practice — Associates, Partnerships, and Technology","body":"A solo practice has a hard ceiling: your hours. Scaling means finding leverage — from systems, from other people, or from productized work.\n\nTHE THREE FORMS OF LEVERAGE:\n1. SYSTEMS. Your file review instrument, report templates, policy templates, training materials, and checklists. Every hour spent building a reusable asset reduces the hours in every future engagement. This is the leverage available to a solo practitioner and it is substantial: a mature instrument set can cut delivery time by 30 to 40 percent.\n2. PEOPLE. Subcontractors for file review, then employees. This multiplies capacity and introduces quality risk, management overhead, and payroll obligations.\n3. PRODUCTS. Training programs delivered to multiple clients, template packages, and recorded courses. High margin, slow to build, and they compete with your consulting time until they do not.\n\nWorked example 1 — the subcontractor decision, with arithmetic. You are turning away work. A qualified subcontractor can perform file review at $55 per hour; you bill that work at an effective $145 per hour within a fixed fee.\n  A 28-file review takes them 14 hours (slower than you at first): cost $770, billed value roughly $2,030.\n  Gross margin per engagement: $1,260, minus your review time of 3 hours to check their work and write the findings.\n  The economics work — but only if quality holds. Build in the review step permanently, not just for the first engagement, and price it into the fee. The most common failure is a consultant who subcontracts to grow and stops checking the work in month four.\n  Note also the data obligations: a subcontractor handling tenant data must be bound by the same confidentiality and data-handling terms you gave the client, in writing.\n\nWorked example 2 — the technology that actually pays for itself. In rough order of return: a structured file review instrument (a spreadsheet or database, not a Word checklist); a document management system with real security for client data; a scheduling and project tracking tool so deadlines are never held in your head; a proposal and engagement letter template library; and a secure file transfer mechanism. None of this is exotic and all of it directly increases the number of engagements you can carry. Resist expensive specialized software until your volume justifies it.\n\nWorked example 3 — the partnership that works and the one that does not. A partnership with a complementary specialist (NSPIRE, tax credits, financial management) can serve larger clients you could not serve alone, and each partner keeps their own practice. A partnership with someone doing the SAME work is harder: you compete for the same clients, you disagree about quality standards, and the revenue split creates friction. If you partner with a peer, do it for a specific engagement first, with written terms, before doing it for the practice.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: the client who wants more than you can deliver. A 40-property portfolio wants a full program. Options: decline; take a defined subset (six properties, with an option to expand); or assemble a team of subcontractors. Taking all forty alone is how consultants destroy their own reputations — you will miss deadlines, quality will fall, and the client will tell everyone. Taking six and doing them excellently usually leads to the other thirty-four anyway.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: growth versus margin. Adding staff increases revenue and reduces margin per engagement while adding management work you may not enjoy. Some consultants are happiest solo at $180,000 to $250,000 with total control; others want a firm. Decide deliberately rather than drifting. The test question: do you want to spend your time doing the work or managing people who do the work? Both are legitimate; drifting into the second by accident is not.\n\nWorked example 6 — the productization sequence. Do not build a course first. The sequence that works: deliver engagements → notice which deliverable you rebuild every time → templatize it → notice which training you deliver repeatedly → package it as a standard program → only then consider a recorded or licensed product. Each step is funded by the previous one, and each is built from work clients have already paid for and validated.\n\nWorked example 7 — the capacity math to run annually. Billable capacity for a solo consultant is roughly 1,000 to 1,200 hours a year after sales, administration, professional development and travel — not 2,080. At an effective rate of $150, that is $150,000 to $180,000 in revenue capacity. If your growth plan requires more than that, it requires leverage, and the plan should say which of the three forms you are using and when. A growth plan that is simply \"get more clients\" runs into the ceiling within eighteen months."},{"heading":"The Long Game — Building a Sustainable Compliance Practice","body":"A compliance practice is a knowledge business in a field that changes continuously. Sustainability comes from three things: knowledge that stays current, relationships that persist between engagements, and revenue that does not depend entirely on new sales.\n\nSTAYING CURRENT — the non-negotiable obligation. This course itself demonstrates the problem: the HOTMA compliance date moved three times in three years; the Equal Access Rule is in active rulemaking; a criminal screening proposed rule is pending; income limits, deduction amounts and the passbook rate change annually; and forms get revised. A consultant working from two-year-old knowledge is a liability. Build a system:\n• Monitor HUD notices and Federal Register activity on a schedule, not by accident.\n• Maintain association membership and attend at least one substantive training annually.\n• Hold a recognized compliance credential and maintain it.\n• Keep a dated CHANGE LOG of your own — what changed, when, effective date, source, and which of your templates it affects. When a figure changes, that log tells you which deliverables to update.\n• Re-verify any figure or date before it goes into a client deliverable. Every one.\n\nRECURRING REVENUE. Project work is lumpy; retainers are not. A practice with 60 to 70 percent of revenue from retainers and recurring annual work (mock MORs on a cycle, HOTMA-style transition projects, annual training) survives a slow quarter. A practice living entirely on new project sales does not.\n\nRELATIONSHIPS THAT PERSIST. Clients change jobs. The regional manager you helped in 2026 is a portfolio director somewhere else in 2029, and they will hire whoever they trusted. Stay in touch with people, not just accounts.\n\nWorked example 1 — the change log in practice. HUD publishes the annual inflationary adjustments each year. Your change log entry: date published, the figures (asset limitation, imputed-income threshold, deduction amounts, passbook rate), effective date, source, and the list of your assets that reference them — the HOTMA worksheet, the training deck, the assessment report template, the client-facing summary. Updating all four takes an hour when the log tells you where the figures live, and takes a week of anxiety when it does not.\n\nWorked example 2 — the annual client cycle that produces predictable revenue. For a retainer client on a 36-month review cycle: Year 1 — post-review verification, quarterly reviews, annual training. Year 2 — quarterly reviews, a policy refresh, annual training. Year 3 — quarterly reviews, mock MOR 90-120 days before the review, desk review package preparation, on-site support, and the post-review corrective action work. The revenue is smooth, the client's readiness never lapses, and the year-3 work is priced as project work on top of the retainer.\n\nWorked example 3 — the reputation event. Eventually you will make a mistake — a wrong figure in a report, a missed deadline, an incorrect interpretation. What happens next determines your practice's trajectory. Tell the client immediately, in writing, with the correction and the impact. Fix it at your own cost. Do not minimize it and do not construct an excuse. Clients in this industry talk to each other constantly, and the story they tell about a consultant who caught and fixed their own error is far better than the story they tell about one who hid it. Your E&O carrier will also want to know promptly.\n\nWorked example 4 — the boundary case: a client who asks you to do something improper. An owner asks you to back-date a distribution log, to omit a finding from a report the CA will see, or to certify something you have not verified. Decline clearly, in writing, and state what you can do instead. If the request is serious enough — falsifying records in a federal program — consider whether the engagement can continue at all, and get your own counsel's advice. A single such engagement can end a practice; declining one costs a fee.\n\nWorked example 5 — the ambiguous case: a field that changes under you. Suppose a future rule change eliminates a service line — HOTMA transition work ends once implementation is complete, for instance. This is predictable and it is why the practice should not be built on one project type. Watch for what replaces it: post-implementation audits, the first review cycle under the new rules, the errors created during transition. Every regulatory change destroys one service line and creates two. Consultants who track rulemaking see the next line coming a year before their competitors.\n\nWorked example 6 — the practice review you run on yourself annually. Six questions: What percentage of revenue was recurring? What was the client retention rate? Which service lines were profitable per hour and which were not? Which engagements went badly and why? What did I learn this year that I did not know last year, and where is it documented? And what would a reviewer find if they audited MY working papers, data handling and engagement letters? An annual honest answer to those six questions is the difference between a practice that compounds and one that merely persists.\n\nWorked example 7 — the closing frame for this course. Everything in these thirteen units reduces to one idea: HUD is trying to determine whether a management system works. Files, policies, logs, notices, calendars and training records are the evidence of a system. A consultant's job is to build the system and to make its operation visible — to the reviewer, to the owner, and to the staff who have to run it after you leave. Do that, and the rating follows; and the client who reaches Above Average and stays there is the client who renews."}]}];
export const MOR_CHAPTER_QUIZZES = {"m71":{"title":"Chapter Quiz","questions":[{"q":"A MOR evaluates:","opts":["Physical condition of units and common areas","Management practices, documentation, policies, and regulatory compliance","The property’s NSPIRE score and capital needs","The accuracy of the appraised value"],"ans":1,"exp":"The MOR reviews administration and compliance. Physical condition is scored separately under NSPIRE."},{"q":"Form HUD-9834 is organized into:","opts":["Two parts: desk review and on-site review","Three parts: Desk Review, On-Site Review with Addenda A/B/C, and Summary Report","Five parts corresponding to the five rating levels","Seven parts, one per review category"],"ans":1,"exp":"Part I Desk Review, Part II On-Site Review (Addendum A tenant file checklist, Addendum B financial, Addendum C required documents), Part III Summary Report."},{"q":"Which review category on Form 9834 is rated only by HUD staff and Mortgagee reviewers, not Contract Administrators?","opts":["Category A","Category C","Category D","Category G"],"ans":2,"exp":"Of the seven lettered categories A through G, Category D is HUD staff/Mortgagee-only; CAs rate the rest."},{"q":"A Not Troubled property scores Satisfactory on its MOR. Its next MOR is due within:","opts":["12 months","24 months","36 months","6 months"],"ans":1,"exp":"Satisfactory produces a 24-month interval. Above Average or Superior at Not Troubled produces 36 months; Below Average or Unsatisfactory produces 12 months."},{"q":"A property classified Troubled scores Superior. Its next MOR is due within:","opts":["36 months","24 months","12 months","No further MOR is required"],"ans":1,"exp":"Above Average or Superior at a Troubled classification caps the interval at 24 months; only Potentially Troubled or Not Troubled properties reach 36 months."},{"q":"A Not Troubled property rated Superior in 2025 has its next MOR scheduled for 2028. In August 2026 HUD approves a new management agent. When is the next MOR due?","opts":["2028, as scheduled — the rating governs","Within 6 months of the approved agent change","Within 12 months of the approved agent change","Within 24 months of the approved agent change"],"ans":1,"exp":"Regardless of risk classification or prior score, a new MOR is required within 6 months of any approved ownership or management agent change."},{"q":"An owner receives a Satisfactory rating and wants to appeal because they believe they earned Above Average. The appeal is:","opts":["Available, within 30 calendar days","Available, within 45 calendar days","Not available — only Below Average and Unsatisfactory ratings may be appealed","Available only if the CA agrees to reopen the review"],"ans":2,"exp":"Appeal rights attach to Below Average and Unsatisfactory ratings. There is no appeal from Satisfactory or above."},{"q":"A MOR report is transmitted March 4. The owner’s appeal must be submitted in writing by approximately:","opts":["March 19","April 3","April 18","May 4"],"ans":1,"exp":"Thirty calendar days from the report/transmittal date, per Handbook 4350.1 Chapter 6 — the clock runs from transmittal, not from internal receipt."},{"q":"HUD’s stated justification for the 2022 risk-based MOR schedule rested on the fact that in FY2021:","opts":["Fewer than half of properties passed their MOR","Over 94 percent of properties scored Satisfactory, Above Average, or Superior","MOR costs exceeded HAP payments at most properties","Contract Administrators were unable to complete annual reviews"],"ans":1,"exp":"HUD cited the 94-plus percent figure to justify spacing out reviews for high performers and concentrating resources on lower-scoring, higher-risk properties."},{"q":"A consultant tells a client in State B that anything below 70 on their MOR is failing, based on a rule used in State A. This is:","opts":["Correct — 70 is HUD’s national cut point","Correct only for Troubled properties","Incorrect — the five-level scale is HUD’s standard, but numeric bands are set by individual Contract Administrators and vary","Incorrect — HUD prohibits numeric scoring entirely"],"ans":2,"exp":"The five-level scale is national; numeric overlays are CA conventions. One agency anchors at 95/85/75/65, another treats below 70 as below-average."},{"q":"Form HUD-9834’s OMB approval has technically been expired since 2018. The practical effect is:","opts":["MORs conducted since 2018 are void","HUD has continued to direct reviewers to use the current version, and the underlying obligations remain contractual and regulatory","Owners may refuse to participate until the form is reapproved","Contract Administrators must use a state-issued substitute form"],"ans":1,"exp":"The form is the vehicle, not the source of the obligation. HUD has continued directing reviewers to use it — a useful example of formal status diverging from operational reality."},{"q":"A management agent outsources certification processing to a compliance vendor, which produces late and erroneous certifications. The MOR finding is issued to:","opts":["The vendor","The Contract Administrator","The owner — the party to the HAP contract","No one; outsourced functions are not scored"],"ans":2,"exp":"The MOR is issued to the owner, who remains responsible for the agent and anyone the agent engages."},{"q":"Which of the following is a Part I (desk review) input the reviewer obtains WITHOUT the owner submitting it?","opts":["The tenant selection plan","HUD system data such as TRACS, iREMS, and submitted financial statements","The AFHMP","House rules"],"ans":1,"exp":"The reviewer independently pulls HUD system data. Owner-submitted policy documents are the other half of Part I."},{"q":"Nine findings are issued; two are factually wrong and seven are valid. Best practice is to:","opts":["Appeal all nine to preserve leverage","Appeal the two and simultaneously submit corrective action documentation on the seven","Wait for the appeal decision before beginning corrective action","Decline to appeal and cure all nine"],"ans":1,"exp":"Corrective action due dates run independently of an appeal. Appeal the errors; cure the rest on schedule."},{"q":"A property has a failing NSPIRE score but a documented work order system with tracked turnaround times. On the MOR:","opts":["The NSPIRE score is imported and lowers the MOR rating","The maintenance-management items can still be rated acceptable, because the MOR scores the management system, not the physical condition","The MOR is deferred until the physical deficiencies are cured","The property is automatically rated Unsatisfactory"],"ans":1,"exp":"The MOR scores the office; NSPIRE scores the building. Form 9834 asks about work order tracking, preventive maintenance and follow-up systems."}]},"m72":{"title":"Chapter Quiz","questions":[{"q":"HUD’s MOR rating scale consists of:","opts":["Pass and Fail","Three levels: Acceptable, Marginal, Deficient","Five levels: Superior, Above Average, Satisfactory, Below Average, Unsatisfactory","A 0–100 numeric score with no verbal labels"],"ans":2,"exp":"The five-level scale is the HUD standard nationally; numeric bands, where used, are Contract Administrator conventions layered on top of it."},{"q":"A client with properties in two states notes one report says \"88 — Above Average\" and the other says only \"Above Average.\" The correct conclusion is:","opts":["The second CA failed to score the property","The two properties received the same HUD rating; the number reflects one CA’s local band","The unscored property will be reviewed sooner","The 88 property is stronger by 88 percent"],"ans":1,"exp":"The five-level rating is the substance. Numeric overlays vary by CA and are not comparable across administrators."},{"q":"Which factor most heavily aggravates a deficiency’s weight in the rating?","opts":["That it appears in a tenant file rather than a policy document","That it was cited on the prior MOR and recurs","That it involves a small dollar amount","That the tenant complained about it"],"ans":1,"exp":"A repeat finding converts an error into a demonstrated failure to correct — the heaviest aggravating factor."},{"q":"A reviewer samples 20 files at a 150-unit property and finds 4 with income errors. The owner argues this is under 3 percent of the property. The reviewer’s view is:","opts":["Correct — 4 of 150 is the relevant rate","The error rate is 4 of 20 — 20 percent — and the sample is used to infer the population","Sampling error rates are not used in MOR ratings","Only files with subsidy overpayment count"],"ans":1,"exp":"The sample denominator governs. Expect a corrective action requiring review of all files, not just the four cited."},{"q":"Which corrective action response element is most often missing and most damaging when absent?","opts":["The finding restated","A statement of root cause and the process change that prevents recurrence","The date the report was received","The name of the Contract Administrator"],"ans":1,"exp":"\"Staff has been counseled\" is the classic failing response: no root cause, no process change, no evidence — and it invites a repeat finding."},{"q":"A corrective action due date cannot realistically be met. The best action is to:","opts":["Submit whatever is complete on the due date without comment","Request an extension in writing BEFORE the due date with a specific alternative schedule and interim deliverable","Wait until the follow-up review to explain","Appeal the finding"],"ans":1,"exp":"Extensions requested in advance are routinely granted; a missed date without notice becomes a failure to correct."},{"q":"A finding cites that a 2022 admission was processed without a pre-admission EIV Existing Tenant Search. The best response is to:","opts":["State that the requirement did not apply in 2022","Acknowledge the historical omission, run the search now and document the result, and install a mandatory pre-move-in control with evidence","Ignore it — the requirement is uncurable","Appeal, since the past cannot be changed"],"ans":1,"exp":"Separate the uncurable historical fact from the curable forward control. Reviewers accept a documented compensating control."},{"q":"For a Not Troubled property, moving from Satisfactory to Above Average is economically significant because it:","opts":["Eliminates the requirement for annual financial statements","Extends the next MOR interval from 24 months to 36 months","Removes the property from EIV monitoring","Increases the HAP contract rent"],"ans":1,"exp":"Satisfactory produces a 24-month interval; Above Average or Superior at Not Troubled produces 36 months."},{"q":"Twelve of 25 files have a missing HUD-9887 signature that was re-executed within the month; ONE file shows an ineligible student household assisted for two years. Which is likely weighted more heavily?","opts":["The 12 signature files, because frequency governs","The single student file, because it goes to eligibility and produces a repayment","Neither — both are minor","They are weighted identically"],"ans":1,"exp":"Severity — effect on eligibility, subsidy amount, tenant rights or program funds — can outweigh raw frequency."},{"q":"A Performance Improvement Plan differs from a Corrective Action Plan in that it:","opts":["Is optional","Addresses organizational capacity — staffing, training, systems — rather than answering specific findings","Is filed with the tenant, not HUD","Replaces the appeal process"],"ans":1,"exp":"A CAP answers findings; a PIP addresses whether the organization has the capacity to operate the property."},{"q":"A troubled owner considers transferring ownership to \"start fresh\" with HUD. The scheduling consequence is:","opts":["The next MOR is deferred 36 months","A new MOR is required within 6 months of the approved change","MOR requirements are suspended during transfer","The prior rating is expunged"],"ans":1,"exp":"Any approved ownership or management agent change triggers a new MOR within 6 months, regardless of score or risk classification."},{"q":"The prior MOR report’s NARRATIVE notes, without citing a finding, that the TSP does not address live-in aides. Best practice is to:","opts":["Ignore it — narrative comments are not scored","Treat it as a finding with a two-year fuse and correct the TSP now","Request that the narrative be removed","Appeal the narrative comment"],"ans":1,"exp":"Narrative observations signal what the next reviewer will examine; an uncorrected observation typically becomes a finding, at a property that was warned."},{"q":"A new management agent takes over a property with an Unsatisfactory rating. The highest-value first deliverable is:","opts":["A rent increase request","A dated, documented baseline file inventory and condition assessment at takeover","A new AFHMP","A request to postpone the next MOR"],"ans":1,"exp":"The takeover baseline separates inherited deficiencies from new ones for the next two review cycles — and a new MOR is due within 6 months."},{"q":"Six minor findings are all cured during the on-site review and documented in front of the reviewer. This most directly supports:","opts":["An appeal of the rating","A higher rating, because self-identification and cure lighten a deficiency’s weight","Removal of the property from the MOR schedule","A reduction in contract rents"],"ans":1,"exp":"Deficiencies found, documented and corrected — especially during the review — weigh materially less than the same items found uncured."},{"q":"Ratings run Below Average (2021), Satisfactory (2023), Satisfactory (2025), but the 2025 findings are entirely different items from 2021. This pattern indicates:","opts":["Steady systematic improvement","Reactive compliance — the property fixes what it is told and generates new gaps elsewhere","Reviewer inconsistency requiring an appeal","That the property should be reclassified Not Troubled"],"ans":1,"exp":"New findings replacing old ones is the signature of reactive compliance; the remedy is a standing self-assessment against the full 9834."}]},"m73":{"title":"Chapter Quiz","questions":[{"q":"Part I of Form 9834 (the desk review) is completed:","opts":["During the exit conference","Before the on-site visit, using owner submissions and HUD system data","After the on-site visit, as part of the summary report","Only for Troubled properties"],"ans":1,"exp":"The desk review is completed before the reviewer arrives, which is why a substantial share of the rating is provisionally formed before the visit."},{"q":"MOR tenant file sampling is best described as:","opts":["Purely random selection","Risk-weighted — deliberately including move-ins, interims, zero-income households, live-in aides, EIV discrepancies, and prior-finding files","Selected by the owner","Limited to files the owner submits in the desk review package"],"ans":1,"exp":"Because the sample is risk-weighted, predicting the risk categories is possible — but preparing only a subset of files is a losing strategy."},{"q":"A reviewer finds errors in the first 5 of 20 sampled files and requests 10 more. This signals:","opts":["A routine procedural step at every review","That the reviewer believes the errors are systemic rather than isolated","That the review will be rescheduled","That the property will be rated Superior"],"ans":1,"exp":"An expanded sample means the reviewer is testing whether the initial errors reflect a process failure."},{"q":"A reviewer notices a dripping faucet and asks for the unit’s work order history. The WORST of the following outcomes is:","opts":["A work order dated four days ago with parts on order","No work order and a tenant who says she called three weeks ago","A work order marked completed two months ago for the same faucet","A work order opened that morning"],"ans":2,"exp":"A \"completed\" work order that did not fix the problem undermines the reliability of every other completed work order — worse than having no record at all."},{"q":"An accessible unit is occupied by a household that does not need its features. The property is in the best position if the file shows:","opts":["That the household pays a higher rent for the unit","That the unit was offered first to qualified households from the waiting list, was declined, and the lease includes the required transfer provision","That the household agreed verbally to move if asked","That the features were removed at the tenant’s request"],"ans":1,"exp":"Documentation of the offer sequence and the transfer provision is what converts this from a Section 504/fair housing finding into compliant management."},{"q":"A staff member tells the reviewer, \"If someone’s income goes up we usually wait until the annual.\" The consequence is:","opts":["Nothing, unless the files show it","A finding, because the statement describes a practice contrary to the written policy and interim recertification requirements","A narrative comment only","An automatic Unsatisfactory rating"],"ans":1,"exp":"Interviews test whether written policy matches actual practice. A statement describing noncompliant practice is itself a finding."},{"q":"A staff member says they tell domestic violence survivors to file a police report \"so we have documentation.\" This is:","opts":["Correct — third-party documentation is required","Incorrect — a survivor may self-certify on HUD-5382, and additional proof may not be demanded unless the provider has conflicting information","Correct only for Section 202 properties","Correct if the property’s policy requires it"],"ans":1,"exp":"VAWA permits self-certification via HUD-5382; demanding police reports or medical records absent conflicting information is a violation."},{"q":"The single highest-value activity at an exit conference is:","opts":["Arguing interpretation of the handbook","Correcting factual errors immediately by producing the document","Requesting a higher rating","Asking the reviewer to delay the report"],"ans":1,"exp":"A factual error corrected on the spot never becomes a finding. Interpretation disputes belong in the written response."},{"q":"After the exit conference, the owner should send the reviewer a written summary within 24–48 hours primarily because:","opts":["HUD requires it","It creates the record of what was discussed, preventing disputes when the report differs","It shortens the appeal deadline","It substitutes for the corrective action response"],"ans":1,"exp":"The contemporaneous summary is what lets an owner credibly say a finding was never raised at the exit conference."},{"q":"A CA’s document list requests an item the property does not have (e.g., a resident council roster). Best practice is to:","opts":["Leave a blank in the binder","State in writing before the visit that the item does not exist and why, and include an N/A page","Create the document","Refuse the request"],"ans":1,"exp":"A written N/A explanation reads as control; a silent gap reads as disorganization."},{"q":"A file contains two 50059s with the same effective date and different figures, one of which was a correction. This is:","opts":["Acceptable if TRACS reflects the correct figures","A file organization finding, because the reviewer cannot determine which document is operative","Only a problem if the tenant complains","Required documentation of the correction process"],"ans":1,"exp":"Superseded documents must be marked as superseded with a date, and corrections should carry a file note explaining what changed."},{"q":"Which category of files do owners most often fail to audit before a review, despite being commonly sampled?","opts":["Recent move-ins","Households with live-in aides","Recent move-outs and terminated households","Zero-income households"],"ans":2,"exp":"Move-out processing — final termination in TRACS, deposit accounting, notice documentation — is a common failure point with no ongoing tenancy to surface errors."},{"q":"A property receives notice on the 3rd for a review on the 17th and knows nine annual recertifications are late. The best action is to:","opts":["Say nothing and hope those files are not sampled","Complete as many as possible, document dates and root cause, and give the reviewer a written self-identification summary","Request an indefinite postponement","Remove the late files from the file room"],"ans":1,"exp":"Self-identification and cure is one of the four factors that lighten a deficiency’s weight; concealment converts a finding into an integrity problem."},{"q":"In the 48 hours before a review, the highest-priority physical items are:","opts":["Fresh paint and landscaping","Life safety items, accessibility features in accessible units, common area lighting, required posted notices, and vacant unit condition","Model unit staging","Parking lot striping"],"ans":1,"exp":"The walk tests management systems and legal requirements, not aesthetics."},{"q":"For a Below Average rating, the appeal must be filed within 30 calendar days of the transmittal date, and corrective action responses:","opts":["Are suspended until the appeal is decided","Run independently and must be submitted by their stated due dates regardless of the appeal","Are due 45 days after the appeal decision","Are optional once an appeal is filed"],"ans":1,"exp":"Corrective action deadlines are not tolled by an appeal; cure the valid findings while appealing the erroneous ones."}]},"m81":{"title":"Chapter Quiz","questions":[{"q":"Which of the following can a reviewer examine WITHOUT the owner submitting anything?","opts":["The tenant selection plan","TRACS certification history, iREMS data, APPS/2530 records, and FASS financial submissions","House rules","The reasonable accommodation log"],"ans":1,"exp":"The desk review draws on HUD system data the reviewer pulls independently, which is why self-auditing against HUD-visible data matters."},{"q":"A property discovers two weeks before submission that it has billed HAP for four months on a unit whose tenant moved out. The correct action is to:","opts":["Leave it — the reviewer may not notice","Correct it, document the correction date, and disclose it in the package","Wait until the exit conference to raise it","Terminate the certification retroactively without repaying"],"ans":1,"exp":"The reviewer will see the TRACS record and rent roll. An uncorrected overbilling found by the reviewer becomes a program integrity matter rather than an administrative error."},{"q":"A household moves from a 1-bedroom to a 2-bedroom in the same property and staff process a move-out and a new move-in. The most significant hidden consequence is:","opts":["The tenant loses their security deposit","The annual recertification anniversary is incorrectly reset to the new \"move-in\" date","The unit is removed from the HAP contract","The utility allowance cannot be recalculated"],"ans":1,"exp":"A unit transfer preserves the recertification anniversary; processing it as a termination plus admission resets it and misstates TRACS history."},{"q":"EIV Income Discrepancy Reports and No Income Reports are retained:","opts":["Three years in the EIV master file","In the tenant file for the term of tenancy plus three years","Until the next annual recertification","Permanently, in the property’s permanent records"],"ans":1,"exp":"Tenant-specific EIV reports belong in the tenant file for term of tenancy plus three years; master-file reports such as New Hires, Multiple Subsidy and Deceased Tenants are retained three years."},{"q":"A property has EIV income reports in every tenant file but maintains no EIV master file. The finding is:","opts":["Not written, because tenant-level documentation suffices","A systemic EIV deficiency for absent property-level monitoring","A tenant file finding for each household","Deferred to the next review"],"ans":1,"exp":"Master-file reports evidence portfolio-level monitoring, a separate obligation from tenant-level EIV use."},{"q":"An occupancy specialist resigned in March; in September the reviewer finds their EIV access was never terminated. This is best characterized as:","opts":["A minor administrative oversight","An EIV security finding, because EIV access carries federal privacy obligations","A payroll issue outside the MOR","Acceptable if the user never logged in after resigning"],"ans":1,"exp":"Terminating EIV access belongs on the employee offboarding checklist, with the coordinator’s confirmation retained."},{"q":"A specialist correctly resolved an EIV income discrepancy but wrote nothing down. At review:","opts":["No finding, because the calculation was correct","A finding is written — the documented resolution memo is the deliverable","The finding is written against the tenant","The reviewer must accept the specialist’s verbal explanation"],"ans":1,"exp":"A correct answer without the dated, filed resolution memo scores the same as a wrong answer."},{"q":"A newly admitted household has no EIV income data yet because the certification has not populated. The file should:","opts":["Contain nothing — the absence is normal","Contain a dated note stating why the report is absent and when it will be obtained","Contain a printed error message only","Be excluded from the review sample"],"ans":1,"exp":"Undocumented absence is indistinguishable from noncompliance. Document the reason and the follow-up date."},{"q":"A waiting list is maintained with application dates but no times, and two same-day applicants were treated differently. This is treated as:","opts":["Acceptable, since dates were recorded","A deficiency — an unprovable selection order is treated as an absent one","A finding only if the applicant complains","Acceptable if the TSP does not require times"],"ans":1,"exp":"Waiting list order is the mechanism by which nondiscriminatory selection is proven; it must be demonstrable."},{"q":"A HUD-approved AFHMP commits to outreach to three named organizations and a foreign-language publication; none has been performed in two years. The correct cure is to:","opts":["Remove the AFHMP from the file","Either perform the marketing with dated evidence, or amend the plan to reflect actual practice and obtain approval","Note that the plan is aspirational","Wait for the next AFHMP update cycle"],"ans":1,"exp":"The AFHMP is a commitment measured against its own terms — never leave a plan on file describing outreach nobody performs."},{"q":"A defensible applicant rejection letter must, at minimum:","opts":["State only that screening criteria were not met","State the specific reason, identify the information relied upon, inform the applicant of the right to dispute its accuracy, and explain how to request a meeting","Be issued within 24 hours","Be signed by the owner personally"],"ans":1,"exp":"Vague letters prevent meaningful response and prevent the owner from later demonstrating consistent application of a TSP criterion."},{"q":"HUD-5380 and HUD-5382 must be provided at which points?","opts":["Every annual recertification","At denial of admission or assistance, at admission, and with any notice of eviction or termination of assistance","Only at admission","Only when a tenant discloses domestic violence"],"ans":1,"exp":"Three trigger points. Properties that over-distribute at recertification often under-distribute at denial, which is a required point."},{"q":"A property’s TSP cites \"Handbook 4350.3 Change-3\" while Change-4 governs. This is:","opts":["Not a finding, since staff practice is correct","A finding — written policies are required to be current, regardless of practice","A finding only if a tenant was harmed","Corrected automatically by HUD"],"ans":1,"exp":"Currency of written policy is itself an obligation; build a policy review calendar with revision logs."},{"q":"A property revised its TSP in March, the board approved in May, it was submitted to the CA in June, and no response has come by September. Best practice is to:","opts":["Continue operating under the old plan silently","Operate under the newest adopted and submitted version, retain the prior version with dates, and disclose the sequence in the desk review package","Withdraw the submission","Adopt a third interim version"],"ans":1,"exp":"Reviewers rarely cite a documented, disclosed transition; they routinely cite an undisclosed one."},{"q":"A rent roll line shows contract rent $1,145, tenant rent $0, and no certification effective date. The reviewer’s follow-up questions include all EXCEPT:","opts":["Whether $0 tenant rent is legitimate or a data error","Whether a utility reimbursement is owed and appears on the voucher","When the household was last certified, and whether TRACS matches","Whether the tenant’s credit score meets the TSP threshold"],"ans":3,"exp":"Credit screening applies at admission, not to a rent roll anomaly. The other three are the standard threads from an anomalous rent roll line."}]},"m82":{"title":"Chapter Quiz","questions":[{"q":"Under HUD’s uniform financial reporting standards, covered multifamily owners must submit annual financial information:","opts":["Within 30 days of fiscal year end","Within 90 days of fiscal year end, electronically through FASSUB","By December 31 each year regardless of fiscal year","Only when requested by the Contract Administrator"],"ans":1,"exp":"24 CFR Part 5, Subpart H sets the 90-day electronic submission requirement; the deadline follows the property’s own fiscal year end."},{"q":"A property with a June 30 fiscal year end must submit its annual financial statements by approximately:","opts":["March 31","September 28","December 31","June 30 of the following year"],"ans":1,"exp":"90 days after June 30 is roughly September 28. Owners with multiple fiscal years routinely apply one deadline to all properties."},{"q":"An auditor’s report includes a going-concern emphasis. The most direct MOR consequence is:","opts":["An automatic Unsatisfactory rating","It feeds the property’s risk classification, which drives MOR frequency under the 2022 rule","The MOR is cancelled","Tenant files must be re-reviewed"],"ans":1,"exp":"A Troubled classification caps the reward for a Superior MOR at 24 months instead of 36 — great files do not always buy the longest interval."},{"q":"Reserve for Replacement and Residual Receipts releases are requested on:","opts":["Form HUD-92547-A","Form HUD-9250, Funds Authorizations","Form HUD-50059","Form HUD-935.2A"],"ans":1,"exp":"HUD-9250 authorizes release of funds from the Reserve for Replacement or Residual Receipts, with supporting documentation."},{"q":"A property charges $9,400 of turnover painting and carpet cleaning to the Reserve for Replacement. This is:","opts":["Proper — turnover work is capital","Improper — routine turnover painting and cleaning are ordinarily operating expenses, and the miscategorization both depletes the reserve and flatters operating results","Proper if the units were vacant over 30 days","Permitted once per fiscal year"],"ans":1,"exp":"A written capitalization policy, actually applied, is the control. Auditors catch this, and MOR reviewers read audits."},{"q":"A property makes every required reserve deposit and holds $211,000 against a capital needs assessment showing $1.4 million over ten years. The property is:","opts":["Noncompliant and inadequate","Compliant with the deposit requirement but inadequately funded — a distinction between MOR compliance and asset management adequacy","Noncompliant but adequate","Exempt from further deposits"],"ans":1,"exp":"The MOR tests compliance with the deposit requirement; adequacy feeds asset management and risk classification — and is the argument for a deposit increase or budget-based adjustment."},{"q":"An annual recertification effective July 1 is transmitted September 12. Beyond the timeliness finding, the consequence is:","opts":["None — TRACS backdates automatically","July and August were billed at the old assistance amount, producing a retroactive adjustment in either direction","The certification is void","The tenant must be recertified again"],"ans":1,"exp":"Late transmission creates retroactive HAP adjustments; a pattern of them is itself a finding."},{"q":"A household’s income was understated by $3,600 for 14 months. The approximate financial exposure is:","opts":["$1,260 owed by the tenant only","$1,260 in HAP overpayment to be repaid AND $1,260 to be pursued from the tenant","$3,600 owed to HUD","No exposure if the error was unintentional"],"ans":1,"exp":"30% of $3,600 ÷ 12 ≈ $90/month × 14 = $1,260. The subsidy made up the difference, so both sides of the error exist."},{"q":"A household has monthly adjusted income of $840 and tenant rent of $252. Under HUD’s affordability benchmark, a monthly repayment of $310 is:","opts":["Appropriate — it repays the balance quickly","Excessive — rent plus repayment would be about 67% of monthly adjusted income, far above the roughly 40% benchmark","Required by regulation","Acceptable if the tenant signs voluntarily"],"ans":1,"exp":"HUD guidance frames repayment terms around affordability, commonly benchmarked at rent plus repayment not exceeding about 40% of monthly adjusted income."},{"q":"A tenant with a $2,140 repayment balance moves out. The owner’s obligation to repay improperly received assistance:","opts":["Is extinguished with the tenancy","Stands independently of whether the owner collects from the tenant","Transfers to the Contract Administrator","Is reduced by the security deposit automatically"],"ans":1,"exp":"The two obligations are not linked; owners should budget for uncollected tenant balances."},{"q":"Which coverage is specifically required by many regulatory agreements for those handling project funds and is routinely missing?","opts":["Umbrella liability","Fidelity bond or crime coverage naming or covering the project","Directors and officers coverage","Business interruption"],"ans":1,"exp":"Misappropriation by someone with routine access to project funds is the most common significant fraud in the industry."},{"q":"A property carries $6,000,000 hazard coverage on $9,000,000 replacement cost under an 80% coinsurance clause. Required coverage to avoid a penalty is:","opts":["$4,800,000","$6,000,000","$7,200,000","$9,000,000"],"ans":2,"exp":"80% of $9,000,000 = $7,200,000. Coverage below that triggers a proportional coinsurance penalty on any loss."},{"q":"An owner takes a $75,000 distribution in a year when surplus cash is $41,000. The result is:","opts":["Acceptable if repaid the following year","An improper distribution of $34,000 that must be returned, plus a regulatory agreement violation — and potentially a residual receipts deficiency from the same act","Acceptable if the property is not insured","A finding only if the auditor reports it"],"ans":1,"exp":"No distribution should be taken until the audited surplus cash computation is complete and reviewed."},{"q":"A property’s utility allowance has not been reviewed in six years. The risk is:","opts":["Only that tenants pay too much","Only that HAP is overpaid","Both — a low allowance harms tenants, a high allowance overpays HAP and overstates reimbursements; either way the stale analysis is a finding","None, since allowances are set by the utility company"],"ans":2,"exp":"Reviewers ask for the analysis and its date; the harm runs in both directions depending on which way the allowance is stale."},{"q":"An owner affiliate provides landscaping at $6,200/month. This identity-of-interest arrangement is defensible if the owner can produce:","opts":["A signed contract only","Contemporaneous market comparison bids, the contract, disclosure of the relationship, required approvals, and evidence services were performed","A statement that the price is reasonable","Proof the affiliate is licensed"],"ans":1,"exp":"Identity-of-interest arrangements are permitted but scrutinized; a market comparison constructed after the fact is rarely persuasive."}]},"m83":{"title":"Chapter Quiz","questions":[{"q":"The desk review package should be organized:","opts":["Alphabetically by document name","Against the reviewer’s own required documents list (Addendum C), in that order, with N/A pages for items that do not apply","Chronologically by document date","By department that produced each document"],"ans":1,"exp":"Submitting against the reviewer’s list, with no gaps, demonstrates records control before any document is evaluated."},{"q":"A required item does not exist at the property. The best treatment is to:","opts":["Omit it silently","Insert an N/A page in its list position with a one-line explanation","Substitute a similar document","Note it verbally at the on-site visit"],"ans":1,"exp":"A gap reads as disorganization; a written N/A reads as control."},{"q":"The rent roll is dated the 1st, the waiting list the 12th, and the occupancy report the 20th. The problem this creates is:","opts":["A regulatory violation","Apparent inconsistencies the reviewer must spend time disproving","Invalid TRACS data","A late submission finding"],"ans":1,"exp":"Choose one as-of date for all point-in-time reports and state it on the document control page."},{"q":"A property discovers nine households never received the VAWA notice at admission. The correct cure is to:","opts":["Back-date a distribution log to the admission dates","Distribute now with the actual current date, memorialize the discovery and the control, and disclose in the cover memo","Wait and distribute at the next annual recertification","Omit the item from the package"],"ans":1,"exp":"Fix forward, never backward. Back-dating converts a routine finding into a fraud problem."},{"q":"In a 30-day cure window, the FIRST triage priority is:","opts":["Tabs, labels and indexing","Items affecting eligibility and subsidy — certifications, ineligible households, Existing Tenant Search omissions, billing for vacated units","Policy currency","Training records"],"ans":1,"exp":"Money and program integrity items carry the most weight; cosmetic organization scores almost nothing."},{"q":"Correcting four income errors will generate retroactive HAP adjustments and tenant rent changes. Best practice is to:","opts":["Transmit corrections one at a time as they are computed","Compute all corrections, quantify exposure, prepare compliant tenant notices and affordability-tested repayment agreements, then transmit and disclose together with a summary schedule","Delay all corrections until after the review","Correct only the files likely to be sampled"],"ans":1,"exp":"Piecemeal corrections in the final week look like an unraveling; a sequenced, disclosed correction reads as control."},{"q":"A correction will raise a household’s rent by $180/month. Regarding tenant notice, the property must:","opts":["Implement immediately, since corrections are exempt from notice requirements","Provide the required advance notice and use the correct effective date, even though this delays the improvement in the numbers","Obtain the tenant’s written waiver of notice","Apply the increase retroactively"],"ans":1,"exp":"A correction is not an exception to notice requirements; shortcutting notice to improve a submission creates a new finding."},{"q":"A package request includes correspondence covered by pending litigation and attorney communications. The correct approach is to:","opts":["Include everything regardless","Omit it silently","Identify in the index that an item is withheld, state the general basis, and have counsel address it with the reviewer","Submit a summary written by staff"],"ans":2,"exp":"Silent omission discovered later is far more damaging than a disclosed, explained withholding."},{"q":"Which is the most avoidable finding in the entire MOR process?","opts":["Late or improperly formatted desk review submission","Income calculation error","Stale utility allowance","Missing EIV master file"],"ans":0,"exp":"Confirm format and deadline in writing, submit early through the designated channel, and obtain affirmative confirmation of receipt."},{"q":"Regarding electronic transmission of tenant data, the correct practice is to:","opts":["Email attachments to the reviewer’s personal address if the portal is slow","Use the CA’s designated secure channel consistent with HUD privacy and EIV security requirements, and document the instruction received","Post files to a public link","Redact all identifying data by default"],"ans":1,"exp":"Emailing unencrypted tenant income data is a security incident, not a shortcut; redaction protocol should be confirmed with the CA, not improvised."},{"q":"The property is missing the current management certification. The most important follow-up question is:","opts":["Whether the reviewer will accept a copy","What the missing document implies about the agent transition process — EIV coordinator reassignment, terminated user access, transition checklist","Whether to appeal","Whether the agent is licensed"],"ans":1,"exp":"One missing document often sits on top of several process failures; always ask what should have produced it."},{"q":"House rules distributed in 2019 were revised in 2024 but never distributed. For enforcement purposes:","opts":["The 2024 version governs upon adoption","Generally the version properly adopted AND distributed to tenants with required notice governs — and the 2024 distribution must be completed","Neither version is enforceable","The lease supersedes both automatically"],"ans":1,"exp":"A rule nobody received cannot fairly be enforced; submit both versions with an explanation and complete distribution properly."},{"q":"A Not Troubled property earns Above Average and will not be reviewed for 36 months. The correct readiness response is to:","opts":["Reduce self-audit activity to match the longer interval","Calendar 12-month and 24-month internal mock reviews the day the report arrives, because long intervals allow process drift and staff turnover","Wait until month 30 and run a sprint","Rely on the next reviewer to identify issues"],"ans":1,"exp":"Long intervals require more self-discipline, not less — and scheduled internal reviews are where recurring consulting revenue lives."},{"q":"A property spends four hours weekly on self-audit and has found nothing for six months. The best response is to:","opts":["Continue unchanged","Reduce frequency in that area and reinvest the time where prior audits actually found issues — risk-weight the self-audit as reviewers risk-weight samples","Stop self-auditing","Double the sample size"],"ans":1,"exp":"Track where your own audits find things and audit there more; readiness should be risk-weighted."},{"q":"The single most persuasive artifact of a readiness posture is:","opts":["A thick policy binder","A readiness record showing issues the property found and fixed itself over the past year, with dates and resolutions","A letter from the owner","A clean prior MOR alone"],"ans":1,"exp":"Evidence of self-identification and cure shifts the review from \"what is wrong here\" to \"does this system work.\""}]},"m91":{"title":"Chapter Quiz","questions":[{"q":"The governing principle of tenant file review is best stated as:","opts":["Substance over form — if the rent is right, the file is right","If it is not documented, it did not happen","Files need only satisfy the reviewer who is present","Electronic files are held to a lower standard"],"ans":1,"exp":"A reviewer cannot score intent or memory; the file is the only evidence a third party can verify."},{"q":"A file shows certifications effective 3/1/22, 3/1/23 and 3/1/25 with none in 2024. The FIRST step is to:","opts":["Assume it was misfiled and move on","Check TRACS to determine whether the certification was processed, never done, or interrupted by a move-out and re-admission","Recertify the household immediately","Report the household to the Contract Administrator"],"ans":1,"exp":"TRACS resolves the question in seconds, and the reviewer will run exactly that check."},{"q":"Form HUD-92006 (Supplement to Application) must be:","opts":["Completed by every applicant","Offered to every applicant, with either a completed form or a documented declination in the file","Filed only for elderly households","Submitted to HUD with the certification"],"ans":1,"exp":"The outcome must be one of two documented states; \"we mention it to everyone\" is not a record."},{"q":"A household is admitted from a 40-name waiting list with no record of position and no time stamps on the list. The most serious exposure is:","opts":["A minor file organization finding","An indefensible admission that reaches fair housing, because nondiscriminatory selection cannot be demonstrated","An income calculation error","A TRACS transmission delay"],"ans":1,"exp":"Waiting list order is the mechanism for proving nondiscriminatory selection; print or export the list at each offer and record the reason each skipped applicant was passed over."},{"q":"A household includes one member who elects not to contend eligible immigration status. The household is:","opts":["Ineligible for assistance","A mixed-status household whose assistance is prorated, with a declaration required for EVERY member","Eligible for full assistance","Required to be terminated at the next recertification"],"ans":1,"exp":"The most common error is a missing declaration for one member, which leaves the entire determination unsupported."},{"q":"A lease term begins June 15 but the 50059 and TRACS show an effective date of June 1. Beyond the overbilling, the lasting consequence is:","opts":["The lease is void","The recertification anniversary is set incorrectly and propagates for the life of the tenancy","The security deposit must be refunded","The utility allowance cannot be applied"],"ans":1,"exp":"Misaligned anniversaries are among the most common quiet errors; fix at move-in and verify at the first annual."},{"q":"A property adopted the VAWA lease addendum (HUD-91067) for new move-ins in 2018 but never executed it for existing households. The correct cure is to:","opts":["Backdate execution to each household’s move-in","Execute now with the actual current date, document the discovery and control, and disclose","Wait for HUD to require it","Rely on the HUD-5380 notice instead"],"ans":1,"exp":"The lease addendum and the HUD-5380/5382 distribution obligations are separate requirements, and both are missed independently. Never backdate."},{"q":"A tenant reports increased hours in March; a manager notes it on a sticky pad and nothing follows. EIV surfaces the wages in November. The property’s own March note:","opts":["Protects the property by showing diligence","Makes the finding worse, because it establishes the property knew and did not act","Is irrelevant to the finding","Shifts responsibility to the tenant"],"ans":1,"exp":"The control must be disposition of every logged report, not merely capture of it."},{"q":"A property mails recertification reminders on schedule but keeps no copies or mailing record. When six households recertify late:","opts":["The property is protected by its mailing practice","The defense is true but unprovable — retain a copy of each notice with date and method of delivery","The tenants are cited, not the property","No finding results if TRACS is current"],"ans":1,"exp":"Retained notices with delivery evidence convert an owner-side finding into a documented tenant-side delay in many cases."},{"q":"During an annual recertification a bank statement obtained by the property reveals a likely unreported settlement. The property must:","opts":["Ignore it, since it was not the subject of the verification request","Resolve the inconsistency and document the inquiry and its resolution in the file","Report the tenant for fraud immediately","Wait for EIV to flag it"],"ans":1,"exp":"Ignoring contradictory information in a document the property itself obtained is the least defensible position available."},{"q":"Tenant files must generally be retained for:","opts":["Three years from move-in","The term of tenancy plus three years","Five years from the last certification","Permanently"],"ans":1,"exp":"Term of tenancy plus three years, with tenant-file EIV documents on the same clock; state law or pending matters can extend it."},{"q":"A tenant vacates March 18; the TRACS termination is entered June 1. The consequences include all EXCEPT:","opts":["An overpayment of assistance for late March through May","A finding for untimely processing","An incorrect rent roll for three months","Automatic forfeiture of the security deposit"],"ans":3,"exp":"Deposit disposition follows state law and the lease; it is unrelated to the TRACS termination date."},{"q":"A tenant enters a rehabilitation facility for an expected 60 days. The property should:","opts":["Terminate assistance immediately","Leave assistance running until the tenant returns","Apply its written extended-absence policy consistently and document the facts, communications, and determination","Transfer the unit to another household"],"ans":2,"exp":"Reviewers rarely second-guess a reasonable documented determination; they always cite an absent one."},{"q":"A property purges a file three years after move-out, but an open repayment balance and a pending fair housing complaint exist. The correct control is:","opts":["Purge on schedule regardless","A retention schedule with a HOLD flag for litigation, complaints, open balances or audits, reviewed before any destruction — and secure, documented destruction","Retain all files permanently","Transfer the file to the tenant"],"ans":1,"exp":"Pending matters extend retention; records with tenant income and identity data must be destroyed securely and the destruction documented."},{"q":"The highest-yield control in tenant file management is:","opts":["Color-coded folders","A five-item second-person check at the close of every certification: signatures, verification support, EIV and discrepancy resolution, effective date and transmission, superseded documents marked","Annual file purges","Storing files off-site"],"ans":1,"exp":"Five minutes per certification by someone other than the preparer is the control most often missing at properties that score Satisfactory rather than Above Average."}]},"m92":{"title":"Chapter Quiz","questions":[{"q":"Under 4350.3 Chapter 7, the annual recertification reminder notices are sent approximately:","opts":["60, 45 and 30 days before the anniversary","120, 90 and 60 days before the anniversary, following an initial notice","90 and 30 days before the anniversary","Only once, 30 days before"],"ans":1,"exp":"An initial notice plus first, second and third reminders at roughly 120, 90 and 60 days; model notices appear at Exhibits 7-1 through 7-4."},{"q":"If a new recertification is not submitted within 15 months of the prior anniversary:","opts":["Nothing happens until the next annual","HUD terminates the certification and the owner must repay assistance for the three-month period from the date the recertification should have been effective through the end of the fifteenth month","The tenant is evicted automatically","The certification is processed retroactively by HUD"],"ans":1,"exp":"The 15-month cliff converts chronic lateness into a cash repayment obligation."},{"q":"A household with a September 1 annual anniversary processes an interim effective March 1. The next annual is due:","opts":["March 1 of the following year","September 1 — an interim does not move the annual anniversary","Twelve months after the interim is transmitted","At the property’s discretion"],"ans":1,"exp":"Resetting the annual clock to an interim date causes drift and eventually a household exceeding twelve months without an annual."},{"q":"A verified income DECREASE reported March 12 and verified March 20 is generally effective:","opts":["March 12","March 20","April 1 — the first of the month following the reported and verified change","At the next annual recertification"],"ans":2,"exp":"Waiting until the annual creates arrears the household cannot pay and a collection problem of the property’s own making."},{"q":"A verified income INCREASE is reported June 3 and verified June 25 at a property with a 30-day notice requirement. The increase takes effect:","opts":["July 1","August 1, following proper advance notice","June 25","Retroactively to June 1"],"ans":1,"exp":"Rent increases require the advance notice the lease and handbook require; implementing without documented notice is a tenant-rights finding."},{"q":"Under HOTMA, an interim is required when income decreases by 10 percent or more or increases by $2,400 or more annually. For HUD Multifamily programs, the mandatory compliance date is:","opts":["January 1, 2024","January 1, 2025","January 1, 2026","January 1, 2027, per Notice H 2025-07"],"ans":3,"exp":"Notice H 2025-07 (December 2025) revised the date from January 1, 2026 (set by H 2025-03) to January 1, 2027 for Multifamily programs."},{"q":"The verification hierarchy, highest to lowest, is:","opts":["Tenant self-certification, oral, written, EIV","EIV/UIV, third-party written, third-party oral, tenant self-certification","Third-party written, EIV, oral, self-certification","EIV, self-certification, oral, written"],"ans":1,"exp":"Verify at the highest level available and document why any lower level was used."},{"q":"A specialist computes income correctly from four pay stubs but the file contains no EIV comparison and no record of an attempted direct verification. The result is:","opts":["No finding — the number is correct","A finding for failure to document verification at the required level","A finding only if the number is wrong","A narrative comment"],"ans":1,"exp":"HUD verifies the process, because the process is the only assurance about the files nobody sampled."},{"q":"EIV shows $34,000 in wages; pay stubs support $28,600. The correct action is to:","opts":["Use the lower figure since stubs are more current","Use the higher figure since EIV is the highest level","Identify the source of the difference, determine correct anticipated annual income, and document the analysis and conclusion in a written resolution memo","Average the two figures"],"ans":2,"exp":"The written resolution memo is the deliverable; its absence is a finding regardless of which figure was used."},{"q":"HUD-9887/9887-A consent forms are valid for:","opts":["12 months","15 months","24 months","The term of tenancy"],"ans":1,"exp":"Fifteen months; verifications themselves are commonly treated as current if obtained within 120 days of the effective date."},{"q":"Total Tenant Payment is:","opts":["30 percent of monthly adjusted income","The greatest of 30% of monthly adjusted income, 10% of monthly gross income, the welfare rent where applicable, and the minimum rent","The lesser of 30% of adjusted income and the contract rent","Contract rent minus the utility allowance"],"ans":1,"exp":"When auditing, recompute all four elements rather than checking the one the software used."},{"q":"A tenant is paid biweekly at $1,180 gross. A specialist annualizes by multiplying by 24. The error is:","opts":["None — 24 is correct for biweekly","Biweekly is 26 pay periods; the income is understated by $2,360, understating TTP by roughly $59/month","Biweekly is 52 pay periods","The gross should be net"],"ans":1,"exp":"26 × $1,180 = $30,680 versus 24 × $1,180 = $28,320. The control is a worksheet requiring pay frequency and matching multiplier."},{"q":"A household has $2,100 in medical expenses and $1,400 in disability assistance expenses, with annual income of $18,000. Under 4350.3 as written, the deduction is:","opts":["$2,420 — the 3% threshold applied to each category","$2,960 — the 3% threshold ($540) applied ONCE across the combined expenses","$3,500 — no threshold applies","$1,560"],"ans":1,"exp":"Applying the threshold twice understates the deduction by $540 and overcharges the tenant — an error reviewers cite as readily as those that harm HUD."},{"q":"A household has an elderly head and an elderly spouse. Under 4350.3 as written, the elderly/disabled deduction is:","opts":["$800 — $400 per elderly person","$400 per FAMILY","$480 per person","$960"],"ans":1,"exp":"The deduction is per family, not per person — one of the most common single errors in the industry."},{"q":"A tenant refuses to sign the 50059, disputing the treatment of a household member’s income. The correct response is to:","opts":["Adjust the figure to obtain the signature","Process the certification as signed","Document the dispute, explain the basis with citation, offer the dispute process, follow the written policy for refusal — and re-verify that the determination is actually correct","Terminate assistance immediately"],"ans":2,"exp":"A tenant’s objection is sometimes the first sign of a real error; never alter a figure merely to obtain a signature."}]},"m93":{"title":"Chapter Quiz","questions":[{"q":"Under 24 CFR 5.403, a live-in aide must satisfy how many prongs?","opts":["One — essential to care","Two — essential to care and not obligated for support","Three — essential to care, not obligated for support, and would not be living in the unit except to provide services","Four, including a minimum hours-per-week requirement"],"ans":2,"exp":"All three prongs must be satisfied and documented; failure of any one means the person is a household member, not an aide."},{"q":"A live-in aide earns $41,000. In the household’s annual income, that amount is:","opts":["Counted in full","Counted at 50 percent","Not counted at all","Counted only above the asset threshold"],"ans":2,"exp":"A live-in aide’s income is excluded entirely; the aide is also not a household member for deduction purposes and has no succession rights."},{"q":"A tenant asks that her adult daughter, who has lived in the unit for three years with her own two children, be designated a live-in aide. The correct determination is:","opts":["Approve — a family member may serve as an aide","Deny live-in aide status — the daughter fails the \"would not be living in the unit except to provide services\" prong; she and her children are household members","Approve if a physician verifies the need","Approve but count half her income"],"ans":1,"exp":"Misclassifying an existing household member as an aide understates income substantially and produces a repayment."},{"q":"A 20-year-old unmarried student with no children, not a veteran, applies alone. His parents’ income exceeds the limit. He is:","opts":["Eligible — only his own income is examined","Ineligible under 24 CFR 5.612, because no exception applies and the parents are not income-eligible","Eligible if he is enrolled part time","Eligible if he signs a self-certification of independence"],"ans":1,"exp":"When no exception applies, BOTH the student’s and the parents’ income must be within limits."},{"q":"The same 20-year-old student has a two-year-old daughter living with him. Now he is:","opts":["Still ineligible","Exempt from the parental income test because he has a dependent child; evaluate his eligibility normally","Eligible only if married","Required to provide parental income anyway"],"ans":1,"exp":"Rejecting him on the parental test would misapply the rule and create a familial status exposure."},{"q":"Which is NOT an exception to the Section 8 student rule?","opts":["Age 24 or older","Veteran status","Enrollment in a graduate rather than undergraduate program","Married"],"ans":2,"exp":"Degree level is irrelevant; the exceptions are age 24+, veteran, married, dependent child, certain persons with disabilities assisted as of 11/30/2005, documented independence, and no locatable parents."},{"q":"A family of five has three eligible members and two who elect not to contend eligible status. If full assistance would be $840, prorated assistance is:","opts":["$336","$504","$560","$840"],"ans":1,"exp":"3 ÷ 5 = 0.60; $840 × 0.60 = $504. The family’s rent obligation rises by the $336 difference."},{"q":"In the same family, one non-contending member moves out. Prorated assistance becomes:","opts":["$504 — unchanged","$630 (3 ÷ 4 = 0.75)","$672","$840"],"ans":1,"exp":"A composition change with \"no income effect\" still changes the proration ratio; failing to process it overcharges the family for months."},{"q":"A family of four submits declarations for three members; staff assumed the 16-year-old was exempt. The result is:","opts":["No issue — minors are exempt","A finding: a declaration is required for EVERY household member, and the proration cannot be verified without it","The family is ineligible","Assistance must be terminated"],"ans":1,"exp":"Check declaration count against household member count on the 50059 at every certification."},{"q":"A household requests a minimum rent hardship exemption on March 12. The suspension is effective:","opts":["March 12","April 1 — the month following the request","When the determination is made","Retroactive to January 1"],"ans":1,"exp":"Suspension beginning the month following the request is mandatory and does not wait for the determination."},{"q":"A household’s four TTP elements are $3.00, $5.00, no welfare rent, and the $25 minimum rent. After a hardship suspension, TTP is:","opts":["$0.00","$3.00","$5.00","$25.00"],"ans":2,"exp":"The exemption removes only the minimum rent element; TTP becomes the greatest of the remaining elements."},{"q":"A household whose TTP is $190 because the welfare rent binds requests a minimum rent hardship exemption. The result is:","opts":["TTP falls to $25","TTP falls to $0","TTP remains $190 — the minimum rent was never the binding element","The request must be denied as frivolous"],"ans":2,"exp":"Explaining this before the family files is a kindness; explaining it afterward is a complaint."},{"q":"A property’s written policy commits to quarterly zero-income reviews and it performed one in eighteen months. The finding is written:","opts":["Only if HUD requires quarterly reviews","Against the property’s own written policy — never commit in writing to a frequency you will not perform","Against the tenants","Only if income was later discovered"],"ans":1,"exp":"If quarterly is unrealistic, write semiannual and perform it."},{"q":"An elderly head of household moves permanently to a nursing facility, leaving her 44-year-old daughter with no disability as the sole member. The certification consequence is:","opts":["No change until the annual","The elderly/disabled family deduction no longer applies, adjusted income rises, and the resulting rent increase requires proper advance notice","The household must be terminated","The daughter automatically receives the deduction"],"ans":1,"exp":"Properties routinely process the composition change and forget the deduction, creating a retroactive correction later."},{"q":"Under VAWA, lease bifurcation removes the household member whose eligibility qualified the household for assistance. The remaining household gets:","opts":["30 days to establish eligibility","90 days to establish eligibility or find alternative housing, with a possible extension of up to 60 additional days","Immediate termination","Six months with no extension"],"ans":1,"exp":"90 days, extendable by up to 60 additional days — and VAWA documentation is kept separately and securely, never in the tenant file or a shared database."}]},"m94":{"title":"Chapter Quiz","questions":[{"q":"The current mandatory compliance date for HOTMA Sections 102 and 104 in HUD Multifamily programs is:","opts":["January 1, 2024","January 1, 2025","January 1, 2026","January 1, 2027, set by Notice H 2025-07"],"ans":3,"exp":"H 2023-10 set January 1, 2025; H 2025-03 moved it to January 1, 2026; H 2025-07 (December 2025) revised it to January 1, 2027."},{"q":"A property operating under 4350.3 has a Tenant Selection Plan reciting HOTMA thresholds. The finding is:","opts":["A HOTMA compliance finding","That the written policy does not describe actual practice — a management systems finding","No finding until the compliance date","A finding against the Contract Administrator"],"ans":1,"exp":"Before the compliance date the live issue is the policy-practice match; pick a regime, state it with an effective date, and align every document."},{"q":"Under HOTMA, a family may self-certify net family assets at or below:","opts":["$5,000","$50,000 statutory, indexed — $52,787 for CY2026","$100,000","$105,574"],"ans":1,"exp":"Self-certification may be used for three years, with full third-party verification at least every third year."},{"q":"The HOTMA net family asset ELIGIBILITY ceiling for CY2026 is:","opts":["$52,787","$100,000","$105,574","No ceiling exists"],"ans":2,"exp":"$100,000 statutory, indexed annually; HUD published $105,574 for CY2026. 4350.3 as written has no asset ceiling at all."},{"q":"A household holds $96,000 in a 401(k) and $14,000 in savings. Under HOTMA, countable net family assets are approximately:","opts":["$110,000 — over the ceiling, deny","$14,000 — retirement accounts are excluded, so the household is under both the ceiling and the self-certification threshold","$96,000","$52,787"],"ans":1,"exp":"Apply the exclusions BEFORE testing the ceiling; failing to do so wrongly denies eligible households."},{"q":"An applicant owns a house in another state that is actively listed for sale with a broker. Under the real property restriction:","opts":["The household is ineligible","An exception applies for property actively offered for sale — document the listing, marketing evidence, and a follow-up at the next certification","The household must sell before applying","The restriction applies only to in-state property"],"ans":1,"exp":"Exceptions also cover property not meeting disability-related needs, DV-related circumstances, and property co-owned with a resident non-household member."},{"q":"An existing tenant inherits $140,000, exceeding the asset ceiling. The provider:","opts":["May waive the requirement","Cannot waive it, but may delay enforcement for current participants up to 6 months under a written, uniformly applied policy","Must terminate assistance immediately","Must transfer the household to another property"],"ans":1,"exp":"The limit cannot be waived; the grace period must be written into policy, applied uniformly, and documented per household."},{"q":"The HOTMA streamlined income determination is available when fixed income is:","opts":["50 percent or more of unadjusted income","75 percent or more","90 percent or more of unadjusted income, with a full third-party examination at least every three years","100 percent of unadjusted income"],"ans":2,"exp":"In intervening years a COLA is applied to each fixed source, and the family certifies both the 90 percent figure and that sources have not changed."},{"q":"A tenant receives $14,880 in Social Security and earns $4,800 part time. The streamlined determination is:","opts":["Available — the household is mostly fixed income","Not available: fixed income is 75.6 percent of unadjusted income, below the 90 percent test","Available only in the third year","Available with supervisory approval"],"ans":1,"exp":"Build the ratio computation into the worksheet; eyeballing a household as \"mostly Social Security\" applies the shortcut to households that do not qualify."},{"q":"Under the streamlined determination, the ANNUAL recertification:","opts":["Is performed only every three years","Still occurs every year — only the verification method is streamlined","Is replaced by a self-certification","Is deferred until income changes"],"ans":1,"exp":"Reading it as triennial recertification produces failures on the annual certification requirement and the 15-month rule."},{"q":"CY2026 HOTMA figures include which set?","opts":["Dependent $480, elderly/disabled family $400, passbook 2.0%","Dependent $500, elderly/disabled family $550, passbook 0.40%","Dependent $550, elderly/disabled family $500, passbook 0.06%","Dependent $500, elderly/disabled family $500, passbook 1.0%"],"ans":1,"exp":"HUD publishes the indexed amounts annually; always use the figures for the certification’s effective-date year."},{"q":"HOTMA raises the health and medical care expense threshold from 3 percent to:","opts":["5 percent","7.5 percent","10 percent, with phased hardship relief at 5 percent then 7.5 percent for qualifying families","15 percent"],"ans":2,"exp":"The phase-in is a hardship exemption with conditions — including that the family was receiving a deduction because expenses exceeded 3 percent as of the applicable date — not an automatic schedule for everyone."},{"q":"Under HOTMA interim triggers, an interim is REQUIRED when income:","opts":["Decreases by any amount, or increases by any amount","Decreases by 10 percent or more, or increases by $2,400 or more annually","Decreases by $2,400 or more, or increases by 10 percent or more","Changes by more than 5 percent in either direction"],"ans":1,"exp":"Note the asymmetry: decreases are measured in percent, increases in dollars, with owner discretion below the thresholds under a written policy."},{"q":"A household’s income falls from $26,400 to $23,400. An interim is:","opts":["Not required — the change is under $2,400","Required — the decrease is 11.4 percent, at or above the 10 percent trigger","Optional under any policy","Required only at the next annual"],"ans":1,"exp":"$3,000 ÷ $26,400 = 11.4 percent. A decrease to $24,600 (6.8 percent) would fall to the owner’s written discretionary policy."},{"q":"The most useful HOTMA transition deliverable a consultant can produce is:","opts":["A summary of the final rule","A two-column report of every household’s current TTP versus post-HOTMA TTP, sorted by size of increase","A new house rules document","A list of HUD notice numbers"],"ans":1,"exp":"The households at the top of that list are the ones needing notice, relief analysis, and a conversation before implementation — not after."}]},"m101":{"title":"Chapter Quiz","questions":[{"q":"Annual income is best defined as amounts:","opts":["Received in the prior calendar year","Anticipated to be received from sources outside the family in the next 12 months, unless specifically excluded","Reported on the family’s tax return","Remaining after payroll deductions"],"ans":1,"exp":"The definition is prospective, gross, from outside the family, and inclusive of everything not on the exclusion list."},{"q":"A tenant’s pay stub shows gross $1,846.15 biweekly and net $1,312.40. Annual income is:","opts":["$34,122.40","$44,307.60","$47,999.90","$22,153.80"],"ans":2,"exp":"$1,846.15 × 26 = $47,999.90. Counting net understates income by nearly $14,000 — roughly $345/month in TTP."},{"q":"A 16-year-old earns $7,800 and receives $9,048 in SSI on her own behalf. Countable income from this member is:","opts":["$0","$7,800","$9,048","$16,848"],"ans":2,"exp":"Earned income of a member under 18 is excluded; unearned income such as SSI is counted in full."},{"q":"A household member’s mother, who lives elsewhere, pays the tenant’s $148 monthly car insurance directly to the insurer. This is:","opts":["Never income, since the tenant never receives it","Generally counted — amounts received on behalf of a family member and regular contributions from persons outside the household are included","Counted only if it exceeds $200 per month","An asset, not income"],"ans":1,"exp":"Document what is paid, how often, by whom, since when, and the conclusion; the reviewer will ask directly."},{"q":"A household with an elderly head and elderly spouse, two dependents, $22,400 annual income and $3,900 unreimbursed medical expenses has total deductions of:","opts":["$4,588","$5,388","$4,188","$5,028"],"ans":0,"exp":"2 × $480 = $960; elderly/disabled family deduction $400 (per family); medical $3,900 − 3% of $22,400 ($672) = $3,228. Total $4,588."},{"q":"The 3 percent threshold for medical and disability assistance expenses is applied:","opts":["Separately to each category","Once, across both categories combined, and computed on ANNUAL income","To adjusted income, once","Only to households with children"],"ans":1,"exp":"Applying it twice understates the deduction and overcharges the tenant — cited as readily as errors harming HUD."},{"q":"A parent pays $6,400 for child care enabling employment that earns $4,800. The deductible child care amount for the work-related portion is:","opts":["$6,400","$4,800 — capped at the earned income enabled","$1,600","$0"],"ans":1,"exp":"The work-related portion is capped at the income earned as a result; care enabling education is analyzed separately."},{"q":"Annual income $24,000; total deductions $18,180; owner pays utilities; contract rent $940. TTP is:","opts":["$145.50","$200.00","$25.00","$282.00"],"ans":1,"exp":"Adjusted income $5,820 → 30% of $485 = $145.50; 10% of gross $2,000 = $200.00. Element (b) binds — the case software most often gets wrong."},{"q":"A family in an as-paid locality has adjusted monthly income $430, gross monthly income $550, and a designated welfare housing portion of $190. TTP is:","opts":["$129.00","$55.00","$190.00","$25.00"],"ans":2,"exp":"The welfare rent is the greatest element. It applies only in as-paid localities; elsewhere element (c) should be permanently n/a."},{"q":"A tenant with $600 annual income has a $84 utility allowance and $760 contract rent. Tenant rent and reimbursement are:","opts":["Tenant rent $25.00, no reimbursement","Tenant rent $0.00 and a utility reimbursement of $59.00","Tenant rent $59.00, no reimbursement","Tenant rent $0.00, no reimbursement"],"ans":1,"exp":"TTP is $25 (minimum rent binds); $25 − $84 is negative, so tenant rent is $0 and $59 is reimbursed."},{"q":"After a minimum rent hardship suspension for a household whose other elements are $58.00, $22.00 and n/a, TTP becomes:","opts":["$0.00","$22.00","$58.00","$25.00"],"ans":2,"exp":"The exemption removes only the minimum rent element; the TTP is the greatest of what remains."},{"q":"A Schedule C shows net profit $25,400 after $5,200 of depreciation. Countable annual income from the business is:","opts":["$25,400","$20,200","$30,600 — depreciation is added back because it is not a cash expense of producing income","$38,400"],"ans":2,"exp":"Virtually every Schedule C has depreciation, so copying net profit understates income on nearly every self-employed household."},{"q":"A tenant’s business shows a $3,200 net loss. The correct treatment is:","opts":["Reduce the household’s wage income by $3,200","Count business income as zero and count all other income in full","Exclude the household from certification","Carry the loss forward to next year"],"ans":1,"exp":"A business loss does not offset other income for HUD purposes."},{"q":"A court orders $450/month in child support; the tenant documents receiving nothing for 14 months. The property should:","opts":["Count $5,400 because it was ordered","Count what is actually received or reliably anticipated, documenting the order, the tenant’s certification, and agency payment records where obtainable","Count half the ordered amount","Exclude child support entirely"],"ans":1,"exp":"State disbursement unit records are usually available and are the strongest evidence; re-examine at each certification."},{"q":"A 19-year-old dependent who is a full-time student earns $9,400. If instead he were the HEAD of household and a full-time student, his earned income would be:","opts":["Still limited by the dependent-student threshold","Counted in full — the dependent full-time student rule does not apply to a head, spouse or co-head","Entirely excluded","Counted at 50 percent"],"ans":1,"exp":"Three questions must be asked explicitly: is the member a dependent, is the member a full-time student, and is the member head/spouse/co-head."}]},"m102":{"title":"Chapter Quiz","questions":[{"q":"The value used for an asset is:","opts":["Market value","Purchase price","Cash value — market value minus reasonable costs of converting it to cash","The balance shown on the most recent statement"],"ans":2,"exp":"Using market value overstates assets and, under HOTMA, can wrongly push a household over the eligibility ceiling."},{"q":"A rental house is worth $184,000 with a $121,000 mortgage and roughly $14,720 in costs of sale. Cash value is:","opts":["$184,000","$63,000","$48,280","$169,280"],"ans":2,"exp":"$184,000 − $121,000 − $14,720 = $48,280. Net rental income is counted separately as asset income."},{"q":"A whole life policy has a $50,000 death benefit, $7,400 cash surrender value and a $600 surrender charge. The asset value is:","opts":["$50,000","$7,400","$6,800","$0 — insurance is never an asset"],"ans":2,"exp":"Cash surrender value net of the surrender charge. Term policies have no cash value and are not assets at all."},{"q":"Under 4350.3 as written, if total cash value of all assets is $9,455 and actual income is $150, the property counts:","opts":["$150","The greater of actual income and imputed income at the published passbook rate","Only imputed income","Nothing, since the family reported actual income"],"ans":1,"exp":"Above the threshold, the comparison runs both ways — high-yield holdings can make actual income the greater figure."},{"q":"A family holds $42,000 in CDs earning $1,470. At a 2 percent imputation convention, the counted asset income is:","opts":["$840","$1,470","$2,310","$0"],"ans":1,"exp":"Actual ($1,470) exceeds imputed ($840). Staff who assume imputation always applies above the threshold understate income."},{"q":"A family holds $13,300 in non-interest-bearing accounts and cash. Counted asset income is:","opts":["$0 — no income is earned","Imputed income on the full cash value, because the family is above the threshold","Only the amount above $5,000, imputed","Actual income, which is zero"],"ans":1,"exp":"A zero-yield balance still generates imputed income above the threshold — the case owners most often miss."},{"q":"A family at $4,980 in assets deposits $30. The effect on counted asset income (2 percent convention, $12 actual) is:","opts":["None","It rises from $12 to $100.20, because imputation now applies to the TOTAL cash value","It rises by $0.60","It falls to zero"],"ans":1,"exp":"The cliff is easy to state and easy to miss, which is exactly why reviewers test it."},{"q":"A tenant transferred a $28,000 CD to her daughter for $1 fourteen months ago. The amount included in family assets is:","opts":["$0 — the asset is gone","$28,000","$27,999 — the difference between cash value and the amount received","$14,000"],"ans":2,"exp":"The imputed amount rolls off two years after the disposal; calendar the roll-off date so the household is not overcharged indefinitely."},{"q":"A tenant sold her car for $6,200, within its market value, and spent the proceeds on rent and medical bills. This is:","opts":["A disposal below fair market value","Not a disposal below fair market value — the family simply has fewer assets","An excluded lump sum","Counted as income"],"ans":1,"exp":"Spending money is not disposing of assets below value; document the sale and disposition because the swing invites the question."},{"q":"A tenant sold land worth $40,000 to a cousin for $22,000. The imputed amount is:","opts":["$0 — money changed hands","$18,000","$40,000","$22,000"],"ans":1,"exp":"The test is fair market value, not whether money changed hands — the case people most often miss."},{"q":"Under HOTMA, a family may self-certify assets at or below the indexed threshold. If the file contains a recent statement showing $80,000, the owner should:","opts":["Accept the self-certification, since HOTMA permits it","Document the conflict, resolve it, and record the resolution — the self-certification option does not require ignoring known contrary information","Deny assistance immediately","Impute income on the self-certified amount"],"ans":1,"exp":"Reviewers look specifically for whether self-certification was used as a shortcut around known facts."},{"q":"A household owns a house worth $142,000 with a $155,000 mortgage. Under HOTMA:","opts":["The household is eligible because the asset value is zero","The negative equity offsets other assets","Asset value is treated as zero, but the REAL PROPERTY ownership prong is still in play unless an exception applies","The household must sell before applying"],"ans":2,"exp":"A household with zero countable asset value can still be ineligible under the ownership prong — the trap in the real property rules."},{"q":"A duplex has cash value $97,200 and produces $6,050 in net income after operating expenses, taxes, insurance and mortgage interest. Which is NOT deductible in computing that net income?","opts":["Mortgage interest","Property taxes","Mortgage principal payments","Insurance"],"ans":2,"exp":"Principal payments build equity and are not an expense of producing income; interest generally is."},{"q":"A 12-year-old household member has a $6,800 custodial account. The correct treatment is:","opts":["Exclude it — income of minors is excluded","Include the asset in total family assets and apply the threshold test to the household total","Count half its value","Treat it as a disposal"],"ans":1,"exp":"A minor’s EARNED income is excluded; asset income attributable to the household is counted — different rules that staff frequently conflate."},{"q":"The most valuable single change to a client’s application form during HOTMA transition is:","opts":["Adding a signature line","Rewriting the real property question to elicit ownership of ANY real property interest anywhere, including inherited and co-owned interests","Adding an emergency contact field","Requesting more pay stubs"],"ans":1,"exp":"Most forms ask only about \"income from real estate,\" which never surfaces a vacant inherited parcel producing nothing."}]},"m103":{"title":"Chapter Quiz","questions":[{"q":"EIV use is mandated by:","opts":["HUD Handbook 4350.1 Chapter 6","24 CFR 5.233, which requires use of the system in its entirety","The HAP contract only","Contract Administrator policy"],"ans":1,"exp":"Failure to use EIV in its entirety may result in sanctions and assessment of disallowed costs for incorrect subsidy or rent calculations."},{"q":"A property prints an EIV income report at every recertification but runs no monitoring reports, keeps no master file, and performs no user access audit. This property is:","opts":["Compliant — income verification is the core requirement","Deficient — EIV compliance has three legs: verification use, monitoring reports, and security administration","Compliant if no discrepancies exist","Exempt if under 50 units"],"ans":1,"exp":"Most EIV findings concern the monitoring reports and security requirements, not the income reports properties dutifully print."},{"q":"The EIV Existing Tenant Search must be run:","opts":["At each annual recertification","Before move-in, as a pre-admission gate","Quarterly for all households","Only when a Multiple Subsidy hit occurs"],"ans":1,"exp":"Running it after admission cannot prevent the double subsidy it exists to catch."},{"q":"Master-file EIV reports (New Hires, Multiple Subsidy, Deceased Tenants, identity verification) are retained:","opts":["Term of tenancy plus three years","Three years","One year","Permanently"],"ans":1,"exp":"Tenant-file reports — Income, Income Discrepancy, No Income Reported — are retained for term of tenancy plus three years."},{"q":"A household member’s name mismatch causes identity verification to fail, so no income data returns. The danger is:","opts":["The tenant will be denied assistance","Staff read the blank result as \"no wages,\" making an earning member effectively invisible","The report cannot be printed","EIV access will be suspended"],"ans":1,"exp":"This is why identity verification reports are run monthly and corrected immediately — an unverified member is an invisible member."},{"q":"A quarterly New Hires report shows new employment; the property notes it and waits until the annual nine months later. This is:","opts":["Acceptable — the annual will capture it","Worse than not running the report, because the property now has a written record showing it knew and did not act","A tenant violation only","Acceptable if the increase is under $2,400"],"ans":1,"exp":"Running a report and not acting on it converts a monitoring gap into a documented failure to act."},{"q":"An EIV income discrepancy is correctly analyzed and resolved, but nothing is written down. The result is:","opts":["No finding — the outcome was correct","A finding: the written resolution memo is the deliverable","A finding only if the tenant complains","A narrative comment"],"ans":1,"exp":"A correctly resolved discrepancy with no memo scores the same as an unresolved one."},{"q":"A discrepancy reveals an unreported second job paying $1,150/month that began 8 months before the certification. The approximate exposure is:","opts":["$1,150 total","About $2,760 in tenant underpayment and about $2,760 in HAP overpayment","$13,800 owed by the tenant","No exposure until the next annual"],"ans":1,"exp":"Income understated by $13,800 → TTP understated roughly $345/month × 8 months on each side. Also examine whether New Hires reports were run during those months."},{"q":"A discrepancy turns out to be caused by the property misapplying the minor-income rule, not by tenant nondisclosure. The correct handling differs in that:","opts":["No correction is needed","There is no repayment agreement premised on nondisclosure; the household must be notified of a correction it did not cause, and the corrective action is training and a worksheet change","The tenant still owes the full amount immediately","The certification is voided"],"ans":1,"exp":"Reviewers notice whether a property distinguishes its own errors from tenant errors."},{"q":"EIV reports an employer the tenant denies, alleging identity theft. The property should:","opts":["Take adverse action based on the federal data","Accept the tenant’s statement without inquiry","Give the tenant an opportunity to contest, attempt verification with the reported employer, advise on correcting federal records, and document each step and the conclusion","Terminate assistance pending investigation"],"ans":2,"exp":"A documented, reasoned process is compliant even when the underlying facts remain unresolved."},{"q":"A staff member resigned in March; her EIV access is still active in September. This is:","opts":["Acceptable if she never logged in","A security finding — access termination belongs on the offboarding checklist with the coordinator’s written confirmation retained","A payroll issue","Acceptable if the coordinator was on leave"],"ans":1,"exp":"Maintaining active federal system access for an unauthorized person is a security matter, not administrative housekeeping."},{"q":"Three staff members share one EIV login \"because it is easier.\" The core problem is:","opts":["Slower system performance","It defeats individual attribution, prevents terminating one person’s access, and renders the signed Rules of Behavior meaningless","It violates the property’s lease","It requires a waiver from the CA"],"ans":1,"exp":"Each user gets their own credentials, with no exceptions for temporary staff."},{"q":"A property discovers it has no master file reports for the past two years. The best response is to:","opts":["Recreate the reports with historical dates","Run current reports immediately, begin the log, document the gap and its discovery in a memo, state the control now in place, and disclose it","Say nothing and hope the master file is not requested","Destroy the remaining records"],"ans":1,"exp":"This converts a \"no system\" finding into a \"system installed, gap acknowledged\" finding — a real difference in weight. Never recreate records with false dates."},{"q":"The single most effective file-level EIV control is:","opts":["A color-coded master file binder","Certification checklist items that BLOCK closure until the EIV income report is obtained and any discrepancy is resolved in writing","Monthly staff meetings","A larger file cabinet"],"ans":1,"exp":"It converts an easily deferred task into a hard dependency."},{"q":"At a 24-unit property with one manager, the \"second person verifies\" control cannot be staffed on site. The correct response is to:","opts":["Leave the control in the written policy anyway","Replace it with one that can be staffed — remote quarterly verification by a regional manager, the owner’s bookkeeper, or an outside consultant — and write it down","Eliminate verification entirely","Have the manager verify her own work"],"ans":1,"exp":"A control that cannot be staffed should be replaced, not left in the policy as fiction."}]},"m111":{"title":"Chapter Quiz","questions":[{"q":"For Section 8 project-based assistance, income targeting under 24 CFR 5.653 requires that of units becoming available in a fiscal year:","opts":["At least 25 percent be leased to very low income families","At least 40 percent be leased to extremely low income families at admission","At least 50 percent be leased to extremely low income families","No targeting requirement applies"],"ans":1,"exp":"Track by fiscal year with a running admissions log and a percentage computed after every admission — not a count at year end."},{"q":"A property fills 14 assisted units in a fiscal year. The minimum number that must be extremely low income at admission is:","opts":["5","6","7","4"],"ans":1,"exp":"40% of 14 = 5.6, so at least 6 — round up; do not round down."},{"q":"A waiting list records application dates but not times, and two same-day applicants were treated differently. This is:","opts":["Acceptable if the TSP does not require times","A deficiency — an unprovable order is treated as an absent one","A finding only if a complaint is filed","Acceptable for properties under 50 units"],"ans":1,"exp":"Capture date AND time at the moment of application, log changes, and preserve a snapshot of the list at each offer."},{"q":"A 2-bedroom is offered down the list past three applicants for documented reasons (size mismatch, written declination, unreachable after three attempts). The file is:","opts":["Deficient — the list order was not followed","Compliant, because every deviation is documented and reconstructable","Deficient unless HUD approved the deviations","Compliant only if the TSP permits skipping"],"ans":1,"exp":"The compliance work is in the notation, not the decision — roughly thirty seconds per applicant."},{"q":"A property closes its waiting list but continues accepting walk-in applications \"just in case,\" entering them later with original dates. This is:","opts":["Good customer service","A discrimination exposure — the beneficiaries are those who happened to be told about the informal practice","Permitted if the TSP is silent","Required to avoid turning applicants away"],"ans":1,"exp":"Either the list is open to everyone or closed to everyone."},{"q":"A purge notice in standard print is sent to an applicant who previously requested large-print communications, and she is removed for non-response. The problem is:","opts":["The purge criteria were unreasonable","The execution as to that applicant was not, creating a fair housing exposure — the list needs a communication-needs field applied to every mass mailing","Purges are prohibited","The response period was too long"],"ans":1,"exp":"A five-minute system change prevents an entire category of complaint."},{"q":"A property purges from 340 names to 96 and retains only the current list. The deficiency is:","opts":["The size of the reduction","No purge file — notice template, mailing list, date mailed, responses, and removal decisions with dates","That the purge was not approved by HUD","That 30 days is too short"],"ans":1,"exp":"Nothing improper may have occurred, and nothing can be shown."},{"q":"The AFHMP is evaluated by a reviewer principally against:","opts":["Whether the property advertises at all","Whether the property did what its own approved plan says it does","The property’s occupancy rate","The number of applicants on the list"],"ans":1,"exp":"The plan is a commitment measured against its own terms; either perform it with dated evidence or amend it and obtain approval."},{"q":"A property fills nearly all vacancies through resident referrals. The fair housing concern is that:","opts":["Referrals are prohibited","A referral pipeline tends to replicate the existing resident population — the precise effect affirmative marketing exists to counterbalance","Referral applicants cannot be screened","Referrals violate the waiting list order automatically"],"ans":1,"exp":"Compare applicant and admission demographics to the market area; if materially less diverse, the outreach commitments must be real."},{"q":"An applicant declines a third-floor unit because she cannot manage stairs. The file should record:","opts":["\"Declined\"","The offer, the response, the accessibility need recorded on the list, and information provided about accessible units and the accommodation process","Removal from the waiting list","A rejection letter"],"ans":1,"exp":"A stairs-related declination is a flag, not merely a \"no\" — otherwise nobody knows when a first-floor unit becomes available."},{"q":"Applicant A is rejected for two late payments; Applicant B, admitted, had three in the same period. The underlying problem is:","opts":["The reviewer’s sampling method","A vague TSP criterion (\"a pattern of late payments may be grounds\") — vague criteria are not lenient, they are indefensible","That credit screening is prohibited","That both should have been rejected"],"ans":1,"exp":"Make criteria objective, or document a non-discriminatory basis for every deviation; keep a screening decision summary in every file."},{"q":"A compliant rejection letter must include all EXCEPT:","opts":["The specific reason for rejection","The information relied upon and its source","The right to dispute accuracy and how to request a meeting","The names of other applicants who were selected instead"],"ans":3,"exp":"Applicant-specific information about other applicants is not disclosed; the other three elements are required for a defensible letter."},{"q":"An applicant asks the property to consider mitigating information about an eviction related to a psychiatric disability. The property:","opts":["Must abandon its screening criteria","Must consider the accommodation request, engage in the interactive process, and make an individualized assessment — documented","May refuse to consider mitigating circumstances as a blanket policy","Must approve the application"],"ans":1,"exp":"Blanket refusal to consider mitigating circumstances is where properties get into serious trouble."},{"q":"A property’s software was configured so a preference intended for certain units applied property-wide for two years. This is:","opts":["A staff training issue only","A systemic finding requiring reconstruction of correct order to determine whether applicants were harmed","Not a finding, since staff followed the system","A vendor problem outside the property’s responsibility"],"ans":1,"exp":"Test configuration against the TSP with constructed cases at setup, after every update, and annually."},{"q":"Site staff keep the official list in software and a faster \"working list\" in a spreadsheet that drives actual selections. This is dangerous because:","opts":["Spreadsheets are prohibited","It looks like concealment even when it is only convenience, and the two records diverge","The software vendor may object","It slows down leasing"],"ans":1,"exp":"One list, in one place — a workaround that becomes the real system is a finding waiting to be written."}]},"m112":{"title":"Chapter Quiz","questions":[{"q":"When a reviewer asks why an applicant was rejected, the only acceptable answer begins:","opts":["\"The manager decided\"","\"Our Tenant Selection Plan says\"","\"The applicant did not seem suitable\"","\"HUD requires it\""],"ans":1,"exp":"The TSP is the yardstick against which every admission and rejection is measured."},{"q":"A TSP permits rejection of applicants who \"do not appear likely to be good residents.\" This criterion is:","opts":["Appropriately flexible","Unenforceable and indefensible — it permits any decision and explains none","Acceptable if applied consistently","Required by 4350.3"],"ans":1,"exp":"Every criterion must be stated so that two staff members applying it to the same facts reach the same result."},{"q":"Which is a MANDATORY denial rather than a discretionary one?","opts":["Any felony conviction","A poor credit score","A person subject to a LIFETIME sex offender registration requirement (24 CFR 5.856)","An eviction filing within three years"],"ans":2,"exp":"Mandatory denials are limited: lifetime sex offender registration, and conviction for manufacturing methamphetamine on federally assisted premises."},{"q":"Under HUD’s 2016 Office of General Counsel guidance on criminal records, arrests that did not result in conviction:","opts":["May be used freely as a basis for denial","Generally may not be the basis for denial, because an arrest does not prove the underlying conduct occurred","May be used only for violent offenses","Must be reported to HUD"],"ans":1,"exp":"Criminal records screening can create disparate impact liability even without discriminatory intent; blanket bans are very difficult to defend."},{"q":"A defensible criminal history policy should include all EXCEPT:","opts":["Defined categories of conduct bearing on safety, with defined lookback periods","Individualized assessment and documented consideration of mitigating factors","Notice of the specific information relied upon and an opportunity to dispute it","A categorical ban on all applicants with any felony conviction"],"ans":3,"exp":"HUD’s April 2024 proposed rule \"Reducing Barriers to HUD-Assisted Housing\" would push further toward individualized assessment — verify its current status before advising a client."},{"q":"An applicant’s criminal record arises from an incident in which she was the survivor of domestic violence. The property:","opts":["May deny based on the record, since a conviction exists","May not deny on the basis of her status as a survivor, and must not use a record that is itself a product of the victimization — with the analysis documented","Must deny to protect other residents","Must obtain a police report to verify"],"ans":1,"exp":"This is where a mechanical screening process produces a fair housing violation and a VAWA violation in a single decision."},{"q":"A property applies the prior year’s income limits for four months after new limits publish. The most serious consequence is:","opts":["A minor administrative finding","Applicants were denied housing on a superseded standard","HAP was overpaid","TRACS rejected the certifications"],"ans":1,"exp":"Calendar the annual income limit publication and document the table update and its effective date."},{"q":"A household of three has verified income of $41,880 against a very low income limit of $41,900. The household is:","opts":["Ineligible — too close to the limit","Eligible, and the file must show the limit used, its effective date, household size, and source","Eligible only with CA approval","Required to be placed at the bottom of the list"],"ans":1,"exp":"A $200 annualization error changes the answer, so the computation must be right to the dollar and the basis documented."},{"q":"A household whose income rises above the limit AFTER admission:","opts":["Must be terminated immediately","Is subject to continued-occupancy rules, which are a different test from initial eligibility","Must reapply through the waiting list","Loses its lease at the next anniversary"],"ans":1,"exp":"Confusing initial eligibility with continued occupancy produces wrongful terminations — one of the more serious errors a property can make."},{"q":"Applicant A is rejected for a $640 prior-landlord balance; Applicant B is admitted with a $900 balance because staff \"knew it was a dispute.\" The problem is:","opts":["That B should also have been rejected","That the exception was not documented — an undocumented exception is indistinguishable from arbitrary treatment","That prior-landlord balances cannot be used","That the amounts differ"],"ans":1,"exp":"If a mitigating explanation is accepted, write down what was explained, what was verified, and why the decision differed."},{"q":"An applicant has NO credit history. The best practice is for the TSP to:","opts":["Treat no credit history as bad credit","State in advance what alternative evidence of payment history will be accepted, since \"no credit history\" is not bad credit and treating it as disqualifying can create disparate impact","Reject the applicant automatically","Require a co-signer in all cases"],"ans":1,"exp":"Decide the policy in advance rather than improvising at the counter."},{"q":"A court record shows an eviction FILING three years ago. Relying on the filing alone is risky because:","opts":["Filings are confidential","Filings are not judgments — cases are dismissed or resolved, and rejecting on a filing may deny an applicant who prevailed","Filings expire after one year","Only landlords may access them"],"ans":1,"exp":"Look at the disposition, share the information with the applicant, and consider mitigating circumstances including VAWA-related ones."},{"q":"A compliant rejection notice must state:","opts":["Only that the application was denied","The specific reason, the information relied upon and its source, the right to dispute accuracy and completeness, and how and by when to request a meeting","The names of approved applicants","The property’s occupancy rate"],"ans":1,"exp":"A notice the applicant cannot respond to is also a notice the property cannot later prove it applied consistently."},{"q":"An applicant requests, as a reasonable accommodation, that the property consider disability-related circumstances bearing on a screening result. The property must:","opts":["Abandon its criteria","Consider the request, engage in the interactive process, make an individualized assessment, and document it","Refer the applicant to another property","Deny the request as untimely"],"ans":1,"exp":"A rejected applicant whose accommodation request went unanswered is the fact pattern most likely to become a complaint."},{"q":"A property whose rejection notices NEVER produce a correction or a meeting request most likely:","opts":["Has excellent screening","Is not issuing notices that actually let applicants understand and respond","Has no rejected applicants","Is exempt from the meeting requirement"],"ans":1,"exp":"The notice makes errors visible; a system that never surfaces one is probably not communicating the basis for the decision."}]},"m113":{"title":"Chapter Quiz","questions":[{"q":"The seven federal protected classes under the Fair Housing Act are:","opts":["Race, color, religion, national origin, sex, familial status, disability","Race, color, religion, national origin, sex, age, income","Race, color, creed, ancestry, sex, marital status, disability","Race, color, religion, national origin, sex, veteran status, disability"],"ans":0,"exp":"Familial status and disability were added by the Fair Housing Amendments Act of 1988. State and local law frequently adds more."},{"q":"A house rule prohibits bicycles and play equipment on the grounds and requires adult supervision of children in all common areas at all times. This most likely constitutes:","opts":["A lawful safety policy","Familial status discrimination — rules that burden households with children must be narrowly tailored to a specific legitimate safety concern","A Section 504 violation","A VAWA violation"],"ans":1,"exp":"Review every house rule asking whether it burdens families with children more than other households, and what the specific tailored reason is."},{"q":"A leasing agent tells an applicant with young children she \"would probably prefer the ground floor near the playground.\" This is:","opts":["Good customer service","Steering — directing a household toward particular units based on familial status","Required by safety rules","Acceptable if the applicant agrees"],"ans":1,"exp":"Describe all available units and their features neutrally and let the household choose."},{"q":"A property requires income of three times the CONTRACT rent from all applicants, including assisted households. The strongest fair housing objection is:","opts":["Disparate treatment","Disparate impact — a less discriminatory alternative (applying the multiple to the tenant’s portion) serves the same legitimate interest","It violates the HAP contract","It is a Section 504 violation"],"ans":1,"exp":"Many jurisdictions also protect source of income, which would independently reach the policy."},{"q":"A reasonable accommodation request must be:","opts":["Submitted on the property’s form","Accepted orally or in writing, from the person or someone acting on their behalf, and responded to promptly","Made only at recertification","Approved by HUD before implementation"],"ans":1,"exp":"A property may offer a form as a convenience but may not require one as a condition of considering a request; unreasonable delay is treated as a denial."},{"q":"For an assistance animal request where the disability is not obvious, a property MAY:","opts":["Require a diagnosis and medical records","Request verification of the disability and the disability-related need from a reliable third party in a position to know","Require certification and specific training for the animal","Charge a pet deposit"],"ans":1,"exp":"Assistance animals are not pets; pet rules, deposits and fees do not apply, and breed or training requirements may not be imposed."},{"q":"For a HUD-ASSISTED property, a resident’s request for grab bars in the bathroom should be:","opts":["Permitted at the tenant’s expense, with restoration required at move-out","Made at the property’s expense under Section 504, subject to an undue financial and administrative burden analysis","Denied as a structural alteration","Referred to the tenant’s insurance"],"ans":1,"exp":"Recipients of federal financial assistance pay for structural modifications needed as an accommodation. \"Install at your own expense\" is the violation."},{"q":"Under 24 CFR 8.22, a newly constructed 84-unit assisted property must provide at least:","opts":["4 mobility-accessible units and 1 hearing/vision unit","5 mobility-accessible units and 2 hearing/vision units","8 mobility-accessible units","2 accessible units total"],"ans":1,"exp":"5% of 84 = 4.2 → at least 5 mobility units; an additional 2% = 1.68 → at least 2 hearing/vision units. Round up; these are minimums."},{"q":"Under 24 CFR 8.23, the substantial alteration trigger applies when a project has 15 or more units and alteration costs are:","opts":["15 percent or more of replacement cost","50 percent or more of replacement cost","75 percent or more of the replacement cost of the completed facility","Any amount over $1 million"],"ans":2,"exp":"Run this computation at the planning stage of any rehabilitation — discovering the trigger during construction is a very expensive problem."},{"q":"A 40-unit property spends $3.4 million on alterations against a $4.1 million replacement cost. The consequence is:","opts":["No accessibility requirement applies","83 percent triggers substantial alteration — the new construction requirements of 8.22 apply","Only the altered portions must be accessible","A waiver is automatic"],"ans":1,"exp":"Below the trigger, the \"other alterations\" rule applies: altered portions accessible to the maximum extent feasible until 5 percent of units are accessible."},{"q":"A deaf resident attends a 45-minute recertification interview involving income and legal obligations. Handing them a pen and paper:","opts":["Satisfies the effective communication obligation","May not satisfy it — the appropriate auxiliary aid depends on the complexity of the communication and the person’s usual method; ask, provide, and do not charge","Is required by HUD","Is acceptable if the resident does not object"],"ans":1,"exp":"Document the request and what was provided; the obligation is effective communication, not merely some communication."},{"q":"An undue financial and administrative burden claim is evaluated against:","opts":["The unit’s monthly rent","The site’s petty cash","All resources available to the program or activity, with alternatives considered and the analysis documented with figures","The tenant’s ability to contribute"],"ans":2,"exp":"A bare assertion of poverty is not an analysis; consider reserve releases via HUD-9250, other funding, and effective alternatives such as a transfer at the property’s expense."},{"q":"A resident requests a rent due-date change as an accommodation AFTER termination proceedings begin. The property:","opts":["May refuse to consider it because litigation has started","Must still consider the request, document the analysis, and show it engaged in the interactive process","Must automatically grant it","Must refer it to HUD"],"ans":1,"exp":"Reflexively refusing to consider accommodations once legal action begins creates a property’s most serious exposures."},{"q":"A maintenance technician receives an accommodation request from a resident in the unit. The correct practice is:","opts":["Tell the resident to come to the office and forget it","Pass it to the office the same day, in writing — a request made to ANY staff member is a request","Grant it on the spot","Ignore it unless it is in writing from the resident"],"ans":1,"exp":"Most stalled accommodation requests were made verbally to a staff member who did not recognize them as requests."},{"q":"A resident alleges a staff member made a discriminatory comment; the staff member denies it and there are no witnesses. The defensible response is to:","opts":["Take no action, since the facts are unresolved","Document the allegation, interview both parties, document the investigation, take appropriate action such as retraining and monitoring, communicate the outcome, and preserve the record","Terminate the employee immediately","Ask the resident to put it in writing and take no other step"],"ans":1,"exp":"An unresolved allegation with a documented investigation is defensible; an unresolved allegation with no record is not."}]},"m121":{"title":"Chapter Quiz","questions":[{"q":"VAWA housing protections cover survivors of:","opts":["Domestic violence only","Domestic violence and sexual assault only","Domestic violence, dating violence, sexual assault, and stalking","Any violent crime occurring on the premises"],"ans":2,"exp":"Protections apply regardless of sex, gender identity or sexual orientation — the statute’s name is historical."},{"q":"HUD-5380 and HUD-5382 must be provided at which points?","opts":["Every annual recertification","At denial of admission or assistance, at admission, and with any notice of eviction or termination of assistance","Only when a tenant discloses abuse","At lease signing only"],"ans":1,"exp":"Recertification is not a required point; the denial point is the one most often missed because rejection letters are issued from an unupdated template."},{"q":"A property distributes both VAWA forms at every annual recertification and at move-in, but not with rejection letters. The property is:","opts":["Compliant — it exceeds the requirement","Non-compliant — it covers admission but misses the denial trigger entirely","Compliant if no applicants were denied","Non-compliant only if a survivor complains"],"ans":1,"exp":"Over-distributing at the easy point does not substitute for the required points; test all three separately."},{"q":"A property issues a termination notice for nonpayment and omits the VAWA forms because \"no violence is involved.\" This is:","opts":["Correct — the forms apply only to DV cases","Incorrect — the forms accompany ANY notice of eviction or termination of assistance, precisely because the provider may not know","Correct if the tenant has never disclosed","Correct for Section 202 properties"],"ans":1,"exp":"Attach the forms to the notice template so they cannot be omitted."},{"q":"When a provider requests documentation of VAWA status, the individual may satisfy the request by submitting:","opts":["A police report only","HUD-5382 alone — self-certification is sufficient, and the choice among permitted forms belongs to the individual","Medical records","A court order"],"ans":1,"exp":"Other permitted forms include records from police, courts or agencies, or documentation signed by a victim service provider, attorney or medical professional with the individual’s statement."},{"q":"A provider may request third-party documentation instead of accepting self-certification when:","opts":["The provider prefers documentation","The provider has received conflicting information about the incident","The tenant is behind on rent","The incident occurred off the premises"],"ans":1,"exp":"Conflicting information is the exception; otherwise demanding police reports or medical records is a violation."},{"q":"When documentation is requested, the individual must generally be given at least:","opts":["3 business days","7 calendar days","14 business days, extendable by the provider","30 calendar days"],"ans":2,"exp":"Document the request, the period given, and any extension — and apply the practice consistently across households."},{"q":"The emergency transfer two-prong test is satisfied if the tenant:","opts":["Has a protective order","Reasonably believes they are threatened with imminent harm from further violence if they remain, OR was a victim of sexual assault on the premises within the preceding 90 days","Has filed a police report within 30 days","Has lived in the unit at least one year"],"ans":1,"exp":"Either prong suffices; a provider that denies on prong two without considering prong one has applied half the test."},{"q":"A tenant reports a sexual assault that occurred on the premises 104 days ago and requests an emergency transfer. The correct analysis is:","opts":["Deny — outside the 90-day window","Evaluate prong one (reasonable belief of imminent harm), which may well be satisfied, and document both analyses","Require a police report","Refer the tenant to another provider"],"ans":1,"exp":"The 90-day window governs prong two only."},{"q":"VAWA documentation must be:","opts":["Filed in the tenant file like all other certifications","Kept in a separate, secure location and never entered into a shared database","Submitted to HUD with the certification","Provided to all adult household members"],"ans":1,"exp":"Other adult household members can typically access the tenant file — and the perpetrator may be one of them."},{"q":"A site manager types \"VAWA transfer — DV situation\" into a portfolio-wide property management system’s notes field. This is:","opts":["Good record-keeping","A prohibited entry in a shared database","Required for the transfer to process","Acceptable if access is password protected"],"ans":1,"exp":"Written instruction plus an audit of notes fields is the control; software vendors do not solve this."},{"q":"Under lease bifurcation, if the removed household member was the one whose eligibility qualified the household, the remaining members get:","opts":["30 days to establish eligibility","90 days, with a possible extension of up to 60 additional days","6 months","Immediate termination"],"ans":1,"exp":"Bifurcation also follows applicable law for terminating the removed member’s tenancy — it is not a self-executing administrative act."},{"q":"In the Section 202 example, the elderly head of household leaves after an altercation, leaving a 58-year-old survivor with no disability as the sole member. The certification consequence is:","opts":["No change — VAWA freezes the rent","The elderly/disabled family deduction no longer applies, adjusted income and TTP rise, an interim is processed, and the rent increase requires proper advance notice","Assistance terminates immediately","The survivor must reapply through the waiting list"],"ans":1,"exp":"VAWA protections and ordinary certification mechanics operate simultaneously: she keeps her housing, her rent may still rise, and she is still owed proper notice."},{"q":"VAWA 2022 expanded compliance review to examine which as separate line items?","opts":["Only notification and confidentiality","Notification, documentation acceptance, confidentiality, emergency transfer, and anti-retaliation","Only emergency transfers","Only lease addendum execution"],"ans":1,"exp":"A property cannot satisfy a reviewer by saying it is \"aware of VAWA\" — each element is tested separately."},{"q":"A property assembles its desk review package and includes a survivor’s completed HUD-5382 as evidence of compliance. This:","opts":["Is the best evidence available","Demonstrates a confidentiality violation in the act of proving compliance — prove the system with policies, logs and templates, never with a survivor’s records","Is required by the reviewer","Is acceptable if the survivor consents verbally"],"ans":1,"exp":"Disclosure requires the tenant’s written request or consent, or a specific legal basis — convenience is not one."}]},"m122":{"title":"Chapter Quiz","questions":[{"q":"A property adds a clause to the HUD model lease charging a $75 fee for maintenance calls determined to be tenant-caused. This is:","opts":["Permitted if disclosed at move-in","An unauthorized modification — the model lease governs, and amounts charged under an unauthorized clause should be refunded","Permitted if the fee is reasonable","Required by the regulatory agreement"],"ans":1,"exp":"Legitimate policies on tenant-caused damage must be implemented through the mechanisms the lease actually provides."},{"q":"Which addendum must be signed by all adult household members at lease signing?","opts":["Form HUD-9250","Form HUD-91067, the VAWA lease addendum","Form HUD-935.2A","Form HUD-27061-H"],"ans":1,"exp":"It is a separate obligation from providing the HUD-5380 notice and HUD-5382 form at the three trigger points."},{"q":"A pre-1978 property cannot locate lead-based paint disclosures for eleven households. The correct remediation is to:","opts":["Back-date disclosures to the move-in dates","Execute now with current dates, document the discovery, make disclosure a blocking move-in checklist item, and disclose the gap","Ignore it — the obligation is EPA’s, not HUD’s","Wait for the next NSPIRE inspection"],"ans":1,"exp":"The federal disclosure obligation carries its own penalties and retention requirements; never back-date."},{"q":"Under the HAP contract, when an owner discovers it billed assistance for a vacated unit, the obligation to repay:","opts":["Arises only if HUD demands it","Already exists — the discovery reveals the obligation rather than creating it","Can be negotiated as part of the next renewal","Is offset against future vacancy claims"],"ans":1,"exp":"Compute it, correct it, disclose it, and repay it — the cost is fixed, and the cost of being found not to have done so is not."},{"q":"A property takes OCAF every year by default rather than analyzing a budget-based adjustment. The risk is:","opts":["None — OCAF is always the correct choice","Operating at a deficit that feeds the property’s risk classification and shortens its MOR cycle","Losing the HAP contract automatically","A fair housing violation"],"ans":1,"exp":"Decide deliberately with the arithmetic in front of the owner: the annual dollar difference against the preparation cost and scrutiny."},{"q":"A termination notice states only \"You are in violation of your lease due to disturbances.\" The defect is:","opts":["It should be delivered by certified mail","It lacks the specificity — dates, conduct, lease provisions — that lets the tenant prepare a defense","It fails to state the rent owed","It must be approved by HUD first"],"ans":1,"exp":"Specific dates, conduct, the lease provisions violated, and prior warnings with proof of delivery are what make a notice enforceable."},{"q":"Any notice of eviction or termination of assistance must be accompanied by:","opts":["A copy of the HAP contract","HUD-5380 and HUD-5382","The AFHMP","Form HUD-9250"],"ans":1,"exp":"The requirement does not depend on the provider believing violence is involved — the notice is how a household may learn it has rights."},{"q":"A household falls behind because of a repayment agreement requiring rent plus repayment far above 40 percent of monthly adjusted income. Before terminating for nonpayment, the property should:","opts":["Proceed — the arrears are real","Re-run the affordability analysis, restructure the agreement if it fails, and document","Refer the household to the Contract Administrator","Increase the repayment amount"],"ans":1,"exp":"The arrears may be attributable to an agreement the property should not have made — and worse if the underlying error was the property’s."},{"q":"Termination of ASSISTANCE and termination of TENANCY are:","opts":["The same action with different names","Different actions — a household may lose assistance while remaining a tenant, depending on the program and circumstances; both require the VAWA forms","Interchangeable at the owner’s option","Both prohibited without HUD approval"],"ans":1,"exp":"Conflating them produces either wrongful tenancy terminations or failures to terminate assistance when required. Use separate notice templates."},{"q":"Which house rule is most clearly a familial status violation?","opts":["\"Quiet hours 10 p.m. to 7 a.m.\"","\"Children must be accompanied by an adult at all times outside the unit\"","\"No storage of items on balconies\"","\"Residents must not obstruct fire exits\""],"ans":1,"exp":"Person-based rules restricting children’s use of the property are the classic violation; rewrite rules to be conduct-based and neutral."},{"q":"A balcony storage rule has produced enforcement notices to 7 households while a property walk shows 14 in violation. The problem is:","opts":["The rule is unreasonable","Selective enforcement — the remedy is documented periodic inspection applied to all units with notices to every household in violation, tracked on a log","That balconies cannot be regulated","That 30 days’ notice was not given"],"ans":1,"exp":"Selective enforcement is where discrimination claims live, even when the underlying rule is perfectly lawful."},{"q":"House rules prohibiting all animals encounter an assistance animal request. The correct outcome is:","opts":["The rule governs; the request is denied","The accommodation obligation overrides the rule, and the house rules should say so explicitly so staff do not apply pet deposits or breed limits","The resident must pay a pet deposit","The animal is permitted only if certified"],"ans":1,"exp":"One sentence in the house rules prevents a whole category of violation."},{"q":"A resident complains the trash area is too far because of a health condition. Best practice is to:","opts":["Log it as a facilities complaint","Route it to the reasonable accommodation process as well as logging it, because it may be an accommodation request carrying a promptness obligation","Refer her to the grievance committee","Ask for medical records"],"ans":1,"exp":"Train intake staff to spot complaints referencing a disability, health condition, or difficulty others do not face."},{"q":"A property reports zero resident complaints over two years. The most likely interpretation is:","opts":["Exceptional management","Residents have no functioning way to raise them — which a reviewer or fair housing tester will eventually surface","The property is too small for complaints","Complaints were resolved verbally, which is sufficient"],"ans":1,"exp":"A documented log with prompt written responses demonstrates the management capacity the MOR exists to assess."},{"q":"An anonymous note alleges a staff member is demanding favors from residents. The correct handling is to:","opts":["Disregard it because it is anonymous","Log it as a routine grievance","Document receipt, escalate immediately to ownership and counsel, investigate what can be investigated, and take protective steps","Post a notice asking the author to identify themselves"],"ans":2,"exp":"The procedure should specify which categories of allegation bypass the ordinary grievance path."}]},"m123":{"title":"Chapter Quiz","questions":[{"q":"A budget projects 97 percent occupancy at a property that has run 89 percent for two years. The consequence is:","opts":["A conservative budget","Unexplainable unfavorable revenue variances every month, because the explanation is the assumption itself","Higher HAP payments","A finding for over-occupancy"],"ans":1,"exp":"Fix the pipeline through the occupancy department, then budget to what the property can achieve, treating improvement as a plan with milestones."},{"q":"Maintenance payroll is $63,000 over budget with no written explanation. The reviewer’s follow-up questions concern:","opts":["Wage rates","Cost allocation, identity-of-interest involvement, and whether the project was charged for services it did not receive","Union agreements","Overtime policy"],"ans":1,"exp":"A one-paragraph variance note written when the variance occurred answers all of it."},{"q":"Three years of favorable variances in maintenance and capital lines alongside declining NSPIRE scores indicate:","opts":["Efficient management","Operating results propped up by underinvestment, which feeds risk classification and shortens the MOR cycle","A budgeting error only","That reserves are overfunded"],"ans":1,"exp":"Read variances in both directions; under-budget is not automatically a win."},{"q":"A property funds an unexpected insurance increase by skipping reserve deposits. This converts:","opts":["A capital problem into an operating problem","An operating problem into a regulatory agreement violation","A finding into an observation","A deficit into surplus cash"],"ans":1,"exp":"Rank the alternatives with the owner and document the decision — reserve deposits are non-discretionary."},{"q":"Required reserve deposits of $4,180/month were skipped for four months. The shortfall and consequence are:","opts":["$16,720; a compliance finding, regulatory agreement default in substance, likely distribution restriction, and a note in the audited statements","$4,180; a narrative comment only","$16,720; no finding if repaid","$50,160; automatic HAP abatement"],"ans":0,"exp":"Control: automated transfer on a fixed date, reconciled monthly by someone other than the initiator."},{"q":"A HUD-9250 release request for $62,000 in roofing is submitted with one proposal and no scope detail. The most likely outcome is:","opts":["Automatic approval","Rejection for insufficient documentation of need and procurement","Approval at half the amount","Referral to the Contract Administrator"],"ans":1,"exp":"A prepared request states the item, age and condition, scope, procurement and bids, amount, reserve balance before and after, and timeline."},{"q":"A property makes every deposit on time and holds $211,000 against $1.4 million of ten-year capital needs. It is:","opts":["Noncompliant","Compliant with the deposit requirement but inadequately funded — the argument for a deposit increase or budget-based adjustment","Exempt from further deposits","Required to suspend all withdrawals"],"ans":1,"exp":"The MOR tests compliance; asset management and risk classification test adequacy."},{"q":"Financial statements are due:","opts":["Within 30 days of fiscal year end","Within 90 days of fiscal year end, submitted electronically through FASSUB","By April 15 annually","Only when HUD requests them"],"ans":1,"exp":"Build a fiscal-year calendar for the whole portfolio; owners with multiple fiscal years routinely apply one deadline to all properties."},{"q":"An audit compliance report notes inconsistent third-party income verification in tested files. For a consultant, this document is:","opts":["Irrelevant to the MOR","An early-warning system to read FIRST at intake, because the MOR reviewer will read it during the desk review","Confidential and unavailable","Only relevant to the lender"],"ans":1,"exp":"Remediate audit findings on their own schedule rather than leaving them to be rediscovered by a reviewer."},{"q":"Audited HAP revenue is $1,412,880 while TRACS vouchers total $1,398,240. The reviewer’s concern is:","opts":["The difference itself","Whether the owner can EXPLAIN it — a prepared one-page reconciliation ends the conversation","That the auditor made an error","That HAP was underbilled"],"ans":1,"exp":"Timing, adjustment vouchers, special claims and actual errors are all possible; an unprepared reconciliation starts an inquiry that reaches tenant files."},{"q":"An income understatement of $3,600 persisting 14 months produces approximately:","opts":["$1,260 owed by the tenant only","$1,260 in HAP overpayment AND $1,260 pursued from the tenant","$3,600 owed to HUD","No exposure if unintentional"],"ans":1,"exp":"30% of $3,600 ÷ 12 ≈ $90/month × 14 months on each side of the transaction."},{"q":"The monthly voucher review that prevents most billing errors checks all EXCEPT:","opts":["Every assisted unit in TRACS is occupied per the rent roll","Every occupied assisted unit has a current certification","Every certification effective this month was transmitted","Every tenant’s credit score was re-pulled"],"ans":3,"exp":"The fourth real check is that utility reimbursements on the voucher tie to the tenant ledgers. Fifteen minutes a month."},{"q":"An owner takes a $75,000 distribution when surplus cash is $41,000. The result is:","opts":["Acceptable if repaid next year","An improper distribution of $34,000 that must be returned, a regulatory agreement violation, and potentially a residual receipts deficiency from the same act","Acceptable with the auditor’s consent","A finding only if the property is insured"],"ans":1,"exp":"No distribution should be taken until the audited surplus cash computation is complete and reviewed."},{"q":"An identity-of-interest landscaping contract is defensible when the owner can show:","opts":["A signed contract only","Disclosure of the relationship, evidence services were actually provided, a contemporaneous market comparison, and required approvals","That the affiliate is licensed","That the price has not increased in three years"],"ans":1,"exp":"Obtain independent bids when the contract is signed; a comparison constructed after the fact is rarely persuasive."},{"q":"Most of the financial findings in this unit are best prevented by:","opts":["Hiring a larger accounting firm","A regulatory agreement calendar with one owner per item, reviewed quarterly — because these are calendar failures rather than judgment failures","Increasing contract rents","Reducing the reserve deposit"],"ans":1,"exp":"Financial statement dates, distributions and surplus cash, reserve deposits, insurance and flood verification, taxes, contract anniversaries, and management renewals all belong on one page."}]},"m131":{"title":"Chapter Quiz","questions":[{"q":"Under the risk-based MOR schedule, the strongest quantifiable value proposition for an owner is:","opts":["A more pleasant reviewer experience","Moving from Satisfactory (24-month cycle) to Above Average (36-month cycle) — being reviewed less often","Lower contract rents","Exemption from NSPIRE"],"ans":1,"exp":"Present it with the schedule matrix, staff-hour cost per cycle, repayment exposure, and reduced disruption."},{"q":"A regional manager wants your services but has no budget authority. The highest-return action is to:","opts":["Spend several meetings persuading her","Equip her with a one-page internal memo — current review posture, portfolio repeat findings, the HOTMA deadline, and a scoped proposal with a portfolio price","Contact the owner directly behind her back","Offer a discount"],"ans":1,"exp":"You are not selling to her; you are enabling her to sell for you. That memo is free and converts at the highest rate."},{"q":"A client asks you to guarantee an Above Average rating. The correct response is:","opts":["Agree, with a fee refund if not achieved","Decline to guarantee an outcome and commit instead to defined scope and deliverables — assessment, remediation list, mock review, training, readiness report","Guarantee it verbally but not in writing","Guarantee it only for Not Troubled properties"],"ans":1,"exp":"The rating depends on reviewer judgment, CA conventions and the client’s own execution. Sell the process, never the outcome."},{"q":"During an assessment, a site manager asks you to \"just fix\" a few files. The problem is:","opts":["Nothing — good service","You are performing remediation inside an assessment budget, and you will later be auditing your own work in the report","Files may not be corrected by consultants","It requires HUD approval"],"ans":1,"exp":"Have the sentence ready: note them on the list and send a short remediation scope the next day. Friendly scope creep costs more than bad pricing."},{"q":"The most valuable single product in the suite, and the closest simulation of the real event, is:","opts":["A policy template package","The mock MOR — a full simulation against Form 9834 with a predicted rating range and a report the client can hand to their owner","A one-hour webinar","A checklist PDF"],"ans":1,"exp":"It includes a risk-weighted file sample, staff interview simulation, document production test, and a prioritized action list."},{"q":"Training deliverables should always include a sign-in sheet and certificate because:","opts":["They look professional","They are the artifact the client needs for the MOR training record — making the deliverable worth more than the session","HUD requires consultant certificates","They justify a higher hourly rate"],"ans":1,"exp":"For review purposes, training without documentation did not occur."},{"q":"A corrective action engagement follows a Below Average rating with eleven findings, two of which are factually wrong. The correct structure is to:","opts":["Appeal all eleven","Appeal the two within 30 days AND submit corrective action documentation on all nine valid findings by their own due dates","Wait for the appeal decision before curing anything","Cure everything and skip the appeal"],"ans":1,"exp":"Corrective action deadlines are not tolled by an appeal — a belief that they are is how a curable finding becomes a failure to correct."},{"q":"A draft corrective action response says \"all 148 files have been reviewed and corrected\" when 31 required correction and 9 remain pending signatures. The correct approach is:","opts":["Leave it — it is substantially true","State the precise numbers with exhibit references and a completion date for the pending items","Omit the file review entirely","Round up for simplicity"],"ans":1,"exp":"Overstatement discovered at a follow-up review converts a recoverable situation into an unrecoverable one — with your work attached to it."},{"q":"A corrective action due date cannot be met at a two-person property. The right step, on day 3 of the engagement, is to:","opts":["Plan to explain at the follow-up review","Prepare a written extension request with a specific alternative schedule and interim deliverable, submitted before the due date","Submit partial work with no comment","Ask the CA informally by phone"],"ans":1,"exp":"Extensions requested in advance are routinely granted; requested after the fact they become a second finding."},{"q":"The default pricing structure for defined-scope work such as assessments and mock MORs is:","opts":["Hourly with no cap","Fixed fee — the client knows the cost and you keep the efficiency gains from your own systems","Contingency based on the rating achieved","Cost-plus"],"ans":1,"exp":"Hourly is reserved for genuinely open-ended work, always with an estimate and a not-to-exceed."},{"q":"A client proposes \"fixed fee, but capped at 60 hours.\" This is:","opts":["A reasonable compromise","A fixed fee with the upside removed — you carry overrun risk and forfeit the efficiency benefit; offer the real hourly-with-cap alternative instead","Standard industry practice","Preferable for the consultant"],"ans":1,"exp":"Give clients the genuine alternative rather than accepting a hybrid that only works against you."},{"q":"In a portfolio engagement where you will use a reduced file sample at some properties, you must:","opts":["Keep the reduction internal for efficiency","State the reduced sample explicitly in the engagement letter","Sample all properties identically regardless of cost","Charge full price for all"],"ans":1,"exp":"An undisclosed reduction in coverage is the one thing that permanently damages a portfolio relationship."},{"q":"Which insurance most directly protects a MOR consultant whose advice produces a repayment?","opts":["General liability","Professional liability (errors and omissions)","Cyber liability","Workers’ compensation"],"ans":1,"exp":"Cyber liability is also essential because you handle tenant income data; general liability is commonly required to be on site."},{"q":"A client asks whether they may terminate a household’s assistance. The correct response is:","opts":["Give your best judgment — you know the rules","Provide the compliance framework and what the file must support, and state that this specific decision requires counsel","Refuse to discuss it at all","Draft the termination notice for them"],"ans":1,"exp":"Consultants drift into legal advice gradually and discover the problem only when something goes wrong; your practice must match your engagement letter."},{"q":"You are engaged by a management agent; three months later the owner asks you to assess that agent’s performance. This is:","opts":["Two unrelated engagements","A conflict requiring disclosure, declination, or informed written consent — and worth declining even with consent","Fine because both are clients","Fine if you use different staff"],"ans":1,"exp":"The evaluative work will damage one relationship regardless of what you find. Keep a client list and check it before every engagement."}]},"m132":{"title":"Chapter Quiz","questions":[{"q":"Which discovery question most often uncovers an unexpected deadline?","opts":["What is your fiscal year end?","Has there been, or is there pending, any change in ownership or management agent?","How many units do you have?","Who is your auditor?"],"ans":1,"exp":"An approved ownership or agent change triggers a new MOR within 6 months regardless of the prior rating — clients routinely do not know this."},{"q":"Asked what changed in the process after the last findings, a client says \"we retrained the staff.\" This indicates:","opts":["A strong corrective action","That nothing in the process changed — retraining does not alter a process that produced the error","That the findings were minor","That the property is ready for review"],"ans":1,"exp":"A corrective action that names a control can be verified at day 90; one that names an intention cannot."},{"q":"A prospect describes \"a few file issues\" but discovery reveals a Below Average rating, an abandoned EIV log, a six-week-old specialist, and eleven late certifications. You should:","opts":["Honor the light-assessment price to win the work","Reprice before quoting and explain why with the specifics","Decline the engagement","Quote low and bill the difference later"],"ans":1,"exp":"Clients who see the reasoning usually accept the higher number; clients quoted low and billed high never forgive it."},{"q":"A consultant’s file sample should be:","opts":["Purely random","Risk-weighted to mirror the reviewer — move-ins, interims, zero income, live-in aides, prior-finding files, EIV discrepancies, move-outs, plus random fill","Chosen by the site manager","Limited to files the client identifies as problematic"],"ans":1,"exp":"Mirroring which files a reviewer will pull matters more than how many."},{"q":"A 28-file sample produces 19 defects, 11 of them the same missing EIV resolution memo. The headline finding is:","opts":["19 errors across 28 files","A single requirement with a 79 percent failure rate — a systemic control gap with a one-line fix","That the specialist is careless","That the sample was too large"],"ans":1,"exp":"Reporting by requirement rather than by file is what converts an audit into a recommendation."},{"q":"A file is complete, signed, and correctly computed, but TRACS shows a different adjusted income. This is caught by:","opts":["The signature check","The reconciliation of transmitted figures to the file and rent roll — the check most consultants skip because it needs system access","The deduction worksheet","The verification date check"],"ans":1,"exp":"Ask for read access to TRACS and the rent roll at engagement start."},{"q":"You find a medical threshold applied twice, overcharging a tenant $16.80/month for 14 months; the client resists correcting because the tenant has not complained. Your position should be:","opts":["Defer to the client — it is their property","State in writing that the certification must be corrected and that leaving a documented error uncorrected is materially worse than the original error","Correct it yourself without telling them","Note it only in the appendix"],"ans":1,"exp":"This is one of the places where a consultant’s professional standing is actually tested."},{"q":"The training session that prevents the most liability per minute and that almost no property runs is:","opts":["A history of the Section 8 program","Maintenance staff training on accommodation requests, assistance animals, VAWA disclosures, privacy, and work order documentation","Advanced TRACS transmission","Budget preparation"],"ans":1,"exp":"Maintenance staff enter units daily and receive requests, complaints and disclosures that never reach the office."},{"q":"In an interview rehearsal, a staff member describes a practice contradicting the written policy. This is valuable because:","opts":["It identifies an employee to discipline","It surfaces a finding before a real reviewer hears it spoken — and it is fixable in one session","It proves the policy is wrong","It justifies a larger engagement"],"ans":1,"exp":"The other recurring pattern is a staff member who guesses instead of saying \"let me get the procedure.\""},{"q":"A mock MOR report should state:","opts":["A single predicted rating","A predicted rating RANGE with the reasoning, plus what is achievable after the corrective actions","No prediction at all","Only the findings"],"ans":1,"exp":"A range with reasoning is credible and useful; a confident single prediction will eventually be wrong in front of a client."},{"q":"The document production test on site measures:","opts":["Document quality","How long each of a set of specific documents takes to produce — because a property that needs 25 minutes to find its AFHMP will need 25 minutes in front of the reviewer","Staff friendliness","File organization aesthetics"],"ans":1,"exp":"Nothing motivates a client to build an index like \"Emergency Transfer Plan — not located, 22 minutes\" in writing."},{"q":"A mock MOR uncovers assistance billed on two units vacant for five months. The correct handling is to:","opts":["Include it in the report delivered in three weeks","Tell the client the day you find it, in writing, with a recommendation to correct and disclose and a note that exposure grows monthly","Correct it yourself","Report it directly to the Contract Administrator"],"ans":1,"exp":"Burying a live billing problem in a later report creates a serious problem for the consultant as well as the client."},{"q":"The optimal timing for a mock MOR is:","opts":["One week before the review","90 to 120 days before the expected review","Immediately after the prior review","Only after a Below Average rating"],"ans":1,"exp":"Enough time to cure, revise policies, train, and rebuild the desk review package — without leaving room for drift before the real event."},{"q":"The post-MOR step that most reliably prevents a repeat finding, and that almost nobody performs, is:","opts":["Filing an appeal","The day-30-to-90 verification that the corrective actions are still operating","A second mock MOR","A rent increase request"],"ans":1,"exp":"Corrective actions fail quietly months after submission — usually when the responsible person is absent and the backup was never trained."},{"q":"The strongest framing of a post-MOR engagement’s goal is:","opts":["\"Fix the findings\"","\"Change the review interval\" — Below Average is a 12-month cycle; Above Average at Not Troubled is 36","\"Satisfy the reviewer\"","\"Reduce staff workload\""],"ans":1,"exp":"Over six years the difference is roughly six reviews versus two, which justifies the retainer in the same conversation."}]},"m133":{"title":"Chapter Quiz","questions":[{"q":"First clients in MOR consulting most often come from:","opts":["Paid advertising","Proximity and existing credibility — former colleagues, association contacts, regional managers who know your work","Cold email campaigns","Trade show booths"],"ans":1,"exp":"A market this small and relationship-driven does not respond to advertising; it responds to demonstrated competence."},{"q":"The most effective free offer for converting a prospect is:","opts":["A discount on the first engagement","A prior review posture summary built from the last two MOR reports — rating history, repeat findings, unkept commitments, and the computed next-review date","A generic compliance brochure","A free hour of phone time"],"ans":1,"exp":"It requires the client to send two documents (a small commitment) and demonstrates competence rather than claiming it."},{"q":"A prospect sends questions weekly, uses the answers, and never engages. The right response is:","opts":["Keep answering to build goodwill","Apply a friendly boundary — offer to keep answering quick questions but scope anything requiring document or file review into a small engagement, and send that scope","Stop responding","Send an invoice for past advice"],"ans":1,"exp":"Either the relationship becomes commercial or it stops consuming your time."},{"q":"The strongest first positioning statement is usually:","opts":["\"Comprehensive affordable housing compliance nationwide\"","Service plus geography — MOR readiness for HUD-assisted properties in a defined region whose CA conventions you know in detail","\"We do everything HUD-related\"","Positioning by fee level"],"ans":1,"exp":"Knowing one state PBCA’s sampling and scoring conventions is genuinely valuable and hard for a national firm to match."},{"q":"Which marketing claim is impermissible?","opts":["Describing your years of experience","Implying HUD endorsement or using HUD’s seal","Publishing a checklist you built","Naming an industry credential you actually hold"],"ans":1,"exp":"Also impermissible: suggesting you can influence a rating through relationships, and using client tenant data in marketing in any form."},{"q":"A defensible case study looks like:","opts":["\"We saved a client from disaster\"","A de-identified account with real numbers — findings, actions taken, corrections made, and the rating and review-cycle outcome","A named client with their rating disclosed","A testimonial without specifics"],"ans":1,"exp":"No client name, no identifying detail, real numbers — and always ask in writing before naming any client."},{"q":"The referral source most structurally likely to produce work is:","opts":["Residents","CPAs who audit HUD-assisted properties — they write findings and cannot remediate what they audit","Utility companies","Local newspapers"],"ans":1,"exp":"Give them an explicit trigger: \"when your compliance report cites tenant file documentation or EIV, that is when I am useful.\""},{"q":"A referred engagement goes badly. The correct action toward the referral source is to:","opts":["Say nothing and hope it does not come up","Tell them factually, without complaint, before they hear a distorted version","Blame the client","Offer them a fee"],"ans":1,"exp":"Referral sources fear exactly this outcome; transparency is what gets you referred again."},{"q":"An attorney refers an owner whose management agent is already your client, in a dispute about the agent. You should:","opts":["Take it — both are separate engagements","Decline promptly and explain why in general terms","Take it and disclose nothing","Take it with the agent’s verbal consent"],"ans":1,"exp":"Declining well signals that you take conflicts seriously — the quality that makes people comfortable referring sensitive work."},{"q":"The leverage available to a solo practitioner without hiring anyone is:","opts":["Raising rates indefinitely","Systems — a reusable file review instrument, report and policy templates, training materials and checklists","Working more weekends","Taking larger portfolios alone"],"ans":1,"exp":"A mature instrument set can cut delivery time by 30 to 40 percent on every future engagement."},{"q":"When subcontracting file review, the step most commonly abandoned — and most necessary — is:","opts":["The engagement letter","Your review of the subcontractor’s work, priced into the fee and performed permanently, not just on the first engagement","Paying on time","Providing a laptop"],"ans":1,"exp":"Subcontractors handling tenant data must also be bound in writing by the same confidentiality and data-handling terms you gave the client."},{"q":"Realistic annual billable capacity for a solo consultant, after sales, administration, development and travel, is roughly:","opts":["2,080 hours","1,600 to 1,800 hours","1,000 to 1,200 hours","600 hours"],"ans":2,"exp":"A growth plan that is simply \"get more clients\" hits this ceiling within about eighteen months; growth beyond it requires systems, people or products."},{"q":"A 40-property portfolio wants a full program from a solo practice. The best response is:","opts":["Take all forty and work harder","Take a defined subset — for example six properties with an option to expand — or assemble a vetted team","Decline outright","Subcontract everything unreviewed"],"ans":1,"exp":"Taking six and doing them excellently usually leads to the other thirty-four anyway; taking forty alone damages the reputation permanently."},{"q":"A consultant discovers they published a wrong figure in a client report. The correct action is to:","opts":["Wait to see whether anyone notices","Tell the client immediately in writing with the correction and impact, fix it at your own cost, and notify your E&O carrier","Quietly reissue the report with no explanation","Blame the changing regulation"],"ans":1,"exp":"The story clients tell about a consultant who caught and fixed their own error is far better than the one they tell about a consultant who hid it."},{"q":"A sustainable practice targets what share of revenue from retainers and recurring annual work?","opts":["Under 20 percent","Roughly 60 to 70 percent","100 percent","It does not matter"],"ans":1,"exp":"Project work is lumpy; recurring revenue is what carries a practice through a slow quarter."}]}};
export const MOR_UNIT_TESTS = {"u7":{"title":"Unit 7: Understanding the MOR Process","passingScore":70,"questions":[{"q":"A Management and Occupancy Review evaluates:","opts":["Physical condition of the buildings","Management practices, documentation, policies, and regulatory compliance","The appraised value of the property","Tenant satisfaction survey results"],"ans":1,"exp":"Physical condition is scored separately under NSPIRE; the MOR scores the office, not the building."},{"q":"Form HUD-9834 is organized into:","opts":["Two parts","Three parts: Desk Review, On-Site Review with Addenda A/B/C, and Summary Report","Five parts matching the rating levels","Seven parts, one per category"],"ans":1,"exp":"Addendum A is the tenant file checklist, B the financial addendum, C the required documents list."},{"q":"Which Form 9834 category is rated only by HUD staff and Mortgagee reviewers?","opts":["Category A","Category D","Category F","Category G"],"ans":1,"exp":"Of the seven lettered categories A through G, Contract Administrators rate all but D."},{"q":"A Not Troubled property rated Above Average has its next MOR due within:","opts":["12 months","24 months","36 months","6 months"],"ans":2,"exp":"Above Average or Superior at Potentially Troubled or Not Troubled produces a 36-month interval."},{"q":"A Troubled property rated Superior has its next MOR due within:","opts":["36 months","24 months","12 months","No further review"],"ans":1,"exp":"A Troubled classification caps the reward for a top score at 24 months."},{"q":"Any property must have a new MOR within 6 months of:","opts":["A NSPIRE inspection","An approved change of ownership or management agent","A rent increase","An audit finding"],"ans":1,"exp":"This override applies regardless of risk classification or prior score — the most common scheduling surprise in the business."},{"q":"HUD justified the 2022 risk-based schedule by citing that in FY2021:","opts":["Most properties failed their MOR","Over 94 percent scored Satisfactory, Above Average, or Superior","Reviews cost more than HAP","CAs could not complete annual reviews"],"ans":1,"exp":"HUD reallocated review capacity toward lower-scoring, higher-risk properties."},{"q":"An owner may appeal which ratings, and within what period?","opts":["Any rating, within 45 days","Below Average or Unsatisfactory, in writing within 30 calendar days of the report/transmittal date","Satisfactory or below, within 60 days","Unsatisfactory only, within 15 days"],"ans":1,"exp":"Per Handbook 4350.1 Chapter 6; appeal decisions are typically due back within 45 calendar days."},{"q":"A report is transmitted March 4 and reaches the owner March 26. The appeal deadline is measured from:","opts":["March 26, the date of actual receipt","The report/transmittal date of March 4","The date the exit conference was held","The date corrective actions are due"],"ans":1,"exp":"Calendar the transmittal date the day the report arrives anywhere in the organization."},{"q":"A consultant tells a client that below 70 is failing, based on another state’s practice. This is:","opts":["Correct nationally","Incorrect — the five-level scale is HUD’s standard; numeric bands are CA conventions that vary","Correct for Troubled properties","Correct only for PBCA reviews"],"ans":1,"exp":"One agency anchors at 95/85/75/65; another treats below 70 as below-average. Read the convention off the actual report."},{"q":"Which factor most heavily aggravates a deficiency’s weight?","opts":["That it involves a small dollar amount","That it was cited on the prior MOR and recurs","That a tenant complained","That it appears in a policy rather than a file"],"ans":1,"exp":"A repeat finding converts an error into a demonstrated failure to correct."},{"q":"A reviewer samples 20 files and finds 4 with errors at a 150-unit property. The reviewer’s inference is:","opts":["4 of 150 — under 3 percent","A 20 percent error rate, inferring the population from the sample","That only those 4 files matter","That the sample was invalid"],"ans":1,"exp":"Expect a corrective action requiring 100 percent file review."},{"q":"The weakest form of corrective action response is one that:","opts":["States root cause and the process control installed","Says \"staff has been counseled and this will not happen again\"","Includes exhibits with dates","Names a responsible person and completion date"],"ans":1,"exp":"No root cause, no evidence, no process change — and it invites a repeat finding."},{"q":"A corrective action due date cannot be met. The correct action is to:","opts":["Submit partial work with no comment","Request an extension in writing BEFORE the due date with a specific alternative schedule","Explain at the next review","Appeal the finding"],"ans":1,"exp":"Extensions requested in advance are routinely granted; requested afterward they become a second finding."},{"q":"Six minor findings are cured during the on-site review and documented in front of the reviewer. The effect is:","opts":["None — the findings are already written","They weigh materially less, supporting a higher rating","The review must be restarted","An automatic Above Average rating"],"ans":1,"exp":"Self-identification and cure is one of the four factors that lighten a deficiency’s weight."},{"q":"The most valuable activity at an exit conference is:","opts":["Arguing handbook interpretation","Correcting factual errors immediately by producing the document","Requesting a higher rating","Discussing the reviewer’s travel schedule"],"ans":1,"exp":"Interpretation disputes belong in the written response; a factual error corrected on the spot never becomes a finding."},{"q":"After the exit conference, the owner should send a written summary within 24–48 hours because:","opts":["HUD requires it","It creates the record of what was discussed if the report later differs","It shortens the appeal period","It substitutes for corrective action"],"ans":1,"exp":"It is what lets an owner credibly say a finding was never raised at the exit conference."},{"q":"A reviewer finds a \"completed\" work order from two months ago for a defect still present. This is worse than no work order because:","opts":["It proves the technician lied","It undermines the reliability of every other completed work order","It violates NSPIRE standards","It requires a repayment"],"ans":1,"exp":"The physical defect is rarely the finding; the record is the finding."},{"q":"An accessible unit is occupied by a household not needing its features. The property is protected if the file shows:","opts":["A higher rent was charged","The unit was offered first to qualified households, declined, and leased with the required transfer provision","The tenant agreed verbally to move","Accessibility features were removed"],"ans":1,"exp":"Without that record, this is a Section 504/fair housing finding, not a maintenance one."},{"q":"Ratings run Below Average, Satisfactory, Satisfactory — but the newest findings are entirely different items. This indicates:","opts":["Systematic improvement","Reactive compliance — fixing what is cited while generating new gaps elsewhere","Reviewer inconsistency","That the property should be reclassified"],"ans":1,"exp":"The remedy is a standing self-assessment against the full Form 9834."}]},"u8":{"title":"Unit 8: Desk Review Preparation","passingScore":70,"questions":[{"q":"Part I of Form 9834 is completed:","opts":["During the on-site visit","Before the visit, from owner submissions and HUD system data","At the exit conference","Only for Troubled properties"],"ans":1,"exp":"A substantial share of the reviewer’s provisional impression forms before anyone arrives."},{"q":"Which does the reviewer obtain WITHOUT the owner submitting it?","opts":["The Tenant Selection Plan","TRACS, iREMS, APPS and FASS data","House rules","The accommodation log"],"ans":1,"exp":"Audit yourself against the data HUD can see, not just the documents you choose to send."},{"q":"A property finds it billed HAP for four months on a vacated unit. It should:","opts":["Leave it and hope","Correct it, document the correction date, and disclose it in the package","Wait for the exit conference","Offset it against a future claim"],"ans":1,"exp":"An uncorrected overbilling found by the reviewer becomes a program integrity matter."},{"q":"Processing an in-property unit transfer as a move-out plus move-in most significantly causes:","opts":["Loss of the security deposit","An incorrectly reset recertification anniversary that propagates for the life of the tenancy","Removal from the HAP contract","An unusable utility allowance"],"ans":1,"exp":"It also creates a spurious termination and admission in TRACS."},{"q":"EIV Income Discrepancy and No Income reports are retained:","opts":["Three years in the master file","In the tenant file for term of tenancy plus three years","Until the next annual","Permanently"],"ans":1,"exp":"Master-file reports — New Hires, Multiple Subsidy, Deceased Tenants, identity verification — are retained three years."},{"q":"A property has EIV income reports in every tenant file but no EIV master file. This is:","opts":["Compliant","A systemic EIV deficiency for absent property-level monitoring","A finding per household","Excused if there are no discrepancies"],"ans":1,"exp":"EIV compliance has three legs: verification use, monitoring reports, and security administration."},{"q":"A correctly analyzed EIV discrepancy with no written resolution memo results in:","opts":["No finding","A finding — the memo is the deliverable","A narrative comment","A finding only if the number was wrong"],"ans":1,"exp":"A correct answer without documentation scores the same as an incorrect one."},{"q":"A staff member resigned in March; her EIV access is active in September. This is:","opts":["A minor oversight","An EIV security finding","A payroll matter","Acceptable if she never logged in"],"ans":1,"exp":"EIV access termination belongs on the offboarding checklist with the coordinator’s written confirmation."},{"q":"A waiting list with dates but no times, where same-day applicants were treated differently, is:","opts":["Acceptable","A deficiency — an unprovable order is treated as an absent one","Acceptable if the TSP is silent","A finding only on complaint"],"ans":1,"exp":"Waiting list order is the mechanism by which nondiscriminatory selection is proven."},{"q":"An approved AFHMP commits to outreach the property has not performed in two years. The cure is to:","opts":["Remove the plan from the file","Perform the marketing with dated evidence, or amend the plan and obtain approval","Treat the plan as aspirational","Wait for the next update cycle"],"ans":1,"exp":"The plan is a commitment measured against its own terms."},{"q":"A defensible rejection letter must include:","opts":["Only the denial","The specific reason, the information relied on and its source, the dispute right, and how to request a meeting","A comparison to other applicants","The property’s occupancy rate"],"ans":1,"exp":"A vague letter prevents response and prevents the owner from later showing consistency."},{"q":"A TSP citing a superseded handbook change is:","opts":["Not a finding if practice is correct","A finding — written policies must be current","A finding only if a tenant is harmed","Corrected automatically by HUD"],"ans":1,"exp":"Maintain a policy register with owners, revision dates and next review dates."},{"q":"In a 30-day cure window, first priority goes to:","opts":["Tabs and indexing","Eligibility and subsidy items — certifications, ineligible households, Existing Tenant Search omissions, billing for vacated units","Policy formatting","Training certificates"],"ans":1,"exp":"Money and program integrity items carry the most weight."},{"q":"A property discovers nine households never received the VAWA notice at admission. It should:","opts":["Back-date a distribution log","Distribute now with actual dates, memorialize the discovery and control, and disclose","Wait for the next recertification","Omit the item from the package"],"ans":1,"exp":"Fix forward, never backward — back-dating converts a routine finding into a fraud problem."},{"q":"Regarding electronic transmission of tenant data, the correct practice is:","opts":["Email to a personal address if the portal is slow","Use the CA’s designated secure channel and document the instruction received","Post to a public link","Redact everything by default"],"ans":1,"exp":"Emailing unencrypted tenant income data is a security incident, not a shortcut."},{"q":"A required document does not exist at the property. Best practice is to:","opts":["Leave a gap","Insert an N/A page in list position with a one-line explanation","Substitute a similar document","Mention it verbally on site"],"ans":1,"exp":"A gap reads as disorganization; a written N/A reads as control."},{"q":"A financial statement submission is due:","opts":["30 days after fiscal year end","90 days after fiscal year end, through FASSUB","By March 31 regardless of fiscal year","Only on request"],"ans":1,"exp":"A June 30 fiscal year end is due roughly September 28 — build a portfolio fiscal-year calendar."},{"q":"Reserve for Replacement and Residual Receipts releases are requested on:","opts":["Form HUD-92547-A","Form HUD-9250","Form HUD-50059","Form HUD-935.2A"],"ans":1,"exp":"HUD-9250, Funds Authorizations, with supporting documentation of need, scope and procurement."},{"q":"A property with $6,000,000 in hazard coverage on $9,000,000 replacement cost under an 80 percent coinsurance clause needs:","opts":["$4,800,000","$6,000,000","$7,200,000","$9,000,000"],"ans":2,"exp":"Below 80 percent of replacement cost, a coinsurance penalty reduces recovery on any loss."},{"q":"The most persuasive evidence of a readiness posture is:","opts":["A thick policy binder","A record showing issues the property found and fixed itself over the past year, with dates","A letter from the owner","A clean prior report alone"],"ans":1,"exp":"It shifts the review from \"what is wrong here\" to \"does this system work.\""}]},"u9":{"title":"Unit 9: Tenant Files & Certifications","passingScore":70,"questions":[{"q":"The governing principle of tenant file review is:","opts":["Substance over form","If it is not documented, it did not happen","Only current documents matter","Electronic files face a lower standard"],"ans":1,"exp":"A reviewer cannot score intent or memory."},{"q":"Form HUD-92006 must be:","opts":["Completed by every applicant","Offered to every applicant, with a completed form or documented declination in the file","Filed only for elderly households","Sent to HUD"],"ans":1,"exp":"There is no third state called \"we mention it to everyone.\""},{"q":"Annual recertification reminder notices are sent at approximately:","opts":["60, 45 and 30 days before the anniversary","120, 90 and 60 days before the anniversary, after an initial notice","90 and 30 days before","30 days before only"],"ans":1,"exp":"4350.3 Chapter 7, with model notices at Exhibits 7-1 through 7-4."},{"q":"If a recertification is not submitted within 15 months of the prior anniversary:","opts":["Nothing happens until the annual","HUD terminates the certification and the owner repays assistance for a three-month period","The tenant is evicted","HUD processes it retroactively"],"ans":1,"exp":"The 15-month cliff turns chronic lateness into a cash repayment obligation."},{"q":"A household with a September 1 annual anniversary processes an interim effective March 1. The next annual is:","opts":["March 1","September 1 — the anniversary does not move","12 months after the interim","At the owner’s discretion"],"ans":1,"exp":"Resetting the annual clock to an interim causes drift and eventually a household exceeding twelve months."},{"q":"A verified income DECREASE reported March 12 and verified March 20 is generally effective:","opts":["March 12","April 1","At the next annual","Retroactive to January"],"ans":1,"exp":"The first of the month following the reported and verified change."},{"q":"A verified income INCREASE verified June 25 at a property with a 30-day notice requirement takes effect:","opts":["July 1","August 1, after proper advance notice","June 25","Retroactively to June 1"],"ans":1,"exp":"Implementing without documented notice is a tenant-rights finding, weighted more heavily than a timing error."},{"q":"The verification hierarchy, highest to lowest, is:","opts":["Self-certification, oral, written, EIV","EIV/UIV, third-party written, third-party oral, tenant self-certification","Third-party written, EIV, oral, self-certification","EIV, self-certification, written, oral"],"ans":1,"exp":"Verify at the highest level available and document why a lower level was used."},{"q":"A specialist computes income correctly from pay stubs with no EIV comparison and no documented verification attempt. The result is:","opts":["No finding","A finding for failure to document verification at the required level","A finding only if wrong","A narrative comment"],"ans":1,"exp":"HUD verifies the process, because process is the only assurance about unsampled files."},{"q":"HUD-9887/9887-A consent forms are valid for:","opts":["12 months","15 months","24 months","The tenancy"],"ans":1,"exp":"Verifications themselves are commonly treated as current within 120 days of the effective date."},{"q":"A live-in aide must satisfy:","opts":["One prong","Two prongs","Three prongs — essential to care, not obligated for support, and would not live in the unit but for providing services","Four prongs"],"ans":2,"exp":"Failure of any prong makes the person a household member whose income counts."},{"q":"A live-in aide earns $41,000. That income is:","opts":["Counted in full","Counted at 50 percent","Not counted at all","Counted above the asset threshold"],"ans":2,"exp":"The aide is also not a dependent and has no succession rights."},{"q":"A 20-year-old unmarried student with no children and income-ineligible parents is:","opts":["Eligible on his own income","Ineligible under 24 CFR 5.612","Eligible if enrolled part time","Eligible with self-certification"],"ans":1,"exp":"With no exception applicable, both the student’s and the parents’ income must be within limits."},{"q":"The same student has a two-year-old daughter living with him. He is now:","opts":["Still ineligible","Exempt from the parental income test — evaluate normally","Eligible only if married","Required to provide parental income anyway"],"ans":1,"exp":"Rejecting him would misapply the rule and create a familial status exposure."},{"q":"A family of five with three eligible members has full assistance of $840. Prorated assistance is:","opts":["$336","$504","$560","$840"],"ans":1,"exp":"3 ÷ 5 = 0.60; $840 × 0.60 = $504."},{"q":"A minimum rent hardship exemption requested March 12 suspends the minimum rent effective:","opts":["March 12","April 1","When the determination is made","Retroactively"],"ans":1,"exp":"Suspension beginning the month following the request is mandatory and does not await the determination."},{"q":"A household with TTP elements of $3.00, $5.00, no welfare rent and $25 minimum rent has, after suspension, a TTP of:","opts":["$0.00","$3.00","$5.00","$25.00"],"ans":2,"exp":"The exemption removes only the minimum rent element."},{"q":"An elderly head moves permanently to a nursing facility, leaving a 44-year-old daughter with no disability. The result is:","opts":["No change until the annual","The elderly/disabled family deduction no longer applies; an interim is processed and the rent increase requires advance notice","Termination of assistance","Automatic transfer of the deduction"],"ans":1,"exp":"Properties routinely process the composition change and forget the deduction."},{"q":"The current mandatory HOTMA compliance date for HUD Multifamily programs is:","opts":["January 1, 2024","January 1, 2025","January 1, 2026","January 1, 2027, per Notice H 2025-07"],"ans":3,"exp":"H 2023-10 set 2025; H 2025-03 moved it to 2026; H 2025-07 revised it to January 1, 2027."},{"q":"Under HOTMA, the streamlined income determination is available when fixed income is:","opts":["50 percent or more","75 percent or more","90 percent or more of unadjusted income, with full third-party examination at least every three years","100 percent"],"ans":2,"exp":"The annual recertification still occurs; only the verification method is streamlined."}]},"u10":{"title":"Unit 10: Income, Assets & EIV Compliance","passingScore":70,"questions":[{"q":"Annual income is:","opts":["Prior-year actual income","Anticipated income from sources outside the family for the next 12 months, unless excluded","Adjusted gross income per the IRS","A three-year average"],"ans":1,"exp":"Prospective, gross, from outside the family, and inclusive of everything not excluded."},{"q":"A tenant grosses $1,846.15 biweekly. Annual income is:","opts":["$34,122.40","$44,307.60","$47,999.90","$22,153.80"],"ans":2,"exp":"$1,846.15 × 26. Counting net understates income by nearly $14,000."},{"q":"A 16-year-old earns $7,800 and receives $9,048 in SSI. Countable income from this member is:","opts":["$0","$7,800","$9,048","$16,848"],"ans":2,"exp":"Earned income of a minor is excluded; unearned income such as SSI is counted in full."},{"q":"An elderly household with income $22,400, two dependents and $3,900 in unreimbursed medical expenses has total deductions of:","opts":["$4,588","$5,388","$4,188","$3,900"],"ans":0,"exp":"2 × $480 + $400 + ($3,900 − $672) = $4,588."},{"q":"The 3 percent medical/disability threshold is applied:","opts":["To each category separately","Once, across both categories combined, on ANNUAL income","To adjusted income","Only for households with children"],"ans":1,"exp":"Applying it twice understates the deduction and overcharges the tenant."},{"q":"A parent pays $6,400 for child care enabling employment earning $4,800. The work-related deduction is:","opts":["$6,400","$4,800","$1,600","$0"],"ans":1,"exp":"Capped at the income enabled; care enabling education is analyzed separately."},{"q":"Total Tenant Payment is:","opts":["30 percent of adjusted monthly income","The greatest of 30% of monthly adjusted income, 10% of monthly gross, the welfare rent, and the minimum rent","The average of the four elements","Contract rent minus the utility allowance"],"ans":1,"exp":"Recompute all four when auditing rather than checking the one the software used."},{"q":"Annual income $24,000 with $18,180 in deductions produces a TTP of:","opts":["$145.50","$200.00","$25.00","$282.00"],"ans":1,"exp":"10 percent of gross monthly income ($200) exceeds 30 percent of adjusted ($145.50) — the case software most often gets wrong."},{"q":"A household with TTP of $67 and a utility allowance of $148 has:","opts":["Tenant rent of $67","Tenant rent of $0 and a utility reimbursement of $81","Tenant rent of $81","No assistance"],"ans":1,"exp":"Reviewers check that the reimbursement is actually paid, appears on the voucher, and ties to the ledger."},{"q":"A Schedule C shows $25,400 net profit after $5,200 depreciation. Countable income is:","opts":["$25,400","$20,200","$30,600","$38,400"],"ans":2,"exp":"Depreciation is added back because it is not a cash expense of producing income."},{"q":"A tenant’s business shows a $3,200 net loss. The treatment is:","opts":["Reduce wage income by $3,200","Count business income as zero and count other income in full","Exclude the household","Carry the loss forward"],"ans":1,"exp":"A business loss does not offset other income for HUD purposes."},{"q":"Under 4350.3 as written, when total asset cash value exceeds $5,000 the property counts:","opts":["Actual income only","Imputed income only","The GREATER of actual and imputed income","The sum of actual and imputed"],"ans":2,"exp":"The comparison runs both ways — high-yield holdings can make actual income greater."},{"q":"A family holds $13,300 in non-interest-bearing accounts and cash. Counted asset income is:","opts":["$0","Imputed income on the full cash value","Imputed on the excess over $5,000 only","Actual income only"],"ans":1,"exp":"A zero-yield balance still generates imputed income above the threshold."},{"q":"A tenant transferred a $28,000 CD to her daughter for $1 fourteen months ago. Family assets include:","opts":["$0","$28,000","$27,999","$14,000"],"ans":2,"exp":"The difference between cash value and amount received, rolling off two years after the disposal."},{"q":"A house worth $184,000 with a $121,000 mortgage and $14,720 in costs of sale has a cash value of:","opts":["$184,000","$63,000","$48,280","$169,280"],"ans":2,"exp":"Market value minus debt minus reasonable costs of conversion."},{"q":"Under HOTMA, a household with $96,000 in a 401(k) and $14,000 in savings has countable net family assets of about:","opts":["$110,000","$14,000 — retirement accounts are excluded","$96,000","$52,787"],"ans":1,"exp":"Apply the exclusions BEFORE testing the ceiling."},{"q":"EIV use is mandated by:","opts":["Handbook 4350.1 Chapter 6","24 CFR 5.233, requiring use of the system in its entirety","The HAP contract alone","Contract Administrator preference"],"ans":1,"exp":"Failure to use EIV in its entirety may result in sanctions and disallowed costs."},{"q":"The EIV Existing Tenant Search must be run:","opts":["At each annual","Before move-in, as a pre-admission gate","Quarterly for all households","Only when a Multiple Subsidy hit occurs"],"ans":1,"exp":"Running it after admission cannot prevent the double subsidy it exists to catch."},{"q":"A name mismatch causes identity verification to fail so no income data returns. The danger is:","opts":["Denial of assistance","Staff read the blank as \"no wages,\" making an earning member invisible","The report cannot print","Access suspension"],"ans":1,"exp":"This is why identity verification reports are run monthly and corrected immediately."},{"q":"A quarterly New Hires report shows new employment and the property waits until the annual nine months later. This is:","opts":["Acceptable","Worse than not running the report — there is now a written record showing the property knew","A tenant violation only","Acceptable under the HOTMA thresholds"],"ans":1,"exp":"Running a report and not acting converts a monitoring gap into a documented failure to act."}]},"u11":{"title":"Unit 11: Waiting List, TSP & Fair Housing","passingScore":70,"questions":[{"q":"Income targeting under 24 CFR 5.653 requires that of Section 8 project-based units becoming available in a fiscal year:","opts":["25 percent go to very low income families","At least 40 percent be leased to extremely low income families at admission","50 percent go to extremely low income families","No targeting applies"],"ans":1,"exp":"Track it with a running fiscal-year admissions log, not a year-end count."},{"q":"A property fills 14 assisted units in a fiscal year. The minimum extremely low income admissions is:","opts":["5","6","7","4"],"ans":1,"exp":"40% of 14 = 5.6 → at least 6. Round up."},{"q":"A 2-bedroom is offered past three applicants for documented reasons. The file is:","opts":["Deficient — order was not followed","Compliant, because every deviation is documented and reconstructable","Deficient without HUD approval","Compliant only if the TSP permits skipping"],"ans":1,"exp":"The compliance work is in the notation — about thirty seconds per applicant."},{"q":"A property closes its list but keeps accepting walk-in applications in a drawer. This is:","opts":["Good service","A discrimination exposure — the beneficiaries are those told about the informal practice","Permitted if the TSP is silent","Required to avoid turning people away"],"ans":1,"exp":"Either the list is open to everyone or closed to everyone."},{"q":"A purge notice in standard print goes to an applicant who requested large print, and she is removed for non-response. The problem is:","opts":["The criteria were unreasonable","The execution created a fair housing exposure — the list needs a communication-needs field applied to every mailing","Purges are prohibited","The response period was too long"],"ans":1,"exp":"A five-minute system change prevents a category of complaint."},{"q":"The AFHMP is evaluated principally against:","opts":["Whether the property advertises at all","Whether the property did what its own approved plan says it does","The occupancy rate","Waiting list length"],"ans":1,"exp":"Perform the plan with dated evidence, or amend it and obtain approval."},{"q":"A TSP criterion permitting rejection of applicants who \"do not appear likely to be good residents\" is:","opts":["Appropriately flexible","Unenforceable and indefensible — it permits any decision and explains none","Acceptable if applied consistently","Required by 4350.3"],"ans":1,"exp":"Criteria must produce the same result when two staff apply them to the same facts."},{"q":"Which is a MANDATORY denial?","opts":["Any felony conviction","A person subject to LIFETIME sex offender registration","A poor credit score","An eviction filing in the last three years"],"ans":1,"exp":"The other mandatory denial is conviction for manufacturing methamphetamine on federally assisted premises."},{"q":"Under HUD’s 2016 guidance on criminal records, arrests not resulting in conviction:","opts":["May be used freely","Generally may not be the basis for denial","May be used for violent offenses only","Must be reported to HUD"],"ans":1,"exp":"An arrest does not prove the underlying conduct occurred; blanket bans are very difficult to defend."},{"q":"An applicant’s criminal record arises from an incident in which she was the DV survivor. The property:","opts":["May deny based on the record","May not deny on the basis of her status as a survivor, and must not use a record that is a product of the victimization","Must deny to protect residents","Must demand a police report"],"ans":1,"exp":"A mechanical screening process here produces a fair housing violation and a VAWA violation at once."},{"q":"Applying last year’s income limit table for four months after new limits publish most seriously results in:","opts":["A minor administrative finding","Applicants denied housing on a superseded standard","HAP overpayment","TRACS rejections"],"ans":1,"exp":"Calendar the annual publication and document the table update and its effective date."},{"q":"A household whose income rises above the limit after admission:","opts":["Must be terminated","Is subject to continued-occupancy rules, a different test from initial eligibility","Must reapply","Loses the lease at the anniversary"],"ans":1,"exp":"Conflating the two tests produces wrongful terminations."},{"q":"The seven federal protected classes are:","opts":["Race, color, religion, national origin, sex, familial status, disability","Race, color, religion, national origin, sex, age, income","Race, color, creed, ancestry, sex, marital status, disability","Race, color, religion, national origin, sex, veteran status, disability"],"ans":0,"exp":"Familial status and disability were added in 1988; state and local law frequently adds more."},{"q":"A rule requiring children to be accompanied by an adult at all times outside the unit is:","opts":["A lawful safety policy","A classic familial status violation","A Section 504 issue","Required by insurance"],"ans":1,"exp":"Rewrite rules to be conduct-based and neutral rather than person-based."},{"q":"A reasonable accommodation request may be:","opts":["Required to be on the property’s form","Made orally or in writing, by the person or someone on their behalf, and must be responded to promptly","Considered only at recertification","Approved only by HUD"],"ans":1,"exp":"Unreasonable delay is treated as a denial."},{"q":"For an assistance animal where the disability is not obvious, the property MAY:","opts":["Require a diagnosis","Request verification of the disability and the disability-related need from a reliable third party in a position to know","Require training certification","Charge a pet deposit"],"ans":1,"exp":"Assistance animals are not pets; pet rules, deposits and breed limits do not apply."},{"q":"At a HUD-assisted property, grab bars requested as an accommodation should be:","opts":["Installed at the tenant’s expense with restoration required","Made at the property’s expense under Section 504, subject to undue burden analysis","Denied as structural","Referred to the tenant’s insurer"],"ans":1,"exp":"\"Install at your own expense\" applies the FHA framework, not the Section 504 framework governing recipients."},{"q":"A newly constructed 84-unit assisted property must provide at least:","opts":["4 mobility and 1 hearing/vision units","5 mobility and 2 hearing/vision units","8 mobility units","2 accessible units total"],"ans":1,"exp":"5% of 84 = 4.2 → 5; an additional 2% = 1.68 → 2. Round up; these are minimums."},{"q":"The Section 504 substantial alteration trigger applies when a project has 15 or more units and alteration costs are:","opts":["15 percent or more of replacement cost","50 percent or more","75 percent or more of the replacement cost of the completed facility","Over $1 million"],"ans":2,"exp":"Run this computation at the planning stage of any rehabilitation."},{"q":"A maintenance technician receives an accommodation request in a unit. He should:","opts":["Tell the resident to come to the office","Pass it to the office the same day in writing — a request to ANY staff member is a request","Grant it on the spot","Ignore it unless written by the resident"],"ans":1,"exp":"Most stalled accommodation requests were made verbally to someone who did not recognize them as requests."}]},"u12":{"title":"Unit 12: VAWA, Leases & Financial Management","passingScore":70,"questions":[{"q":"VAWA housing protections cover survivors of:","opts":["Domestic violence only","Domestic violence and sexual assault","Domestic violence, dating violence, sexual assault, and stalking","Any violent crime on the premises"],"ans":2,"exp":"Protections apply regardless of sex, gender identity or sexual orientation."},{"q":"HUD-5380 and HUD-5382 must be provided:","opts":["At every annual recertification","At denial of admission or assistance, at admission, and with any notice of eviction or termination of assistance","Only when abuse is disclosed","At lease signing only"],"ans":1,"exp":"Recertification is not a required point; denial is the point most often missed."},{"q":"A property provides both forms at recertification and at move-in but not with rejection letters. It is:","opts":["Compliant — it exceeds requirements","Non-compliant — the denial trigger is missed entirely","Compliant if no one was denied","Non-compliant only on complaint"],"ans":1,"exp":"The most common VAWA notification error in the industry."},{"q":"When documentation of VAWA status is requested, the individual may satisfy it with:","opts":["A police report only","HUD-5382 alone — the choice among permitted forms belongs to the individual","Medical records","A protective order"],"ans":1,"exp":"Third-party documentation may be requested only where the provider has conflicting information."},{"q":"When documentation is requested, the individual must generally be given at least:","opts":["3 business days","7 calendar days","14 business days, extendable","30 calendar days"],"ans":2,"exp":"Document the request, the period given, and any extension, and apply the practice consistently."},{"q":"The emergency transfer two-prong test is met if the tenant:","opts":["Has a protective order","Reasonably believes they are threatened with imminent harm, OR was a victim of sexual assault on the premises within the preceding 90 days","Filed a police report within 30 days","Has lived there a year"],"ans":1,"exp":"Either prong suffices; evaluate and document both."},{"q":"VAWA documentation must be:","opts":["Filed in the tenant file","Kept in a separate secure location and never entered into a shared database","Sent to HUD","Shared with all adult household members"],"ans":1,"exp":"Other adult household members can typically access the tenant file — and the perpetrator may be one of them."},{"q":"Under bifurcation, if the removed member was the one whose eligibility qualified the household, the remaining members get:","opts":["30 days","90 days, extendable by up to 60 additional days","6 months","No period"],"ans":1,"exp":"Bifurcation also follows applicable law for terminating the removed member’s tenancy."},{"q":"In the Section 202 example where the elderly head leaves after an altercation, the survivor’s rent:","opts":["Is frozen by VAWA","Rises because the elderly deduction no longer applies — processed by interim, with proper advance notice","Falls automatically","Is unaffected until the annual"],"ans":1,"exp":"VAWA protections and ordinary certification mechanics operate simultaneously."},{"q":"VAWA 2022 expanded compliance review to examine which as separate line items?","opts":["Notification and confidentiality only","Notification, documentation acceptance, confidentiality, emergency transfer, and anti-retaliation","Emergency transfers only","Lease addendum execution only"],"ans":1,"exp":"A property cannot satisfy a reviewer by asserting general awareness."},{"q":"Which form is the VAWA lease addendum?","opts":["HUD-5380","HUD-5382","HUD-5383","HUD-91067"],"ans":3,"exp":"HUD-5383 is the emergency transfer request; 5380 the notice; 5382 the certification."},{"q":"A property adds a $75 maintenance-call fee clause to the HUD model lease. This is:","opts":["Permitted if disclosed","An unauthorized modification; amounts charged under it should be refunded","Permitted if reasonable","Required by the regulatory agreement"],"ans":1,"exp":"Legitimate damage policies must run through the mechanisms the lease provides."},{"q":"A termination notice stating only \"You are in violation of your lease due to disturbances\" is defective because:","opts":["It was not certified mail","It lacks the specificity that lets the tenant prepare a defense","It omits the rent owed","HUD did not approve it"],"ans":1,"exp":"Specific dates, conduct, lease provisions, and prior warnings with proof of delivery."},{"q":"Termination of ASSISTANCE and termination of TENANCY are:","opts":["The same action","Different actions — a household may lose assistance while remaining a tenant, depending on program and circumstances","Interchangeable at the owner’s option","Both prohibited without HUD approval"],"ans":1,"exp":"Both require the VAWA forms; use separate notice templates for each."},{"q":"A balcony-storage rule produced notices to 7 households while 14 are in violation. The problem is:","opts":["The rule is unreasonable","Selective enforcement — the remedy is documented periodic inspection applied to all units, tracked on a log","Balconies cannot be regulated","Insufficient notice"],"ans":1,"exp":"Selective enforcement is where discrimination claims live, even when the rule is lawful."},{"q":"Financial statements must be submitted:","opts":["Within 30 days of fiscal year end","Within 90 days of fiscal year end through FASSUB","By April 15","On request only"],"ans":1,"exp":"24 CFR Part 5, Subpart H — and the deadline follows each property’s own fiscal year."},{"q":"Required reserve deposits of $4,180/month skipped for four months produce a shortfall of:","opts":["$4,180","$16,720","$50,160","$8,360"],"ans":1,"exp":"Plus a compliance finding, a regulatory agreement default in substance, and likely restriction on distributions."},{"q":"A property makes every reserve deposit and holds $211,000 against $1.4 million of ten-year needs. It is:","opts":["Noncompliant","Compliant with the deposit requirement but inadequately funded","Exempt from further deposits","In default"],"ans":1,"exp":"The MOR tests compliance; asset management and risk classification test adequacy."},{"q":"An owner takes a $75,000 distribution when surplus cash is $41,000. The result is:","opts":["Acceptable if repaid next year","An improper distribution of $34,000 that must be returned, plus a regulatory agreement violation","Acceptable with the auditor’s consent","A finding only if insured"],"ans":1,"exp":"And potentially a residual receipts deficiency from the same act."},{"q":"An income understatement of $3,600 persisting 14 months produces roughly:","opts":["$1,260 owed by the tenant only","$1,260 in HAP overpayment AND $1,260 pursued from the tenant","$3,600 owed to HUD","No exposure if unintentional"],"ans":1,"exp":"30% of $3,600 ÷ 12 ≈ $90/month × 14 months on each side."}]},"u13":{"title":"Unit 13: Building Your MOR Consulting Business","passingScore":70,"questions":[{"q":"The strongest quantifiable value proposition under the risk-based schedule is:","opts":["A better relationship with the reviewer","Moving from Satisfactory (24 months) to Above Average (36 months) — being reviewed less often","Higher contract rents","NSPIRE exemption"],"ans":1,"exp":"Present it with the matrix, staff-hour cost per cycle, repayment exposure and disruption."},{"q":"A client asks you to guarantee an Above Average rating. You should:","opts":["Agree with a refund clause","Decline to guarantee an outcome and commit to defined scope and deliverables","Guarantee verbally only","Guarantee for Not Troubled properties only"],"ans":1,"exp":"Sell the process, never the outcome — the rating depends on judgment and execution you do not control."},{"q":"A regional manager without budget authority wants your help. The highest-return move is:","opts":["Several persuasion meetings","Equip her with a one-page internal memo and a scoped proposal she can send upward","Go around her to the owner","Offer a deep discount"],"ans":1,"exp":"You are enabling her to sell for you; that memo is free and converts at the highest rate."},{"q":"During an assessment a manager asks you to \"just fix\" some files. The risk is:","opts":["None","Performing remediation inside an assessment budget and later auditing your own work","Consultants may not correct files","It requires HUD approval"],"ans":1,"exp":"Note them and send a short remediation scope the next day."},{"q":"The mock MOR report should state:","opts":["A single predicted rating","A predicted rating RANGE with reasoning, plus what is achievable after corrective actions","No prediction","Findings only"],"ans":1,"exp":"A confident single prediction will eventually be wrong in front of a client."},{"q":"The optimal timing for a mock MOR is:","opts":["One week before","90 to 120 days before the expected review","Right after the prior review","Only after a bad rating"],"ans":1,"exp":"Enough time to cure, train and rebuild the package without leaving room for drift."},{"q":"A consultant’s file sample should be:","opts":["Random","Risk-weighted to mirror the reviewer — move-ins, interims, zero income, live-in aides, prior-finding files, EIV discrepancies, move-outs","Chosen by the site manager","Only problematic files"],"ans":1,"exp":"Which files a reviewer will pull matters more than how many."},{"q":"A 28-file sample yields 19 defects, 11 being the same missing EIV memo. The headline is:","opts":["19 errors","A single requirement failing 79 percent of the time — a systemic gap with a one-line fix","Careless staff","An oversized sample"],"ans":1,"exp":"Reporting by requirement rather than by file converts an audit into a recommendation."},{"q":"A corrective action engagement with eleven findings, two factually wrong, should:","opts":["Appeal all eleven","Appeal the two within 30 days AND submit corrective action on all nine valid findings by their due dates","Wait for the appeal outcome","Skip the appeal"],"ans":1,"exp":"Corrective action deadlines are not tolled by an appeal."},{"q":"A draft response says \"all 148 files reviewed and corrected\" when 31 required correction and 9 remain pending. You should:","opts":["Leave it — substantially true","State precise numbers with exhibits and a completion date for pending items","Omit the file review","Round up"],"ans":1,"exp":"Overstatement found at a follow-up converts a recoverable situation into an unrecoverable one."},{"q":"The default pricing structure for assessments and mock MORs is:","opts":["Hourly, uncapped","Fixed fee","Contingency on the rating","Cost-plus"],"ans":1,"exp":"Hourly is for genuinely open-ended work, always with an estimate and a not-to-exceed."},{"q":"A client proposes \"fixed fee, capped at 60 hours.\" This is:","opts":["A fair compromise","A fixed fee with the upside removed — offer the genuine hourly-with-cap alternative instead","Standard practice","Better for the consultant"],"ans":1,"exp":"You would carry overrun risk and forfeit the efficiency benefit."},{"q":"In a portfolio engagement using a reduced file sample at some properties, you must:","opts":["Keep it internal","State the reduced sample explicitly in the engagement letter","Sample all identically","Charge full price regardless"],"ans":1,"exp":"An undisclosed reduction in coverage permanently damages a portfolio relationship."},{"q":"Which insurance most directly protects a consultant whose advice produces a repayment?","opts":["General liability","Professional liability (errors and omissions)","Cyber liability","Workers’ compensation"],"ans":1,"exp":"Cyber liability is also essential because you handle tenant income data."},{"q":"A client asks whether they may terminate a household’s assistance. You should:","opts":["Give your best judgment","Provide the compliance framework and what the file must support, and state the decision requires counsel","Refuse to discuss it","Draft the notice for them"],"ans":1,"exp":"Your practice must match the disclaimer in your engagement letter."},{"q":"You are engaged by an agent; the owner later asks you to evaluate that agent. This is:","opts":["Two separate engagements","A conflict requiring disclosure, declination, or informed written consent","Fine — both are clients","Fine with different staff"],"ans":1,"exp":"The evaluative work damages one relationship regardless of the finding."},{"q":"Realistic annual billable capacity for a solo consultant is roughly:","opts":["2,080 hours","1,600–1,800 hours","1,000–1,200 hours","600 hours"],"ans":2,"exp":"A plan that is simply \"get more clients\" hits this ceiling within eighteen months."},{"q":"The post-MOR step that most reliably prevents a repeat finding is:","opts":["Filing an appeal","Verifying at day 30–90 that the corrective actions are still operating","A second mock MOR","A rent increase request"],"ans":1,"exp":"Corrective actions fail quietly months after submission, usually when the responsible person is absent."},{"q":"A consultant discovers they published a wrong figure in a client report. They should:","opts":["Wait to see if anyone notices","Tell the client immediately in writing with the correction and impact, fix it at their own cost, and notify their E&O carrier","Reissue quietly","Blame the changing regulation"],"ans":1,"exp":"Clients tell a much better story about a consultant who caught and fixed their own error."},{"q":"A sustainable practice targets what share of revenue from retainers and recurring work?","opts":["Under 20 percent","Roughly 60 to 70 percent","100 percent","It does not matter"],"ans":1,"exp":"Project work is lumpy; recurring revenue carries a practice through a slow quarter."}]}};
